const e={P0C00:["Drive Motor A Current Low","P0C00 reports that the current of drive motor A is lower than the controller expects for the commanded torque, the counterpart of P0C01 (current high). No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM and Stellantis use only P0C01 from this group. CARB requires hybrids to monitor electric motor performance.",["High-resistance or loose three-phase connection at motor A or the inverter","Inverter power stage for motor A not delivering current","Open or damaged winding in drive motor A"],["Weak or missing electric drive torque","Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Vehicle runs on the engine only or in limp mode"],4,3,["P0BFF","P0C01","P0C03","P0C05","P0BFD","P0A78"],"זרם מנוע הנעה‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0C01:["Drive Motor A Current High","P0C01 is the overcurrent check of drive motor A, meant to protect the inverter's IGBTs. GM sets it when any phase current exceeds 725 A on the 2011 Volt, 600 A on the 2012 two-mode trucks and in the 2016 to 2019 tables, or 396 A on eAssist; since 2016 a too negative d-axis current (about -540 to -310 A, depending on stator temperature) also counts. A fast and a slow counter catch sudden and intermittent faults;",["Damaged or improperly torqued three-phase cable joints (Stellantis S2608000006)","Damaged stator winding of motor A (GM PIP5246)","Outdated hybrid control software (Jeep TSB 08-144-23)","Failing power switch or current measurement in the inverter"],["Limp mode with warning lamp","Reduced propulsion or engine unavailable message","Rough running or loud noise from the motor generator"],4,3,["P0BFF","P0C00","P0C04","P0C05","P0BFD","P0BE5","P0BE9","P0BED"],"זרם מנוע הנעה‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0C02:["Drive Motor B Current","P0C02 is the general current code for drive motor B, the second electric machine, such as the second motor of a two-motor hybrid or the rear motor of an electric all-wheel-drive system. It applies to a current fault that is neither classed as low (P0C03) nor high (P0C04). No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly. CARB requires monitoring of electric motor performance.",["Loose, corroded or damaged three-phase connection of motor B","Fault in the motor B inverter or its current measurement","Damaged stator winding of drive motor B"],["Hybrid or electric drive warning lamp","Reduced power or loss of all-wheel drive","Limp mode"],4,3,["P0C03","P0C04","P0BFF","P0C08","P0BFE","P0BF1","P0BF5","P0BF9"],"זרם מנוע הנעה‏ B"],P0C03:["Drive Motor B Current Low","P0C03 indicates that drive motor B draws less current than the controller expects for the requested torque. It is the low counterpart of P0C04 and the motor B twin of P0C00. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly, and the GM hybrid tables use only P0C04 and P0C08 for motor B. CARB requires hybrids to monitor electric motor performance.",["High-resistance or loose three-phase connection of motor B","Inverter power stage for motor B not delivering current","Open or damaged winding in drive motor B"],["Weak or missing torque from motor B","Warning lamp for the hybrid or electric drive","Reduced performance or limp mode"],4,3,["P0C02","P0C04","P0C00","P0C08","P0BFE","P0A79"],"זרם מנוע הנעה‏ B — ערך נמוך"],P0C04:["Drive Motor B Current High","P0C04 is the overcurrent check of drive motor B and protects the IGBTs of its inverter. GM flags it when the highest phase current exceeds 725 A on the 2011 Volt or 600 A on the 2012 two-mode trucks and in the 2016 to 2019 hybrid tables; the newer tables also fail a d-axis current below a stator-temperature curve of roughly -556 to -315 A. Fast and slow counters cover sudden and intermittent peaks, Type A, one trip.",["Cross-threaded or loose three-phase cable bolts with metal debris (Stellantis S2608000006)","Shorted or damaged stator winding of motor B","Failing power switch or current measurement in the motor B inverter"],["Limp mode with red wrench or malfunction lamp","Sudden loss of electric drive power"],4,3,["P0C02","P0C03","P0C01","P0C08","P0BFE","P0BF1","P0BF5","P0BF9"],"זרם מנוע הנעה‏ B — ערך גבוה"],P0C05:["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C05 detects an open or missing phase between the inverter and drive motor A. GM checks each phase as the rotor passes its axis: if the peak phase axis current stays below 9 A while at least 23 A is commanded, with the rotor within 30 degrees of that axis and over 35 V on the inverter, about 200 failed samples set the code. It is Type A, one trip, on the Volt and two-mode trucks and Type B, two trips, on eAssist.",["Loose or open three-phase terminal between inverter and motor A","Software fault, cured by update (Jeep TSB 08-144-23)","Internal fault of the motor generator unit or open stator winding","One-off event that does not return after clearing (Volvo TJ 36863)"],["Propulsion system performance message or warning lamp","Reduced electric drive power","Loss of 12 V charging or noise from the motor generator on mild hybrids"],4,3,["P0C06","P0C07","P0C08","P0C01","P0BFD","P0BE5","P0BE9","P0BED"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ A U-V-W — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C06:["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C06 belongs to the phase circuit group of drive motor A and reports that a U, V or W phase line sits too low, typically a short to the negative high-voltage rail or to chassis ground. P0C05 covers an open or missing phase, P0C07 the opposite high state. No manufacturer documents P0C06 for a drive motor publicly; GM's hybrid tables use only P0C05 from this trio.",["Phase cable or winding shorted to ground or housing","Shorted low-side power switch in the motor A inverter","Insulation damage from heat, moisture or chafing"],["Electric drive shut down with warning lamp","Overcurrent or insulation codes stored at the same time"],4,3,["P0C05","P0C07","P0C09","P0C01","P0BFD"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ A U-V-W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C07:["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C07 is the high member of the drive motor A phase circuit group: a U, V or W phase line reads higher than it should, most likely through a short to the positive high-voltage rail or a power switch that stays on. Its siblings are P0C05 for an open phase and P0C06 for a phase pulled low. No manufacturer documents P0C07 publicly; among these three, GM uses only P0C05, which watches for missing phase current.",["Shorted high-side power switch in the motor A inverter","Phase cable shorted to the positive high-voltage path","Faulty gate driver holding a switch on"],["Immediate shutdown of electric drive with warning lamp","Reduced performance or engine-only operation"],4,3,["P0C05","P0C06","P0C0A","P0C01","P0BFD"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ A U-V-W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C08:["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C08 is the open-phase check for drive motor B, the motor B twin of P0C05. GM runs it on the 2011 Volt and the 2012 two-mode trucks: each phase is checked as the rotor passes its axis, and a phase axis current below 9 A while at least 23 A is commanded, with the rotor within 30 degrees and over 35 V on the inverter, counts as a miss. Around 200 failed samples set it, Type A, one trip.",["Loose or open three-phase terminal between inverter and motor B","Open stator winding in drive motor B","Inverter stage for motor B not conducting"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion power","Engine running more often than usual"],4,3,["P0C09","P0C0A","P0C05","P0C04","P0BFE","P0BF1","P0BF5","P0BF9"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ B U-V-W — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C09:["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C09 marks a U, V or W phase line of drive motor B that is pulled low, for example by a short to the negative high-voltage rail, to ground or by a power switch that conducts when it should not. It is the motor B twin of P0C06; the siblings P0C08 and P0C0A cover an open phase and a phase stuck high. No manufacturer documents P0C09 publicly, and GM's hybrid tables use only P0C08 for motor B phase circuits.",["Phase cable or winding of motor B shorted to ground or housing","Shorted low-side power switch in the motor B inverter","Insulation damage from heat, moisture or chafing"],["Motor B shut down with warning lamp","Overcurrent or insulation codes stored at the same time"],4,3,["P0C08","P0C0A","P0C06","P0C04","P0BFE"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ B U-V-W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C0A:["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C0A reports that a phase line of drive motor B reads too high, typically a short to the positive high-voltage rail or a high-side power switch that stays on. It mirrors P0C07 for motor A and completes the motor B trio with P0C08 (open) and P0C09 (low). No manufacturer documents P0C0A publicly; GM only uses P0C08 for motor B phase circuits, based on missing phase current.",["Shorted high-side power switch in the motor B inverter","Phase cable of motor B shorted to the positive high-voltage path","Faulty gate driver holding a switch on"],["Immediate shutdown of motor B with warning lamp","Reduced performance or loss of electric all-wheel drive"],4,3,["P0C08","P0C09","P0C07","P0C04","P0BFE"],"פאזת מנוע הנעה‏ B U-V-W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C0B:["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit/Open","P0C0B flags a lost or interrupted supply inside the motor A inverter. GM reads it from the IGBT module status: when the gate drive supply falls below its threshold, the module reports a bias fault; on eAssist the 5 V rail below 3.5 to 4.3 V or the 15 V rail below 10.4 to 12.6 V counts, with run/crank above 7 V. The 2012 trucks enable it between 9.5 and 18 V;",["Failed gate-drive power supply inside the motor A inverter","Low 12 V feed, blown fuse or poor ground to the inverter control board","Open high-voltage feed to the inverter, e.g. in a belt starter-alternator (Mercedes LI15.40-P-077947)"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Electric drive or motor generator unavailable","Engine cannot be started, only electric driving possible (Mercedes P3 hybrids)"],4,3,["P0C0C","P0C0D","P0C0E","P0A78","P0BE6","P0BEA","P0BEE","P0D2E"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C0C:["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit Low","P0C0C reports that the supply voltage of the motor A inverter is present but too low, while P0C0B covers a lost or open supply and P0C0D a supply that is too high. Which supply is meant depends on the maker: GM ties its sibling P0C0B to the gate-drive supply of the IGBTs, Mercedes to the high-voltage feed.",["Internal fault of the drive power electronics (Audi TSB 2081725)","Weak 12 V supply, poor ground or high-resistance connector at the inverter","Weak 12 V battery or DC/DC converter output"],["Red high-voltage system warning asking to stop safely","Loss of drive power or vehicle will not get ready to drive"],4,3,["P0C0B","P0C0D","P0C0F","P0A78","P0E8D"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C0D:["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit High","P0C0D reports that the supply of the motor A inverter is above its permitted range, while P0C0B stands for a lost or open supply and P0C0C for a supply that is too low. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM's tables for the sibling P0C0B show what the supply is: the gate-drive rails of the IGBT module, on eAssist a 5 V and a 15 V rail that are each watched against limits.",["Faulty voltage regulation of the gate-drive supply inside the motor A inverter","Excessive 12 V system voltage from the DC/DC converter or a charger","Supply or sense line shorted to a higher voltage in the harness"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced or no electric propulsion from motor A","Engine runs more often or the vehicle enters limp mode"],4,3,["P0C0B","P0C0C","P0C10","P0A78","P0D2E"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C0E:["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit/Open","P0C0E is the motor B counterpart of P0C0B: the inverter for the second motor has lost the supply of its gate-drive board. GM documents it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks. The IGBT module reports a bias fault through its status line when the supply of phase A, B or C fails.",["Failed gate-drive power supply inside the motor B inverter","Weak 12 V feed, blown fuse or poor ground at the inverter control board","Loose or corroded low-voltage connector at the power inverter module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion message, motor B unavailable","Engine runs continuously instead of electric driving"],4,3,["P0C0F","P0C10","P0C0B","P0A79"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C0F:["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit Low","P0C0F means the supply of the motor B inverter is present but below its permitted range. P0C0E covers a lost or open supply, P0C10 a supply that is too high, and P0C0C is the same fault on motor A. No manufacturer documents P0C0F publicly. GM's tables for P0C0E name the supply in question: the power feed of the gate-drive board, checked phase by phase with run/crank voltage between 9.5 and 18 V on the 2012 trucks.",["Weak 12 V battery or DC/DC converter output feeding the inverter electronics","Corroded connector or poor ground raising resistance in the inverter supply","Failing internal gate-drive power supply in the motor B inverter"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced propulsion, motor B unavailable","Fault appears mainly with low 12 V voltage, e.g. after standing"],4,3,["P0C0E","P0C10","P0C0C","P0A79"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C10:["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit High","P0C10 flags a supply of the motor B inverter that is above its permitted range. Its siblings are P0C0E for a lost or open supply and P0C0F for a low one; P0C0D is the motor A version. No manufacturer documents P0C10 publicly. GM uses only the open-circuit code P0C0E for this supply and describes it as the power feed of the gate-drive board, monitored through the status line of the IGBT module.",["Faulty voltage regulation of the gate-drive supply inside the motor B inverter","Excessive 12 V system voltage from the DC/DC converter or an external charger","Supply or sense line shorted to a higher voltage in the harness"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced propulsion, motor B unavailable","Vehicle limited to engine or reduced-power operation"],4,3,["P0C0E","P0C0F","P0C0D","P0A79"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C11:["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase U Over Temperature","P0C11 is a protective code: the temperature sensor at phase U of the motor A inverter reads a valid but excessive value. GM uses it across its hybrids. The 2011 Volt reads PIM sensor A against 98 °C, the 2012 two-mode trucks against 88 °C with a reset at 85 °C, both Type A; the 2016 Volt sets it above 126 °C for 1.2 s within 2 s, the 2019 table above 128 °C, Type A, one trip.",["Low coolant level or air in the power electronics cooling loop","Failed or weak electric coolant pump of the inverter loop","Clogged power electronics radiator or inoperative cooling fan","Sustained heavy load such as towing uphill in high ambient heat"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often with a hybrid system overheat message","Reduced propulsion power until the inverter cools down","Electric drive unavailable, engine runs continuously"],3,3,["P0C12","P0C13","P0C14","P0AED","P0AEE","P0A3C","P0A93"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A U — חום יתר"],P0C12:["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase V Over Temperature","P0C12 sets when the inverter for motor A measures an excessive temperature at phase V while its sensor itself is plausible. In GM's 2011 Volt table that is PIM sensor C above 98 °C; the two-mode trucks of 2012 use 88 °C with an 85 °C reset, the 2016 Volt 126 °C for 1.2 s out of 2 s and the 2016 ELR 102.5 °C, all Type A, one trip.",["Low coolant level or trapped air in the inverter cooling loop","Inoperative electric coolant pump of the power electronics","Blocked low-temperature radiator or failed cooling fan","Degraded thermal contact or failing power stage inside the inverter"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Propulsion power reduced, especially under load or in hot weather","Hybrid system overheat or service message"],3,3,["P0C11","P0C13","P0C15","P0BD1","P0BD2","P0A3C","P0A93"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A V — חום יתר"],P0C13:["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase W Over Temperature","P0C13 covers phase W of the motor A inverter: the controller sees a credible sensor reading that exceeds the overtemperature limit. GM's 2011 Volt uses PIM sensor E with 98 °C, the 2012 two-mode trucks 88 °C, the 2016 and 2018 tables 126 °C for 1.2 s within a 2 s window and the 2019 table 128 °C for 1.5 s, always Type A, one trip, so the MIL comes on at once.",["Coolant loss or air pocket in the power electronics loop","Electric coolant pump for the inverter stopped or running slow","Debris on the low-temperature radiator or cooling fan not running"],["Malfunction indicator lamp immediately after the fault","Reduced propulsion until temperatures drop","Engine runs more often, little or no electric driving"],3,3,["P0C11","P0C12","P0C16","P0BDB","P0BDC","P0A3C","P0A93"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A W — חום יתר"],P0C14:["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase U Over Temperature","P0C14 is the motor B counterpart of P0C11: phase U of the second inverter has become too hot while its temperature sensor reads plausibly. GM documents it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks. The 2011 Volt compares PIM sensor B with 98 °C, the 2012 trucks use 88 °C with a reset at 85 °C, the 2016 Volt 131 °C for 1.2 s in 2 s and the 2019 table 131 °C for 1.5 s, all Type A, one trip.",["Low coolant level or air in the power electronics cooling loop","Failed electric coolant pump of the inverter loop","Clogged low-temperature radiator or failed cooling fan","Long periods of high load on motor B, e.g. towing or steep climbs"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion power, especially under load","Hybrid system overheat message"],3,3,["P0C15","P0C16","P0C11","P0AF2","P0AF3","P0A3D","P0C31"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B U — חום יתר"],P0C15:["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase V Over Temperature","P0C15 reports overtemperature at phase V of the motor B inverter, based on a sensor reading that is valid but above the limit. GM applies it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks: PIM sensor D above 98 °C on the 2011 Volt, 88 °C on the 2012 trucks, 102.5 °C on the 2016 ELR, 131 °C for 1.2 s within 2 s on the 2016 Volt and in the 2018 table, every time Type A with one trip.",["Coolant loss or air in the inverter cooling loop","Electric coolant pump of the power electronics not working","Blocked radiator for the power electronics or fan failure","Poor heat transfer or ageing power stage inside the inverter"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion until the inverter has cooled","Engine runs more often than usual"],3,3,["P0C14","P0C16","P0C12","P0BD6","P0BD7","P0A3D","P0C31"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B V — חום יתר"],P0C16:["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase W Over Temperature","P0C16 is set by the motor B inverter when phase W runs too hot and the temperature sensor itself shows no fault. GM's tables give PIM sensor F above 98 °C for the 2011 Volt, above 88 °C with an 85 °C reset for the 2012 two-mode trucks, and 131 °C for 1.2 s within 2 s for the 2016 Volt, with 1.5 s in the 2019 table; each is Type A, one trip.",["Low coolant level or air in the power electronics loop","Electric coolant pump of the inverter failed or blocked","Dirty low-temperature radiator or inoperative fan"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion, notably at high load or high ambient temperature","Hybrid system overheat message"],3,3,["P0C14","P0C15","P0C13","P0BE0","P0BE1","P0A3D","P0C31"],"פאזת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B W — חום יתר"],P0C17:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Not Learned","P0C17 means the controller has no valid learned offset for the resolver of motor A, which it needs to align rotor position with the stator field. GM runs the learn at key-off and sets the code if it fails without a stored value: motor speed above 50 rpm, DC voltage below 192 V (200 V on the 2016 Volt), phase current below 15 A or a learn time over 1.4 s. The Volt is Type A, the 2012 two-mode trucks Type B.",["Offset learn not performed after replacing the inverter, motor or resolver","High voltage not reaching the inverter during the learn, e.g. contactor or HV cable fault","Loose three-phase ring terminals after a repair","Damaged motor stator winding (GM 21-NA-218)"],["Engine starts and stalls or will not start after a repair","Malfunction indicator lamp with reduced propulsion message","Electric drive unavailable"],4,3,["P0C18","P0C4E","P0A3F","P0A40","P0C01"],"חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A — לא בוצע לימוד"],P0C18:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Not Learned","P0C18 is the motor B version of P0C17: the resolver offset of the second motor has not been learned and no valid earlier value is stored. GM performs the learn at key-off; it fails if motor speed exceeds 50 rpm, DC voltage stays below 192 V, phase current stays below 15 A or the learn takes longer than 1.4 s. The Volt tables rate it Type A, one trip, the 2012 two-mode trucks Type B, two trips.",["Offset learn not completed after replacing the inverter, motor B or its resolver","No high voltage at the drive motor control module, e.g. contactor fault or disconnected HV cable","Control module needs reprogramming to enable a wide position sensor learn"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after a repair or module programming","Reduced propulsion or vehicle will not enter ready mode","Motor B unavailable for driving or charging"],4,3,["P0C17","P0C4F","P0A45","P0A46"],"חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B — לא בוצע לימוד"],P0C19:["Drive Motor A Torque Delivered Performance","P0C19 is a plausibility check: the torque that motor A actually delivers, calculated by the motor control processor, does not follow the slewed torque command. GM sets it when the difference exceeds 123 Nm for 0.1875 s within 0.2 s on the 2016 and 2018 tables, Type A, one trip; eAssist uses 36 Nm as Type B, two trips.",["Outdated power inverter module software (Stellantis 08-059-25, 08-231-24)","Implausible rotor position signal from the motor A resolver or its wiring","Faulty phase current sensing distorting the calculated torque","Internal fault of the inverter power stage or motor winding"],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Electric drive shuts off and the engine takes over","Engine stop-start no longer works or a service hybrid system warning appears"],4,3,["P0C1A","P0A1B","P0A78","P0A3F","P0BE6"],""],P0C1A:["Drive Motor B Torque Delivered Performance","P0C1A compares the torque command for motor B with the torque the motor control processor calculates as actually delivered. GM's 2016, 2018 and 2019 tables set it when the difference exceeds 132 Nm for 0.1875 s in a 0.2 s window, Type A, one trip, and only while listed resolver and motor control faults are inactive.",["Short in the internal transmission wiring connector (Chrysler Pacifica Hybrid 21-008-23)","Implausible rotor position signal from the motor B resolver","Hybrid control or inverter module software requiring an update","Faulty phase current sensing for motor B"],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Vehicle in limp mode with reduced propulsion","Hybrid system warning, engine runs more often"],4,3,["P0C19","P0A1C","P0A79","P0A45","P0BF2"],""],P0C1B:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Too High","P0C1B is a protection code: the electronics that drive the auxiliary transmission fluid pump measure a valid but excessive internal temperature. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly. GM shows the same function on the 2016 Cadillac ELR pump controller under its own code P1E37, an in-range overtemperature above 98 °C that could damage the inverter, checked only when the sensor itself is plausible.",["High transmission fluid temperature from heavy load, towing or a weak fluid cooler","Pump running against high resistance (binding, clogged filter) and heating its driver","Poor heat transfer from the module (mounting, contamination) or internal module fault"],["Engine auto-stop temporarily unavailable","Transmission or hybrid warning, MIL depending on manufacturer","Harsh engagement when driving off after a stop"],3,3,["P0C1C","P0C1D","P0C1E","P0C1F","P0B0D","P2797"],"טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0C1C:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0C1C is the general circuit fault of the temperature sensor inside the auxiliary pump control module, used when the signal is invalid without fitting the low, high or range checks. No manufacturer documents it publicly.",["Temperature sensor or its measuring circuit on the module board faulty","Unstable supply or ground of the pump module distorting the sensor reading","Moisture or fluid inside the module housing"],["Warning lamp or transmission message","Start-stop function inactive"],3,3,["P0C1B","P0C1D","P0C1E","P0C1F","P0B0D"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C1D:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0C1D means the internal temperature sensor of the auxiliary pump module delivers an electrically valid value that does not fit the other temperatures in the vehicle. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Board temperature sensor drifted or measuring circuit out of tolerance","Reference value wrong because the transmission fluid or coolant temperature sensor is faulty","Internal fault of the pump control module"],["MIL after repeated detection","Start-stop restricted or unavailable"],3,1,["P0C1B","P0C1C","P0C1E","P0C1F","P0711"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C1E:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0C1E reports that the signal voltage of the module's internal temperature sensor is below its valid range. With the usual NTC thermistor, low voltage corresponds to a very high temperature, so a short to ground looks like an overheated board. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Board temperature sensor or its signal path shorted to ground inside the module","Measuring circuit of the pump control module faulty","Fluid or moisture on the module board"],["Pump switched off as if overheated, auto-stop unavailable","MIL after two drive cycles"],3,3,["P0C1B","P0C1C","P0C1D","P0C1F"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C1F:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0C1F means the internal temperature sensor of the auxiliary pump module delivers a voltage above its valid range, which for an NTC thermistor reads as an implausibly cold board and usually indicates an open sensor path. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Board temperature sensor open or its solder joint cracked","Measuring circuit of the pump control module faulty","Ground offset at the pump module shifting the sensor voltage"],["MIL after two drive cycles","Start-stop restricted, pump in protective mode"],3,3,["P0C1B","P0C1C","P0C1D","P0C1E"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C20:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C20 means at least one of the three motor phases of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump carries no current, typically an open lead, terminal or winding. GM uses it in the pump controller of the Chevrolet Volt (2011-15 tables) and the 2016 Cadillac ELR: with the inverter in Run, high voltage above 35 V and the rotor within 30 degrees of a phase axis, the peak current of that phase is checked;",["Motor connector of the pump not fully seated, or loose phase terminal","Open phase lead between pump inverter and motor","Power switch of one phase in the pump inverter not conducting","Open winding in the pump motor"],["MIL and Service Vehicle Soon message","Reduced propulsion or no electric drive mode","Engine restart harsh or delayed"],3,3,["P0C21","P0C22","P0B01","P0B04","P0B07","P0C28"],"פאזת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ U-V-W — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C21:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C21 reports a low condition on the phase U, V or W circuits of the auxiliary pump motor, usually a phase lead shorted to ground or a voltage at a phase terminal that stays below what the inverter commands. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Phase lead chafed through and shorted to ground or the transmission case","Transmission fluid or water in the motor connector creating a leakage path","Low-side switch or phase sensing in the pump inverter faulty","Motor winding shorted to the stator core"],["MIL and transmission or hybrid warning","Auxiliary pump off, no engine auto-stop"],3,3,["P0C20","P0C22","P0C28","P0B01","P0B04","P0B07"],"פאזת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ U-V-W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C22:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C22 reports a high condition on the U-V-W phase circuits of the auxiliary pump motor, for example a phase lead shorted to the supply or a phase voltage that stays high while the inverter should pull it down. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Phase lead shorted to supply voltage or to another phase","High-side switch of the pump inverter stuck conducting","Phase current sensing in the inverter reads too high","Shorted turns in the pump motor winding"],["MIL and hybrid or transmission warning","Pump shuts down, harsh engagement after stops"],3,3,["P0C20","P0C21","P0C28","P0B02","P0B05","P0B08"],"פאזת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ U-V-W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C23:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit/Open","P0C23 is set by the controlling module, usually the PCM, TCM or hybrid controller, when the circuit to the auxiliary pump control module is open. J2012 does not name the line further; in practice it is the command or enable wire. Ford's 2016 F-150 2.7L bulletin shows such a command: the PCM drives the pump with a duty cycle that a scan tool can vary from 20 to 90 percent.",["Open command or enable wire between controller and pump module","Pushed-out or corroded terminal at the pump module or controller connector","Input stage of the pump control module faulty"],["Start-stop no longer works","MIL or transmission warning","Harsh engagement after an engine stop"],2,3,["P0C24","P0C25","P0B0D","P0C2B","P2796","U0287"],"מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C24:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit Low","P0C24 means the circuit from the controlling module to the auxiliary pump control module is held low, typically a short to ground on the command line. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly. How the line is used is visible in Ford's 2016 F-150 2.7L bulletin, where the PCM commands the start-stop pump by duty cycle and the technician checks the pump by sweeping that command from 20 to 90 percent.",["Command wire shorted to ground, e.g. chafed at the transmission case","Fluid or corrosion in the pump connector pulling the line low","Output stage in the PCM/TCM or input of the pump module faulty"],["No engine auto-stop","MIL or transmission warning"],2,3,["P0C23","P0C25","P0C2D","P2796","P2798"],"מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C25:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit High","P0C25 reports that the circuit from the controlling module to the auxiliary pump control module sits high, for instance a command wire shorted to battery voltage or an output that cannot pull the line down. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly. Ford's 2016 F-150 2.7L bulletin shows the type of signal involved: a PCM duty-cycle command that can be swept from 20 to 90 percent to prove pump operation.",["Command wire shorted to battery or another supply voltage","Output stage in the PCM/TCM stuck high","Input stage of the pump control module faulty"],["MIL or transmission warning","Pump runs continuously or start-stop is disabled"],2,3,["P0C23","P0C24","P0C2E","P2796"],"מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C26:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current","P0C26 is the general motor current fault of auxiliary pump A: the current drawn does not match what the commanded operating point should need, without clearly being too low or too high. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Low or aerated transmission fluid at the pump intake","Pump binding or contaminated, changing the motor load","Current sensing in the pump driver implausible","Outdated control software of the pump or controlling module"],["MIL or transmission warning","Harsh engagement after an auto-stop","Start-stop deactivated"],3,3,["P0C27","P0C28","P0C2A","P2797"],"זרם מנוע משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ A"],P0C27:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current Low","P0C27 means motor A of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump draws less current than expected, a sign that the pump is not loaded, for example because it sucks air or turns without delivering. Ford documents it twice.",["Air entering the pump supply tube (Ford 6F35, new tube with check ball)","Calibration of PCM and hybrid module (Ford Fusion/C-Max Energi)","Low transmission fluid level letting the pump draw air","High resistance or open circuit in the pump motor wiring"],["MIL on","Harsh transmission engagement after a start-stop event"],2,1,["P0C26","P0C28","P0C2A","P2797","P0B01"],"זרם מנוע משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0C28:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current High","P0C28 is the overcurrent protection of auxiliary pump motor A. GM uses it on Volt, ELR and later hybrids: the inverter trips when any of the U, V or W phases exceeds 35 A, in 2016 within about 4 ms of a 62 ms window, or when the d-axis current falls below a stator temperature limit, Type A, MIL on the first trip.",["Pump control software faulty (Cadillac CT6 update 18199, Ford Energi TSB 18-2282)","Pump seized, contaminated or running against blocked flow","Short between motor phases or to ground","Current sensing or power stage in the pump inverter faulty"],["MIL or service wrench lamp","Auxiliary pump shuts down, no auto-stop or electric mode"],3,3,["P0C26","P0C27","P0C2A","P0C22","P0B02","P0B05","P0B08"],"זרם מנוע משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0C29:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Driver Circuit Performance","P0C29 means the driver stage of auxiliary pump A does not behave as commanded, for instance the pump does not reach its target or the activation is reported as implausible. Volkswagen Group documents it on the 7-speed DSG 0GC (DQ381): VW lists P0C29 for the 2018 Golf R as implausible activation of auxiliary hydraulic pump 1 (V475), often with clutch and gear actuator codes as consequences;",["Auxiliary hydraulic pump V475 of early production faulty (VW/Audi 0GC)","Poor contact at the pump electrical connection","Transmission control software not updated per service action"],["Gearbox Malfunction warning in the instrument cluster","Consequential clutch or gear actuator codes, limp mode"],4,3,["P0C2A","P0C26","P0C27","P0C28","P2797"],"נהג משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C2A:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Stalled","P0C2A means motor A of the auxiliary pump is commanded but does not turn, or turns far slower than required. Ford documents it in TSB 17-2056 for the 2016 F-150 2.7L with start-stop built from 1 May 2016: with the fluid above 38 °C the technician commands the pump through the PCM at duty cycles from 20 to 90 percent; if it runs, the PCM gets new software, if not, the auxiliary pump is replaced.",["Auxiliary pump seized or jammed by debris (Ford F-150 2.7L replaces the pump)","PCM calibration causing false stall detection (Ford TSB 17-2056)","Very cold, thick fluid or wrong fluid type raising drag","Open phase so the motor cannot develop torque"],["MIL on","Start-stop inactive, harsh engagement after stops"],2,1,["P0C26","P0C27","P0C28","P0C29","P2797"],""],P0C2B:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal","P0C2B concerns the status signal the auxiliary pump control module sends back to the controlling module. GM uses it on the Two-Mode hybrid trucks and SUVs (Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Silverado, Sierra, 2ML70): the hybrid controller expects a complete fault status message from the pump controller every 1.5 s;",["Wiring or terminal tension fault between hybrid controller and pump control module","Early pump control module (GM date code 089-11 or older)","Key held in crank for a long time during a no-start"],["MIL after two drive cycles","Bumps, lulls or surges around auto-stops"],2,1,["P0C2C","P0C2D","P0C2E","P2797","P0C23"],"אות משוב מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר"],P0C2C:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal Range/Performance","P0C2C means the feedback signal from the auxiliary pump control module is present and electrically valid but does not make sense, for example a reported speed or status that contradicts the command. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Electronics of the pump module reporting wrong status or speed","Intermittent contact on the feedback line distorting the signal","Pump not following the command mechanically, so feedback and command differ"],["Service Vehicle Soon or MIL","Reduced propulsion power or no start-stop"],2,3,["P0C2B","P0C2D","P0C2E","P2797","P2798"],"אות משוב מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C2D:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal Low","P0C2D reports that the feedback signal from the electric transmission fluid pump module stays low, below its valid range. GM documents it for the 2021-23 Chevrolet Bolt EV and 2022 Bolt EUV in bulletin 21-NA-148: the driver sees Propulsion Power is Reduced and a Service Vehicle Soon lamp, with P0C2D, P2797 or P2798 stored.",["Faulty solder joints in the electric transmission fluid pump (Chevrolet Bolt, GM 21-NA-148)","Feedback line shorted to ground","Corroded or loose pump connector"],["Propulsion Power is Reduced message","Service Vehicle Soon lamp"],2,3,["P0C2B","P0C2C","P0C2E","P2797","P2798"],"משוב מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות נמוך"],P0C2E:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal High","P0C2E means the feedback line from the auxiliary pump control module sits above its valid range, for example shorted to supply voltage or no longer pulled down by the module. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",["Feedback line shorted to supply voltage","Output stage of the pump control module no longer switching","Lost ground at the pump module letting the line float high"],["MIL or Service Vehicle Soon lamp","Start-stop or electric drive restricted"],2,3,["P0C2B","P0C2C","P0C2D","P0C25","P2797"],"משוב מודול בקרת משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר — אות גבוה"],P0C2F:["Internal Control Module Drive Motor/Generator - Engine Speed Sensor Performance","P0C2F is an internal plausibility check of the hybrid control processor: the engine speed it reads through its own sensor path must match other speed information. In GM's Two-Mode truck tables (12OBDG06, 13OBDG06) the HCP compares its SPI-sensed engine speed with the calculated transmission input speed and with the engine speed received over CAN;",["Control module software flagging the comparison falsely (Ford Mach-E, fixed by reprogramming)","Speed signals disagree because of a faulty input or a disturbed CAN signal","Internal fault of the hybrid control module"],["MIL or hybrid system warning","One pedal drive or regenerative functions switched off (Ford Mach-E)","Reduced hybrid assist or limited power"],4,3,["P0335","P0336","P0725","P0A3F","P0C1A"],""],P0C30:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack State of Charge High","P0C30 is the counterpart of P0A7D: the battery controller finds that the state of charge of the high-voltage pack stays above its usable window. CARB requires hybrid OBD to flag a state of charge that can no longer be kept inside the manufacturer's range.",["Wrong SOC estimate from a drifting pack current or voltage measurement","Battery or hybrid control software not drawing the charge down as intended","Electric assist blocked by another fault, so the pack cannot discharge"],["MIL after two trips","Reduced or no regenerative braking","Changed brake pedal feel and higher fuel consumption"],4,3,["P0A7D","P0A7F","P0A80","P0A1F","P0AFA"],""],P0C31:["Inverter B Cooling System Performance","P0C31 reports that the cooling circuit of a second power inverter does not remove heat as expected; inverter A has its own code, P0A93. Vehicles with two separately cooled power electronics units, for example a front and a rear drive unit, need this second code. CARB requires hybrid OBD to monitor the parts used for inverter thermal management and, where feasible, to check the cooling performance itself.",["Low coolant level or air trapped in the inverter B circuit","Weak or failed electric coolant pump of the circuit","Clogged radiator or inoperative cooling fan"],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced drive power, especially under load or in heat","Coolant level low in the electronics reservoir"],2,3,["P0A93","P0B38","P0C74","P0C73","P2C72"],"מערכת קירור אינוורטר‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C32:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cooling System A Performance","P0C32 is the functional check of the battery thermal system that CARB asks for. GM tunes it per vehicle: the 2012 Volt (12OBDG01A) fails it when the average pack temperature stays above 40 °C in active cooling or below −17 °C in active heating, after 4200 s or 9000 s, Type A, one trip.",["Battery cooling fan stopping at low 12 V system voltage (GM eAssist)","Low coolant, air or a weak pump in a liquid-cooled pack circuit","Blocked cooling air intake or ducts of an air-cooled pack","Failed pack heater or chiller valve"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Reduced electric range or assist","Charging system or battery warning message (eAssist)"],2,3,["P0A7E","P2D3F","P2D40","P0C44","P0AAD"],"מערכת קירור סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C33:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit","Battery temperature sensor F is the sixth thermistor in the high-voltage pack; GM's Volt bulletin 18-NA-342 lists the F codes under temperature sensor 6 of nine. P0C33 is the general circuit code for an electrical fault the controller does not classify as low, high or erratic. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly: GM's hybrid tables use only the more specific siblings P0C34 to P0C37.",["Loose or corroded connector of sensor F at the sensing board","Failed thermistor F","Faulty input on the battery sensing board or module"],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced power or charging rate","Battery cooling running more than usual"],4,3,["P0C34","P0C35","P0C36","P0C37","P0BC2","P0C72","P0C86"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C34:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Range/Performance","P0C34 checks whether sensor F, GM's sensor 6, agrees with the rest of the pack. The controller compares its reading with the average of the other battery temperature sensors. GM's 2016 hybrid table fails it above 25 °C difference in 50 of 67 samples; the Volt and plug-in tables of 2016 to 2019 use 20 °C, in 2018 for 5 s within 6.7 s;",["Sensor F drifting or poorly seated on its cell group","Module software setting the code falsely in the cold (GM Volt)","Real local temperature difference from a weak cell group or uneven cooling"],["MIL on after two trips","Battery too cold or reduced power message","Limited charge and discharge power"],4,3,["P0C33","P0C35","P0C36","P0C37","P0BC3","P0C72","P0C86"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C35:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Low","Sensor F is an NTC thermistor with a pull-up on the sensing board, so its voltage drops as temperature rises; a short to ground looks like an extremely hot cell group. Low refers to the signal, not to the temperature. GM's 2016 table sets P0C35 when the converted value exceeds 160 °C in 8 of 10 samples; the Volt battery tables of 2016 to 2019 use above 87.5 °C for 1.4 s within 2 s;",["Sensor F signal wire chafed and shorted to ground","Internally shorted thermistor F","Faulty input on the battery sensing board"],["MIL after two trips","Battery cooling running at high speed","Strongly reduced charge and drive power"],4,3,["P0C36","P0C34","P0C33","P0A7E","P0BC4"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C36:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit High","P0C36 means the voltage from battery temperature sensor F sits above its valid range. With GM's NTC thermistor and pull-up, an open circuit, a broken wire or an unplugged connector pulls the input up and reads as an impossibly cold cell group.",["Open circuit or unlatched connector of sensor F","Thermistor F open internally","Module software setting the code falsely in the cold (GM Volt)"],["MIL after two trips","Battery too cold, plug in to warm message","Reduced electric power"],4,3,["P0C35","P0C34","P0C33","P0C37","P0BC5"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C37:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C37 targets a sensor F signal that jumps instead of following the slow temperature change of a heavy battery. GM documents the logic in its 2016 to 2018 hybrid tables (16OBDG02, 17OBDG02, 18OBDG02): every 100 ms the controller adds up the absolute changes of the sensor value over a window of 10 samples;",["Loose terminal or connector of sensor F not fully latched after pack work","Cracked sensor wire with intermittent contact","Faulty battery sensing board, often with several sensors affected"],["MIL after two trips","Intermittent hybrid warning, often on rough roads","Temporarily reduced power"],4,3,["P0C33","P0C34","P0C35","P0C36","P0BC6"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C38:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The DC/DC converter steps the high-voltage battery down to the 12 V system and replaces the alternator in hybrids and EVs. Its power stage carries temperature sensors so the controller can derate before the semiconductors overheat. P0C38 is the general circuit code for sensor A.",["Control unit software misjudging the sensor after long parking (Hyundai Palisade Hybrid)","Fault in the circuit of sensor A inside the converter or power control unit","Internal fault of the DC/DC converter requiring replacement"],["Hybrid system warning light","12 V charging reduced or switched off","Code appearing after the car has stood for a long time"],4,3,["P0C39","P0C3A","P0C3B","P0C3C","P0C3D","P0D32"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C39:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","P0C39 is the plausibility check of DC/DC converter temperature sensor A: its reading lies within the electrical range but does not fit the converter's operating state or a second sensor. FCA uses it on the 2021 Ram 1500 with the 3.6 litre eTorque mild hybrid, where the code is called temperature sensor 1 performance.",["Outdated hybrid control software (Ram 1500 eTorque, bulletin 08-037-21)","Sensor A drifting or with poor thermal contact to the power stage","Uneven cooling of the converter making the readings disagree"],["MIL, after one trip with updated Ram software","Mild hybrid assist or 12 V supply reduced"],4,3,["P0C38","P0C3A","P0C3B","P0C3C","P0C3E","P0E59"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C3A:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Low","P0C3A means the signal of DC/DC converter temperature sensor A is below its valid range. Low refers to the signal voltage: with an NTC thermistor and pull-up, as GM uses for its hybrid temperature sensors, a short to ground or a failed element reads as extreme heat. No manufacturer documents P0C3A publicly.",["Sensor A signal shorted to ground inside the converter or its harness","Shorted temperature element A, usually requiring converter replacement","Converter really overheated due to a cooling fault"],["Hybrid or EV system warning","12 V battery warning, system voltage dropping","Reduced power or restricted functions"],4,3,["P0C3B","P0C38","P0C39","P0C3C","P0C3F","P0D32"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0C3B:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A High","P0C3B reports a signal from DC/DC converter temperature sensor A above its valid range, typically a short to positive or an open circuit pulled up by the input. Mercedes-Benz documents it on series 243 (EQB) and 465 (G-Class, including the electric G 580): information LI54.10-P-079533 describes P0C3B00 as a short circuit to positive of temperature sensor A, stored together with P0E9700, P0E5400 and P0C4000 after…",["Faulty wake-up behaviour of the converter putting it into an error state (Mercedes 243/465)","Open circuit or short to positive in the sensor A circuit","Internal defect of the DC/DC converter"],["Red 12 V battery warning in the cluster","White or yellow high-voltage system warning","12 V battery no longer charged while driving"],4,3,["P0C3A","P0C38","P0C39","P0C3C","P0C40","P0E54"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0C3C:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","P0C3C covers a signal from DC/DC converter temperature sensor A that drops out or jumps for short moments while staying mostly within range. The heat sink of a converter warms and cools slowly, so fast jumps point to the signal path rather than to real temperature changes. No manufacturer documents P0C3C publicly.",["Loose terminal or moisture at the converter connector","Harness damaged by vibration with intermittent contact","Intermittent fault inside the converter"],["Intermittent hybrid or EV warning","Occasional 12 V charging interruptions"],4,3,["P0C38","P0C39","P0C3A","P0C3B","P0C41","P0E5C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ A — אות לא יציב"],P0C3D:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0C3D is the general circuit code for the second temperature sensor of the DC/DC converter, which turns high-voltage battery power into 12 V supply. Converters with two sensors watch separate parts of the power stage or cross-check one sensor against the other. No manufacturer documents P0C3D publicly.",["Connector or wiring fault in the sensor B circuit","Failed temperature element B inside the converter","Converter or hybrid control software misjudging the sensor"],["Hybrid or EV system warning","12 V battery warning"],4,3,["P0C3E","P0C3F","P0C40","P0C41","P0C38"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C3E:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Range/Performance","P0C3E flags DC/DC converter temperature sensor B as electrically valid but implausible, for instance when it disagrees with sensor A or with what the converter load should produce. FCA uses it on the 2021 Ram 1500 with the 3.6 litre eTorque mild hybrid under the name temperature sensor 2 performance.",["Outdated hybrid control software (Ram 1500 eTorque, bulletin 08-037-21)","Sensor B drifting or with poor thermal contact","Uneven converter cooling making sensors A and B disagree"],["MIL, after one trip with updated Ram software","Mild hybrid assist or 12 V supply reduced"],4,3,["P0C3D","P0C3F","P0C40","P0C41","P0C39","P0D32"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C3F:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Low","P0C3F is stored when the voltage from the second temperature sensor of the DC/DC converter falls below the lowest plausible value. These sensors are normally NTC thermistors fed through a pull-up resistor, so a low voltage corresponds to an extremely hot reading; a short to ground or a shorted element produces exactly that. No manufacturer documents P0C3F publicly.",["Sensor B signal line shorted to ground in the converter or harness","Shorted thermistor B, usually only fixable by replacing the converter","Converter actually overheating because its cooling fails"],["Hybrid or EV system warning in the cluster","12 V battery warning or falling on-board voltage","Reduced power or switched-off comfort consumers"],4,3,["P0C3D","P0C3E","P0C40","P0C41","P0C3A","P0D32"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ B — ערך נמוך"],P0C40:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B High","P0C40 means the second temperature sensor of the DC/DC converter delivers a voltage above its valid window, which on a thermistor circuit points to a short to positive or an open line pulled up to the reference. Mercedes-Benz documents it for the EQB (series 243) and the G-Class including the electric G 580 (series 465).",["Converter entering a fault state after incorrect wake-up (Mercedes 243/465)","Open circuit or short to positive in the sensor B wiring","Internal defect of the DC/DC converter"],["Red 12 V battery message in the instrument cluster","White or yellow high-voltage warning","12 V battery no longer charged while driving"],4,3,["P0C3D","P0C3E","P0C3F","P0C41","P0C3B","P0E54"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ B — ערך גבוה"],P0C41:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","P0C41 concerns a reading from DC/DC converter temperature sensor B that jumps or briefly drops out while it mostly remains inside the valid range. A converter heat sink changes temperature slowly, so abrupt steps indicate a signal problem rather than real heating. No manufacturer documents P0C41 publicly.",["Loose terminal or moisture at the converter connector","Harness chafed or cracked by vibration","Intermittent contact inside the converter"],["Hybrid or EV warning that comes and goes","Short interruptions of 12 V charging"],4,3,["P0C3D","P0C3E","P0C3F","P0C40","P0C3C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ B — אות לא יציב"],P0C42:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","P0C42 is the general circuit code for the first coolant temperature sensor in the liquid loop of a hybrid or EV battery, typically placed at the pack inlet. The battery controller uses it to decide when to pump, heat or chill the coolant.",["Battery controller software flagging the circuit wrongly (Volvo XC90 T8)","Corroded or loose connector or damaged wire at the coolant sensor","Failed coolant temperature sensor A"],["Check engine lamp, often without other complaints","Battery cooling or heating running on substitute values","Reduced charging or drive power in hot or cold weather"],3,3,["P0C43","P0C44","P0C45","P0C46","P0CD5"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C43:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0C43 flags battery coolant temperature sensor A as electrically valid but not believable. GM's 2019 hybrid tables describe a cold-soak comparison: once all high-voltage contactors and the battery heater have been off for at least 14,400 s, the hybrid controller compares inlet coolant temperature with the average cell temperature and with outside air, failing when the gaps reach about 23 °C and 22 °C;",["Too tight plausibility limits in the control software (Audi Q5 TFSI e, update 80C9)","Coolant sensor A drifting away from its characteristic","Added resistance in the sensor circuit shifting the reading"],["MIL after a cold start, often with no other complaint","Battery thermal management working on substitute values"],3,1,["P0C42","P0C44","P0C45","P0C46","P0CE1","P0A9C","P0CD6"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C44:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0C44 reports that the voltage of battery coolant temperature sensor A is too low. The sensor is an NTC thermistor, so a low voltage translates into a very hot reading, and a short to ground or a shorted element is the classic cause.",["Sensor signal wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Internally shorted coolant temperature sensor","Coolant or water in the connector bridging the pins"],["MIL and hybrid or EV system message","Coolant temperature shown implausibly high in live data","Limited charging or drive power"],3,3,["P0C42","P0C43","P0C45","P0C46","P0CD7"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C45:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0C45 indicates that the signal of battery coolant temperature sensor A sits near the reference voltage. On an NTC circuit with pull-up this looks like extreme cold and usually comes from an open circuit, an unplugged connector or a short to voltage.",["Open signal or ground wire, or connector not latched","Coolant temperature sensor open internally","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"],["MIL after two trips","Coolant temperature shown at about -40 °C in live data","Reduced charging power in extreme temperatures"],3,3,["P0C42","P0C43","P0C44","P0C46","P0CD8"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C46:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C46 points to a battery coolant temperature signal that jumps faster than liquid in the loop can physically change temperature. GM's 2019 hybrid tables explain the check: every 100 ms the hybrid controller takes the difference to the previous inlet coolant value, adds up ten of these absolute steps and counts a failure when the sum exceeds 64 °C.",["Loose or spread terminal at the coolant sensor connector","Corrosion at connector or ground point","Harness rubbing or cracked near the battery coolant lines"],["MIL that may go out again for a while","Jumping coolant temperature in live data"],3,1,["P0C42","P0C43","P0C44","P0C45","P0CD9"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C47:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit/Open","P0C47 means the controller sees an electrical fault on the line that commands battery coolant pump A. GM's 2019 tables catch open, short to ground and short to power by current: with the pump on, less than 200 mA or more than 3 A on the control line count as failures, with the pump off less than 120 µA; 4 s within 5 s set a Type B code.",["Corroded or painted ground point or corroded pump connector (Porsche Cayenne 92A)","Corroded splice or chafed harness in the pump circuit (Audi Q4 e-tron, Bentley)","Failed electric coolant pump or its relay"],["MIL, frequently the only sign","Electrical or drive system warning with reduced power","Vehicle refuses to become ready to drive"],3,3,["P0C48","P0C49","P0C4A","P0C4B","P0E1F","P0E1A"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0C48:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Low","P0C48 narrows a fault on the control line of battery coolant pump A down to a signal that stays too low, as caused by a short to ground or a driver output that cannot pull the line up. Ford lists the code for the 2020 Explorer, Police Interceptor Utility and Lincoln Aviator with the hybrid powertrain.",["Battery control module software (Ford/Lincoln 2020 hybrids, TSB 20-2345)","Pump control wire shorted to ground","Internal fault in the pump electronics pulling the line low"],["MIL on","Hybrid system message, battery power possibly limited"],2,3,["P0C47","P0C49","P0C4A","P0C4B","P0CFF","P2B29","P0E1B"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0C49:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit High","P0C49 is set when the control signal to battery coolant pump A remains too high, for example because the line is shorted to battery voltage or, on a pulled-up input, interrupted. Ford uses it on the 2020 Explorer, Police Interceptor Utility and Lincoln Aviator hybrids. Bulletins 20-2293 and 20-2345 list it right after P0C48 among a group of MIL codes for the battery and electronics coolant pumps;",["Battery control module software (Ford/Lincoln 2020 hybrids, TSB 20-2293/20-2345)","Control wire shorted to battery voltage","Control line interrupted at the pump connector"],["MIL on","Battery power reduced during long climbs or fast charging"],2,3,["P0C47","P0C48","P0C4A","P0C4D","P0CFF","P0E1C"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0C4A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Performance","P0C4A says pump A runs electrically but does not achieve its cooling effect. The 2011 Chevrolet Volt fails the test when the pump has been on for over 60 s and the pack inlet coolant temperature has moved by less than 5 °C, rated Type B. GM's bulletin PI0798A (Volt 2011–2015, Ampera 2012, ELR 2014–2016) traces the code after draining and refilling to trapped air, cured by a vacuum purge.",["Air trapped in the battery coolant loop, often after a coolant service","Low coolant level or a leak in the low-temperature loop","Worn or failing coolant pump","Battery control module software (Ford/Lincoln 2020 hybrids)"],["MIL shortly after cooling system work","Electric drive warning, reduced power or capped EV speed","Gurgling noise from the coolant loop"],2,3,["P0C47","P0C48","P0C49","P0CFF","P2B28","P2B29","P0C73","P0E1D"],"בקרת משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C4B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","P0C4B refers to the power feed of battery coolant pump A rather than its command line: the controller sees no or only an implausible supply voltage at the pump, typically because a fuse, relay, connector or wire in the feed is open. Ford documents the code on its 2020 hybrids.",["Battery control module software (Ford Escape HEV, Explorer and Aviator 2020)","Blown fuse or failed relay in the pump supply","Open feed wire or pushed-out pin at the pump connector"],["MIL on","Battery coolant pump not running, power limited when warm"],2,3,["P0C4C","P0C4D","P0C47","P0C48","P0C49","P0CFF"],"מתח אספקה משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0C4C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit Low","P0C4C reports that the supply voltage reaching battery coolant pump A is below the expected level while the circuit is not fully open. Typical reasons are high resistance in the feed, a corroded fuse contact, a weak relay, a poor ground or a short to ground that drags the line down. No manufacturer documents P0C4C publicly.",["High resistance from corroded fuse, relay contacts or connector","Poor pump ground connection","Supply wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["MIL or hybrid or EV warning","Coolant pump running slowly or intermittently","Battery power reduced when the pack warms up"],2,3,["P0C4B","P0C4D","P0C47","P0C48","P0CFF"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מתח נמוך במעגל מתח האספקה"],P0C4D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit High","P0C4D is the counterpart to P0C4C: the voltage measured on the supply of battery coolant pump A is higher than the controller allows. That can come from a feed wire shorted to another, more highly loaded circuit, from a relay that stays closed when the pump should be off, or from an overall excessive on-board voltage, for example a faulty DC/DC converter. No manufacturer documents P0C4D publicly.",["Supply wire shorted to another voltage-carrying circuit","Pump relay sticking closed","Excessive 12 V system voltage from a faulty DC/DC converter"],["MIL or hybrid or EV warning","Coolant pump running although the system is switched off"],2,3,["P0C4B","P0C4C","P0C47","P0C49"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מתח גבוה במעגל מתח האספקה"],P0C4E:["Drive Motor A Position Exceeded Learning Limit","P0C4E says the resolver offset of drive motor A was learned, yet the learned value is unacceptable. That offset lines up the zero point of the resolver with the rotor magnets. GM rejects a correction angle beyond 30 degrees, or a new value that differs from the stored one by more than 10 degrees; the learn happens at key-off with the motor under 20 rpm and over 192 V on the DC link.",["Resolver or rotor position shifted after replacing the motor, resolver stator or transaxle","Internal transaxle fault on affected Kia Sorento PHEV models (TSB TRA112)","Hybrid or motor control software that needs an update","Damaged resolver that delivers a shifted angle"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often first noticed after a repair","Jerk or rough feel when switching between electric and hybrid drive","Reduced electric drive power"],4,3,["P0C4F","P0C17","P0A3F","P0A40"],""],P0C4F:["Drive Motor B Position Exceeded Learning Limit","P0C4F covers the second traction motor: its resolver offset learn finished, but the angle it produced lies outside what the controller will accept. GM applies the same two tests as for motor A, an absolute correction above 30 degrees or a change of over 10 degrees against the value kept from earlier learns, both checked at key-off with the rotor nearly still and more than 192 V available.",["Motor B resolver stator fitted in a different position after a repair","Learn disturbed by a moving rotor or unstable high voltage at key-off","Damaged motor B resolver giving a shifted angle"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after shutdown cycles","Reduced propulsion or motor B unavailable","Unusual noise or shudder under electric drive"],4,3,["P0C4E","P0C18","P0A45","P0A46"],""],P0C50:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A","A resolver has two pickup windings set 90 degrees apart, one giving a sine and one a cosine signal. The codes P0C50 onward judge each of those circuits on its own, while P0A3F to P0A43 judge the combined angle signal. GM names the S1/S3 winding Circuit A and the S2/S4 winding Circuit B.",["Open wire or loose terminal in the Circuit A (first pickup winding) lines","Corroded or damaged connector between resolver and motor control module","Defective pickup winding inside the resolver stator","Fault in the resolver input stage of the motor control module"],["Hybrid or drive system warning together with the check engine lamp","Electric drive switched off or reduced power","Vehicle may not reach ready mode"],3,3,["P0C51","P0C52","P0C53","P0C54","P0C5A","P0A3F"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A A"],P0C51:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Range/Performance","P0C51 means the first resolver winding of motor A (Circuit A) delivers a signal that is electrically present but not believable, for example unsteady or out of step with the second winding. Bentley uses it on 2025 Continental GT, GTC and Flying Spur hybrids, logged by the e-drive control unit at address 0051 next to P0A4000 and later P0C5B00, with a drive system message in the cluster.",["Weak terminal grip or bent pins at the sensor or power electronics connector","Moisture, oil or corrosion in the resolver connectors","Faulty 90-degree connector on the affected Bentley models (TPI 2080242/3)","Resolver winding with degraded output"],["Drive system fault message in the instrument cluster","No electric or hybrid driving mode available","Fault may come and go with temperature or vibration"],4,3,["P0C50","P0C52","P0C53","P0C54","P0C5B","P0A40"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C52:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Low","P0C52 reports that the DC reference level on Circuit A of the motor A resolver, the S1/S3 winding in GM terms, has dropped too low, which points to a short to ground or a line pulled down. GM fails it below 0.5 V on the 2011 Volt, 2012 two-mode trucks, 2013 eAssist and 2016 ELR, and below 0.10 V on the 2019 Volt.",["Chafed or pinched transmission harness shorting a Circuit A line to ground (Bentley TPI 2066714)","Water or corrosion in the resolver connector","Shorted S1/S3 winding inside the resolver stator","Faulty resolver input in the motor control module"],["Check engine lamp, often with P0C5C stored at the same time","Electric drive shut off or power reduced","On some hybrids the engine runs constantly or the car will not start"],3,3,["P0C53","P0C5C","P0C50","P0C54","P0A41","P0A2D"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A A — ערך נמוך"],P0C53:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A High","P0C53 is the high counterpart of P0C52: the reference level on the S1/S3 winding circuit of the motor A resolver rises above its window, typical of a short to a voltage source or an open line that floats up. GM uses an upper limit of 4.5 V on the 2011 Volt, 2013 eAssist and 2016 Volt and ELR, 4.9 V on the 2019 Volt and only 3.0 V on the 2012 two-mode Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Sierra and Silverado hybrids.",["Open S1 or S3 line or a terminal that has backed out, letting the input drift high","Circuit A line shorted to a voltage-carrying wire in the harness","Broken S1/S3 winding in the resolver stator","Faulty resolver input in the motor control module"],["Check engine lamp with hybrid system message","Reduced propulsion or electric drive unavailable","Rough engine running on vehicles that use motor A for starting"],3,3,["P0C52","P0C5D","P0C50","P0C54","P0A42"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A A — ערך גבוה"],P0C54:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Intermittent/Erratic","P0C54 stands for short dropouts or jumps on Circuit A of the motor A resolver that never last long enough to count as a steady low or high fault. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Loose or poorly crimped terminal in a Circuit A line","Harness rubbing or flexing at a point that moves with the powertrain","Moisture or contamination in the resolver plug"],["Warning lamp that appears and disappears between trips","Brief loss of electric drive or a short jerk","Fault often gone after a restart"],3,3,["P0C50","P0C52","P0C53","P0C5E","P0A43"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A A — אות לא יציב"],P0C55:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit A","P0C55 is the general circuit code for the first pickup winding (Circuit A) of the resolver on the second traction motor. It applies when the motor B controller recognises a fault on that winding but cannot assign it to a low or high level. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM, which runs two motors in the Volt, ELR and the two-mode trucks, files motor B winding faults under P0C57 and P0C58 instead.",["Open wire or loose terminal in the motor B Circuit A lines","Damaged or corroded motor B resolver connector","Defective first pickup winding in the motor B resolver"],["Check engine lamp with hybrid system warning","Motor B unavailable, reduced propulsion","Engine may run more often because the second motor is offline"],3,3,["P0C56","P0C57","P0C58","P0C59","P0C5F","P0A45"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B A"],P0C56:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit A Range/Performance","P0C56 flags a plausibility problem on Circuit A of the motor B resolver: the winding is connected and its level sits inside the electrical window, yet the signal it carries does not fit, for instance because it wanders or no longer matches the second winding. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Poor pin fit or contamination at the motor B resolver connector","Damaged shield or twisted pair letting interference onto Circuit A","Motor B resolver winding with weakened output"],["Hybrid warning and check engine lamp","Motor B torque limited or switched off","Occasional shudder when motor B takes load"],4,3,["P0C55","P0C57","P0C58","P0C59","P0C60","P0A46"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C57:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit A Low","P0C57 means the reference level on Circuit A of the motor B resolver, the S1/S3 winding in GM wiring, has sunk below its limit. GM checks it exactly like the motor A line: under 0.5 V on the 2011 and 2016 Volt and the 2012 two-mode trucks, under 0.10 V on the 2019 Volt, with Type A on the early cars and Type B from 2016. In the field it rarely stands alone.",["Faulty motor B position sensor (resolver stator) on Volt and ELR (GM PIC5856)","Circuit A line of motor B shorted to ground in the harness","Drive motor generator control module not on the latest calibration"],["Service engine soon lamp, current or in history","Often stored together with P0C58 and P0C62","Reduced propulsion"],4,3,["P0C58","P0C62","P0C55","P0C61","P0A47"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B A — ערך נמוך"],P0C58:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit A High","P0C58 sets when the S1/S3 winding circuit of the motor B resolver shows a reference level above its window, as from an open lead drifting up or a short to voltage. GM limits are 4.5 V on the 2011 and 2016 Volt, 4.9 V on the 2019 Volt and 3.0 V on the 2012 two-mode trucks, with the fast high-side timing of 20 out of 30 samples on early cars.",["Faulty motor B position sensor on 2011-13 Volt and 2014-16 ELR (GM PIC5856)","Open S1 or S3 line or spread terminal at the resolver connector","Motor control module running an older calibration"],["Check engine lamp, frequently together with P0C62","Reduced propulsion power","Fault may only be in history memory"],4,3,["P0C57","P0C62","P0C55","P0C59","P0A48"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B A — ערך גבוה"],P0C59:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit A Intermittent/Erratic","P0C59 reports that Circuit A of the motor B resolver drops out or jumps for moments only, without a fault that persists as low, high or implausible. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. Where a dual-motor system uses it, the cause is nearly always mechanical: a terminal that loses grip under vibration, a cable section that flexes with the transmission, or water creeping into a plug.",["Poorly crimped or loose terminal in the motor B Circuit A path","Wire breaking inside its insulation where the harness flexes","Water ingress at the resolver or pass-through connector"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Short loss of motor B assistance or a jerk","Intermittent start problems on some hybrids"],3,3,["P0C55","P0C57","P0C58","P0C63","P0A49"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B A — אות לא יציב"],P0C5A:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit B","P0C5A is the base circuit code for Circuit B, the second pickup winding of the motor A resolver; GM calls it the S2/S4 winding, set 90 degrees from the S1/S3 winding of Circuit A. The code is meant for a fault on that winding which the controller cannot class as a low or high level. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM checks the S2/S4 line only through P0C5C and P0C5D.",["Open wire or loose terminal in the S2/S4 (Circuit B) lines","Damaged or corroded resolver connector","Defective second pickup winding in the resolver stator"],["Check engine lamp with drive system warning","Electric drive limited or unavailable","Vehicle may refuse to enter ready mode"],3,3,["P0C5B","P0C5C","P0C5D","P0C5E","P0C50","P0A3F"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A B"],P0C5B:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit B Range/Performance","P0C5B is set when the second resolver winding of motor A (Circuit B) stays within its electrical limits yet carries a signal that does not make sense next to the first winding. Bentley added it in revision 3 of bulletin 2080242 for the Continental GT, GTC and Flying Spur hybrids: P0C5B00 now appears alongside P0C5100 and P0A4000, which shows that both windings lose plausibility at once when the shared connection is…",["Insufficient terminal grip at the sensor or power electronics connector","Faulty 90-degree connector on affected Bentley hybrids (TPI 2080242/3)","Corrosion, oil or moisture in the resolver plugs"],["Drive system fault shown in the cluster","Electric drive mode not available","Usually stored together with P0C51 and P0A40"],4,3,["P0C5A","P0C5C","P0C5D","P0C5E","P0C51","P0A40"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C5C:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit B Low","P0C5C tells that the reference level on the S2/S4 winding of the motor A resolver, Circuit B, has fallen below its floor. GM uses 0.5 V on the 2011 Volt, 2012 two-mode trucks, 2013 eAssist and the 2016 Volt and ELR, 0.10 V on the 2019 Volt; the low side needs a longer confirmation than the high side, for example 0.525 s within 0.8375 s on the 2016 Volt. On the single-motor eAssist it is a two-trip Type B code.",["Transmission harness chafed or pinched, grounding an S2/S4 line (Bentley TPI 2066714)","Moisture or corrosion in the resolver connector","Shorted S2/S4 winding in the resolver stator"],["Engine warning light, often with P0C52 and a motor temperature code","Electric drive limited or switched off","On mild hybrids loss of start-stop and assist"],3,3,["P0C5D","P0C52","P0C5A","P0C5E","P0A41","P0A2D"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A B — ערך נמוך"],P0C5D:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit B High","P0C5D means the S2/S4 winding circuit of the motor A resolver, Circuit B, reads a reference level above its ceiling. This hints at an open wire whose input floats toward the supply, or contact with a voltage-carrying conductor. GM applies 4.5 V on most of its hybrids, 4.9 V on the 2019 Volt and a much lower 3.0 V on the 2012 two-mode Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Sierra and Silverado.",["Open S2 or S4 wire or backed-out terminal","S2/S4 line touching a voltage-carrying conductor","Broken S2/S4 winding in the resolver stator","Faulty resolver input stage in the motor control module"],["Check engine lamp after one or two trips depending on the vehicle","Electric assist or drive unavailable","Auto stop disabled on mild hybrids"],3,3,["P0C5C","P0C53","P0C5A","P0C5E","P0A42"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A B — ערך גבוה"],P0C5E:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit B Intermittent/Erratic","P0C5E captures brief interruptions or spikes on Circuit B, the second winding of the motor A resolver, that clear again before a firm low, high or plausibility fault can be confirmed. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM leaves such events to the debounce of P0C5C and P0C5D, which on its eAssist cars tolerates short outages and only reports a repeating pattern.",["Loose or weak terminal in the S2/S4 path","Harness rubbing on the transmission because of a missing or broken clip","Moisture entering the resolver plug"],["Warning light appearing only now and then","Momentary drop of electric torque","No fault found on a stationary check"],3,3,["P0C5A","P0C5C","P0C5D","P0C54","P0A43"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A B — אות לא יציב"],P0C5F:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit B","P0C5F is the umbrella circuit code for Circuit B of the motor B resolver, which GM wires as the S2/S4 winding of the second traction motor. It fits a fault on that winding that the controller notices but cannot assign to a low or high reading. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; on GM's dual-motor hybrids the same winding is covered by P0C61 and P0C62 only.",["Open wire or loose terminal in the motor B S2/S4 lines","Damaged motor B resolver or pass-through connector","Defective second pickup winding in the motor B resolver"],["Hybrid warning and check engine lamp","Motor B shut off, lower propulsion","Possible no-start on hybrids that crank with motor B"],3,3,["P0C60","P0C61","P0C62","P0C63","P0C55","P0A45"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B B"],P0C60:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit B Range/Performance","P0C60 indicates that the second winding of the motor B resolver (Circuit B) is electrically intact but delivers values the controller rejects as unrealistic, for example an amplitude that no longer agrees with the first winding or an angle that jumps against the measured speed. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Raised contact resistance at the motor B resolver connector","Motor B resolver stator shifted or damaged","Interference from the high-voltage phase cables through a damaged shield"],["Hybrid system warning with check engine lamp","Motor B output limited","Vibration or noise when motor B is under load"],4,3,["P0C5F","P0C61","P0C62","P0C63","P0C56","P0A46"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C61:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit B Low","P0C61 reports a reference level below the lower limit on the S2/S4 winding circuit of the motor B resolver. GM uses 0.5 V on the 2011 and 2016 Volt and the 2012 two-mode trucks, 0.10 V on the 2019 Volt, and needs the low reading for 50 of 80 samples on early cars, 0.25 s within 0.375 s on the 2019 model.",["Poor crimp at the transmission pass-through connector (GM PIP4607A, 2008 Tahoe/Yukon Hybrid)","S2/S4 line of motor B shorted to ground","Shorted S2/S4 winding in the motor B resolver"],["Engine does not crank or cranks only intermittently","Check engine lamp with several resolver codes of motor B","Reduced propulsion"],4,3,["P0C62","P0C57","P0C5F","P0C63","P0A45","P0A46"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B B — ערך נמוך"],P0C62:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit B High","P0C62 means the reference level on the S2/S4 winding circuit of the motor B resolver climbs above its upper limit. GM triggers it at 4.5 V on the 2011 and 2016 Volt and ELR, 4.9 V on the 2019 Volt and 3.0 V on the 2012 two-mode trucks; on the 2016 Volt the level must stay high for 0.2125 s within 0.3125 s, rated Type B over two trips.",["Faulty motor B position sensor on 2011-13 Volt and 2014-16 ELR (GM PIC5856)","Open S2 or S4 wire or terminal backed out of the connector","Drive motor generator control module without the latest calibration"],["Service engine soon lamp, often with P0C58","Reduced propulsion power","Code may return after clearing until the sensor is replaced"],4,3,["P0C61","P0C58","P0C57","P0C5F","P0A48"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B B — ערך גבוה"],P0C63:["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit B Intermittent/Erratic","P0C63 is reserved for short-lived dropouts or erratic jumps on Circuit B, the second winding of the motor B resolver, that do not settle into a steady fault. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Weak crimp or loose terminal in the motor B S2/S4 path, including pass-through connectors","Intermittent wire break near moving powertrain parts","Moisture or corrosion causing unstable contact"],["Intermittent warning lamp","Occasional start problems or short loss of motor B","Fault not present during a workshop check"],3,3,["P0C5F","P0C61","P0C62","P0C59","P0A49"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ B B — אות לא יציב"],P0C64:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit A","P0C64 is the catch-all entry for the first pickup circuit of the generator resolver, the sensor that reports rotor angle so the inverter can energise the generator phases at the right moment. It is used when the controller judges that circuit faulty without sorting the fault into low, high, implausible or sporadic. No manufacturer documents P0C64 publicly.",["Open, chafed or shorted lead in the first resolver pickup winding circuit","Corroded or loose resolver connector between generator and inverter","Damaged pickup winding inside the generator resolver stator","Fault in the generator control electronics of the inverter or power drive unit"],["Hybrid system or malfunction indicator lamp on","Engine runs but the high-voltage battery is no longer charged by the generator","Reduced power or hybrid functions disabled"],4,3,["P0C65","P0C66","P0C67","P0C68","P0C69","P0A4B"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ A"],P0C65:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit A Range/Performance","P0C65 flags circuit A of the generator resolver as electrically present but not believable. The trace stays between the hard low and high limits, yet its amplitude, centre voltage or relation to the second winding no longer fits the rotor angle the controller expects, for example after the sensor stator has shifted or when the phase cables couple noise into it. No manufacturer documents P0C65 publicly.",["Resolver stator loose or shifted, for instance after work on the generator or transaxle","Interference from the phase cables because of a misrouted or damaged resolver harness","Partially damaged winding that weakens the circuit A signal","Generator control software that misjudges a sound signal"],["Hybrid system warning light","Generator use restricted, less charging while driving","Occasional roughness when the engine starts or stops in hybrid mode"],4,3,["P0C64","P0C66","P0C67","P0C6A","P0A4C"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C66:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit A Low","P0C66 means the signal of circuit A of the generator resolver has dropped below its lower limit. Mitsubishi uses it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV for the sine winding: the generator control unit, supplied with at least 8 V, watches the line continuously and sets the code when the sine voltage stays at or below 2.000 V for 0.21 s. A short to ground on a sine lead or a damaged winding are the classic causes;",["Sine lead of the resolver shorted to ground or to the housing","Shorted or damaged sine winding in the generator resolver","Faulty input stage in the power drive unit (generator control unit)","Damaged or contaminated resolver connector pins"],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning shortly after switching on","Generator out of service, no charging by the engine","Reduced driving power"],4,3,["P0C64","P0C67","P0C6B","P0A4D","P0A4B"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0C67:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit A High","With P0C67 the controller sees circuit A of the generator resolver stuck above its upper limit. On the 2023 Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi assigns it to the sine winding and trips it once that voltage stays at or above 4.900 V for 0.21 s; the check runs continuously while the generator control unit has 8 V or more. Note the uneven window, since the lower limit is 2.0 V.",["Sine lead of the resolver shorted to a supply or excitation potential","Open sine lead that floats high at the input stage","Faulty resolver input in the power drive unit","Insulation fault between windings inside the resolver"],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Engine-driven charging stops, battery charge falls faster","Power limitation or hybrid functions switched off"],4,3,["P0C64","P0C66","P0C6C","P0A4E"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0C68:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit A Intermittent/Erratic","P0C68 is stored when circuit A of the generator resolver drops out or jumps for short moments and then looks normal again, so neither the low nor the high limit is held long enough to set P0C66 or P0C67. Vibration at a connector, a chafed lead or contacts that loosen with heat are the usual suspects. No manufacturer documents P0C68 publicly.",["Resolver connector with poor contact that opens under vibration","Harness rubbing through intermittently in the transaxle or generator area","Bursts of interference from nearby phase cables due to harness routing","Contact or winding joint that fails only at high temperature"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief jolts or interruptions in generator operation","Fault appears mainly on bad roads or after long drives"],4,3,["P0C64","P0C66","P0C67","P0C6D","P0A4F"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ A — אות לא יציב"],P0C69:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit B","P0C69 is the general fault entry for the second pickup winding of the generator resolver. A resolver needs both windings, set 90 degrees apart, to give an unambiguous rotor angle; with only one, the inverter can no longer place the current in the generator phases correctly. The code applies when circuit B is judged faulty but not classified further. No manufacturer documents P0C69 publicly.",["Open or damaged lead in the second resolver pickup winding circuit","Bent, corroded or pushed-back pins at the resolver connector","Faulty second pickup winding in the resolver stator","Internal fault in the generator control electronics"],["Hybrid warning or malfunction indicator lamp","Generator switched off, battery is no longer recharged by the engine","Limited driving power"],4,3,["P0C64","P0C6A","P0C6B","P0C6C","P0C6D","P0A4B"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ B"],P0C6A:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit B Range/Performance","P0C6A concerns circuit B of the generator resolver, the cosine side at Mitsubishi: its signal stays inside the electrical limits but does not agree with what the controller expects. Because sine and cosine together describe one angle, a cosine trace with the wrong amplitude or a drifting centre shows up as a mismatch against circuit A, even though no line is shorted.",["Loose or twisted resolver stator changing the cosine amplitude","Partially shorted cosine winding with weakened output","Noise from phase leads due to damaged shielding or poor routing","High contact resistance in the circuit B connector"],["Hybrid warning lamp","Generator used only partly or not at all","Unusual noise or vibration from the transaxle under load"],4,3,["P0C69","P0C6B","P0C6C","P0C65","P0A4C"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C6B:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit B Low","P0C6B tells you that circuit B of the generator resolver, the cosine winding, reads too low. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV, Mitsubishi stores it if the cosine signal is no higher than 2.000 V throughout 0.21 s; the test never pauses while the generator control unit receives 8 V or more.",["Cosine lead of the resolver shorted to ground","Pinched or crushed resolver harness near the transaxle","Shorted cosine winding in the generator resolver","Faulty cosine input in the power drive unit"],["Malfunction indicator lamp right after switching on","No charging of the drive battery by the engine","Reduced performance in hybrid mode"],4,3,["P0C69","P0C6C","P0C66","P0A4D"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ B — ערך נמוך"],P0C6C:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit B High","P0C6C is the high-side fault of circuit B in the generator resolver. On the 2023 Outlander PHEV, where circuit B is the cosine winding, Mitsubishi triggers it when the cosine voltage sits at 4.900 V or above for 0.21 s; the monitor stays active as long as the generator control unit is supplied with 8 V.",["Cosine lead shorted to a supply or excitation line","Damaged resolver connector with bridged pins","Internal fault in the power drive unit holding the generator control unit","Open cosine lead that drifts high at the input"],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Generator inactive, state of charge drops while driving","Engine may run more often or the car limits power"],4,3,["P0C69","P0C6B","P0C6D","P0C67","P0A4E"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ B — ערך גבוה"],P0C6D:["Generator Position Sensor Circuit B Intermittent/Erratic","P0C6D records short, irregular disturbances on circuit B of the generator resolver: the signal flickers, loses samples or jumps, but each event is too brief to count as a hard low or high reading (P0C6B, P0C6C).",["Terminal with reduced contact pressure in the circuit B connector","Moisture entering through a damaged connector seal","Harness moving against a bracket or edge and breaking contact","Interference spikes from phase cables near the resolver lines"],["Intermittent warning lamp","Short jerks or charging interruptions in hybrid mode","Fault hard to reproduce in the workshop"],4,3,["P0C69","P0C6B","P0C6C","P0C68","P0A4F"],"מעגל חיישן מצב גנרטור‏ B — אות לא יציב"],P0C6E:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A/B Correlation","P0C6E compares two battery temperature sensors, A and B, with each other rather than judging either one alone. After a long rest the pack should be close to one temperature, so a lasting gap between the two points to a sensor that reads wrongly while staying electrically valid. No manufacturer documents P0C6E publicly; GM and Mitsubishi run the same idea against the pack average and store per-sensor codes instead.",["Thermistor A or B drifted while staying inside its electrical range","Sensor no longer seated against its cell or module","Genuine temperature difference from uneven cooling or a weak cell group","Battery management software with an over-sensitive comparison"],["Hybrid or EV system warning lamp","Reduced charge and discharge power","Battery fan or cooling running more than usual"],4,3,["P0C6F","P0C70","P0A9C","P0AC6"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A/B — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C6F:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B/C Correlation","P0C6F sets when battery temperature sensors B and C disagree by more than the calibrated margin. Sensor B also takes part in the A/B check (P0C6E), so the pattern of stored codes helps: if P0C6E and P0C6F appear together, B is the shared suspect, while P0C6F alone points more towards C. No manufacturer documents P0C6F publicly.",["Sensor C reading off, especially when P0C6F is stored alone","Sensor B reading off, likely when P0C6E is stored as well","Sensor output stuck at one value while the pack temperature changes","Real local heating in a module, for example from a cell with high resistance"],["Hybrid or EV system warning","Power or charging rate limited","Displayed battery temperatures differ noticeably in live data"],4,3,["P0C6E","P0C70","P0AC6","P0ACB"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B/C — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C70:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C/D Correlation","P0C70 is the correlation check between battery temperature sensors C and D. The controller expects both to settle at nearly the same value once the pack has rested, and it stores the code when their difference stays too large. No manufacturer documents P0C70 publicly. Where GM and Mitsubishi publish this monitor, they compare each sensor with the pack average and name the single sensor;",["Thermistor C or D drifted out of agreement with the other","Battery control software that sets the code falsely, for example in cold weather","Sensor C or D detached from its cell group","Uneven cooling across the pack, such as a blocked air duct or coolant channel"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or EV system warning","Message asking to plug in or warm the battery","Reduced electric power and slower charging"],4,3,["P0C6F","P0C71","P0ACB","P0AE9"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C/D — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C71:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D/E Correlation","P0C71 checks whether battery temperature sensors D and E agree with each other. Both sit in the same pack, so after a long soak they should report almost the same value; a lasting gap means one of them reads wrong although its circuit looks healthy. No manufacturer documents P0C71 publicly.",["Thermistor D or E drifted while staying inside its electrical range","Sensor no longer pressed against its cell or module","Genuine temperature difference from uneven cooling or a weak cell group","Battery management software with an overly tight comparison"],["Hybrid or EV system warning lamp","Reduced battery charge and discharge power","Battery cooling running more often than usual"],4,3,["P0C70","P0C72","P0AE9","P0BC3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ D/E — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C72:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E/F Correlation","P0C72 is the agreement test between battery temperature sensors E and F. The controller stores it when the two readings stay apart by more than a calibrated margin after the pack has had time to even out. No manufacturer documents P0C72 publicly; GM names the matching single-sensor plausibility codes P0BC3 (sensor 5) and P0C34 (sensor 6) and compares each with the pack average, Type B.",["Thermistor E or F drifted without an electrical fault","Battery module software that reports sensor faults falsely, fixed by reprogramming","Sensor detached from its cell group","Uneven coolant or air flow through the pack"],["Hybrid or EV system warning lamp","Battery-too-cold or similar message although it is not very cold","Reduced electric power and range"],4,3,["P0C71","P0C70","P0BC3","P0C34"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E/F — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C73:["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Performance","P0C73 reports that coolant pump A of the motor electronics, the loop that cools the inverter and often the drive motor, does not run the way the controller commands. Kia's bulletins for Niro and Carnival hybrids use exactly this name. The 2018 Outlander PHEV sets it when, with at least 30 % pump command for over 2 s, pump speed stays between 100 and 1,000 rpm or exceeds 5,300 rpm for 0.5 s;",["Air trapped in the inverter cooling loop, often after coolant service","Water ingress and corrosion at the pump connector or inside the pump housing","Low coolant level, wrong mixture or frozen coolant","Electric pump worn or seized","Control-unit software with faulty judgement logic"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid warning lamp","Coolant warning in the instrument cluster","Reduced electric drive power at high load"],2,3,["P0C74","P2BD7","P2600","P2601","P0C4A"],"בקרת משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת מנוע חשמלי‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C74:["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump B Control Performance","P0C74 is the performance check for coolant pump B of the motor electronics, found on vehicles with a second electric pump in the power electronics cooling. The controller compares how the pump should run with how it actually runs and stores the code when the two disagree for longer than calibrated.",["Hybrid control or inverter module software, fixed by an update","Air pocket in the power electronics coolant loop","Pump B worn, blocked or seized","Damaged wiring or corroded connector at pump B"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Reduced electric drive power when the electronics run hot"],2,3,["P0C73","P2BD7","P2600"],"בקרת משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת מנוע חשמלי‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0C75:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Discharge Time Too Short","P0C75 is logged when the high-voltage bus loses its voltage faster than expected after the contactors open. At shutdown the inverter's DC-link capacitor is drained through a discharge resistor or an active discharge circuit, and from capacitance and resistance the controller knows roughly how long that should take.",["DC-link capacitor lost capacitance through ageing or damage","Additional load or leakage path on the high-voltage bus","Bus voltage measured incorrectly by the inverter or battery module","Discharge resistor or circuit with too low a resistance"],["Hybrid or EV system warning lamp","Vehicle may not become ready to drive"],4,3,["P0C76","P0C77","P0C78"],""],P0C76:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Discharge Time Too Long","P0C76 means the high-voltage bus is still too high a set time after the contactors opened, so the inverter capacitor was not drained as intended. GM publishes thresholds: the 2011 Volt flags more than 200 V after 3.5 s (Special Type C, no MIL), the 2013 eAssist cars more than 65 V after 6.5 s and the 2019 tables more than 200 V after 3.5 s, both Type A after two failed runs.",["Active discharge circuit or discharge resistor in the inverter failed","Main contactor welded closed so the battery keeps feeding the bus","Loose high-voltage cable or terminal at the inverter","Low 12 V supply during shutdown or module software"],["Hybrid warning lamp or service high voltage message","Vehicle may not start or become ready","Loss of propulsion"],4,3,["P0C75","P0C77","P0C78","P0AA1","P0AA4"],""],P0C77:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Precharge Time Too Short","P0C77 is stored when the inverter side reaches battery voltage too quickly during precharge. Before the main contactor closes, the controller charges the inverter capacitor through a precharge resistor and watches the ratio of bus to battery voltage. GM's 2011 Volt sets the code if that ratio passes 95 % within 50 ms of precharge start, Type A;",["Main contactor welded or closing before precharge ends","Precharge resistor bypassed or shorted","Loose high-voltage cable leaving the inverter capacitor partly disconnected","Faulty bus or battery voltage measurement"],["Malfunction indicator or hybrid warning lamp","Vehicle does not become ready or loses propulsion"],4,3,["P0C78","P0C75","P0C76","P0AE2","P0D25"],""],P0C78:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Precharge Time Too Long","P0C78 means precharge took too long: the inverter side did not come up to battery voltage within the allowed time. GM's 2011 Volt allows 700 ms to reach 95 % (Type A), the 2013 eAssist 1,000 ms or more than 5 A for over 100 ms (Type B), and the 2019 tables demand 95-105 % at 0.700 s, Type A. The 2018 Outlander PHEV flags a gap of 12 V or more between battery and inverter after 1 s.",["Precharge resistor, precharge contactor or high-voltage fuse open","Faulty high-voltage consumer such as a coolant heater loading the bus","Battery module software, fixed by an update","Loose high-voltage cable or bus bar"],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive","Malfunction indicator lamp and drive system message","Loss of propulsion"],4,3,["P0C77","P0C76","P0C75","P0AE3","P0D26"],""],P0C79:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Too High","P0C79 reports that the DC voltage at the inverter of drive motor A rose above its protection limit. Overvoltage can come from regenerative energy the battery cannot absorb, from a contactor opening under load or a loose high-voltage connection, and it can also be a false reading. In the 2018 Outlander PHEV a hardware comparator in the front motor control unit trips at 370 V;",["Battery cable at the inverter loose or not at specified torque","Inverter or its voltage detection faulty","Voltage spike from regeneration or a contactor opening under load","Motor control software, fixed by an update"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp, electrical system fault message","Drive system will not activate or shuts down until restarted","Reduced drive power"],4,3,["P0C7A","P0C7B","P0A78","P0D2E"],"מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0C7A:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Too High","P0C7A is the overvoltage code for the inverter of drive motor B, which on many all-wheel-drive hybrids is the rear motor. Mitsubishi documents it for the Outlander PHEV: in the 2018 model the rear motor control unit has a hardware high-voltage switch that closes at 420 V, a higher limit than the 370 V of the front unit, and the code sets after a single trip with the power stage active or after 0.5 s with it switched…",["Rear battery cable connection loose at the rear motor control unit","Rear motor control unit or its voltage detection faulty","Voltage spike from regeneration or a contactor event"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Reduced drive power, rear axle drive may be unavailable"],4,3,["P0C79","P0C7B","P0A79","P0DA4"],"מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0C7B:["Generator Inverter Voltage Too High","P0C7B sets when the inverter of the generator, the motor-generator the engine drives to make electricity, sees DC voltage above its limit. Mitsubishi is the only maker found documenting the code by number: in the 2018 Outlander PHEV the generator control unit trips a hardware high-voltage switch at 370 V, and one occurrence with the power stage running, or 0.5 s with it switched off, is enough.",["Front battery cable at the power drive unit not at specified torque","Generator control unit or its voltage detection faulty","Voltage spike when a contactor opens while the generator charges"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Engine-driven charging or hybrid drive restricted"],4,3,["P0C79","P0C7A","P0A7A"],"מתח אינוורטר גנרטור — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0C7C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G Circuit","P0C7C is the umbrella circuit code for battery temperature sensor G, used when the controller sees an electrical fault in that input without sorting it into low, high or erratic. No manufacturer documents P0C7C publicly. GM numbers sensor G as sensor 7 of the nine thermistors in the Volt pack (bulletin 18-NA-342), yet its hybrid tables only carry the narrower codes P0C7D to P0C80;",["Corroded or loose connector between sensor G and the sensing board","Thermistor G failed electrically","Faulty input stage on the cell monitoring board or battery module"],["Hybrid or EV system warning lamp, check-engine lamp on many models","Reduced electric power and slower charging","Battery cooling or heating running without obvious need"],4,3,["P0C7D","P0C7E","P0C7F","P0C80","P0C33","P0C81","P0C86"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C7D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G Circuit Range/Performance","P0C7D flags sensor G when its value is electrically valid but does not fit the rest of the pack. GM's 2012 Volt table (12OBDG01A) compares it with the average of the other sensors once propulsion has been off for more than 21,600 s and fails it beyond 25.3 °C in 50 of 67 samples, Type B with two trips; bulletin 18-NA-342 calls it sensor 7 performance.",["Thermistor G drifted while staying inside its electrical range","Sensor G no longer seated against its cell group","Battery module software setting the code falsely (GM Volt, 18-NA-342)","Real temperature difference from a weak cell or blocked cooling channel"],["Check-engine or hybrid warning lamp after a long parking period","Reduced battery power, sometimes a battery-too-cold message"],4,3,["P0C7C","P0C7E","P0C7F","P0C80","P0C34","P0C82","P0C86","P0C87"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C7E:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G Circuit Low","P0C7E means the sensor G voltage has fallen below its valid range. The thermistor has a negative temperature coefficient and a pull-up on the sensing board, so a short to ground looks like an extremely hot cell group; low describes the signal, not the temperature.",["Sensor G signal line chafed through and shorted to ground","Thermistor G shorted internally, often after moisture entry","Input stage of the cell monitoring board pulled low"],["Warning lamp with sharply reduced electric power","Battery cooling fan or chiller running constantly","Engine runs more often on plug-in hybrids"],4,3,["P0C7F","P0C7D","P0C7C","P0C80","P0A7E","P0C35","P0C83"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C7F:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G Circuit High","With P0C7F the sensor G input reads above its valid voltage range. Because the pull-up raises the line when the thermistor is disconnected, an open circuit, broken wire or unplugged connector shows up as an implausibly cold cell group. GM's 2016 Cadillac ELR table (16OBDG01) sets it below −40 °C for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, two trips, and the 2019 table 19OBDG02 below −69 °C in 8 of 10 samples, Type B.",["Open circuit or unplugged connector at sensor G","Thermistor G open internally","Module software setting it falsely in mild cold (GM Volt, 18-NA-342)"],["Battery-too-cold or plug-in-to-warm message although it is not very cold","Reduced electric power or no electric drive","Battery heater switched on without need"],4,3,["P0C7E","P0C7D","P0C7C","P0C80","P0C36","P0C84"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C80:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C80 catches a sensor G signal that jumps around instead of following the slow thermal drift of a large battery. GM spells out the logic in its hybrid tables 18OBDG02 and 19OBDG02: every 100 ms the module sums the absolute changes of the reading across a 10-sample window, a total above 10 fails that window, and 4 failed windows out of 5 set the code as Type B, two trips, provided system voltage exceeds 10.2 V and…",["Loose or fretting terminal in the sensor G connector","Conductor cracked inside the insulation, breaking under vibration","Cell monitoring board with unstable reference or ground"],["Warning lamp that may come and go between trips","Jumping sensor G value in live data","Occasional power reduction"],4,3,["P0C7C","P0C7D","P0C7E","P0C7F","P0C37","P0C85"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C81:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor H Circuit","P0C81 reports an electrical fault on the input of battery temperature sensor H that the controller does not narrow down further. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly. In GM's Volt pack sensor H is sensor 8 (bulletin 18-NA-342), and both the 2016 Cadillac ELR table (16OBDG01) and the later Volt tables list only the specific H codes P0C82 to P0C85.",["Poor contact at the sensor H connector","Thermistor H defective","Damaged measuring channel on the sensing board"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Limited acceleration in electric mode","Charging slower than usual"],4,3,["P0C82","P0C83","P0C84","P0C85","P0C7C","P0C88","P0C87"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C82:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor H Circuit Range/Performance","P0C82 is the plausibility test for sensor H: its circuit is fine, but the temperature it reports strays too far from what the rest of the pack says. On the 2016 Cadillac ELR (16OBDG01) GM fails it when the reading differs from the average of the other module groups by more than 20 °C in 50 of 67 samples, Type B;",["Thermistor H reading off while still electrically valid","Sensor H lifted off its module, measuring air instead of cells","Outdated battery module software (GM Volt, 18-NA-342)","Genuine local temperature difference from poor coolant flow"],["Check-engine or hybrid lamp, often after the car has stood for a while","Battery power limited"],4,3,["P0C81","P0C83","P0C84","P0C85","P0C7D","P0C89","P0C87"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C83:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor H Circuit Low","P0C83 stands for a sensor H voltage under its permitted range, which with an NTC thermistor reads as heat: a short to ground or a shorted element makes the cell group seem far too hot. GM's 2016 Cadillac ELR table (16OBDG01) fails it above 87.5 °C, an ADC count below 680, for 1.4 s in a 2 s window, two trips; the Volt bulletin 18-NA-342 files it as the low-voltage fault of sensor 8.",["Sensor H signal shorted to ground or to the pack housing","Shorted thermistor H","Fault in the cell monitoring unit that reads sensor H"],["Hybrid warning lamp and strong power reduction","Live data shows sensor H far hotter than its neighbours","Battery cooling running at full effort"],4,3,["P0C84","P0C82","P0C81","P0C85","P0A7E","P0C7E","P0C8A"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C84:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor H Circuit High","P0C84 is set when the sensor H line sits too high in voltage, which the controller converts into an impossible cold reading. The pull-up drives an interrupted circuit upward, so an open wire, a pushed-out pin or a broken thermistor are the classic triggers. In GM's 2012 Volt table (12OBDG01A) the limit is below −40 °C, an ADC count above 4000, held for 1.4 s within a 2 s window;",["Broken wire or backed-out pin in the sensor H circuit","Thermistor H open","False set by battery module software (GM Volt, 18-NA-342)"],["Battery-too-cold message in mild weather","Electric drive limited or unavailable"],4,3,["P0C83","P0C82","P0C81","P0C85","P0C7F","P0C8B"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C85:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor H Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C85 points to a sensor H reading that flickers. A battery pack warms and cools slowly, so fast swings in one channel suggest an electrical contact problem rather than real heat. GM's 2018 hybrid table 18OBDG02 adds up how much the value moves within a 10-sample window at 100 ms per sample;",["Intermittent contact at the sensor H terminal","Partly broken sensor H wire inside the pack harness","Noisy measuring channel on the sensing board"],["Intermittent warning lamp","Sensor H value jumping in live data"],4,3,["P0C81","P0C82","P0C83","P0C84","P0C80","P0C8C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C86:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F/G Correlation","P0C86 compares the neighbouring battery temperature sensors F and G with each other. After the pack has rested, both should settle to nearly the same value; a persistent gap means at least one reads wrong although neither circuit is open or shorted. No manufacturer documents P0C86 publicly.",["Thermistor F or G drifted without an electrical fault","Sensor F or G no longer in contact with its module","Real temperature difference between the two module groups","Battery management calibration with an overly narrow window"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Somewhat reduced battery power","Sensors F and G far apart in live data after a cold soak"],4,3,["P0C72","P0C87","P0C34","P0C7D","P0C33","P0C7C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F/G — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C87:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor G/H Correlation","P0C87 checks that battery temperature sensors G and H tell a consistent story. The controller would set it when, after enough rest for the pack to equalise, the two values remain apart by more than a calibrated margin. No manufacturer documents this pair code publicly.",["Thermistor G or H drifted while staying in range","Sensor G or H detached from its cell group","Real temperature gap from a weak cell group or blocked cooling","Battery module software needing an update"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Reduced charge and discharge power"],4,3,["P0C86","P0C72","P0C7D","P0C82","P0C7C","P0C81"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G/H — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0C88:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor I Circuit","P0C88 is the general circuit code for battery temperature sensor I, chosen when the fault is electrical but not classified as low, high or erratic. No manufacturer documents P0C88 publicly. In GM's Volt pack sensor I is the ninth and last thermistor (bulletin 18-NA-342); the 2012 Volt table 12OBDG01A and GM's later tables cover it only through the specific codes P0C89 to P0C8C.",["Connector fault on the sensor I circuit","Thermistor I failed","Defective input channel in the battery sensing electronics"],["Hybrid or EV warning lamp","Reduced electric power","Generic scan tools may show the code without a description"],4,3,["P0C89","P0C8A","P0C8B","P0C8C","P0C81","P0C8D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C89:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor I Circuit Range/Performance","P0C89 says sensor I delivers an electrically valid but implausible temperature. GM checks it against the average of the remaining sensors: the 2019 table 19OBDG02 fails it beyond 25 °C in 50 of 67 samples, Type B, two trips, and suspends the test while a low, high or communication fault is present; the 2016 Cadillac ELR table (16OBDG01) uses 20 °C.",["Thermistor I drifted within its electrical range","Module software setting the code falsely, fixed by reprogramming (GM Volt)","Sensor I displaced from its measuring point","Real local heating at the cell group of sensor I"],["Check-engine or hybrid lamp, often after a long rest","Battery power reduced"],4,3,["P0C88","P0C8A","P0C8B","P0C8C","P0C82","P0C8E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C8A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor I Circuit Low","P0C8A records a sensor I voltage below the valid band. As the element is an NTC thermistor fed through a pull-up, a low voltage translates into a cell group that seems dangerously hot, typically caused by a short to ground. GM's 2012 Volt table (12OBDG01A) trips above 87.5 °C, an ADC count under 680, for 1.4 s within 2 s, two trips; the 2019 table 19OBDG02 instead fails above 160 °C in 8 of 10 samples, Type B.",["Sensor I signal shorted to ground","Thermistor I shorted, for example after coolant or moisture entry","Cell monitoring unit input for sensor I faulty"],["Warning lamp with reduced power","Sensor I shown extremely hot in live data","Battery cooling running continuously"],4,3,["P0C8B","P0C89","P0C88","P0C8C","P0A7E","P0C83"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C8B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor I Circuit High","P0C8B covers a sensor I signal stuck above its valid voltage, which the controller interprets as extreme cold. An interrupted circuit lets the pull-up lift the line, so the root cause is usually an open connection or a failed thermistor rather than the weather. On the 2016 Cadillac ELR (16OBDG01) GM fails it below −40 °C, an ADC count above 3992, for 1.4 s within 2 s, two trips.",["Open wire or loose connector in the sensor I circuit","Thermistor I open","Battery module software causing false cold readings (GM Volt, 18-NA-342)"],["Battery-too-cold message although outside temperature is mild","Electric drive limited or unavailable","Battery heater running unnecessarily"],4,3,["P0C8A","P0C89","P0C88","P0C8C","P0C84"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C8C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor I Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C8C reports that sensor I changes faster and more often than any real battery temperature could. GM is the only maker that publishes the test: in 19OBDG02, the 2019 hybrid powertrain control module 2 table, each 10-sample window at 100 ms per sample is scored by the summed absolute change of the reading, a score above 10 counts as a failed window, and 4 failed windows out of 5 set the code, Type B, two trips, with…",["Fretting or loose terminal on the sensor I connector","Sensor I wire broken inside its insulation","Sensing board with unstable supply or ground"],["Warning lamp appearing now and then","Sensor I value jumping in live data","Short phases of reduced power"],4,3,["P0C88","P0C89","P0C8A","P0C8B","P0C85"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C8D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor J Circuit","P0C8D covers electrical trouble in the circuit of battery temperature sensor J that the controller cannot pin down to a low, high or jumping signal. There is no public manufacturer documentation for P0C8D. GM's 2011 hybrid table counts J as temperature sensor 10 in a list of sixteen, yet like its later tables it only defines the narrower J codes P0C8E to P0C91;",["Corroded or loose plug between sensor J and the monitoring board","Thermistor J electrically defective","Faulty input channel on the cell monitoring board"],["Hybrid system warning, on many models also the check-engine lamp","Less electric power and slower charging"],4,3,["P0C8E","P0C8F","P0C90","P0C91","P0C88","P0C92"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C8E:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor J Circuit Range/Performance","P0C8E sets when sensor J delivers an electrically valid temperature that disagrees with the other modules. For the 2012 Volt, table 12OBDG01A runs the check only after propulsion, off-board charging and battery conditioning have all been inactive for more than 21,600 s, so the pack should be evenly soaked; a gap above 25.3 °C to the mean of the remaining sensors in 50 of 67 samples fails it, Type B.",["Thermistor J drifted but still within its electrical range","Sensor J no longer pressed against its cell group","Actual temperature difference from a weak cell or blocked cooling path"],["Warning lamp appearing after the car has been parked for many hours","Battery power reduced while the pack is cold or warm"],4,3,["P0C8D","P0C8F","P0C90","P0C91","P0C89","P0C93"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C8F:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor J Circuit Low","P0C8F means the voltage on sensor J has dropped below its valid window. The NTC thermistor is powered via a pull-up resistor, so a grounded signal line reads as an absurdly hot cell group; low refers to the signal. GM's HPC2 table of 2019 (19OBDG02) converts the voltage and fails the test if the result is over 160 °C for 8 samples out of 10, sampled every 100 ms and rated Type B.",["Sensor J signal wire chafed and shorted to ground","Thermistor J shorted internally, for example after moisture","Monitoring board input pulling the line low"],["Warning lamp with clearly reduced electric power","Battery fan or chiller running constantly","Engine starts more often on plug-in hybrids"],4,3,["P0C90","P0C8E","P0C8D","P0C91","P0A7E","P0C8A","P0C94"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C90:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor J Circuit High","P0C90 reports the opposite case: sensor J reads above its voltage window, which with a pull-up circuit points to an open thermistor, a broken lead or a disconnected plug, and the controller sees an impossibly cold module. In GM's 2016 and 2019 tables (16OBDG02, 19OBDG02) a converted value colder than −69 °C in 8 out of 10 readings, one every 100 ms, fails the test;",["Open lead or unplugged connector at sensor J","Thermistor J open internally","Terminal pushed back in the monitoring board plug"],["Battery-too-cold message although the weather is mild","Little or no electric drive power","Battery heater switching on without need"],4,3,["P0C8F","P0C8E","P0C8D","P0C91","P0C8B","P0C95"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C91:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor J Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C91 watches for a sensor J reading that changes too fast and too often for a heavy battery. GM's tables 16OBDG02 and 19OBDG02 add up how far the value moves between samples over a 10-sample window taken every 100 ms; a sum above 10 fails the window, and 4 failing windows out of 5, about half a second, store the code, Type B over two trips. The check needs more than 10.2 V and working communication.",["Fretting or loose terminal in the sensor J plug","Conductor broken inside the insulation, opening under vibration","Unstable reference or ground on the monitoring board"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes between drives","Sensor J value jumping in live data"],4,3,["P0C8D","P0C8E","P0C8F","P0C90","P0C8C","P0C96"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C92:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor K Circuit","P0C92 is stored when the input from battery temperature sensor K has an electrical fault that the controller leaves unclassified. Public manufacturer documentation for exactly this code does not exist. In GM's 2011 rationality list K appears as sensor 11 of sixteen, and GM's later hybrid tables from 2012 up to 19OBDG02 only define the specific K codes P0C93 to P0C96.",["Poor contact at the sensor K plug","Thermistor K failed","Defective input channel on the cell monitoring board"],["Hybrid or EV warning message","Reduced electric power until the fault is repaired"],4,3,["P0C93","P0C94","P0C95","P0C96","P0C8D","P0C97"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C93:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor K Circuit Range/Performance","P0C93 compares sensor K with its neighbours: the reading is electrically fine but too far from the pack average. GM's 2016 hybrid table 16OBDG02 sets it when the absolute difference exceeds 25.00 °C for 50 samples out of 67 taken every 25 ms, roughly 1.7 s, rated Type B; the check is suspended whenever a low, high or erratic code for the battery sensors is active. GM's 2011 table lists K as sensor 11.",["Thermistor K reading off while still electrically valid","Sensor K lifted off its cell group","Real temperature spread from uneven coolant flow or a weak cell"],["Warning lamp, often first noticed after a cold start","Charging and discharging power limited"],4,3,["P0C92","P0C94","P0C95","P0C96","P0C8E","P0C98"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C94:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor K Circuit Low","With P0C94 the controller sees the sensor K signal pulled below its lower limit, a pattern produced by a chafed wire touching ground or an internally shorted thermistor, which the conversion turns into an extreme temperature. GM's 2016 table 16OBDG02 counts a failure whenever the converted value is hotter than 160 °C and needs 8 such readings out of 10, one every 100 ms, Type B;",["Sensor K signal line shorted to ground","Thermistor K shorted internally","Moisture or coolant reaching the sensor K connection"],["Warning lamp and reduced electric power","Battery cooling running although the pack is not hot"],4,3,["P0C95","P0C93","P0C92","P0C96","P0A7E","P0C8F","P0C99"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C95:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor K Circuit High","P0C95 is set when sensor K delivers a voltage above its valid range. In the usual NTC layout a broken circuit allows the bias resistor to drive the input high, so the controller sees an impossibly cold module. In 19OBDG02 (GM, 2019) a reading colder than −69 °C in 8 out of 10 checks counts as failed, Type B; the 2012 Volt table accepts readings only down to −40 °C, equal to an ADC count of 4,000.",["Open circuit or disconnected plug at sensor K","Thermistor K open internally","Sensor lead damaged during earlier work on the pack"],["Battery-too-cold or plug-in-to-warm message in mild weather","Electric power reduced or unavailable"],4,3,["P0C94","P0C93","P0C92","P0C96","P0C90","P0C9A"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C96:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor K Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C96 flags an unsteady sensor K signal, the kind of noise a loose contact produces while the value still sits inside its valid band. GM describes the monitor in 16OBDG02 and 18OBDG02: each 100 ms sample adds to a running total of absolute changes across 10 samples, a total above 10 marks the window as failed, and 4 failed windows in 5 store the code, Type B, so two bad trips are needed.",["Loose contact in the sensor K connector","Partly chafed sensor lead touching intermittently","Monitoring board with unstable supply or ground"],["Intermittent warning lamp","Short spells of reduced power"],4,3,["P0C92","P0C93","P0C94","P0C95","P0C91","P0C9B"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C97:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor L Circuit","P0C97 is the catch-all for battery temperature sensor L, used when the input misbehaves electrically but fits none of the low, high or erratic categories. No manufacturer publishes this exact code. GM's 2011 hybrid table counts L as temperature sensor 12 of sixteen, and its 2012 and later tables cover L only with P0C98 to P0C9B.",["Connector fault between sensor L and the monitoring board","Thermistor L defective","Faulty measuring channel on the monitoring board"],["Hybrid or EV warning, possibly with check-engine lamp","Limited battery power"],4,3,["P0C98","P0C99","P0C9A","P0C9B","P0C92","P0CA8"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0C98:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor L Circuit Range/Performance","P0C98 is the plausibility code for sensor L: its reading is electrically valid but strays too far from the other sensors. GM's table for the 2012 Volt (12OBDG01A) runs the comparison only after more than 21,600 s with propulsion, off-board charging and thermal conditioning all off; a deviation over 25.3 °C, seen in 50 of 67 samples, counts as a Type B failure.",["Thermistor L drifted within its electrical range","Sensor L no longer in thermal contact with its cells","Genuine local temperature difference in that pack section"],["Warning lamp after a long standstill","Reduced battery power"],4,3,["P0C97","P0C99","P0C9A","P0C9B","P0C93","P0CA9"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0C99:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor L Circuit Low","P0C99 reports a sensor L voltage below the valid range. Because the thermistor works against a bias resistor on its monitoring board, any ground fault or an internally shorted sensor drags the voltage down, which reads as a very hot module. GM's 2019 HPC2 table 19OBDG02 flags it once the converted value exceeds 160 °C in 8 out of 10 readings at 100 ms intervals; it is a Type B code and needs supply above 10.2 V.",["Sensor L signal shorted to ground","Thermistor L shorted internally","Monitoring board channel pulling the input low"],["Warning lamp and reduced battery power","Battery cooling running for no apparent reason"],4,3,["P0C9A","P0C98","P0C97","P0C9B","P0A7E","P0C94","P0CAA"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0C9A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor L Circuit High","P0C9A is the high-voltage fault of sensor L: an open thermistor, a broken lead or a disconnected plug lets the bias resistor raise the input, and the reading looks like deep frost. GM's 2016 table 16OBDG02 sets it when 8 readings out of 10, taken every 100 ms, are colder than −69 °C; it is Type B and requires more than 10.2 V supply plus working communication.",["Open circuit or unplugged connector at sensor L","Thermistor L open internally","Corroded terminal with very high contact resistance"],["Battery-too-cold indication in mild conditions","Electric power reduced, engine running more on plug-ins"],4,3,["P0C99","P0C98","P0C97","P0C9B","P0C95","P0CAB"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0C9B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor L Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C9B covers a sensor L signal that jitters. GM's tables 18OBDG02 and 19OBDG02 integrate the absolute sample-to-sample changes over ten readings spaced 100 ms apart; when the integral exceeds 10, that window fails, and 4 failures within 5 windows store the code, classed Type B. Supply must exceed 10.2 V and communication with the battery interface module must be present.",["Fretting pin in the sensor L connector","Cracked conductor in the sensor L lead","Loose ground on the cell monitoring board"],["Warning lamp that may switch off again by itself","Erratic sensor L value in live data"],4,3,["P0C97","P0C98","P0C99","P0C9A","P0C96","P0CAC"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0C9C:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor B Circuit","P0C9C flags an electrical problem, not further classified, with current sensor B of the 14 volt power module, the DC/DC converter that supplies the 12 V network from the high-voltage battery; GM calls it the accessory power module (APM). In GM's 2013 eAssist table sensor B is the APM output current sensor, while sensor A (P0A86 to P0A8A) measures the input side. No manufacturer documents P0C9C publicly;",["Current sensor B inside the power module failed","Poor contact at the power module signal connector","Control software of the power module or hybrid controller"],["Warning lamp, sometimes a charging-system message","12 V battery running low over time"],4,1,["P0C9D","P0C9E","P0C9F","P0CA0","P0A86","P0CC5"],""],P0C9D:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0C9D means the output current reported by sensor B of the 14 V power module is electrically valid but implausible for the operating state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM's 2013 eAssist table checks this kind of plausibility through the correlation code P0CC5 instead: the output power derived from sensor B is compared with the input power from sensor A multiplied by an efficiency factor between…",["Offset or gain drift of current sensor B","High resistance at the 12 V output cable or ground","Converter output really differing from the commanded power"],["Warning lamp without obvious driveability change","12 V battery charge state remaining low"],4,1,["P0C9C","P0C9E","P0C9F","P0CA0","P0CC5","P0A87"],""],P0C9E:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor B Circuit Low","P0C9E is set when current sensor B of the 14 V power module reads too little. GM documents it for the eAssist accessory power module in its 2013 table 13OBDG05C: the APM output current measured by the sensor must stay below 2 A for 1.5 s, counted as 60 of 80 samples at 25 ms, with the vehicle in run/crank; the code is Type B, so the MIL comes on after the second failing trip.",["Output current sensor B failed inside the module","Sensor signal open or shorted to ground","Converter not delivering output although enabled"],["Check-engine lamp after two drives","Low 12 V voltage, flickering lights or warning messages"],4,1,["P0C9F","P0C9C","P0C9D","P0CA0","P0A88","P0CC5"],""],P0C9F:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor B Circuit High","P0C9F reports an output current from sensor B of the 14 V power module that exceeds what the converter can plausibly supply. GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C sets it once the APM output current reading tops 123 A while calculated APM input power is between 0.25 and 1.8 kW, supply voltage is 9 to 32 V and the vehicle is in run/crank; 1.5 s out of a 2 s window fail it, Type B, two trips.",["Internal defect of output current sensor B","Sensor signal shorted to a voltage source","Poor ground of the power module shifting the reading"],["Check-engine lamp","Converter output reduced, 12 V battery discharging"],4,1,["P0C9E","P0C9C","P0C9D","P0CA0","P0A89","P0CC5"],""],P0CA0:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor B Circuit Intermittent","P0CA0 covers short dropouts or spikes in the signal of current sensor B, the output-side sensor of the 14 V power module (DC/DC converter) in GM's numbering. Between the events the reading looks normal, which separates it from the hard low and high faults P0C9E and P0C9F. No manufacturer documents P0CA0 publicly;",["Loose or fretting connector at the power module","Corroded ground or 12 V output connection","Current sensor B failing intermittently inside the module"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Occasional dim lights or voltage messages"],2,1,["P0C9C","P0C9D","P0C9E","P0C9F","P0A8A","P0CC5"],""],P0CA1:["Drive Motor Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","P0CA1 is a request code, not a component fault: the drive motor control module has detected an emissions-relevant fault of its own and asks the engine control module to light the MIL, which stores P0CA1. Subaru documents this on the 2014 XV Crosstrek Hybrid in bulletin 07-82-14R: P0CA1 sits in the engine module and C1424 in the stability control module, both MIL requests comparable to P0700.",["Emissions-relevant fault stored in the drive motor control module","Module software reacting to a low 12 V battery (Subaru 07-82-14R)","Weak or discharged 12 V auxiliary battery"],["Check-engine lamp, often with further warning lamps such as stability control","Hybrid drive behaving normally or with reduced electric assistance"],2,1,["P0A7B","P0AC4","P0700","P0CDD","P0A3F"],""],P0CA2:["DC/DC Converter Step Down Voltage Performance","P0CA2 means the step-down function of the DC/DC converter, which brings high-voltage battery power down to the 12 V network, is not working correctly. GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C uses it for a shoot-through fault inside the 14 volt power module: if the module detects a failed silicon power switch, a single 25 ms detection at 9 to 32 V sets the code as Type A, so the MIL lights on the first trip.",["Failed power semiconductor in the converter's step-down stage","Internal fault of the power module or power pack assembly","Spurious detection without a hardware fault, for example after supply dips"],["Check-engine lamp on the first trip, often with a charging warning","Falling 12 V voltage, low-voltage codes such as P0562","Hybrid assistance or auto-stop unavailable"],4,3,["P0CA3","P0A94","P0A8D","P0A8F","P0562"],""],P0CA3:["DC/DC Converter Step Up Voltage Performance","P0CA3 says the boost stage of a DC/DC converter fails to lift its output to the voltage the controller has requested. In full hybrids this stage, often called a boost or variable voltage converter, raises the traction battery voltage to a higher bus level that feeds the inverters. No manufacturer documents P0CA3 on a publicly reachable official server.",["Failed power transistor or driver in the boost stage","Internal fault of the inverter/converter assembly, e.g. reactor or capacitor","Wrong reading from the voltage sensor before or after the boost stage","Stored as a consequence of another hybrid system fault"],["Hybrid system warning with check-engine lamp","Noticeably reduced electric drive power or limp mode","Engine running more often than usual"],4,3,["P0CA2","P0A94","P0C79","P0D2E","P0E57"],""],P0CA4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Charging Voltage High","P0CA4 is stored when the traction battery voltage climbs above its permitted limit while energy flows into the pack, for instance because a charger or the drive keeps pushing current although the cells are already full. Audi lists the code in TSB 2062631/5 from 2024 for the e-tron and Q8 e-tron family, there stored by the high-voltage charger module J1050 and described as an excessive battery load voltage.",["Charger or battery control software storing a false entry (Audi 2062631/5)","Charging current not reduced in time as the pack approaches full charge","Faulty pack or cell voltage measurement in the battery management"],["Charging stops early or does not start","Code found during a check without any noticeable symptom"],4,3,["P0CA5","P0CA6","P0AFB","P0DE7","P0D28"],"טעינת סוללת מתח גבוה — מתח גבוה"],P0CA5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Discharging Voltage High","P0CA5 concerns a battery voltage judged too high while the pack is discharging, that is while it supplies the drive motor or other high-voltage consumers. Under load the terminal voltage normally sags, so an excessive value in this state points more to a measurement or plausibility fault than to genuine overcharge. No manufacturer documents P0CA5 publicly.",["Faulty pack voltage sensing in the battery management","Battery or hybrid control software evaluating the voltage wrongly","Loose or corroded high-voltage or sense connection"],["Hybrid or EV warning, often with check-engine lamp","Limited battery power output"],4,3,["P0CA4","P0CA7","P0AFB","P0DE7"],""],P0CA6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Charging Current High","P0CA6 flags a current into the traction battery that exceeds what the control system expects. Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV (2018 and 2022) stores it in the PHEV-ECU when, in charging mode, the measured battery current stays above the calculated charger output request for 5 s; suspects are the BMU, the onboard charger/DC-DC converter and the PHEV-ECU, and the repair is confirmed with drive cycle pattern 7.",["Battery control module software (Stellantis S2308000117, 08-025-25)","Onboard charger or DC-DC converter delivering more current than requested","Pack current sensor or battery management unit reading wrongly","Fault in the hybrid/PHEV control unit"],["Check-engine lamp, often found after charging","Charging interrupted or not possible","Vehicle not entering ready mode on some EVs"],4,3,["P0CA7","P0CA4","P0ABF","P0AC0","P0D2A"],"זרם טעינת סוללת מתח גבוה — ערך גבוה"],P0CA7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Discharging Current High","P0CA7 is set when more current flows out of the hybrid or EV battery than the controller allows. Audi documents it on 48 V mild hybrids (A6, A7, A8, later also Q7, Q8 and S/RS versions, 2018 to 2024): the battery management of the 48 V battery, J1120, stores P0CA700 as discharge current too high, often with U046900 in the engine module, and the cluster shows an electrical system warning.",["Starter/generator or battery module software (Audi 2058831, Stellantis 08-054-22)","Belt starter generator drawing excessive current","Battery current sensor or battery management misreading"],["Electrical system or battery warning in the cluster","Start-stop or electric assistance temporarily unavailable","Check-engine lamp on plug-in hybrids"],4,3,["P0CA6","P0CA5","P0ABF","P0AC0","P0AFA"],""],P0CA8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor M Circuit","P0CA8 is the general circuit code for battery temperature sensor M, meant for electrical faults that the controller does not sort into low, high or erratic. No manufacturer publishes this exact code. In GM's 2011 Volt table the rationality list gives M the thirteenth of sixteen positions, yet GM only defines P0CA9 to P0CAC for it.",["Poor contact at the plug of sensor M on the monitoring board","Thermistor M damaged","Faulty input channel of the cell monitoring board"],["Hybrid or EV warning, possibly with check-engine lamp","Electric power reduced until repaired"],4,3,["P0CA9","P0CAA","P0CAB","P0CAC","P0C97","P0CAD"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CA9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor M Circuit Range/Performance","P0CA9 reports that sensor M stays within its electrical limits yet disagrees with what the remaining pack sensors measure. GM's 2012 Volt table 12OBDG01A only runs the comparison once drive system, plug-in charging and battery conditioning have rested for over 21,600 s; a spread above 25.3 °C from the mean in 50 of 67 samples is a Type B failure.",["Thermistor M drifting while staying in its electrical range","Sensor M lost thermal contact with its module","Module section genuinely warmer or colder, e.g. cooling fault or weak cell"],["Warning lamp appearing after a long parking period","Battery power limited"],4,3,["P0CA8","P0CAA","P0CAB","P0CAC","P0C98","P0CAE"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CAA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor M Circuit Low","P0CAA means the voltage at sensor M has dropped below its valid band. Sensor M is an NTC element supplied via a bias resistor on the board, so a grounded lead or a shorted element drags the voltage down and reads as an extremely hot spot. GM's 2018 HPC2 table 18OBDG02 fails the test once the converted temperature exceeds 160 °C in 8 of 10 readings taken every 100 ms, Type B, with more than 10.2 V supply.",["Sensor M signal shorted to ground inside the pack","Thermistor M internally shorted","Measuring input of the cell monitoring unit defective"],["Warning lamp with strongly reduced battery power","Battery cooling running without reason"],4,3,["P0CAB","P0CA9","P0CA8","P0CAC","P0A7E","P0C99","P0CAF"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CAB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor M Circuit High","P0CAB is logged when the input of sensor M rises beyond its upper voltage limit. If the thermistor, one of its wires or the plug opens up, the pull-up resistor lifts the input, and the controller reads a temperature far below anything plausible. GM's HPC2 table 18OBDG02 sets it when 8 of 10 readings at 100 ms intervals are colder than −69 °C, as a Type B fault needing over 10.2 V and intact communication.",["Open circuit or unplugged connector of sensor M","Thermistor M open internally","Corroded or spread terminal at the monitoring board"],["Battery shown as very cold in mild weather","Reduced electric power, engine running more on plug-ins"],4,3,["P0CAA","P0CA9","P0CA8","P0CAC","P0C9A","P0CB0"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CAC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor M Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CAC catches a sensor M signal that jumps around without necessarily leaving its permitted band. GM's HPC2 tables (16OBDG02 to 19OBDG02) add up the absolute changes between successive samples over a ten-sample window at 100 ms; whenever that total passes 10 the window is marked bad, and four bad windows in five lead to a Type B code after two trips.",["Fretting or loose contact in the sensor M circuit","Cracked conductor or solder joint on the monitoring board","Unstable ground of the monitoring board"],["Warning lamp that may go out again on its own","Jumping sensor M value in live data"],4,3,["P0CA8","P0CA9","P0CAA","P0CAB","P0C9B","P0CB1"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CAD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor N Circuit","P0CAD stands for an electrical problem with battery temperature sensor N that the controller cannot assign to a specific failure type. Public manufacturer documentation for this exact code does not exist. GM's 2011 hybrid rationality list places N fourteenth among sixteen sensors, while its tables from 2012 onward cover N only with the specific codes P0CAE to P0CB1.",["Connector or wire fault between sensor N and the monitoring board","Thermistor N defective","Faulty input channel of the cell monitoring board"],["Hybrid or EV system message","Battery power reduced until repaired"],4,3,["P0CAE","P0CAF","P0CB0","P0CB1","P0CA8","P0CB2"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CAE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor N Circuit Range/Performance","P0CAE is the plausibility check for sensor N: the value is electrically acceptable, yet out of line with its neighbours. GM's 2012 Volt table 12OBDG01A demands a rest of more than 21,600 s without propulsion, external charging or thermal conditioning and then fails the test at a deviation above 25.3 °C in 50 of 67 samples, Type B.",["Thermistor N reading off while electrically valid","Sensor N detached from its cell or module surface","Real temperature difference in that pack section"],["Warning lamp after the car has stood for hours","Limited battery power"],4,3,["P0CAD","P0CAF","P0CB0","P0CB1","P0CA9","P0CB3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CAF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor N Circuit Low","P0CAF sets when the signal of sensor N is pulled under its lower voltage limit. A ground fault in the lead or a shorted thermistor causes this, and because the NTC sensor works against a pull-up resistor the controller interprets it as an implausibly hot module.",["Sensor N lead shorted to ground","Thermistor N internally shorted","Measuring input of the cell monitoring unit defective"],["Warning lamp and markedly reduced battery power","Battery cooling running at full effort"],4,3,["P0CB0","P0CAE","P0CAD","P0CB1","P0A7E","P0CAA"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CB0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor N Circuit High","P0CB0 reports a sensor N input above its upper voltage limit, which a pack controller reads as an unrealistically cold module. The NTC sensor is biased by a pull-up resistor, so any interruption in the element, its leads or its plug makes the input rise. GM's 2018 HPC2 table 18OBDG02 lists P0CB0 by name as sensor N circuit high and fails it when 8 of 10 readings at 100 ms are below −69 °C, Type B, two trips;",["Open lead or disconnected plug of sensor N","Thermistor N open internally","Corroded terminal at the monitoring board"],["Battery reported as far too cold in mild conditions","Less electric range or power, engine starting more often"],4,3,["P0CAF","P0CAE","P0CAD","P0CB1","P0CAB","P0CB5"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CB1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor N Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CB1 points to an unsteady sensor N signal rather than a lasting short or open. GM's 2019 table 19OBDG02 evaluates windows of ten samples, one every 100 ms, by integrating the absolute value of each change; above 10 the window counts as failed, and 4 failed windows within 5 set the code, Type B, two trips. It needs more than 10.2 V supply and an active data link to the battery interface control module.",["Loose or fretting pin in the sensor N circuit","Chafed or partly broken sensor N lead","Cracked track on the monitoring board"],["Intermittent warning lamp","Sensor N value jumping in live data"],4,3,["P0CAD","P0CAE","P0CAF","P0CB0","P0CAC","P0CB6"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CB2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor O Circuit","P0CB2 is the catch-all electrical code for battery temperature sensor O, reserved for circuit faults that are not classified as low, high or erratic. Publicly, no manufacturer documents this exact code. Counting the rationality list in GM's 2011 Volt table puts O in position 15 of 16, but GM only uses P0CB3 to P0CB6 for it.",["Poor contact at the sensor O connector on the monitoring board","Damaged lead between thermistor O and the board","Faulty measuring channel in the cell monitoring unit"],["Hybrid or EV system warning, possibly with check-engine lamp","Reduced electric power or charging rate"],4,3,["P0CB3","P0CB4","P0CB5","P0CB6","P0CAD","P0CB7"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ O — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CB3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor O Circuit Range/Performance","P0CB3 is a plausibility code: sensor O delivers an electrically valid reading, yet it strays too far from the rest of the pack. In GM's 2012 Volt table 12OBDG01A the check waits until propulsion, plug-in charging and battery thermal conditioning have all been off for more than 21,600 s; a gap above 25.3 °C to the average in 50 of 67 samples then counts as a Type B failure.",["Thermistor O drifting while its voltage stays in range","Sensor O no longer seated firmly against its module","Module section really warmer or colder, e.g. weak cell or uneven cooling"],["Warning lamp typically appearing after the car has stood for hours","Battery power limited"],4,3,["P0CB2","P0CB4","P0CB5","P0CB6","P0CAE","P0CB8"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ O — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CB4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor O Circuit Low","P0CB4 reports a signal voltage of sensor O below its lower limit. The sensor is an NTC thermistor biased by a resistor on the measuring board, so a short to ground or a shorted element looks like extreme heat. GM's HPC2 table 18OBDG02 fails the test when the converted value is above 160 °C in 8 of 10 readings at 100 ms, Type B, with more than 10.2 V supply;",["Signal lead of sensor O shorted to ground in the pack","Thermistor O shorted internally","Input stage of the cell monitoring unit defective"],["Live data showing an implausibly hot module","Strongly reduced battery power with warning lamp","Battery cooling running without obvious need"],4,3,["P0CB5","P0CB3","P0CB2","P0CB6","P0A7E","P0CAF","P0CB9"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ O — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CB5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor O Circuit High","P0CB5 is stored when the input of sensor O sits above its upper voltage limit. An open thermistor, a broken wire or a loose plug lets the pull-up resistor raise the input, so the controller sees a temperature colder than any real pack could be.",["Open lead or unplugged connector of sensor O","Thermistor O open internally","Corroded or loose terminal at the sensing board"],["Pack shown as extremely cold regardless of weather","Less electric power, engine running more on plug-in hybrids"],4,3,["P0CB4","P0CB3","P0CB2","P0CB6","P0CB0","P0CBA"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ O — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CB6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor O Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CB6 targets a sensor O signal that jitters or jumps instead of changing smoothly. Only GM's later HPC2 tables (16OBDG02 to 19OBDG02) define it: every 100 ms a sample is taken, the absolute steps between ten consecutive samples are summed, and a sum above 10 marks that window as failed. Four failed windows out of five set the code as Type B, so the lamp follows on the second trip;",["Fretting or loose terminal in the sensor O circuit","Cracked solder joint or trace on the sensing board","Chafed lead touching ground only under vibration"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Sensor O value jumping in live data"],4,3,["P0CB2","P0CB3","P0CB4","P0CB5","P0CB1","P0CBB"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ O — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CB7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor P Circuit","P0CB7 covers an electrical fault of battery temperature sensor P that the controller cannot pin down as low, high or intermittent. No manufacturer lists this code on a public official server. In GM's 2011 Volt rationality table P is the sixteenth and last sensor, and GM gives it only P0CB8 to P0CBB; Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV treats P as module sensor [15] with rationality, short and open codes.",["Loose or corroded plug of sensor P","Damaged wiring of sensor P inside the pack","Measuring channel for sensor P in the monitoring unit faulty"],["Hybrid or EV warning message","Limited electric power until repaired"],4,3,["P0CB8","P0CB9","P0CBA","P0CBB","P0CB2"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ P — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CB8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor P Circuit Range/Performance","P0CB8 flags sensor P as implausible: its value is electrically fine, but after a long rest it disagrees with the temperature of the other modules. GM runs this comparison in the 2012 Volt table 12OBDG01A only when drive system, external charging and battery conditioning have rested for over 21,600 s, so the whole pack should have settled; 50 of 67 samples more than 25.3 °C from the average give a Type B fault.",["Thermistor P drifted out of calibration","Sensor P detached from its module surface","Real temperature difference of that module, e.g. faulty cell"],["Warning appearing at the first drive after a long stand","Battery output restricted"],4,3,["P0CB7","P0CB9","P0CBA","P0CBB","P0CB3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ P — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CB9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor P Circuit Low","P0CB9 means the voltage of sensor P has fallen under the valid range. As with the other NTC sensors, low voltage corresponds to high temperature, so a short to ground or a collapsed thermistor makes the module appear to be glowing hot.",["Sensor P signal shorted to ground","Thermistor P internally shorted","Damaged input of the cell monitoring unit for sensor P"],["Module P shown as extremely hot in live data","Severe power limitation with warning lamp"],4,3,["P0CBA","P0CB8","P0CB7","P0CBB","P0A7E","P0CB4"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ P — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CBA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor P Circuit High","P0CBA appears when the signal of sensor P rises above its upper voltage limit, the typical picture of an open circuit: without a closed path through the thermistor, the pull-up resistor drives the input high and the computed temperature drops to an absurd value.",["Open circuit or disconnected plug of sensor P","Thermistor P broken internally","Spread or corroded terminal on the sensing board"],["Very low battery temperature displayed on a warm day","Battery heater or engine running more than usual"],4,3,["P0CB9","P0CB8","P0CB7","P0CBB","P0CB5"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ P — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CBB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor P Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CBB is about short dropouts or spikes in the signal of sensor P that do not last long enough, or go far enough, to trip the low or high code. GM defines it in the HPC2 tables 16OBDG02 to 19OBDG02 and judges how restless the signal is: within each window of ten samples taken 100 ms apart the size of every change is added up, a total above 10 fails the window, and four bad windows in five produce the code, Type B,…",["Intermittent contact at the sensor P plug","Hairline crack in the sensing board near the sensor P input","Unstable reference or ground of the monitoring unit"],["Intermittent warning that may clear by itself","Erratic sensor P reading in live data"],4,3,["P0CB7","P0CB8","P0CB9","P0CBA","P0CB6"],"חיישן טמפרטורת סוללת מתח גבוה‏ P — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CBC:["Drive Motor A Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0CBC is the general code for the sensor that measures coolant temperature in the cooling loop of drive motor A. Volvo and Polestar document it as P0CBC62 in the inverter module IEM of the Polestar 2 and the XC40/C40 Recharge (Technical Journals 37523 and 37521 from 2025): failure type 62 marks a signal comparison fault, and the vehicle displays a propulsion service message.",["Faulty one-way valve in the drive unit coolant hose (Volvo/Polestar TJ 37521/37523)","Low coolant or trapped air in the drive motor loop","Sensor or wiring fault in the temperature circuit","Internal fault of the inverter module"],["Propulsion system service message","Loss of drive power, car may stop on single-motor versions","Half power only on dual-motor versions"],4,2,["P0CBD","P0CBE","P0CBF","P0CC0","P0CC1","P0A3C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CBD:["Drive Motor A Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0CBD reports that the coolant temperature sensor of drive motor A gives a believable voltage but an implausible temperature. GM uses it on the 2013 eAssist models, where the drive motor is the belt-driven motor generator with its own small cooling loop.",["Coolant temperature sensor drifted out of its curve","Added resistance from a corroded connector","One of the comparison sensors (stator, intake air, transmission oil) reading wrongly"],["Check-engine lamp after the second faulty trip","Reduced electric assist or regeneration"],2,3,["P0CBC","P0CBE","P0CBF","P0CC0","P0A2B","P0CC2"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CBE:["Drive Motor A Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0CBE says the coolant temperature input of drive motor A is below its valid range, meaning the sensor resistance has become very small. GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C sets the code when the raw reading is under 100.9 Ω, which equals 120 °C, in 50 of 62 samples taken every 100 ms with the vehicle in run/crank; it is a Type B fault, so the lamp comes on after the second trip.",["Harness rubbing on crank or compressor pulley or belt (GM PIC5720A)","Coolant temperature sensor shorted internally","Loop genuinely very hot, e.g. pump or coolant fault"],["Check-engine lamp, possibly with ABS or brake assist warning","Hum or buzz from the right front","Electric assist restricted"],2,3,["P0CBF","P0CBD","P0CBC","P0CC0","P0A2C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CBF:["Drive Motor A Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0CBF is the code for an excessive signal from the coolant temperature sensor of drive motor A. With an NTC element a very high resistance stands for extreme cold, so in practice an open sensor, a broken wire or a disconnected plug is behind it.",["Open signal or return wire, or unplugged sensor","Sensor element open internally","Corroded pins at sensor or control module"],["Check-engine lamp after two trips","Coolant temperature of the motor shown at minimum value"],2,3,["P0CBE","P0CBD","P0CBC","P0CC0","P0A2D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CC0:["Drive Motor A Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0CC0 is meant for a coolant temperature signal of drive motor A that drops out or jumps for short moments. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. GM's 2013 eAssist table, the most detailed public source for this sensor, monitors it only with P0CBD for plausibility and P0CBE/P0CBF for out-of-range values; Volvo and Polestar report P0CBC with failure type 62.",["Loose or spread terminal in the sensor connector","Chafed wire making contact only on bumps","Moisture in the connector"],["Warning lamp that appears and disappears","Jumping coolant temperature value in live data"],2,3,["P0CBC","P0CBD","P0CBE","P0CBF","P0A2E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — תקלה לסירוגין במעגל"],P0CC1:["Drive Motor A Coolant Pump Control Circuit/Open","P0CC1 concerns the control circuit of the electric pump that moves coolant through the loop of drive motor A, here specifically an open circuit. GM documents it for the 2013 eAssist hybrids, where the table 13OBDG05C calls the part the motor generator coolant pump: the output driver reports an open-circuit status, and 20 of 25 checks at 250 ms intervals with the vehicle in run/crank and the pump commanded off fail…",["Broken control or supply wire to the pump","Corroded or unplugged pump connector","Open winding inside the pump motor"],["Check-engine lamp after two trips","Less electric assist and regeneration"],2,3,["P0CC2","P0CC3","P0CC4","P0CBD","P0C73"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0CC2:["Drive Motor A Coolant Pump Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0CC2 judges whether the pump for drive motor A actually moves coolant, not only whether its wiring is intact. GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C infers flow from temperature: when the model predicts more than 17 °C between the jacket coolant of the motor generator and the coolant reaching the loop sensor, a running pump should make the sensor warm up quickly.",["Air in the motor generator cooling loop, often after service","Worn or seized pump impeller","Low coolant level or kinked or blocked hose"],["Check-engine lamp","Electric assist reduced as the motor generator heats up"],2,3,["P0CC1","P0CC3","P0CC4","P0CBD","P0A2F","P0C73"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0CC3:["Drive Motor A Coolant Pump Control Circuit Low","P0CC3 indicates that the control line of the drive motor A coolant pump is pulled to ground. GM lists it for the 2013 eAssist cars in table 13OBDG05C: the hardware status of the output driver signals a low-circuit fault, and 20 of 25 samples at 250 ms with the pump commanded off and the vehicle in run/crank fail the test, Type B, so the MIL is lit after the second trip.",["Chafed control wire touching ground","Internal short in the pump","Faulty output stage in the control module"],["Check-engine lamp after two trips","Pump running constantly or not at all","Reduced electric assist"],2,3,["P0CC4","P0CC1","P0CC2","P0CBC"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0CC4:["Drive Motor A Coolant Pump Control Circuit High","P0CC4 stands for a control line of the drive motor A coolant pump that is held at supply voltage or overloaded. In GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C the output driver reports a high-circuit status, and unlike the open and low checks this one runs while the pump is commanded on; 20 failures in 25 samples at 250 ms in run/crank set a Type B code, two trips.",["Control wire shorted to a voltage-carrying line","Jammed pump drawing excess current","Water ingress in the pump connector"],["Check-engine lamp after two trips","Pump silent when it should run"],2,3,["P0CC3","P0CC1","P0CC2","P0CBC"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מנוע הנעה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0CC5:["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A/B Correlation","P0CC5 compares the two current sensors of the 14 V power module, the DC/DC converter that feeds the 12 V network from the high-voltage battery. In GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C, where the unit is the accessory power module (APM), sensor A measures the input and sensor B the output side.",["Offset or gain drift of one current sensor","Internal fault of the power module, e.g. real efficiency loss","Calibration or software fault in the module"],["Check-engine lamp after the second faulty trip","12 V battery not fully charged or low-voltage warning"],4,1,["P0A87","P0A88","P0A89","P0C9D","P0C9E","P0C9F"],""],P0CC6:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Wake-Up Circuit","On plug-in vehicles the button that asks for the charge flap to open must also be able to rouse a sleeping control module, so it drives a separate wake-up line besides its normal signal. P0CC6 records that these two signals disagree.",["Open, chafed or shorted wake-up wire between switch and control module","Worn or contaminated contact in the open request switch","Corroded or loose pins at the switch or module connector","Faulty wake-up input inside the control module"],["Charge flap opens only after several presses or not at all","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)","Possible extra drain on the 12 V battery if the module is kept awake"],2,1,["P0CC7","P0CC9","P0CCA","P0CCB","P2E92"],""],P0CC7:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Circuit","P0CC7 is the general circuit fault of the button that requests opening of charge flap A. GM reads this switch as a percentage of its reference and expects defined states; on the 2011 to 2013 Chevrolet Volt the code sets when the reading sits in a forbidden band between 65.9 and 81.6 percent, which indicates a wrong resistance, for instance a damaged switch or a partly broken wire.",["Open request switch with wrong internal resistance","Partly broken wire or corroded connector raising circuit resistance","Moisture in the switch housing","Faulty switch input in the control module"],["Charge flap does not open with the button","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)","Opening via key, app or screen still possible on some models"],2,1,["P0CC6","P0CC8","P0CC9","P0CCA","P0CCB","P2E93"],""],P0CC8:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Circuit Performance","P0CC8 covers a plausibility fault of the charge flap A open request switch: the signal is electrically within limits, yet its behaviour makes no sense, for example a button that reports pressed for far too long or never changes state over many wake-ups. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Mechanically sticking or jammed open request switch","Worn switch contact giving an implausible signal","Wiring fault that keeps the signal in an unexpected state"],["Charge flap opens unexpectedly or ignores the button","Message about the charge flap in the instrument cluster"],2,0,["P0CC6","P0CC7","P0CC9","P0CCA","P0CCB","P2E94"],""],P0CC9:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Circuit Low","P0CC9 means the open request switch of charge flap A reads lower than any valid state, which typically results from the signal wire touching ground or from a switch that has failed short. In the Chevrolet Volt tables of 2011 to 2013, GM sets it once the reading drops under 22.9 percent of the reference in six of eight samples; the code is Type B, so the warning lamp follows after two consecutive trips.",["Signal wire chafed through to body ground","Open request switch shorted internally","Water in the switch connector creating a leakage path to ground"],["Charge flap does not react to the button","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)"],2,1,["P0CC6","P0CC7","P0CCA","P0CC8","P2E95"],""],P0CCA:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Circuit High","With P0CCA the control module sees the charge flap A open request switch signal above its highest valid level. Depending on the circuit, a wire shorted to battery voltage or an interrupted ground or switch path pushes the reading up.",["Signal wire shorted to a 12 V supply","Interrupted ground or switch return path","Pin pushed back in the connector"],["Charge flap cannot be opened with the button","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)"],2,1,["P0CC6","P0CC7","P0CC9","P0CCB","P2E96"],""],P0CCB:["Charge Port Door A Open Request Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CCB flags a charge flap A open request switch whose signal jumps between states without a matching press, or drops out briefly. Such signals usually come from loose terminals, a cracked solder joint in the switch or a harness that flexes with a door or body panel. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Loose or spread terminal at the switch or module connector","Cracked solder joint or worn contact inside the switch","Harness damaged where it flexes with a door or panel"],["Charge flap opens only occasionally when the button is pressed","Fault comes and goes, often with temperature or road vibration"],2,0,["P0CC6","P0CC7","P0CC8","P0CC9","P0CCA","P2E97"],""],P0CCC:["Charge Port Door A Position Sensor Circuit","The position sensor of charge flap A tells the vehicle whether the flap is open or shut; GM has patented a version in which a magnet on the flap acts on a magnetic sensor in the housing. P0CCC is the general circuit code for this sensor.",["Faulty flap position sensor or switch","Sensor or its harness pigtail not fully seated in the housing","Damaged wire or corroded connector in the sensor circuit"],["Flap-open message although the flap is closed","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)","Charging or charge pilot checks may be blocked"],2,1,["P0CCD","P0CCE","P0CCF","P0CD0","P0CF4","P2E98"],"חיישן מצב דלת פתח טעינה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CCD:["Charge Port Door A Position Sensor Circuit Performance","P0CCD is set when the charge flap A position signal is electrically valid but does not fit what the flap should be doing, for example a state that disagrees with the last commanded movement. GM bulletin 25-NA-049 (March 2025) names the code for the Ultium models Cadillac Lyriq 2023 to 2025, Chevrolet Blazer EV and Equinox EV 2024 to 2025, and Cadillac Optiq and Escalade IQ 2025: drivers see a Service High Voltage…",["Software anomaly in the battery control module (GM Ultium, bulletin 25-NA-049)","Position sensor giving a wrong end-position signal","Faulty harness between flap module and control unit","Flap not reaching its end position because of mechanical binding"],["Service High Voltage message (GM Ultium models)","Flap status shown wrongly; another flap may be locked"],4,0,["P0CCC","P0CCE","P0CCF","P0CD0","P0CD2","P2E99"],"חיישן מצב דלת פתח טעינה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CCE:["Charge Port Door A Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0CCE reports that the charge flap A position sensor signal has fallen below its lowest valid level, the classic sign of a signal wire shorted to ground or a sensor that has failed short. GM documents it for the 2011 to 2013 Chevrolet Volt: the hybrid control module 2 sets it when the reading is under 22.9 percent of reference in six of eight samples, and as a Type B code the lamp comes on after two trips.",["Sensor signal wire shorted to ground","Position sensor shorted internally","Water in the flap area or sensor connector"],["Wrong flap status in the display","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)","Charging may be refused"],2,1,["P0CCC","P0CCD","P0CCF","P0CD0","P0CF4","P2E9A"],"חיישן מצב דלת פתח טעינה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CCF:["Charge Port Door A Position Sensor Circuit High","P0CCF stands for a charge flap A position sensor signal above its highest valid level. A signal wire touching a 12 V feed, or an interrupted sensor ground that lets the input float upwards, are the usual explanations. In GM's Chevrolet Volt tables from 2011 to 2013 the threshold is 94.5 percent of reference, confirmed in six of eight samples; the 2013 table samples at 500 ms.",["Sensor signal wire shorted to a 12 V feed","Interrupted sensor ground or open sensor","Unplugged or damaged sensor connector"],["Flap status stuck or shown as open","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)"],2,1,["P0CCC","P0CCD","P0CCE","P0CD0","P0CF4","P2E9B"],"חיישן מצב דלת פתח טעינה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CD0:["Charge Port Door A Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CD0 completes the position sensor group of charge flap A: the module sees the flap signal flicker or drop out for moments, without a lasting open, short or implausible state. Loose connector terminals, a sensor that is not held firmly in its seat, or a harness strained by the flap mechanism are the usual triggers. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Loose terminal in the position sensor connector","Harness to the flap module chafed or under strain","Sensor or magnet not held firmly in its mounting"],["Flap status in the display changes without reason","Sporadic charging or flap warnings"],2,0,["P0CCC","P0CCD","P0CCE","P0CCF","P2E9C"],"חיישן מצב דלת פתח טעינה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CD1:["Charge Port Door A Unlock Control Circuit","P0CD1 is the circuit fault of the output that releases charge flap A, normally a relay or driver that briefly powers the latch actuator. A GM patent shows such an actuator retracting a post so the spring-loaded flap can pop open.",["Unlock actuator coil open or shorted","Faulty charge flap relay or its control wire","Chafed or corroded wiring to the actuator","Output driver in the control module damaged"],["Charge flap does not unlock","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)","Emergency release needed to reach the charge socket"],2,1,["P0CD2","P0CD3","P0CD4","P0CCC","P2E9D"],""],P0CD2:["Charge Port Door A Unlock Control Circuit Performance","P0CD2 is a functional test: after commanding charge flap A to unlock, the module waits for the position sensor to report the flap open. GM documents it for the Chevrolet Volt. The 2011 table allows 5 seconds, the 2012 and 2013 tables 13 seconds, and the 2013 version only runs above minus 12 °C.",["Flap frozen, stuck to the seal or binding at the hinge","Weak or mechanically blocked unlock actuator","Misaligned charge port housing or hinge pin (GM PI0936)","Position sensor not registering the open flap"],["Flap does not open, especially in frost","Warning lamp after two trips (GM Volt)"],2,1,["P0CD1","P0CD3","P0CD4","P0CCC","P0CCD","P2E9E"],""],P0CD3:["Charge Port Door A Unlock Control Circuit Low","P0CD3 narrows the unlock output fault of charge flap A down to a low level: the monitored voltage on the actuator or relay control line stays near ground when it should be higher, which typically means a short to ground on the control wire or a failed actuator coil pulling the line down. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Actuator control wire shorted to ground","Shorted winding in the unlock actuator","Water in the actuator connector"],["Charge flap does not unlock","Possibly blown fuse in the actuator supply"],2,0,["P0CD1","P0CD2","P0CD4","P2E9F"],""],P0CD4:["Charge Port Door A Unlock Control Circuit High","P0CD4 is the high-level variant of the charge flap A unlock output fault. The control line reads high when the driver should be pulling it low or holding it off, as happens with a short to a 12 V wire, and on many low-side drivers also with an open actuator or broken wire. A flap actuator that is powered all the time may overheat, while an open one simply never releases. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Actuator control wire shorted to a 12 V feed","Open actuator winding or broken wire","Output driver in the control module damaged"],["Charge flap does not unlock or unlocks unexpectedly","Actuator may become hot from continuous current"],2,0,["P0CD1","P0CD2","P0CD3","P2EA0"],""],P0CD5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0CD5 is the general circuit fault for the second coolant temperature sensor in the liquid loop of a hybrid or EV battery. In GM's tables for the Chevrolet Volt and Cadillac ELR, sensor B is the one at the pack outlet, an NTC thermistor read by the battery energy control module, while sensor A sits at the inlet. GM splits electrical faults of sensor B into P0CD7 and P0CD8 and does not list P0CD5 itself.",["Loose, corroded or unplugged connector or damaged wire at the outlet sensor","Coolant temperature sensor B defective","Input circuit or reference in the battery control module faulty"],["Check engine or hybrid system warning lamp","Reduced charging or drive power, especially in heat or cold","Battery heating or cooling runs on substitute values"],3,3,["P0CD6","P0CD7","P0CD8","P0CD9","P0C42"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CD6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0CD6 means that coolant temperature sensor B at the battery outlet is electrically fine, yet its value does not fit the rest of the thermal system. GM runs the check in the hybrid controller of the Volt with the coolant pump above 20 % and no circuit faults present: if the outlet reading is more than 10 °C away from both the inlet reading and the mean cell temperature (2011 Volt, 80 of 100 samples at 100 ms), or at…",["Outlet coolant temperature sensor reading off (drift)","Contact resistance at connector or terminals","Air or low coolant in the battery loop after service","Control module software sets the code incorrectly (e.g. Volvo XC90 T8)"],["MIL on, often without noticeable driving symptoms","Battery cooling or heating behaves unexpectedly"],3,1,["P0CD5","P0CD7","P0CD8","P0C43","P0A9C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CD7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0CD7 reports that the signal of battery coolant temperature sensor B at the pack outlet has dropped toward ground. On an NTC thermistor with pull-up resistor a low voltage reads as very hot coolant, so a short to ground or a damaged sensor is the usual explanation rather than real overheating.",["Signal wire of sensor B shorted to ground","Sensor B internally shorted or water-damaged","Input stage of the battery control module faulty"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Displayed outlet coolant temperature implausibly high","Charging or drive power reduced"],3,3,["P0CD8","P0CD5","P0CD6","P0C44"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CD8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0CD8 is the high-voltage fault of battery coolant temperature sensor B at the pack outlet. On the usual NTC circuit the signal rises to the reference when the loop is broken, so the controller sees an impossible temperature below -40 °C. The battery energy control modules of the 2011 Chevrolet Volt and 2016 Cadillac ELR use exactly this limit, an ADC count above 3823, held for 1.75 s within 2.5 s;",["Connector of sensor B unplugged or not latched after service","Broken signal or ground wire, corroded terminals","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source","Sensor element internally open"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Outlet coolant temperature shows about -40 °C","Limited power or charging in cold weather"],3,3,["P0CD7","P0CD5","P0CD9","P0C45"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CD9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CD9 covers a signal from battery coolant temperature sensor B that drops out or jumps for short moments while staying inside the electrical limits most of the time. It is meant for loose terminals, chafed wires or a sensor that misbehaves only with vibration or temperature.",["False detection after charging in warm weather (Volvo, software update)","Loose or corroded terminal at the sensor or module connector","Chafed harness that moves with the battery or body","Sensor B with an intermittent internal fault"],["Check engine light, often after charging","Jumping outlet temperature value in live data"],2,1,["P0CD7","P0CD8","P0CD5","P0C46"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CDA:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit C","P0CDA belongs to the rotor position sensor of drive motor A, normally a resolver: an excited stator winding induces signals in pick-up windings so the motor controller can work out rotor angle and speed. SAE counts the circuits of this sensor as A, B and C; GM, for instance, assigns circuit A to the S1/S3 winding and circuit B to S2/S4. What circuit C carries is not described in any public manufacturer document;",["Loose, corroded or damaged resolver connector between motor and inverter","Damaged resolver wiring","Resolver winding of circuit C defective","Evaluation circuit in the motor control module faulty"],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Electric drive limited or unavailable, engine runs more","Reduced regenerative braking"],3,3,["P0CDB","P0CDC","P0CDD","P0CDE","P0A3F","P0C50","P0C5A"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A C"],P0CDB:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit C Range/Performance","P0CDB flags circuit C of the drive motor A position sensor as present but implausible: its amplitude or phase, or the angle calculated from it, no longer agrees with the other circuits or with the observed motor speed. GM runs comparable tests on the main resolver signals, for example an amplitude check of the sine and cosine windings under P0A3F rated Type A, and limits for circuits A and B.",["Winding or terminal of the resolver changing its values when hot","Resolver shifted on its mount or rotor bearing play","Motor control module software or calibration"],["Hybrid system warning, MIL","Reduced electric power, engine starts in EV mode","Fault appears more often when the drivetrain is warm"],4,3,["P0CDA","P0CDC","P0CDD","P0CDE","P0A40","P0C51","P0C5B"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0CDC:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit C Low","P0CDC reports that circuit C of the rotor position sensor on drive motor A reads below its permitted window. Typical reasons are a short to ground, a short between windings or a low-resistance path through moisture in the connector.",["Circuit C wire shorted to ground or to another resolver wire","Moisture or corrosion in the resolver connector","Resolver winding internally shorted"],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Electric drive limited, engine runs instead","Reduced or no regenerative braking"],3,3,["P0CDD","P0CDA","P0CDB","P0CDE","P0A41","P0C52","P0C5C"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A C — ערך נמוך"],P0CDD:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit C High","P0CDD indicates that circuit C of the drive motor A position sensor reads above its limit, which in general points to an open winding, a disconnected plug or a short to a voltage source. Subaru uses the code in the drive motor control module of the 2014 XV Crosstrek Hybrid. Bulletin 07-82-14R explains that P0CDD together with P0A3F could be stored whenever the 12 V auxiliary battery sagged below specified limits;",["12 V auxiliary battery voltage dips with outdated module logic (Subaru)","Open winding, broken wire or disconnected resolver plug","Circuit C wire shorted to a voltage source"],["MIL on (requested via P0CA1 on Subaru)","Hybrid system warning, electric assist missing"],3,3,["P0CDC","P0CDA","P0CDE","P0A3F","P0CA1","P0A42"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A C — ערך גבוה"],P0CDE:["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit C Intermittent/Erratic","P0CDE describes a circuit C signal from the drive motor A position sensor that drops out or jumps briefly instead of failing for good. That pattern suits loose terminals at the resolver plug, a harness strained by drivetrain movement, or a winding or contact that opens only when hot. Short glitches matter because the inverter needs the rotor angle continuously;",["Loose or spread terminal in the resolver connector","Harness strained or chafed by drivetrain movement","Resolver winding or joint that opens when hot"],["Occasional jolt or hybrid system warning","Temporary switch to engine operation","Fault disappears after restart"],3,3,["P0CDA","P0CDC","P0CDD","P0A43","P0C54","P0C5E"],"מעגל חיישן מצב מנוע הנעה‏ A C — אות לא יציב"],P0CDF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Control Circuit/Open","P0CDF signals an electrical fault in the drive circuit of coolant control valve A, the valve that routes or shuts off coolant to the hybrid or EV battery. Mercedes-Benz uses it in the battery management unit N82/2 of the S-, E- and C-Class hybrids, where it marks the actuation of the battery module coolant shutoff valve as electrically faulty or interrupted (service information LI83.30-P-061224, together with the…",["Valve coil or actuator motor open","Broken wire or corroded connector at the valve","Driver stage in the battery or thermal control unit failed"],["MIL or hybrid system warning","Battery cooling or heating not working as intended","Reduced charging or drive power"],3,3,["P0CE2","P0CE3","P0CE0","P0CE1","P0D1A","P0DE8"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0CE0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Performance/Stuck Off","P0CE0 means coolant control valve A of the battery loop is electrically sound but does not reach or hold its commanded position. GM's 2011 Chevrolet Volt checks the four-port valve in stages: an end-stop learn, reaching the target within 1.5 s, drift above 3 % and a noisy feedback; 1280 of 1600 samples at 6.25 ms set a Type B code. GM's 2016 ELR tables use P1F56 for this.",["Control software or limits too strict (Audi, Toyota updates)","Valve sticking or actuator too weak","Position feedback drifted or noisy"],["MIL on, often without other symptoms","Battery temperature regulation less effective"],3,1,["P0CE1","P0CDF","P0CE5","P0CE2","P0CE3"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים או תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0CE1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Stuck On","P0CE1 reports that coolant control valve A of the battery circuit remains in its active position although the controller commanded a change. Manufacturers phrase it differently: Mercedes hybrid battery management speaks of a shutoff valve that does not open, Volvo of a valve stuck on in the BECM of the XC90 T8 from model year 2016.",["Monitoring limits or software too strict (Audi, Volvo updates)","Low coolant level or air in the battery coolant circuit","Changeover valve mechanically stuck"],["MIL on, usually without driving symptoms","Battery cooled or heated longer than needed"],2,1,["P0CE0","P0CDF","P0CE5","P0C43"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0CE2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Control Circuit Low","P0CE2 points to a ground short on the drive circuit of coolant control valve A. In the 2011 Chevrolet Volt and 2016 Cadillac ELR, GM's four-port battery valve is moved by a small motor with two driver lines. The hybrid controller sets P0CE2 when driver line 1 reads low while the valve should be travelling in reverse, with system voltage above 9 V (2011) or 10.2 V (2016);",["Driver wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Water or corrosion in the valve connector","Valve actuator motor internally shorted"],["MIL after two trips (GM)","Valve does not move, battery thermal conditioning limited"],3,3,["P0CE3","P0CDF","P0CE0","P0D1D"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0CE3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Control Circuit High","P0CE3 is the short-to-voltage fault of the coolant control valve A drive circuit. GM tests it on the motor-driven four-port battery valve of the 2011 Chevrolet Volt and 2016 Cadillac ELR: driver line 1 reads high while the valve should be stopped or moving forward. System voltage must exceed 9 V or 10.2 V, and 90 of 100 samples at 6.25 ms set a Type B code with the MIL on the second trip.",["Driver wire pinched against a supply line","Harness damage near the battery or valve","Driver stage in the control module faulty"],["MIL after two trips (GM)","Battery heating or cooling in fallback mode"],3,3,["P0CE2","P0CDF","P0CE1","P0D1E"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0CE4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve Position Sensor Circuit","P0CE4 is the general circuit fault of the position feedback from the battery coolant control valve. On motor-driven valves the actuator reports its actual position as a share of the sensor supply voltage, letting the controller confirm that coolant really flows toward the battery, chiller or heater. GM's Volt and ELR monitor this feedback but report its electrical faults as P0CE6 and P0CE7;",["Open signal, reference or ground wire of the position sensor","Damaged or corroded connector at the valve","Position sensor inside the valve actuator failed"],["MIL or hybrid system warning","Valve held in a default position, thermal conditioning limited"],3,3,["P0CE5","P0CE6","P0CE7","P0CE8","P0CDF"],"חיישן מצב שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CE5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0CE5 means the position feedback of the battery coolant control valve is electrically valid, but the valve has not arrived where the controller sent it. In GM's 2016 Cadillac ELR tables the hybrid controller allows up to 22 s; if the feedback still shows a different position once the end-stop learn routine P1F56 has finished, 1280 of 1600 samples at 6.25 ms set a Type B code with the MIL on the second trip.",["Valve stiff from deposits or low temperature","Actuator motor weak or gearing worn","Position sensor drifted from learned end stops","Air or low coolant in the battery circuit"],["MIL after two trips (GM)","Battery reaches its target temperature more slowly"],3,1,["P0CE4","P0CE6","P0CE7","P0CE0","P0CE1"],"חיישן מצב שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CE6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0CE6 reports that the position feedback of the battery coolant control valve has fallen below its permitted window. GM expresses the signal relative to the reference voltage: the 2011 Chevrolet Volt fails it below 5 % in 40 of 50 samples at 100 ms, the 2016 Cadillac ELR below 28 % in 640 of 800 samples at 6.25 ms, a value under the lowest end stop the ELR accepts during learning (just over 30 %).",["Feedback signal wire shorted to ground","Sensor reference voltage missing or open","Worn or defective sensor track in the actuator"],["MIL after two trips (GM)","Valve position shown as near 0 % in live data"],3,3,["P0CE7","P0CE4","P0CE5","P0CE8"],"חיישן מצב שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CE7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve Position Sensor Circuit High","P0CE7 is the high-side fault of the battery coolant control valve position feedback. GM sets it when the signal exceeds 95 % of the reference voltage in the 2011 Chevrolet Volt (40 of 50 samples at 100 ms) or 72 % in the 2016 Cadillac ELR (640 of 800 samples at 6.25 ms), in both cases with system voltage above 9 V or 10.2 V and rated Type B, so the MIL lights on the second trip.",["Sensor ground wire interrupted","Signal wire shorted to reference or battery voltage","Worn sensor track in the actuator"],["MIL after two trips (GM)","Valve position shown near 100 % in live data"],3,3,["P0CE6","P0CE4","P0CE5","P0CE8"],"חיישן מצב שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CE8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0CE8 stands for a valve position feedback that drops out or jumps briefly while the hard low and high limits are not violated long enough to set P0CE6 or P0CE7. Such patterns come from loose pins at the valve plug, a cracked solder joint or a worn sensor track that loses contact at certain angles.",["Loose or corroded pin in the valve connector","Worn sensor track losing contact at certain positions","Cracked solder joint inside the actuator"],["Occasional warning lamp that may go out again","Jumping valve position in live data"],2,1,["P0CE6","P0CE7","P0CE4","P0CE5"],"חיישן מצב שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CE9:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit/Open","The hybrid controller checks the control line of the electric pump that moves coolant through the power electronics loop, which in GM's Volt and ELR serves the inverter module and the high-voltage charger. GM tables from 2011 to 2016 fail it when the output driver reports an open line or a short to ground or voltage in 16 of 20 samples at 250 ms.",["Open or chafed control wire, or corroded connector at the electronics coolant pump","Failed electronics in the pump itself, drawing no current or far too much","Coolant loss from a damaged hose letting the pump run dry (Audi e-tron/Q8 e-tron bulletin)","Faulty output driver in the hybrid or thermal management control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid/drive system warning on","Reduced drive power because inverter and charger temperatures rise","Charging power reduced or charging interrupted","Pump not audible after switch-on when commanded via scan tool"],2,3,["P0CEA","P0CEB","P0CEC","P0CED","P2B2A","P2E41"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0CEA:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Pump A Performance","Here the pump circuit is electrically intact, yet the coolant it should move does not seem to arrive. GM documents it in the VICM tables of 2014 and 2016, the latter explicitly for the Cadillac ELR: with the pump enabled above 30 % speed for more than 300 s, the controller compares the power electronics coolant temperature with the temperature of the high-voltage charger.",["Air trapped in the power electronics coolant loop after service or a leak","Low coolant level in the electronics loop reservoir","Worn or partly blocked pump delivering too little flow","Skewed coolant or charger temperature signal distorting the comparison"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system message","Charging slows down or stops, especially in hot weather","Reduced propulsion power during long or demanding drives"],2,3,["P0CE9","P0CED","P0CEF","P2B2A","P2E42"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0CEB:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Low","The control line of the electronics coolant pump sits at too low a level, typically because it is shorted to ground or the driver cannot pull it up. No manufacturer publishes setting criteria for this code. GM's 2019 HPC2 table names P0CEB only as a blocking code alongside P0CE9, P0CEC and P0CED: while any of them is active, the plausibility test of the electronics coolant temperature sensor (P0CEF) does not run.",["Control wire chafed through and touching body ground","Pump control input shorted internally","Water or corrosion in the pump connector bridging pins to ground","Faulty output stage in the controlling module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid warning","Electronics coolant pump does not run","Power or charging limited as electronics temperatures rise"],2,3,["P0CE9","P0CEC","P0CED","P2E43"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0CEC:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit High","P0CEC flags a pump control line that is held too high, normally a short to battery voltage on the output. GM's HPC2 tables of 2016 and 2019 (16OBDG02, 19OBDG02) look for it while the pump enable is on and the commanded duty cycle is between 0 and 10 %: a line reading high when it should be nearly off counts as a failure, and 16 of 20 samples at 250 ms set the code, Type B, two trips, with system voltage above 10.2…",["Control wire shorted to a 12 V feed in the harness","Moisture or corrosion in the pump connector linking supply and control pins","Pump electronics feeding voltage back onto the control line","Output stage in the control module stuck high"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Pump runs permanently or does not respond to speed changes","Possible power limitation of the hybrid system"],2,3,["P0CE9","P0CEB","P0CED","P2E44"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0CED:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Pump A Enable Circuit","Some electronics coolant pumps receive two signals: a speed command and a separate enable wire that switches the pump on. P0CED concerns that enable wire. In GM's 2011 Volt (11OBDG01) any open or short on it sets the code after 16 of 20 samples at 250 ms with more than 9 V supply, Type B, two trips.",["Broken enable wire or terminal pushed out of the connector","Enable wire shorted to ground or voltage (codes it on older GM controllers)","Defective enable input inside the pump","Faulty enable driver in the control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Electronics coolant pump remains off","Reduced power or charging rate when electronics get warm"],2,3,["P0CE9","P0CEA","P0CEB","P0CEC","P2E45"],"אפשור משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CEE:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","Sensor A reports the coolant temperature in the loop that cools the hybrid power electronics. P0CEE is the general circuit code for it, used where a controller does not tell low from high. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM, for example, reports the same sensor only through P0CF0 (below 2 or 4 % of the reference voltage), P0CF1 (above 98 %) and the plausibility and noise checks P0CEF and P0CF2.",["Loose, corroded or water-soaked sensor connector","Damaged signal or ground wire of the sensor","Failed temperature sensor element"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Electronics coolant pump runs continuously","Reduced drive or charging power as a precaution"],2,3,["P0CEF","P0CF0","P0CF1","P0CF2","P2B20"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0CEF:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The sensor stays electrically within range but reports a temperature that does not fit its surroundings. GM uses two kinds of comparison. In the 2011 Volt and the ELR it is checked against the high-voltage charger temperature with the pump running at 30 % or more for over 60 or 300 s; the limit is 38 to 40 °C (2011) or 35 °C (ELR).",["Sensor element drifted and reads offset from the real coolant temperature","Added resistance from corroded terminals shifting the signal","Genuine temperature gap from poor flow or air in the electronics loop","Comparison sensor (charger, outside air or transmission fluid) reading wrong without its own code"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on, often without noticeable drivability change","Electronics coolant pump runs at odd times or longer than usual","Occasional power or charging limitation"],2,1,["P0CEE","P0CF0","P0CF1","P0CF2","P0CEA","P0071","P0711","P2B21"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CF0:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The signal voltage of the electronics coolant temperature sensor drops close to zero, which almost always means the signal wire touches ground or the sensor is shorted internally. Low refers to the voltage, not to the temperature. GM sets the code below 2 % of the 5 V reference (0.1 V) in the 2011 Volt, the ELR and the 2016 HPC2 table, and below 4 % (0.2 V) in the 2016 Volt and the 2019 HPC2 table.",["Sensor signal wire shorted to ground","Internally shorted temperature sensor","Water in the connector bridging signal and ground"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid warning","Scan tool shows an implausibly high coolant temperature","Pump running constantly, power or charging reduced"],2,3,["P0CEE","P0CEF","P0CF1","P0CF2","P2B22"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CF1:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The sensor signal rises nearly to the 5 V reference, the typical picture of an open circuit, an unplugged connector or a signal wire shorted to voltage. GM sets it above 98 % of the reference (4.9 V) in all published tables from the 2011 Volt to the 2019 HPC2 controller, 16 of 20 samples at 250 ms or 4 s within 5 s, Type B, two trips.",["Water or corrosion in an underbody inline connector (Chrysler Pacifica Hybrid XY291A)","Open signal or ground wire, or unplugged sensor","Sensor element open internally","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"],["Service Hybrid Electrical System or similar warning","Scan tool shows an implausibly low coolant temperature","Charging or drive power limited"],2,3,["P0CEE","P0CEF","P0CF0","P0CF2","P2B23"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CF2:["Hybrid/EV Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","This code catches a coolant temperature signal that jumps around faster than a liquid can actually change temperature, without necessarily leaving the valid voltage window. GM computes a string length: the controller adds up the absolute steps between four consecutive readings taken 250 ms apart. If that total exceeds 90 °C (2016 Volt, 19OBDG01) or 95 °C (16OBDG02), the window counts as failed;",["Loose or spread terminal in the sensor connector","Chafed wire making intermittent contact with ground or another circuit","Cracked internal connection in the sensor"],["Malfunction indicator lamp, sometimes coming and going","Coolant temperature value jumps in live data","Pump speed or power limits change without reason"],2,3,["P0CEE","P0CEF","P0CF0","P0CF1"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת היברידי/חשמלי‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CF3:["Control Pilot Circuit/Open","The control pilot is the signal line between charging station and vehicle: the station drives a 1 kHz square wave of about ±12 V, the vehicle loads it through a diode and resistors and thereby moves the level between the charging states. P0CF3 means the charge control module sees this line as interrupted, so no usable pilot reaches it although a connector is expected.",["Charging or hybrid control module software misjudging the pilot (FCA reflash bulletins)","Bent, pushed-back or corroded pilot pin in the charge inlet, or a broken pilot wire","Defective charging cable or station that delivers no pilot signal"],["Charging does not start although the cable is plugged in","Charging aborts shortly after connection","Malfunction indicator lamp on and code not clearing (FCA)"],2,1,["P0CF4","P0CF5","P0CF6","P0CF7","P0D57","P0D2A"],"פיילוט בקרה — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0CF4:["Control Pilot Circuit Range/Performance","P0CF4 flags a pilot signal that is electrically present but does not fit what the vehicle expects. GM spells out the test for the Chevrolet Volt (2011-2019, VICM/HPC2): with no cord connected and the car moving at 8 km/h or more, pilot voltage divided by battery voltage must stay below 0.03 and both processor logic states must agree;",["Faulty pilot measuring or self-test circuit inside the charging/hybrid control module","Module software needing an update (FCA Pacifica and 4xe bulletins)","Moisture or dirt in the charge inlet creating a leakage path on the pilot line","Charge door position fault blocking or distorting the test (GM Volt enable condition)"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on after one trip (GM)","Charging refused or interrupted","Charge status indicator shows a fault"],2,1,["P0CF3","P0CF5","P0CF6","P0CF7","P0CF9","P0D01","P0CCC"],"פיילוט בקרה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0CF5:["Control Pilot Circuit Low","Circuit Low means the pilot level sits under the lowest value any regular charging state can produce, as happens when the pilot wire is shorted to ground or to the protective earth contact. GM checks it in the Chevrolet Volt VICM/HPC2 tables from 2012 to 2019: with the cord unplugged, the controller feeds its own test signal through the internal diagnostic switch, and the ratio of pilot voltage to 12 V battery…",["Pilot wire chafed through and touching ground or the protective earth conductor","Water or corrosion bridging pilot and earth pins in the charge inlet","Failed pilot input or test circuit in the charging control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on (GM Type A)","Station does not detect the vehicle, charging never starts","Charge status indicator shows a fault after plugging in"],2,1,["P0CF3","P0CF4","P0CF6","P0CF7"],"פיילוט בקרה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CF6:["Control Pilot Circuit High","A pilot level above the valid range usually means the line touches a supply voltage, or the measuring path in the module reads wrongly. GM evaluates it on the Chevrolet Volt (VICM/HPC2, 2012-2019) only while no cord is connected and the car is travelling at 8 km/h or faster, a moment when the pilot should be practically dead: a normalized pilot voltage (pilot voltage over battery voltage) above 0.55, or 0.58 in the…",["Pilot wire shorted to a 12 V feed in the harness or inlet","Pilot measuring circuit in the charging control module reading too high","Moisture in a connector coupling a neighbouring voltage onto the pilot"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on after a drive (GM Type A)","Charging does not begin at the station","Charging fault message in the cluster"],2,1,["P0CF3","P0CF4","P0CF5","P0CF7"],"פיילוט בקרה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CF7:["Control Pilot Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","This code stands for a pilot signal that comes and goes or jumps between levels, typically from a loose contact in the charge inlet, a plug that is not fully latched or interference on the line. Ford documents P0CF7 on the 2021-2022 Mustang Mach-E and the 2022 e-Transit, logged by the SOBDM (secondary OBD module A), and on the 2022 F-150 Lightning by the same unit, there called BCCM, the charger control module.",["Charger module software reacting too sensitively (Ford SOBDM/BCCM reprogramming)","Charging plug not fully seated or latched in the inlet","Worn or loose pilot contact in the inlet or on the charging cable","Charging station producing an unstable pilot signal"],["Charge status indicator flashing red (Ford)","Charging stops intermittently","Additional warning lamp with related codes such as P0A7F"],2,0,["P0CF3","P0CF4","P0CF5","P0CF6","P0A7F"],"פיילוט בקרה — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0CF8:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Circuit/Open","On the vehicle side the pilot is loaded permanently by about 2.7 kΩ, which drops the station's +12 V to roughly 9 V once a cable is connected. To ask for energy the vehicle closes charging switch A, often called S2, which adds a 1.3 kΩ branch and brings the level to about 6 V, the state in which the station closes its contactors.",["Open control wire or loose connector between module and charging switch","Charging switch or its 1.3 kΩ branch open inside the module or inlet electronics","Failed driver stage in the charging control module"],["Station detects the vehicle but charging never begins","Charging fault message after plugging in"],2,0,["P0CF9","P0CFA","P0CFB","P0CFC","P0CFD","P0CFE"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0CF9:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Range/Performance","Charging switch A is the vehicle-side switch that adds a resistor across the pilot to tell the charging station that the car is ready to take current. P0CF9 says the effect of that switch is implausible.",["Charging switch resistor branch out of tolerance or switch with high contact resistance","Internal fault in the VICM/charging module measuring or switching circuit","Leakage on the pilot line from moisture in the charge inlet"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on (GM Type A)","Charging does not start or is refused"],4,1,["P0CF4","P0CF8","P0CFA","P0CFB","P0CFD","P0D01"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0CFA:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Circuit Low","Charging switch A shifts the control pilot from the connected state at about 9 V to the charge-request state at about 6 V by switching in an extra 1.3 kΩ path. P0CFA indicates that the control or feedback line of this switch reads too low, typically a harness wire touching ground or a driver stage in the charging module that has failed shorted. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Switch control or feedback wire shorted to ground","Shorted driver stage in the charging control module","Corrosion in the inlet or module connector bridging to ground"],["Charging does not start, or starts as soon as the plug is inserted","Charging fault message in the cluster"],2,0,["P0CF8","P0CF9","P0CFB","P0CFC","P0CFE"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0CFB:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Circuit High","P0CFB is the high-side counterpart: the control or feedback line of charging switch A, which loads the pilot with an extra 1.3 kΩ to request charging, shows a voltage above its valid band. Typical are a short to a supply voltage in the harness or connector, or an output stage in the charging module that no longer pulls the line down. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Switch control or feedback wire shorted to a supply voltage","Output stage in the charging module no longer switching to ground","Lost ground at the switch letting the line float high"],["Vehicle detected at the station but no energy flows","Charging cannot be stopped normally"],2,0,["P0CF8","P0CF9","P0CFA","P0CFC","P0CFD"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0CFC:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Intermittent/Erratic","With P0CFC the module sees the circuit of charging switch A, which places the charge request on the pilot line, change state erratically: the signal toggles briefly or vanishes for moments, whereas lasting faults would set P0CF8, P0CFA or P0CFB. Likely triggers are a loose pin, corrosion in the charge inlet or module connector, or a chafed wire that loses contact only under vibration or temperature change.",["Loose or spread terminal in the switch circuit connector","Corrosion or moisture in the charge inlet","Chafed wire losing contact under vibration or temperature change"],["Charging interrupted repeatedly, sometimes restarting by itself","Code returns after clearing"],2,0,["P0CF7","P0CF8","P0CF9","P0CFA","P0CFB"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — אות לא יציב"],P0CFD:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Stuck Off","Stuck Off means the module commands charging switch A closed, but the pilot does not show the expected effect: the level stays at the connected value of about 9 V instead of dropping to around 6 V, so the station never receives a charge request. Possible are a switch or relay that no longer closes, an open resistor in the 1.3 kΩ branch, or a fault in the pilot measuring path.",["Charging switch or relay in the module no longer closing","Open resistor or solder joint in the 1.3 kΩ branch","Charging module software not issuing the close command correctly"],["Vehicle shows plugged in, but charging never begins","Charging station remains in standby or connected state"],4,0,["P0CF8","P0CF9","P0CFA","P0CFB","P0CFE"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0CFE:["Control Pilot Charging Switch A Stuck On","Stuck On describes a charging switch A that stays closed while the module wants it open, so the pilot keeps signalling a charge request. Mercedes-Benz documents P0CFE00 in the AC charger N83, whose fault text states that the charger’s internal charge-start switch does not open.",["On-board charger software, cured by SCN coding (Mercedes-Benz N83)","Charging switch or relay contacts sticking closed","Control line fault holding the switch permanently on"],["Charging error message in the instrument cluster after charging","Charging aborts at home socket, wallbox or public station","Fault appears mainly with pre-entry climate control active (Mercedes-Benz)"],4,0,["P0CF8","P0CF9","P0CFA","P0CFB","P0CFD"],"מתג טעינת פיילוט בקרה‏ A — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0CFF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Stuck/Stalled","Unlike the electronics codes above, P0CFF belongs to the pump that circulates coolant through the high-voltage battery. It means the pump is commanded but does not turn, or stops. Volkswagen and Audi log P0CFF00 in the battery management unit J840 (address 008C): VW bulletin 93-21-02 for the 2021 ID.4 blames trapped air when pump V590 did not run during the fill-and-bleed routine, and Audi's 2068363 series for the…",["Air trapped in the battery coolant loop, pump running dry and stalling (VW ID.4, Audi Q4 e-tron)","Battery control module software misjudging pump state (Ford 2020 hybrids)","Seized or mechanically blocked battery coolant pump","Low coolant level in the battery loop"],["Warning such as Electric drive fault or Electrical system not working correctly","Malfunction indicator lamp on (Ford)","Reduced charging speed, especially DC fast charging","Reduced drive power when the battery warms up"],2,3,["P0C47","P0C4A","P2B28","P2B29","P2CF3","P2CF5"],""],P0D00:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Circuit/Open","Some battery types can give off gases while charging; for that case the conductive charging standards provide a state in which the vehicle asks the station for ventilation. The vehicle signals it by switching a much smaller resistor of about 270 Ω onto the pilot, which drags the level to around 3 V; the station then only delivers power where ventilation is assured.",["Open control wire or loose connector of the ventilation switch circuit","Ventilation switch or its resistor branch open inside the module","Failed driver stage in the charging control module"],["Stored code, charging usually still works","Charging system warning in the cluster"],2,0,["P0D01","P0D02","P0D03","P0D04","P0D05","P0D06"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0D01:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Range/Performance","The ventilation switch lets the vehicle request charging state D, in which a small resistor pulls the pilot down to about 3 V and the station may only deliver power with ventilation. P0D01 means this switch does not produce a plausible pilot level.",["Internal fault in the VICM/charging module switching or measuring circuit","Ventilation resistor branch out of tolerance or high contact resistance","Leakage on the pilot line, for example moisture in the charge inlet"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on (GM Type A)","Charging possibly refused at stations without ventilation"],4,1,["P0CF4","P0CF9","P0D00","P0D02","P0D03","P0D05","P0D06"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D02:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Circuit Low","P0D02 marks the control or feedback line of the ventilation switch as too low, usually a harness wire grounded somewhere along its route or a charging module driver that has failed shorted. That switch places a resistor of about 270 Ω across the pilot so that the level falls to roughly 3 V, the state in which a vehicle demands charging with ventilation. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Ventilation switch control or feedback wire shorted to ground","Shorted driver stage in the charging control module","Corrosion in a connector bridging the line to ground"],["Charging refused at stations without ventilation","Stored code without noticeable effect"],2,0,["P0D00","P0D01","P0D03","P0D04","P0D06"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D03:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Circuit High","Here the line that drives or reports the ventilation switch reads above its valid range, which points to a short to a supply voltage or a driver stage that no longer switches. The ventilation switch is the vehicle-side element that requests charging with ventilation by loading the control pilot with roughly 270 Ω and lowering it to around 3 V. No manufacturer documents this code publicly;",["Ventilation switch control or feedback wire shorted to a supply voltage","Output stage in the charging module no longer switching","Lost ground at the switch letting the line float high"],["Stored code, sometimes with a charging system warning","Charging refused if the switch is forced closed"],2,0,["P0D00","P0D01","P0D02","P0D04","P0D05"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D04:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Intermittent/Erratic","P0D04 covers short, irregular disturbances in the ventilation switch circuit: the signal drops out or flickers, while lasting faults would be stored as P0D00, P0D02 or P0D03. Worn or corroded contacts in the charge inlet or module connector, and wires that lose contact under vibration, are the obvious suspects.",["Loose or spread terminal in the ventilation switch circuit","Corrosion or moisture in the charge inlet or module connector","Chafed wire with contact loss under vibration"],["Charging sessions stop without obvious reason","Code returns after clearing"],2,0,["P0D00","P0D01","P0D02","P0D03","P0CF7"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — אות לא יציב"],P0D05:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Stuck Off","Stuck Off for the ventilation switch means the module commands the switch closed, yet the pilot does not drop to the level around 3 V that marks a request for charging with ventilation. Possible causes are a switch that no longer closes, an open resistor in the roughly 270 Ω branch, or a measuring fault in the module.",["Ventilation switch in the module no longer closing","Open resistor or solder joint in the ventilation branch","Pilot measuring fault in the charging module"],["Stored code, charging normally still possible","Charging system warning in the cluster on some vehicles"],4,0,["P0D00","P0D01","P0D02","P0D03","P0D06"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0D06:["Control Pilot Charging Ventilation Switch Stuck On","Stuck On means the ventilation switch remains closed when it should be open, so the pilot is held near 3 V and the station reads a permanent demand for charging with ventilation. Stations that cannot provide ventilation do not supply power in that state, which in practice stops charging altogether.",["Ventilation switch contacts welded or sticking closed","Short across the ventilation resistor branch","Module output or software holding the switch on"],["Charging station does not supply power","Charging error message after plugging in"],4,0,["P0D00","P0D01","P0D02","P0D03","P0D05","P0CFE"],"מתג אוורור טעינת פיילוט בקרה — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0D07:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Circuit","P0D07 is the general circuit code for positive contactor A of the charging system, the switch that joins the plus side of a charging path to the traction battery. Depending on the design this path comes from the DC fast-charge inlet or from the on-board charger; the Chevrolet Volt, for instance, places charging contactors between charger and pack.",["Contactor coil open or shorted","Damaged wiring or corroded connector in the contactor branch","Fault in the module that drives and monitors the contactor"],["Charging not possible or aborted","Charging system warning in the instrument cluster"],4,0,["P0D08","P0D09","P0D0A","P0D0E","P0AA0"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D08:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Stuck Closed","P0D08 means positive charging contactor A has stayed closed although the control module commanded it open, typically because the contacts have welded. The module notices this when voltage remains on the charging side, for instance at the charge port once a session has ended, as a Toyota patent on charge-relay sticking explains. GM stores P0D08 in the BECM of the 2023 Cadillac Lyriq;",["Contactor contacts welded, for example after switching under load","Battery module software misjudging the contactor state (GM Lyriq, Ram ProMaster EV bulletins)","Driver stage or coil supply that does not switch off"],["Malfunction indicator lamp and high-voltage service message","Charging refused","Vehicle may not switch to ready mode"],4,3,["P0D07","P0D09","P0D0F","P0AA1"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקוע במצב סגור"],P0D09:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Stuck Open","P0D09 is set when positive charging contactor A fails to close: after the command the expected voltage never shows up on the charger side. GM documents the check for the Chevrolet Volt (VICM 2016, HPC2 2019): during charger precharge, with charging and precharge contactors on and the negative main contactor still off, a charger voltage under 10 V ends the test;",["Contactor mechanically failing to close or burnt contacts","Fault in the on-board charger or integrated charging unit (Kia ICCU bulletin)","Control software needing an update (Stellantis, GM Lyriq)","Missing coil supply because of a wiring or fuse fault"],["Charging does not start or ends shortly after plugging in","Malfunction indicator lamp after one trip (GM Type A)","Charging or high-voltage warning in the cluster"],4,1,["P0D07","P0D08","P0D0A","P0D10","P0D26","P0AA2"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקוע במצב פתוח"],P0D0A:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Control Circuit/Open","P0D0A covers the drive circuit of positive charging contactor A: the module compares the commanded state of its driver with the voltage and current it actually sees on the coil line. GM spells this out for the Chevrolet Volt and Cadillac ELR: with the driver off, an output above 4 V means an open line and one close to supply voltage a short to power;",["Open or high-resistance coil control wire or connector","Contactor coil open","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage","Faulty high-side driver in the control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp (GM Type A or B)","Vehicle does not charge"],4,1,["P0D07","P0D09","P0D0B","P0D0C","P0D0D","P0D11"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D0B:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0D0B reports that the control circuit of positive charging contactor A works electrically but does not behave plausibly – coil current or line voltage outside the window expected for the commanded state, or a response that comes too slowly. No manufacturer documents P0D0B publicly.",["Contact resistance or corrosion in the control circuit connectors","Contactor coil with changed resistance","Weakening driver stage in the control module"],["Charging sessions fail sporadically","Stored code, possibly with a charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D0A","P0D0C","P0D0D","P0D09","P0D12"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0D0C:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Control Circuit Low","P0D0C stands for a control line of positive charging contactor A that reads too low, usually a short to ground or a driver output pulled down. When the coil is fed through a high-side driver, a grounded line leaves the contactor without power or overloads the driver. No manufacturer documents P0D0C publicly.",["Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Moisture in a connector creating a path to ground","Shorted contactor coil"],["Charging does not start","Charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D0A","P0D0B","P0D0D","P0D13"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0D0D:["Battery Charging System Positive Contactor A Control Circuit High","P0D0D means the control line of positive charging contactor A sits too high, typically from a short to a supply voltage or a driver that no longer switches off. The coil may then stay energised, so the contactor could close without a command, which the module has to prevent. No manufacturer documents P0D0D publicly;",["Control wire shorted to a supply voltage","Driver stage in the control module stuck on","Open ground return of the coil letting the line float high"],["Charging blocked","Charging or high-voltage system warning"],4,0,["P0D0A","P0D0B","P0D0C","P0D08","P0D14"],"מגען חיובי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0D0E:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Circuit","P0D0E is the general circuit code for negative contactor A of the charging system, which connects the minus side of the charging path – from DC inlet or on-board charger – to the traction battery. It is used when the module sees an electrical fault on that contactor that it does not classify more precisely. No manufacturer documents P0D0E publicly.",["Contactor coil open or shorted","Damaged wiring or corroded connector in the contactor branch","Fault in the module that drives and monitors the contactor"],["Charging not possible or aborted","Charging system warning in the instrument cluster"],4,0,["P0D07","P0D0F","P0D10","P0D11","P0AA3"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D0F:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Stuck Closed","P0D0F flags negative charging contactor A as stuck closed: it still conducts after the open command, usually through welded contacts or a misjudged state. Ford stores it in the BECM of the 2021–2022 Mustang Mach-E together with main-contactor codes such as P0AA1 and P0AA4;",["Faulty high-voltage battery junction box with the charging contactors (Ford Mach-E)","Battery module software misjudging the contactor (Stellantis 4xe, GM Lyriq)","Welded contactor contacts"],["Malfunction indicator lamp or wrench lamp","Stop Safely Now message or no ready mode (Ford)","Discharged 12 V battery"],4,3,["P0D08","P0D0E","P0D10","P0AA1","P0AA4"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקוע במצב סגור"],P0D10:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Stuck Open","P0D10 means negative charging contactor A does not close when told to, so the charging path never gets its return line and the session fails. Ford names it in the BECM of the 2021–2023 Mustang Mach-E next to main-contactor codes; the bulletins (23-2020, 23-2257) replace the high-voltage battery junction box and update the BECM.",["Faulty high-voltage battery junction box with the charging contactors (Ford Mach-E)","Battery module software needing an update (GM Lyriq, Stellantis)","Contactor failing to close or burnt contacts"],["Charging does not start or stops","Malfunction indicator lamp and high-voltage service message","Vehicle may not start (Lyriq, Stellantis)"],4,3,["P0D09","P0D0E","P0D0F","P0D11","P0AA5"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — תקוע במצב פתוח"],P0D11:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Control Circuit/Open","P0D11 is the open-circuit and driver check for negative charging contactor A. GM uses it on the Chevrolet Volt (2011, 2014) and the Cadillac ELR: the module compares the commanded driver state with what the line shows; with the driver off, an output above 4 V (2011: 2 V) means open and one close to supply voltage a short to power, and with the driver on a coil current below 57 mA is judged open as well.",["Open or high-resistance coil control wire or connector","Contactor coil open","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage","Faulty driver stage in the control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one trip (GM Type A)","Vehicle does not charge"],4,1,["P0D0A","P0D0E","P0D10","P0D12","P0D13","P0D14"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D12:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0D12 points to a control circuit of negative charging contactor A that is electrically intact yet implausible: current, voltage or switching response do not fit the command. No manufacturer documents P0D12 publicly.",["Contact resistance or corrosion in the control circuit connectors","Contactor coil with shifted resistance","Poor ground of the control module or coil return"],["Charging sessions fail sporadically","Stored code, possibly with a charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D11","P0D13","P0D14","P0D10","P0D0B"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0D13:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Control Circuit Low","P0D13 means the control line of negative charging contactor A reads too low, which points to a short to ground or a driver output held down. The coil then receives no current, or too little, and the contactor stays open. No manufacturer documents P0D13 publicly;",["Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Moisture in a connector creating a path to ground","Shorted contactor coil"],["Vehicle refuses to charge","Charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D11","P0D12","P0D14","P0D0C"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0D14:["Battery Charging System Negative Contactor A Control Circuit High","P0D14 is set when the control line of negative charging contactor A reads too high, for example through a short to the 12 V supply or a driver stage that will not switch off. That can keep the coil energised and the contactor closed without a command, so the module blocks charging. No manufacturer documents P0D14 publicly;",["Control wire shorted to a supply voltage","Driver stage in the control module stuck on","Open ground return of the coil letting the line float high"],["Charging blocked","Charging or high-voltage system warning"],4,0,["P0D11","P0D12","P0D13","P0D0F","P0D0D"],"מגען שלילי מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0D15:["Battery Charging System A High Voltage Interlock Circuit/Open","P0D15 reports an open high-voltage interlock loop in charging system A. This low-voltage loop runs through the connectors and covers of the charging path – inlet, charger and their HV leads – and breaks as soon as one of them is unplugged, so the module can keep that path de-energised. It is separate from the interlock of battery and drive, which uses P0A0A ff.",["HV connector in the charging path not fully seated or latched (Volvo XC40)","Broken interlock wire or pushed-back terminal","Missing or loose cover on a charging component"],["AC charging not possible","Charge inlet light indicates a fault"],2,0,["P0D16","P0D17","P0D18","P0D19","P0A0A"],"נעילת בטיחות מתח גבוה מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0D16:["Battery Charging System A High Voltage Interlock Circuit Performance","P0D16 flags a charging interlock loop that delivers a signal, just not the one expected – for example a reading that fails to follow when the module switches its test source on and off. No manufacturer documents P0D16 publicly.",["Contact resistance or corrosion in an interlock connector","HV connector in the charging path inserted only partway","Fault in the module's interlock source or measuring stage"],["Charging refused","Charging system warning"],2,0,["P0D15","P0D17","P0D18","P0D19","P0A0B"],"נעילת בטיחות מתח גבוה מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D17:["Battery Charging System A High Voltage Interlock Circuit Low","P0D17 means the sensed voltage of the charging HVIL loop is too low. GM documents the check for the Chevrolet Volt (2011, 2014) and the Cadillac ELR: while the module sources 5 V into the loop, a sensed value under 30 % of the reference in two samples out of two at 12.5 ms sets the code as Type A, with the MIL on after one trip.",["Interlock wire chafed and touching ground","Moisture or dirt in an interlock connector causing leakage","Fault in the interlock source or measuring circuit of the control module"],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one trip (GM Type A)","Charging refused"],2,1,["P0D15","P0D16","P0D18","P0D19","P0A0C"],"נעילת בטיחות מתח גבוה מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D18:["Battery Charging System A High Voltage Interlock Circuit High","P0D18 flags a charging HVIL signal that sits too high. On the Chevrolet Volt and Cadillac ELR GM tests the loop in two states: with the 5 V source off, a sensed level above 24 % of reference (5 of 6 samples in 2011, 5 of 8 for 2014 and ELR), and with it on, a level above 44 % (4 of 6), set the code as Type A, MIL after one trip.",["High resistance or partial break in the interlock loop","Battery module software needing an update (Polestar 2)","Faulty integrated charging unit (Kia Niro EV ICCU)","Interlock wire shorted to a voltage source"],["Malfunction indicator lamp (GM Type A)","Propulsion or high-voltage warning in the cluster","Slow or no AC charging"],4,3,["P0D15","P0D16","P0D17","P0D19","P0A0D"],"נעילת בטיחות מתח גבוה מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D19:["Battery Charging System A High Voltage Interlock Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D19 covers a charging interlock loop that drops out and comes back, so the signal flips between valid and invalid within a short time. Loose terminals, a connector that is not fully latched, a chafing wire that only touches over bumps and vibration-sensitive solder joints are typical causes. No manufacturer documents P0D19 publicly.",["Loose or spread terminal in an interlock connector","HV connector in the charging path not fully latched","Chafed interlock wire making contact only under vibration"],["Charging stops unexpectedly","Intermittent charging system warning"],2,0,["P0D15","P0D16","P0D17","P0D18","P0A0E"],"נעילת בטיחות מתח גבוה מערכת טעינת סוללה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D1A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Control Circuit/Open","P0D1A flags an open or otherwise faulty drive circuit of coolant control valve B, a second actuator in the coolant loop of the hybrid or EV battery. Which physical valve gets the letter B, and what it switches, is up to the vehicle maker. No manufacturer documents P0D1A in a public bulletin or OBD table.",["Broken wire or corroded connector in the valve B drive circuit","Open coil or motor winding inside valve B","Faulty output stage in the controlling module"],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced charging or drive power because battery temperature cannot be managed"],3,3,["P0D1D","P0D1E","P0D1B","P0CDF","P0DE8"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D1B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Performance/Stuck Off","P0D1B is stored when coolant control valve B of the battery loop is electrically intact but fails to reach or hold the position the controller asks for, or stays in its off state. How a maker judges this varies: for valve A, GM's 2011 Chevrolet Volt learns the end stops, expects the target within 1.5 s and watches for drift above 3 %, while Audi and Toyota infer a wrong valve state from coolant temperatures.",["Valve B mechanically stiff or blocked by debris","Worn valve actuator that no longer reaches its target","Diagnostic limits in the module software set too tightly","Low coolant or trapped air distorting the temperature-based check"],["MIL or battery thermal system warning","Battery temperature rises during fast charging or hard driving","Charging or drive power limited"],3,1,["P0D1C","P0D1A","P0CE0","P0CE5","P0DE9"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים או תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0D1C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Stuck On","P0D1C means coolant control valve B of the battery circuit remains in its active position although the controller has commanded a change. What active means depends on the design: on valve A, Mercedes hybrid battery management distinguishes a shutoff valve that will not close (P0CE000) from one that will not open (P0CE100), which shows that makers map on and off to opposite valve states.",["Valve B jammed in its active position","Output stage or supply that keeps the valve energised","Module software misjudging the temperature response of the loop"],["MIL or battery thermal system warning","Battery warms slowly in cold weather or charging is slower than usual"],2,1,["P0D1B","P0D1A","P0CE1","P0CE5","P0DEA"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0D1D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Control Circuit Low","P0D1D reports that the drive line of coolant control valve B reads too low, which normally means a short to ground. No manufacturer publishes P0D1D itself. The closest documented test is GM's check of valve A in the 2011 Chevrolet Volt and the 2016 Cadillac ELR: the motor-driven valve has two driver lines, and a low reading on line 1 while the valve should run in reverse, with system voltage above 9 V or 10.2 V,…",["Valve B driver wire chafed and shorted to ground","Moisture or corrosion in the valve connector","Internal short in the valve motor"],["MIL after two trips (where the maker uses a two-trip code)","Battery cooling or heating restricted, charging or power limited"],3,3,["P0D1E","P0D1A","P0CE2","P0DEB"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0D1E:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Control Circuit High","P0D1E is the short-to-voltage counterpart for coolant control valve B: the controller sees its drive line high at a moment when it should be low. P0D1E is not described in any public manufacturer document.",["Valve B driver wire pinched against a supply line","Output stage in the controlling module stuck high","Damaged or contaminated connector bridging two pins"],["MIL after two trips (where the maker uses a two-trip code)","Battery thermal management in fallback mode, charging or power limited"],3,3,["P0D1D","P0D1A","P0CE3","P0DEC"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0D1F:["Control Module Date/Time Synchronization Error","P0D1F says a control module could not align its internal date and time with the reference the rest of the vehicle uses. Hybrid modules need a trustworthy clock because several checks depend on how long the car has been parked: CARB, for example, ties cold-start monitoring to at least six hours off, and GM separately checks its module power-off timer against the on-timer (P262B, more than 5.6 % deviation on the 2012…",["Module software misdetecting the time comparison (Volvo BECM software 2.11)","Timers not yet resynchronised after programming because the car has not slept","Missing or wrong time information from the module providing the reference clock"],["Check engine light without a noticeable driving fault","Code returns as a variant after a software update until the car has slept"],4,1,["P262B"],""],P0D20:["Battery Charger A Output Voltage Performance","P0D20 concerns the on-board charger in its constant-voltage phase, typically near the end of a charge, when it should hold its high-voltage output at a set value. The code means the measured output voltage strays too far from that target.",["Fault in the charger's power stage or voltage control","Inaccurate output voltage measurement","Charge control software in the hybrid control module"],["Charging stops early or the battery does not reach its target level","MIL and charging system message"],4,1,["P0D21","P0D85","P0D22","P2E3A"],"מתח יציאת מטען סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D21:["Battery Charger A Output Voltage Too Low","P0D21 is set when the high-voltage output of the on-board charger stays below what the controller expects. GM's Volt and ELR tables split the test in two: during normal charging the charger bus must not fall under 150 V, and when the charger only powers the battery heater, the bus must reach at least 75 % of an expected value calculated from charger current times 70 ohms.",["Charger module software (Stellantis IDCM bulletins)","Failing power stage inside the charger","High resistance or fault in the charger's high-voltage output path or heater load"],["Vehicle does not charge","MIL, often after two charging trips","Battery not preheated while plugged in during cold weather"],4,1,["P0D20","P0D85","P0D4E","P2E3B"],"מתח יציאת מטען סוללה‏ A — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0D22:["Battery Charger A Output Current Performance","P0D22 means the on-board charger does not deliver the high-voltage current it was asked for. GM's Volt compares actual and desired current in constant-current mode against a table: 150 of 300 samples set it in 2011, One Trip Type A; from 2014 a second part also catches a charger that was switched on, with more than 201 V commanded, but never enabled its output after 6 s.",["Charge control software in the hybrid or battery control module (GM PIP bulletins)","Internal fault in the on-board charger","Faulty output current measurement"],["Vehicle does not charge or charging stops","MIL on after a charge session ends","Problem shows mainly on AC level 2 stations"],4,1,["P0D20","P0D23","P0D53","P2E3C"],"זרם יציאת מטען סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D23:["Battery Charger A Output Shorted","P0D23 points to a short on the high-voltage output side of the on-board charger. GM runs this check while the charger contactor is only in its precharge state and the multi-purpose contactor is open, a moment when almost no current should flow on the charger bus.",["Internal short in the charger's high-voltage output stage","Damaged high-voltage cable between charger and battery","Charging contactor path conducting when it should not"],["Charging refused immediately after plugging in","MIL on at once"],4,1,["P0D22","P0D26","P0D53","P2E3D"],"יציאת מטען סוללה‏ A — קצר"],P0D24:["Battery Charger A Temperature Too High","P0D24 is stored when the on-board charger reports an internal temperature above its permitted limit. Chargers protect themselves by reducing power or stopping, so the driver typically notices slow or interrupted AC charging.",["Internal defect in the charger or charging control unit (Kia ICCU replacement)","Low coolant, failed pump or blocked air flow on the charger cooling path","Charger temperature sensor reading too high"],["AC charging slow, interrupted or impossible while DC fast charging works","Warning message for the electric system, sometimes MIL"],4,1,["P0D8F","P0D67","P0D22"],"טמפרטורת מטען סוללה‏ A — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0D25:["Battery Charger System Precharge Time Too Short","P0D25 is about the precharge of the charger's high-voltage bus, the short phase before the charging contactors close in which the bus capacitance is brought up to battery voltage through a limiting path. The code means this voltage arrived sooner than physically plausible. No manufacturer documents P0D25 publicly.",["Precharge resistor bypassed or shorted","Charging contactor welded or closing too early","Bus or pack voltage sensor reporting wrong values","Bus capacitance much lower than expected"],["Charging refused at the start of a session","Charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D26","P0C77","P0D23"],""],P0D26:["Battery Charger System Precharge Time Too Long","P0D26 means the charger's high-voltage bus did not come up to battery voltage within the allowed precharge time. GM's Volt 2011 to 2015 and ELR 2014 to 2016 require the bus to sit within 5 % of pack voltage for 0.25 s before 10 s have passed; the 2016 and 2017 Volt allow only 0.700 s to reach 95 % (later 95 to 105 %); all are One Trip Type A.",["Open precharge resistor, fuse or contactor in the precharge path","Blown fuse in the manual service disconnect (Bolt EV bulletin)","Erratic proximity signal from a wet or corroded charge port","Outdated hybrid control module software"],["Vehicle does not charge on 120 V or 240 V","MIL on at once","With a blown disconnect fuse, loss of propulsion"],4,1,["P0D25","P0C78","P0D23"],""],P0D27:["Battery Charger Input Voltage Too Low","P0D27 says the AC voltage reaching the on-board charger sits lower than the charger accepts while charging is permitted. Unlike most charger codes, the cause often lies outside the car. Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV stops charging and tells technicians to check, in order, the customer's power supply, the charging cable, the high-voltage wiring between charge inlet and charger and finally the charger itself.",["Weak or overloaded AC supply at the charging location","Faulty charging cable or EVSE","Charger module software (Stellantis IDCM reflash)","Damaged wiring or loose contact between charge inlet and charger"],["AC charging does not start or stops","House circuit breaker trips when plugging in","MIL"],2,1,["P0D28","P0D3F","P0D3E","P0D29"],"מתח כניסת מטען סוללה — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0D28:["Battery Charger Input Voltage Too High","P0D28 is the mirror case of P0D27: the AC voltage at the charger input lies above the range the charger can accept. Mitsubishi evaluates both on the Outlander PHEV while the PHEV-ECU allows charging; the charger then stops and the technician works from the outside in, beginning with the supply and the charging cable, then the wiring between inlet and charger, and only last the charger.",["Input voltage sensing inside the charger reading too high","Charger software storing the code without a real fault (Audi ghost DTC)","Actual overvoltage or unstable supply at the charging location","Faulty charging cable or EVSE"],["AC charging stops or does not start","Code found in memory without any complaint"],2,0,["P0D27","P0D40","P0D3E"],"מתח כניסת מטען סוללה — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0D29:["Battery Charger Input Current Too Low","P0D29 is set when the on-board charger draws less AC current than expected for the power it was asked to deliver, while voltage and charging permission are present. No manufacturer documents P0D29 publicly.",["Charger power stage not ramping up","High resistance at the charge inlet, cable or plug contacts","Input current sensor reading too low","Charging station or cable limiting current more than signalled"],["AC charging much slower than usual","Charging stops or charging system warning"],2,0,["P0D2A","P0D3A","P0D39","P0D27"],"זרם כניסת מטען סוללה — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0D2A:["Battery Charger Input Current Too High","P0D2A reports that the on-board charger draws more AC input current than allowed. GM's Volt and ELR use two limits: more than 17 A at 185 V or above, more than 13 A below 185 V; 240 failures in 300 samples set it as One Trip Type A.",["Fault in the charger's input or PFC stage (Toyota/Lexus charger replacement)","Charger or powertrain module software (Stellantis, Ford bulletins)","Input current sensor reading too high"],["AC charging stops shortly after starting","MIL on","On-board charging inoperative while DC charging may still work"],4,1,["P0D29","P0D3B","P0D39","P0CF3","P0CF4"],"זרם כניסת מטען סוללה — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0D2B:["Control Pilot Indicator Control Circuit/Open","P0D2B concerns the driver circuit of the control pilot indicator, a lamp or LED with which the vehicle signals the state of the pilot connection to the charging station. The code covers the lamp's own wiring, not the pilot signal itself.",["Damaged wiring or loose connector to the indicator","Failed indicator LED","Faulty LED driver in the controlling module"],["Indicator stays dark or lit regardless of charging state","MIL, depending on model after one or two trips"],2,1,["P0D2C","P0CF3","P0CF4"],"חיווי פיילוט בקרה — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D2C:["Charge Status Indicator A Control Circuit/Open","P0D2C is set for a fault in the driver circuit of charge status indicator A, the light that tells the driver whether the car is charging. On the Chevrolet Volt that light is part of the diagnosis itself: bulletin PIP4905E has technicians watch whether the charge indicator on the instrument panel stays steady green.",["Broken wire or corroded connector to the indicator","Failed charge status LED","Faulty LED driver in the controlling module"],["Charge status light dark or permanently lit","MIL, depending on model after one or two trips"],2,1,["P0DE5","P0D2B","P0D86"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D2D:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit","Drive motor A's inverter measures the DC link voltage it switches, and voltage sensor A is the channel that passes this value to the motor controller. P0D2D is the general circuit fault for that channel: the signal is missing or electrically unusable, without a more specific low or high verdict. Ford lists P0D2D among its MIL codes.",["Inverter or powertrain module software fault (Ford 21B02)","Loose connector or damaged signal wiring of the voltage sensor","Voltage sensing circuit inside the inverter failed"],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Vehicle does not become ready to drive or electric drive is reduced","No start after charging and remote start (Mach-E)"],4,3,["P0D2E","P0D2F","P0D30","P0D31","P0DA3","P0E8F"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D2E:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0D2E means the inverter voltage reading of drive motor A is electrically valid but disagrees with other measurements of the same high-voltage bus. Mitsubishi shows the logic for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: with voltage control active, the power drive unit compares capacitor 1 with the rear motor unit and with both channels of capacitor 2;",["Voltage sensing in the power drive unit drifts or reads wrong","Fault in another HV component, e.g. belt starter-alternator (Mercedes)","High resistance or loose high-voltage connection between modules"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Engine will not start, only electric driving possible (Mercedes)","Reduced drive power"],4,3,["P0D2D","P0D2F","P0D30","P0C79","P0E90","P0DA4"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D2F:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Low","P0D2F reports that the signal of inverter voltage sensor A for drive motor A sits at the bottom of its range, typical of an open circuit, a short to ground or no high voltage at all. In the 2018 Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi sets it in the front motor control unit when the sensor output stays below 0.5 V, equal to about 10 V on the bus, for 0.3 s while the propulsion system is active.",["Blown high-voltage fuse or disconnected HV cable","Voltage sensing in the power drive unit failed (open or short to ground)","High-voltage battery delivers no voltage"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Vehicle not ready to drive or electric drive unavailable"],4,3,["P0D30","P0D2E","P0D2D","P0E91","P0DA5"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D30:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit High","P0D30 is the high-side partner of P0D2F: the inverter voltage signal of drive motor A stays near its upper limit. Mitsubishi checks it on the 2018 Outlander PHEV with the front motor unit supplied at 8 to 16 V; a sensor output above 4.7 V, which stands for roughly 440 V, for 0.3 s sets the code.",["Voltage sensing in the power drive unit shorted to supply","Real overvoltage from battery or HV circuit (both motor units report)","Loose or damaged high-voltage connection"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Electric drive switched off or reduced"],4,3,["P0D2F","P0D2E","P0D2D","P0C79","P0E92","P0DA6"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D31:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D31 covers a voltage signal from the drive motor A inverter that drops out or jumps for brief moments without staying out of range long enough for P0D2F or P0D30. No manufacturer documents this code publicly. Related checks show the time scale: Mitsubishi rates the same sensor as failed only after 0.16 to 0.3 s outside its window, so shorter glitches need a separate test.",["Loose or corroded low-voltage connector at the inverter","Chafed signal wiring with intermittent contact","Sensing circuit in the power electronics fails with heat or vibration"],["Hybrid system warning that comes and goes","Brief power loss or jerky electric drive"],4,3,["P0D2D","P0D2E","P0D2F","P0D30","P0E93","P0DA7"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D32:["DC/DC Converter A Over Temperature","P0D32 means DC/DC converter A, which supplies the 12 V network from the high-voltage battery, has become too hot. Unlike the sensor codes P0C38-P0C3C, the reading itself is trusted; the temperature is the problem.",["Coolant loss in the power electronics circuit, e.g. damaged PCU radiator (Honda)","Weak electronics coolant pump or air in the cooling circuit","Radiator clogged or airflow restricted","Internal converter fault with excess heat"],["Several warning lights including MIL","Reduced hybrid system performance","12 V battery discharges, charging warning"],3,3,["P0C38","P0C39","P0A94","P0A3C","P0A3E"],"ממיר DC/DC‏ A — חום יתר"],P0D33:["DC/DC Converter Current High","P0D33 says the DC/DC converter carries more current than its limit. The converter links the high-voltage or 48 V system with the 12 V network; an internal short in the power stage, a shorted 12 V cable or consumer, or a failed output stage can push the current beyond what the converter accepts, and it then switches off to protect itself.",["Internal circuit board fault in the converter (Land Rover MHEV)","Short in a 12 V cable or consumer on the converter output","Damaged high-voltage or 48 V supply cable"],["Charging system fault message","12 V battery not charged, later no start","Electrical consumers switched off"],4,3,["P0D32","P0A94","P0CA2","P0CA3"],"זרם ממיר DC/DC — ערך גבוה"],P0D34:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Current","P0D34 is the general code for the current flowing through the high-voltage battery system as a whole. The pack current sensor codes P0ABF-P0AC3 judge the sensor signal; this group judges whether the current itself fits the operating state. No manufacturer documents P0D34 publicly, and its set conditions are not published.",["Battery current sensor reads wrong but within range","Battery controller software or calibration","Unexpected current path in the HV system (contactor, consumer)"],["Hybrid system warning or MIL","Reduced electric power or charging restrictions"],4,3,["P0D35","P0D36","P0D37","P0ABF","P0AC0"],"זרם מערכת סוללת מתח גבוה"],P0D35:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Current Unstable","P0D35 flags a battery system current that swings more than the operating state explains, for example rapid changes while the requested power is steady. No manufacturer publishes set conditions for this code.",["Loose high-voltage connection or chattering contactor","Current sensor or its wiring with intermittent signal","HV consumer (compressor, heater) cycling abnormally"],["Hybrid system warning, possibly intermittent","Power reduction or jerky electric drive"],4,3,["P0D34","P0D36","P0D37","P0AC3"],""],P0D36:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Current Low","P0D36 means the battery system delivers or accepts less current than the controller expects for the power it commanded. No manufacturer documents a threshold publicly. Low current can come from high resistance in the high-voltage path, a contactor that does not close properly, an opened fuse in a branch circuit, a pack with high internal resistance, or a sensor that reads too low.",["High resistance in the HV path or contactor not closing properly","Current sensor stuck at a plausible but too low value","Aged battery pack with high internal resistance"],["Reduced electric power and regeneration","Slow charging","Hybrid system warning or MIL"],4,3,["P0D34","P0D35","P0D37","P0AC0","P0AC1"],"זרם מערכת סוללת מתח גבוה — ערך נמוך"],P0D37:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Current High","P0D37 records excessive current in the high-voltage battery system. Mitsubishi describes it for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: the PHEV-ECU watches the HV circuit for overcurrent whenever the car is ready or charging. Because such a current can damage the contact points of the contactors, the manual requires junction box No.",["Short or fault in an HV component (charger, DC/DC, A/C, inverter)","Contactor points damaged by the overcurrent (junction box)","Damaged high-voltage cable","Hybrid control unit or battery fault (code returns)"],["EV or hybrid system warning, with MIL on some makes","High-voltage system switches off, car not ready or not charging"],4,3,["P0D36","P0D34","P0D35","P0AC2","P0C78"],"זרם מערכת סוללת מתח גבוה — ערך גבוה"],P0D38:["Battery Charger A Input Current Sensor Circuit","P0D38 is the general circuit code for the sensor that measures the AC current entering battery charger A, the on-board charger. It sets when the signal is missing or electrically invalid; range, low, high and intermittent faults have their own codes P0D39-P0D3C. No manufacturer documents P0D38 with set conditions in a public source.",["Current sensing inside the on-board charger failed","Open or shorted sensor wiring or connector","Charger software or supply voltage fault"],["AC charging does not start or stops","Charging fault indication on the dashboard or charge port"],4,0,["P0D39","P0D3A","P0D3B","P0D3C","P0D29","P0D2A"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D39:["Battery Charger A Input Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D39 flags an implausible reading from the sensor that measures alternating current at the input of charger A, the on-board charger. Both documented users test its zero point. GM's charger module in the 2011 Volt sets the code as a Type A fault with MIL when the current value sits 5.4 A or more away from zero for 512 of 640 ms although no current should flow; the 2016 ELR table uses 1.4 A.",["Zero point of the current sensor inside the charger has drifted","Measuring electronics of the on-board charger failed","Charger calibration out of date"],["AC charging does not start","MIL on (GM)","Charging fault shown at the charge port or in the cluster"],4,1,["P0D38","P0D3A","P0D3B","P0D3C","P0D29","P0D2A"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D3A:["Battery Charger A Input Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0D3A is the low-signal fault of the charger A input current sensor. Mitsubishi defines it for the 2022 Outlander PHEV as a short to ground: if the sensor output falls below 0.344 V, which corresponds to roughly -54 A, for 0.5 s while the charger supply is 10-16 V, charging is stopped and the combined charger and DC/DC unit is swapped.",["Current sensor signal shorted to ground inside the charger","Failed on-board charger/DC-DC unit","Unstable 12 V supply to the charger module"],["AC charging stops or never begins","MIL on (GM)"],4,1,["P0D3B","P0D39","P0D38","P0D3C"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D3B:["Battery Charger A Input Current Sensor Circuit High","P0D3B reports that the charger A input current sensor reads too high. On the 2022 Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi interprets a sensor voltage above 4.658 V, equivalent to more than about 54 A, lasting 0.5 s as a short to the supply side; charging ends and the integrated charger/DC-DC unit is the listed part.",["Current sensor signal shorted to supply voltage inside the charger","Failed on-board charger/DC-DC unit","Genuine overcurrent at the charger input from an internal short"],["AC charging quits shortly after plugging in","MIL on (GM)"],4,1,["P0D3A","P0D39","P0D38","P0D3C","P0D2A"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D3C:["Battery Charger A Input Current Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D3C covers a charger A input current signal that drops out or jumps for short moments instead of staying wrong. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM's Volt and Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV, which use the neighbouring codes P0D39-P0D3B, list no intermittent variant.",["Loose or corroded connector at the on-board charger","Current measuring element inside the charger failing","Charger software fault"],["Charging stops at random and may restart","Charge sessions take longer than usual"],4,0,["P0D38","P0D39","P0D3A","P0D3B","P0D41"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D3D:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor A Circuit","P0D3D is the basic circuit code for voltage sensor A at the input of charger A, which measures the AC supply voltage arriving from the charge inlet. It stands for a missing or electrically invalid signal; plausibility, low, high and sporadic faults have their own codes P0D3E-P0D41. No manufacturer publishes set conditions for P0D3D itself.",["Open or shorted voltage sense circuit in the charger","Failed on-board charger","Damaged AC wiring between charge inlet and charger"],["AC charging is refused","Charging fault indication"],4,0,["P0D3E","P0D3F","P0D40","P0D41","P0D27","P0D28"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D3E:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0D3E means the AC input voltage seen by the on-board charger does not agree with other information. GM uses it across generations: the 2011-2014 Volt charger module compares the AC peak voltage with two internal bulk voltage readings and sets a Type A fault when both differ by more than 34 V (59 V in 2014);",["Charger module software reacting to certain charging stations (GM BCCM update)","Charging station or cord with unusual behaviour, quick re-plugging","Voltage measurement inside the on-board charger faulty","Faulty duty-cycle signal line between charger and controller (2016-2019 Volt)"],["Charge incomplete or interrupted","MIL on (GM)","Problem appears only at certain charging stations"],4,1,["P0D3D","P0D3F","P0D40","P0D41","P0D27","P0D28"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D3F:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor A Circuit Low","P0D3F reports that the charger's AC input voltage reading is too low. In the 2011-2014 Volt GM's charger module sets it when the AC peak stays below 90 V while AC is present (1.5 s in 2011, 5.5 s in 2014), Type A; the 2016-2019 Volt instead watches a duty-cycle signal at or below 8 %.",["Weak, interrupted or noisy AC supply (brown-out, shared circuit)","DC-only adapter used on an AC charger (GM PIT6353)","Charger module software needs update (GM PIC5803D)","Voltage sensor signal shorted to ground in the charger"],["Charging does not start or is interrupted","MIL on (GM)","Charge cord LEDs flash or the charge indicator changes colour repeatedly"],2,1,["P0D40","P0D3E","P0D3D","P0D41","P0D27"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D40:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor A Circuit High","P0D40 is the high-signal fault of the charger's input voltage sensor. GM's Volt charger module (2011 and 2014 tables) sets it when the measured AC peak exceeds 422 V for 160 of 200 ms and lights the MIL as a Type A fault; in the 2016 and 2019 Volt the controller reads a duty-cycle signal and fails it at 92 % and above or at a constant 0 %, Type B over two trips.",["Voltage sensor signal shorted to supply inside the charger","Open duty-cycle signal line between charger and controller (2016-2019 Volt)","Failed on-board charger"],["AC charging is refused","MIL on (GM)"],4,1,["P0D3F","P0D3E","P0D3D","P0D41","P0D28"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D41:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D41 is reserved for an input voltage sensor A signal at charger A that is intermittent or erratic, i.e. it jumps or drops out briefly. No manufacturer documents this code with set conditions; GM and Mitsubishi only use P0D3E-P0D40 for this sensor.",["Electrical noise or poor grounding on the AC supply","Loose contact between charge inlet and charger","Voltage measurement in the charger degrading"],["Charging stops and restarts unpredictably","Fault appears only at one charging location"],2,0,["P0D3D","P0D3E","P0D3F","P0D40","P0D3C"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D42:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Voltage Sensor Circuit","P0D42 is the circuit code for the sensor that measures voltage at the 14 V output of the battery charger, a low-voltage DC stage built into the on-board charger and distinct from the vehicle's main DC/DC converter. GM's charger module in the Volt and ELR has such an output and monitors its voltage with P0D43-P0D45, yet no manufacturer publishes P0D42 itself.",["Open or shorted voltage sense circuit of the charger's low-voltage stage","Poor 12 V or ground connection at the charger","Failed on-board charger electronics"],["12 V battery discharges while the car is plugged in","Charging is interrupted"],4,0,["P0D43","P0D44","P0D45","P0D46","P0D47","P0D5B"],""],P0D43:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D43 says the voltage reported for the charger's 14 V output does not fit other measurements of the low-voltage system. GM's battery charger control module in the 2011-2014 Volt and the ELR compares the reading with the run/crank signal voltage and with the voltage of a further enable line (HVEMB); a difference of 6 V or more for 4 of 5 s stores a Type A fault with MIL.",["Low-voltage sense circuit inside the charger reads wrong","Poor ground or 12 V feed at the charger skews the comparison","Charger module software"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging interrupted or not completed"],4,1,["P0D42","P0D44","P0D45","P0D46","P0D5B"],""],P0D44:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Low","P0D44 is logged once the voltage sensor of the charger's 14 V output delivers an implausibly low raw value. In GM's charger module for the 2011-2014 Volt and the ELR the limit is a sensor signal of 1.87 V or less, held for 400 of 500 ms while the low-voltage section is awake; it is a Type A fault and lights the MIL.",["Voltage sense signal shorted to ground inside the charger","Failed low-voltage stage of the on-board charger","14 V output collapsed by an external short or overload"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging may stop or not complete"],4,1,["P0D45","P0D43","P0D42","P0D46","P0D5B"],""],P0D45:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Voltage Sensor Circuit High","P0D45 is stored when the charger's 14 V output voltage sensor reads too high. GM's battery charger control module in the Volt and ELR fails it at a reading of 16.88 V or above for 400 of 500 ms; the 2011 table rates it Type A, the 2014 table Type B, meaning the MIL comes on only after two consecutive trips.",["Voltage sense signal shorted to a higher potential inside the charger","Charger low-voltage stage regulating too high","Charger module software"],["MIL on (GM, after two trips in later model years)","Charging may stop"],4,1,["P0D44","P0D43","P0D42","P0D46","P0D5B"],""],P0D46:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D46 stands for a sporadic or unstable signal from the voltage sensor at the 14 V output of the on-board charger. No manufacturer documents it publicly. GM monitors this output in the Volt and ELR charger only for plausibility, low and high readings (P0D43-P0D45) and lists no intermittent variant.",["Loose 12 V, ground or signal connection at the charger","Voltage sense circuit inside the charger failing intermittently","Electrical interference on the low-voltage network"],["12 V battery not kept charged while plugged in","Occasional charging interruptions"],4,0,["P0D42","P0D43","P0D44","P0D45","P0D4B"],""],P0D47:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Current Sensor Circuit","P0D47 is the general circuit code for the current sensor in the charger's 14 V output, the measurement of how much current the charger's internal low-voltage stage delivers. No manufacturer publishes this code; GM's Volt and ELR charger module measures the same current but reports only low and high readings (P0D49, P0D4A), with 54 A as its upper limit. Range and sporadic faults have the codes P0D48 and P0D4B.",["Open or shorted current sense circuit of the low-voltage stage","Failed on-board charger electronics","Damaged 12 V output connector at the charger"],["12 V battery discharges during long charging sessions","Charging may be interrupted"],4,0,["P0D48","P0D49","P0D4A","P0D4B","P0D42","P0D5B"],""],P0D48:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D48 marks a current reading at the charger's 14 V output that lies within its electrical range but makes no sense in context. No manufacturer documents P0D48. GM covers a related check differently: its Volt and ELR charger module multiplies the 14 V output voltage by the current and sets P0D5B when this low-voltage power exceeds a limit derived from the measured AC input power plus 125 W.",["Current sense circuit inside the charger has drifted","Charger module software or calibration","Failed low-voltage stage of the charger"],["Charging fault indication","12 V support during charging reduced or switched off"],4,0,["P0D47","P0D49","P0D4A","P0D4B","P0D5B","P0D43"],""],P0D49:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0D49 reports that the current sensor in the charger's 14 V output reads too low. GM's battery charger control module in the 2011-2014 Volt and the ELR sets it when the raw current value is 0.293 A or less for 400 of 500 ms while the low-voltage section is awake, as a Type A fault with MIL; the 2016 ELR charger table also lists it as a precondition for its output power checks.",["Open or grounded current sense path inside the charger","Failed low-voltage stage of the on-board charger","12 V supply or fuse of the charger interrupted"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging may fail"],4,1,["P0D4A","P0D48","P0D47","P0D4B","P0D5B"],""],P0D4A:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Current Sensor Circuit High","P0D4A sets when the current sensor in the charger's 14 V output reports 54 A or more. GM's battery charger control module in the 2011-2014 Volt and the ELR applies that threshold for 400 of 500 ms with the low-voltage section awake and treats the result as a Type A fault with MIL. Either the sensor signal is stuck high or the output is genuinely overloaded or shorted; GM's tables do not separate the two.",["Current sense signal stuck high inside the charger","Overload or short on the charger's low-voltage output","Failed on-board charger"],["Vehicle will not charge","MIL on (GM)"],4,1,["P0D49","P0D48","P0D47","P0D4B","P0D5B"],""],P0D4B:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Current Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D4B covers an erratic or intermittent signal from the current sensor in the battery charger's 14 V output. No manufacturer documents it publicly; GM's Volt and ELR charger module uses only the low and high codes P0D49 and P0D4A for this measurement.",["Loose low-voltage connector or ground at the charger","Intermittent contact in the charger's current sense circuit","Electrical interference on the low-voltage network"],["Charging interrupts now and then","12 V battery voltage fluctuates while plugged in"],4,0,["P0D47","P0D48","P0D49","P0D4A","P0D46"],""],P0D4C:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Voltage Sensor Circuit","P0D4C is the general circuit code for the sensor that reports the DC voltage the on-board charger delivers to the high-voltage traction battery. No manufacturer documents P0D4C publicly; GM (Volt, ELR) and Mitsubishi (Outlander PHEV) split electrical faults of this measurement into the low and high codes P0D4E and P0D4F instead, and GM's Volt checks plausibility with P0D4D.",["Voltage sensing electronics inside the on-board charger failed","Interrupted signal path or poor internal connection in the charger","Charger software fault, fixed by reprogramming"],["Plug-in charging does not start or stops shortly after starting","Charging system warning in the instrument cluster","Vehicle can still be driven on stored energy or fuel"],4,0,["P0D4D","P0D4E","P0D4F","P0D50","P0D51","P0D5C"],"חיישן מתח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D4D:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D4D means the charger's output voltage reading is electrically valid but fails to match the rest of the system. GM's second-generation Volt (2016-2019 tables) compares it with the traction battery voltage while charging is ready: a gap of more than 30 V sets the code, in 2019 after 320 of 400 samples taken every 25 ms, as a Type B fault with MIL after two trips.",["Output voltage measurement inside the charger drifted or inaccurate","Integrated charging unit failed (Kia ICCU, GM charger module)","Reference value from the battery voltage measurement wrong"],["MIL on (GM Volt, Bolt)","Slow AC charging or no AC charging","Check electric vehicle system message (Kia)"],4,1,["P0D4C","P0D4E","P0D4F","P0D50","P0D52","P0D5C"],"חיישן מתח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D4E:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Low","P0D4E reports a signal below the valid range for the sensor measuring the charger's DC output toward the traction battery. In GM's charger module for the 2011-2014 Volt and ELR the raw value must stay at or below 2.62 V for 400 of 500 ms; the fault is Type A and lights the MIL at once. The 2016-2019 Volt reads the sensor as a duty cycle and treats 8 % or less, or a signal stuck at 100 %, as low, Type B.",["Voltage sensor signal shorted to ground inside the charger","On-board charger (Mitsubishi charger/DC-DC unit) failed","Internal reference supply of the measuring circuit missing"],["Charging stops or does not start","MIL on (GM)","Charging fault message"],4,1,["P0D4F","P0D4C","P0D4D","P0D50","P0D53","P0D5C"],"חיישן מתח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D4F:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Voltage Sensor Circuit High","P0D4F is the high-side fault of the charger's output voltage sensor: the reading climbs beyond anything the battery could actually present. GM's 2011-2014 charger module for the Volt and ELR flags a decoded value of 482 V or more for 400 of 500 ms as a Type A fault with immediate MIL. In the 2016-2019 Volt the sensor delivers a pulse-width signal;",["Voltage sensor signal shorted to a supply voltage inside the charger","Measuring stage of the on-board charger failed","Charger software misinterprets the signal"],["AC charging refused","MIL on (GM)","Charging interrupted with fault message"],4,1,["P0D4E","P0D4C","P0D4D","P0D50","P0D54","P0D5C"],"חיישן מתח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D50:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Voltage Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D50 stands for an unsteady output voltage signal from the on-board charger: the value jumps or drops out briefly instead of following the slowly changing battery voltage. No manufacturer documents P0D50 publicly. GM and Mitsubishi only publish fixed limits for this sensor (P0D4E, P0D4F) and, at GM, a comparison with the battery voltage (P0D4D), so a dedicated check for signal spikes would be an additional layer.",["Loose contact or cracked solder joint in the charger's measuring path","Electrical interference from the charger's power stage","Ageing voltage sensing electronics in the charger"],["Charging repeatedly interrupted","Charge time noticeably longer than usual","Sporadic charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D4C","P0D4D","P0D4E","P0D4F","P0D55"],"חיישן מתח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D51:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Current Sensor Circuit","P0D51 covers a circuit fault of the sensor that measures the direct current the on-board charger pushes into the traction battery. No manufacturer documents P0D51 itself. GM's charger module for the Volt and ELR and Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV use the low and high codes P0D53 and P0D54 for electrical faults of this sensor, and Mitsubishi adds P0D52 for an offset check.",["Current sensor or its evaluation circuit in the charger failed","Open signal path between sensor and charger controller","Charger software fault"],["AC charging does not start","Charging system warning shown","Driving not affected"],4,0,["P0D52","P0D53","P0D54","P0D55","P0D4C","P0D5C"],"חיישן זרם יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D52:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D52 flags an output current reading that lies inside the sensor's range yet cannot be right. Mitsubishi documents the check for the 2022 Outlander PHEV as a zero-point test: while the PHEV-ECU has not granted charging, no current should leave the charger, so an absolute reading of 1.5 A or more lasting 1 s sets the code; charging is halted and the charger/DC-DC unit is replaced.",["Zero point of the output current sensor has drifted","Charger or integrated charging unit (Kia ICCU) failed","Outdated charger software"],["Poor or no AC charging","MIL or electric system warning (Kia)","DC fast charging may still work (Kia)"],4,1,["P0D51","P0D53","P0D54","P0D55","P0D4D","P0D5C"],"חיישן זרם יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D53:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0D53 is set when the charger's output current sensor reports a value below its valid floor. GM's charger module in the 2011 and 2014 Volt and the ELR uses a decoded current of 0.098 A or less, present for 400 of 500 ms with the low- and high-voltage sections awake; the fault is Type A, so the MIL lights after one trip, and the power checks P0D5C and P1ECE stay disabled while it is active.",["Current sensor output shorted to ground inside the charger","On-board charger measuring electronics failed","Supply of the current sensor missing"],["Charging stops or never begins","MIL on (GM)","Charging fault indicated at the charge port or in the cluster"],4,1,["P0D54","P0D51","P0D52","P0D55","P0D4E","P0D5C"],"חיישן זרם יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D54:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Current Sensor Circuit High","P0D54 marks a current reading at the charger's high-voltage output that exceeds the sensor's upper limit. The limit sits well above normal charging current, so the value counts as an electrical fault rather than real current. GM's 2011 Volt charger module triggers at 18 A or more, the 2014 Volt/ELR table at 17.7 A, each over 400 of 500 ms and rated Type A with MIL.",["Current sensor output shorted to supply voltage in the charger","Charger current measuring stage failed","Sensor output stuck at its upper end"],["AC charging interrupted or refused","MIL on (GM)","Charge level stays unchanged after plugging in"],4,1,["P0D53","P0D51","P0D52","P0D55","P0D4F","P0D5C"],"חיישן זרם יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D55:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Current Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D55 describes an output current signal from the charger that flickers, jumps or briefly drops out rather than tracking the regulated charging current. No manufacturer documents P0D55 publicly. GM and Mitsubishi publish only fixed limits (P0D53 and P0D54) and, at Mitsubishi, a zero-point test (P0D52) for this sensor; a sudden step that returns to normal would not necessarily trip those.",["Intermittent contact in the current measuring path of the charger","Solder joints fatigued by charging heat cycles","Switching noise coupling into the sensor signal"],["Charging pauses and resumes repeatedly","Charging takes much longer than usual","Occasional charging system warning"],4,0,["P0D51","P0D52","P0D53","P0D54","P0D50"],"חיישן זרם יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D56:["Proximity Detection Circuit A","P0D56 is the general circuit fault of proximity detection circuit A, the line through which the vehicle notices that a charging plug sits in its inlet. Under SAE J1772 this proximity pin runs to protective earth through resistors in the plug and a switch tied to the latch button, so the car can block driving away and end charging before the plug is pulled;",["Damaged or corroded proximity contact in the charge inlet","Open or chafed wire between charge inlet and evaluating module","Evaluation input in the charger or control module failed"],["Charging does not start although the cable is plugged in","Vehicle refuses to drive or shows the cable as connected without a plug","Charging system warning"],2,0,["P0D57","P0D58","P0D59","P0D5A","P2E87","P0CF3"],""],P0D57:["Proximity Detection Circuit A Range/Performance","P0D57 means the plug-present signal of proximity circuit A is electrically within limits but its value or behaviour does not fit the situation, for instance a level between the defined states for plugged, unplugged and latch pressed.",["Hybrid control software needs update (FCA HCP/AHCP)","Worn or contaminated proximity contact shifts the resistance","Charging cable or adapter with off-standard proximity resistor"],["MIL on (FCA)","Charging starts unreliably or not at all","Plug status shown incorrectly"],2,1,["P0D56","P0D58","P0D59","P0D5A","P2E88","P0CF4"],""],P0D58:["Proximity Detection Circuit A Low","P0D58 reports a voltage on proximity circuit A that is too low while no plug can possibly be inserted. GM's Volt checks this in its hybrid control processor: in 2011 with the charge door closed or above 12.4 mph in a range other than Park, from 2016 above 20 km/h outside Park, the signal must sit in a window, and less than 4.2 V for 30 of 50 samples (later 240 of 400) sets the code as a Type A fault with immediate…",["Proximity signal wire shorted to ground or chafed against body","Loose low-voltage connector at the on-board charger (smart)","Moisture or contamination bridging the inlet contacts","Evaluation input of the control module failed"],["MIL on (GM)","Vehicle will not start or shift because a plug appears connected","High-voltage battery cannot be charged"],2,1,["P0D59","P0D56","P0D57","P0D5A","P2E89","P0CF5"],""],P0D59:["Proximity Detection Circuit A High","P0D59 is logged when proximity circuit A shows a voltage above its expected resting level at a moment when the charge port must be empty. GM's Volt uses the same plausibility window as for P0D58 but trips above 4.8 V: in 2011 with the door shut or above 12.4 mph in a range other than Park, from 2016 at more than 20 km/h outside Park, over 30 of 50 or 240 of 400 samples; the fault is Type A and turns on the MIL.",["Proximity signal wire shorted to a supply voltage","Interrupted ground or reference path of the detection circuit","Loose connector at the charger or charge inlet"],["MIL on (GM)","Inserted charging plug not recognised, no charging","Start-up problems after charging (smart)"],2,1,["P0D58","P0D56","P0D57","P0D5A","P2E8A","P0CF6"],""],P0D5A:["Proximity Detection Circuit A Intermittent/Erratic","P0D5A points to a proximity signal on circuit A that flips between states or jumps erratically, so the vehicle sees the plug come and go although nobody touched it. No manufacturer documents P0D5A publicly. The function itself is laid down in SAE J1772: the plug-present contact tells the car when a connector is seated and when its latch button is pressed, which lets it stop drawing current before disconnection.",["Loose contact in the inlet or at the charger connector","Worn latch switch or contacts in the charging plug","Moisture in the charge inlet"],["Charging aborts sporadically","Plug status message appears and disappears","Occasional refusal to drive off"],2,0,["P0D56","P0D57","P0D58","P0D59","P2E8B"],""],P0D5B:["Battery Charger 14 Volt Output Power Performance","P0D5B checks whether the power leaving the charger's built-in 14 V output is physically possible. GM's charger module for the Volt and ELR multiplies the measured 14 V output voltage and current and compares the product with the AC input power times a factor of about 2 plus an offset: 2.5 W in the 2011 table, 125 W in the 2014 Volt/ELR and 2016 ELR tables.",["Voltage or current measurement of the 14 V stage reads too high","AC input power measured too low","Low-voltage stage of the on-board charger faulty"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging interrupted","12 V battery not supported while plugged in"],4,1,["P0D5C","P0D44","P0D45","P0D49","P0D4A","P0D42"],""],P0D5C:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV Battery Output Power Performance","P0D5C judges the power the charger feeds into the traction battery against what it draws from the grid. GM's first-generation Volt and ELR charger module calculates high-voltage output power and flags it when it exceeds AC input power times about 2 plus 30 W (2011) or 120 W (2014 and 2016 ELR), for 1.6 of 2 s, Type A.",["Power stage of the charger degraded, efficiency too low","Module software miscalculates power (Ford SOBDM update)","Output voltage/current or AC input measurement inaccurate","Insufficient cooling of the power electronics during charging"],["MIL on after a charging session","Charging slower than expected","Charging interrupted"],4,1,["P0D5B","P0D4E","P0D4F","P0D53","P0D54","P0D22"],"כוח יציאת סוללת מתח גבוה מטען סוללה — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D5D:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV System Discharge Time Too Short","P0D5D concerns the active discharge of the charger's high-voltage side after charging ends: the stored voltage collapses faster than the circuit design allows. No manufacturer documents P0D5D publicly; GM's Volt only publishes the opposite case P0D5E.",["Discharge switch inside the charger stuck closed","Link capacitor lost capacitance","Charger voltage measurement reads falsely low"],["Charging system warning after the end of charging","Next charging session may not start","No noticeable effect while driving"],4,0,["P0D5E","P0D4D","P0D4E","P0D4F"],""],P0D5E:["Battery Charger Hybrid/EV System Discharge Time Too Long","P0D5E means the high-voltage side of the charger did not drop to a safe level in time after it was disconnected. GM's 2011 Volt charger module fails the test if 60 V or more remain 1.5 s after the discharge command, or 6.75 s after a confirmed communication loss with the hybrid controller; a backup routine then checks for battery current that would reveal a stuck contactor.",["Active discharge circuit of the charger not working","High-voltage contactor stuck closed keeps the bus energised","Lost communication between charger and hybrid controller","Charger voltage sensor reads falsely high"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging blocked after the fault","Further high-voltage codes such as contactor faults may be stored"],4,1,["P0D5D","P0AA4","P0AA1","P0D4E","P0D4F"],""],P0D5F:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Sense Circuit/Open","Where a traction battery is cooled by two fans, each one can report its actual speed back to the controller on a separate sense line. P0D5F is the general circuit code for that return path of fan 2: the line is open or delivers nothing the controller can evaluate. No manufacturer documents P0D5F publicly.",["Broken feedback wire or terminal pushed out at the fan 2 connector","Corroded or loose connector between fan 2 and the battery controller","Speed signal output inside the fan 2 motor electronics failed","Feedback input of the battery control module defective"],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Reduced electric assist or charging power when the battery warms up","Often no noticeable change while fan 2 still runs"],2,3,["P0D60","P0D61","P0D62","P0D63","P0BC7","P0A96"],"חישת מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ 2 — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0D60:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Sense Range/Performance","P0D60 means the feedback of battery cooling fan 2 is electrically present but does not fit what the fan was told to do. Volkswagen documents it for the 2014 Touareg Hybrid: the battery controller reads the fan 2 feedback as a voltage and flags this code when it crosses a limit that moves with the commanded duty, somewhere between 1.37 and 3.43 V; fan 1 is judged the same way under P0BC8.",["Fan 2 runs off its commanded speed due to worn bearing or debris on the impeller","Clogged intake grille, filter or duct of the battery cooling air","Drifting speed feedback stage in the fan electronics","High resistance in the feedback or supply connection of fan 2"],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Fan noise that is louder, rougher or weaker than usual","Battery temperature rises faster and electric power is limited"],2,3,["P0D5F","P0D61","P0D62","P0D63","P0BC8","P0A97"],"חישת מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ 2 — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D61:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Sense Circuit Low","P0D61 reports that the feedback signal of battery cooling fan 2 sits at or near zero. In Volkswagen's 2014 Touareg Hybrid the battery controller sets it once the fan 2 feedback voltage drops below 0.1 V; the matching fan 1 code is P0BC9.",["Fan 2 feedback wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Fan 2 without supply (fuse, relay or supply wire), so no signal is generated","Feedback output of the fan 2 electronics failed low or motor seized","Feedback input of the battery control module defective"],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Fan 2 audibly silent although battery cooling is requested","Reduced electric power, especially in warm conditions"],2,3,["P0D62","P0D60","P0D5F","P0D63","P0BC9"],"מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ 2 — אות נמוך במעגל החישה"],P0D62:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Sense Circuit High","P0D62 is set when the feedback of battery cooling fan 2 rises above its plausible maximum. Volkswagen gives the threshold for the 2014 Touareg Hybrid as a fan 2 feedback voltage above 3.45 V, mirroring P0BCA for fan 1.",["Fan 2 feedback wire shorted to a supply or neighbouring signal","Open ground at fan 2 letting the feedback line float high","Fan 2 electronics stuck at their upper feedback level","Feedback input of the battery control module defective"],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Electric power limited when the battery gets warm","Fan 2 may still run normally"],2,3,["P0D61","P0D60","P0D5F","P0D63","P0BCA"],"מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ 2 — אות גבוה במעגל החישה"],P0D63:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Sense Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D63 flags a feedback signal from battery cooling fan 2 that drops out or jumps in steps no spinning fan could produce. The typical culprit is a loose pin or cracked wire that opens and closes with vibration, or interference coupled into the feedback line. No manufacturer documents P0D63 publicly; Volkswagen's 2014 Touareg Hybrid watches the fan 2 feedback only for performance, low and high (P0D60 to P0D62).",["Loose or spread terminal in the fan 2 feedback connector","Cracked feedback wire that opens under vibration","Electrical interference from nearby high-current or high-voltage wiring","Unstable feedback stage in the fan 2 electronics"],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional short phases of reduced electric power","Often no noticeable change in fan operation"],2,3,["P0D5F","P0D60","P0D61","P0D62","P0BCB"],"חישת מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ 2 — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D64:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Enable Circuit/Open","Besides its speed command, a battery cooling fan can have a separate enable line that switches the fan electronics on. P0D64 means the controller has found this enable output open. GM documents it in its hybrid OBD tables 16OBDG02 to 19OBDG02 under the battery system module, there named fan 1 enable: with the enable commanded off and system voltage above 10.2 V, an output voltage stuck between 1.14 and 2.81 V counts…",["Broken enable wire between battery controller and fan","Fan connector unplugged or not latched, often after battery or trim work","Open enable input inside the fan electronics","Enable driver in the battery system module defective"],["Check engine light or hybrid warning after two trips","Battery cooling fan does not run","Reduced electric assist when the battery gets hot"],2,3,["P0D65","P0D66","P0A81","P0BC8"],"אפשור מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0D65:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Enable Circuit Low","P0D65 reports a short to ground on the enable output of the battery cooling fan: the line stays low even though the controller is trying to switch the fan on. GM's 2013 eAssist mild hybrid table 13OBDG05C detects it from the output driver's fault flag for the power pack fan, 20 failures in 25 samples at 250 ms, Type B.",["Enable wire chafed and shorted to body ground","Shorted enable input inside the fan electronics","Moisture or corrosion bridging the enable pin to ground","Enable driver in the battery controller defective"],["Check engine light or hybrid warning after two trips","Battery cooling fan never switches on","Engine runs more and electric assist drops in hot weather"],2,3,["P0D64","P0D66","P0A84","P0BC9"],"אפשור מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D66:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Enable Circuit High","P0D66 stands for a short to voltage on the enable output of the battery cooling fan. Because the line is pulled high from outside, the fan may run although the controller wants it off, or the controller simply loses authority over it. GM's 2013 eAssist table 13OBDG05C flags it through the output driver's fault status, 20 failures in 25 samples at 250 ms, Type B.",["Enable wire rubbed through and touching a 12 V supply wire","Moisture in the fan connector bridging enable and supply pins","Internal fault in the fan electronics feeding voltage back onto the enable line","Enable driver in the battery controller defective"],["Check engine light or hybrid warning after two trips","Battery cooling fan runs continuously, even with the vehicle off","Discharged 12 V battery after standing"],2,3,["P0D64","P0D65","P0A85","P0A83"],"אפשור מאוורר קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D67:["Battery Charger Control Module A Performance","P0D67 says that the control electronics of the on-board charger are not working properly. Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV manuals (2019 and 2022) spell out what that means there: with the charger supplied at 10 to 16 V, the code sets when one of its internal supply rails leaves its window for 1 s, 3.0 to 3.6 V for the 3.3 V rail, 5.3 to 6.8 V for the 6 V rail or 9.4 to 14.9 V for the 12 V rail.",["Internal low-voltage supply of the on-board charger out of tolerance","On-board charger or integrated charging unit failed","Weak 12 V supply or poor ground at the charger","Charger software fault, fixed by an update where available"],["Plug-in charging stops or does not start","Electric vehicle system warning in the cluster, possibly MIL","Vehicle can still be driven on stored energy or fuel"],4,1,["P0E5E","P0D85","P0D4D","P0D5C","P0D5B"],"מודול בקרת מטען סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D68:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor A Circuit","An electric A/C compressor in a hybrid or EV runs its motor from the high-voltage battery through an inverter in the compressor housing, and a sensor there measures the incoming high voltage. P0D68 is the general circuit code for that sensor: its signal is missing or unusable as a whole, without a specific low or high reading. No manufacturer documents P0D68 publicly.",["Voltage measurement inside the electric compressor's inverter failed","Compressor reports its sensor value over a faulty data link","Poor high-voltage connection at the compressor"],["Air conditioning does not cool","Warning message or warning light, depending on the vehicle","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be limited"],4,0,["P0D69","P0D6A","P0D6B","P0D6C"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D69:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor A Performance","P0D69 is a plausibility check: the high voltage that the electric A/C compressor measures at its input should match the traction battery voltage, since both see the same supply. GM's Volt sets it when the two differ by more than 15 V in the 2011 table (35 of 40 samples at 250 ms) and by more than 30 V in the 2016 and 2019 tables (8 of 10 s in 2019), each Type B with MIL after two trips and only with contactors…",["Drifting high-voltage measurement inside the electric compressor","Fault on the data bus that carries the compressor voltage value","Hybrid or vehicle controller misjudging the comparison","Error in the battery voltage measurement used as reference"],["Check engine light","Air conditioning weak or switched off","Often no other noticeable change"],4,1,["P0D68","P0D6A","P0D6B","P0D6C","P0ABB"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D6A:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor A Circuit High","P0D6A means the electric A/C compressor reports an input voltage higher than the high-voltage system can actually deliver. GM's 2011 Volt table sets it at 450 V or more (30 of 60 samples at 50 ms); the 2016 and 2019 Volt tables use more than 527.25 V, judged by the compressor itself and sent over the data bus (2019: 4 of 5 s). Both are Type B, MIL after two trips.",["Voltage measurement in the compressor inverter reading too high","Electric compressor assembly defective","Hybrid controller or data link fault"],["Check engine light","Air conditioning stops cooling","Driving otherwise normal"],4,1,["P0D6B","P0D69","P0D68","P0D6C","P0D6E"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D6B:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor A Circuit Low","P0D6B reports that the high-voltage input reading of the electric A/C compressor sits below the plausible range. GM's first-generation Volt (2011–2015) and the 2016 ELR set it at 190 V or less in 30 of 60 samples at 50 ms, checked only with the contactors closed for over a second and fault-free. The 2016–2019 Volt uses below 27.75 V, judged by the compressor and passed on over GMLAN (2019: 4 of 5 s);",["Voltage measurement in the compressor inverter reads too low","Faulty data link between compressor and hybrid controller (named by Mitsubishi)","Hybrid or vehicle controller evaluates the value incorrectly"],["Check engine light after two trips (GM) or immediately (Mitsubishi 2023)","Air conditioning stops cooling","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be restricted"],4,1,["P0D6A","P0D69","P0D68","P0D6C","P0D6D"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D6C:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D6C covers a voltage-sensor signal from the electric A/C compressor that jumps or drops out briefly instead of staying wrong: single readings outside the expected window or abrupt steps that do not last long enough for the low or high code. No manufacturer documents P0D6C publicly.",["Loose or corroded low-voltage connector at the compressor","Intermittent fault on the compressor's inverter board","Marginal high-voltage contact at the compressor connector"],["Air conditioning cools intermittently","Code returns sporadically after clearing","Often no warning light"],4,0,["P0D68","P0D69","P0D6A","P0D6B"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D6D:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage A Low","P0D6D concerns the high voltage actually reaching the electric A/C compressor motor rather than the sensor circuit: the supply is too low to run it. Mazda documents it for the CX-90 PHEV (2024–2026) and CX-70 PHEV (2025–2026) in bulletin 07-002/26: an overcurrent can short the compressor internally and blow the A/C high-voltage fuse, after which the PCM stores P0D6D, often with U0605.",["Internal short in the compressor that has blown the A/C high-voltage fuse (Mazda)","Broken high-voltage cable in the supply path (Bentley, battery to on-board charger)","Open A/C fuse or poor high-voltage connection without a compressor defect"],["Air conditioning does not cool at all","Further codes such as lost communication with the compressor","High-voltage battery may not charge (cable fault)"],4,0,["P0D6E","P0D6B","P0D6F","P0AA6"],"מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0D6E:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage A High","P0D6E means the high voltage supplied to the electric A/C compressor motor is above what the compressor is built to accept, as opposed to a sensor circuit reading too high (P0D6A). No manufacturer documents P0D6E publicly. GM handles the situation its own way: the 2011–2015 Volt and 2016 ELR compressor sets P0D6A at 450 V or more and a separate maker code, P1ECA, for a single peak of 480 V;",["Voltage measurement in the compressor inverter reads too high","Voltage spikes on the high-voltage bus during charging or regeneration","Charger or DC/DC converter regulating the bus voltage incorrectly"],["Air conditioning switches off","Possibly further high-voltage codes","Warning message depending on the vehicle"],4,0,["P0D6D","P0D6A","P0D69","P0D6F"],"מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0D6F:["A/C Compressor Motor Current High","P0D6F flags excessive current drawn by the electric A/C compressor on its high-voltage DC link while the motor is running. GM's first-generation Volt (2011–2015) and the 2016 Cadillac ELR set it when the DC link current exceeds 27 A in 30 of 60 samples taken every 50 ms with a compressor speed request above zero; it is Type B, MIL after two trips. A separate maker code, P1EC9, catches momentary peaks above 60 A.",["Compressor mechanically stiff or seizing","Shorted winding in the compressor motor","Refrigerant overcharge or excessive high-side pressure","Failing power stage in the compressor inverter"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning cuts out under load","Unusual noise from the compressor"],4,1,["P0D7A","P0D7B","P0D7C","P0D7D","P0D7E","P0D7F","P0D6D"],"זרם מנוע מדחס מזגן — ערך גבוה"],P0D70:["A/C Compressor Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The control electronics inside an electric A/C compressor carry a temperature sensor on the processor board, called internal sensor A; the compressor uses it to protect itself and reports the value to the vehicle. P0D70 is the general circuit code for it: the value is missing or invalid without a defined low or high reading. No manufacturer documents P0D70 publicly.",["Temperature sensor or its circuit on the compressor board failed","Value not transmitted correctly over the data link","Unstable 12 V supply or ground of the compressor electronics"],["Air conditioning weak or off","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be limited","Warning depending on the vehicle"],4,0,["P0D71","P0D72","P0D73","P0D74","P0D75"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D71:["A/C Compressor Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Performance","P0D71 is a plausibility test for the compressor control module's internal (CPU) temperature sensor: after a long rest, every temperature in the compressor should be close to its surroundings. GM's 2011 Volt compares it with intake air and with the output-stage sensor and fails above 6 °C in 35 of 40 samples at 250 ms.",["Drifting internal temperature sensor in the compressor electronics","Outside air, intake air or IGBT sensor used for comparison is wrong","Compressor not fully cooled down when the test ran"],["Check engine light after two trips","Usually no noticeable change in A/C operation"],4,1,["P0D70","P0D72","P0D73","P0D76","P0072","P0073"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D72:["A/C Compressor Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0D72 is the high-signal circuit code for the compressor module's internal temperature sensor. With the usual NTC thermistor a high signal means the reading looks extremely cold, and that is how GM defines it: every Volt and ELR table from 2011 to 2019 sets it when the reported CPU temperature is below −40 °C.",["Open circuit in the internal temperature sensor path","Cracked solder joint on the compressor electronics board","Faulty value transmission to the host controller"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning limited or switched off"],4,1,["P0D73","P0D71","P0D70","P0D74","P0D77"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D73:["A/C Compressor Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0D73 is the low-signal circuit code for the internal (CPU) temperature sensor of the compressor control module. For an NTC sensor a low signal corresponds to an implausibly hot reading. GM sets it above 274 °C in the 2011–2015 Volt and 2016 ELR, and above 150 °C in the 2016–2019 Volt, where the compressor itself detects the condition and reports it over GMLAN;",["Short circuit in the internal temperature sensor path","Real overheating of the compressor electronics","Faulty value transmission to the host controller"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning reduced or switched off"],4,1,["P0D72","P0D71","P0D70","P0D74","P0D78"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D74:["A/C Compressor Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D74 reports an unsteady signal from the internal temperature sensor of the A/C compressor control module: the reading jumps or drops out in short bursts rather than sitting at one wrong value. No manufacturer publishes a threshold for P0D74.",["Cold or cracked solder joint on the compressor board","Loose low-voltage connector or ground at the compressor","Electrical interference on the sensor signal"],["Air conditioning cuts out now and then","Code appears sporadically"],4,0,["P0D70","P0D71","P0D72","P0D73","P0D79"],"חיישן טמפרטורה פנימית מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D75:["A/C Compressor Control Module Output Driver Temperature Sensor Circuit","The output driver temperature sensor watches the power transistors (IGBTs) that switch the three motor phases of an electric A/C compressor; they heat up under load, and the compressor reduces speed or stops before they overheat. P0D75 is the general circuit code for this sensor without a defined low or high value. No manufacturer documents P0D75 publicly;",["Temperature sensor at the IGBT module or its circuit failed","Sensor value not transmitted correctly to the vehicle","Unstable 12 V supply or ground of the compressor electronics"],["Air conditioning switched off","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be limited"],4,0,["P0D76","P0D77","P0D78","P0D79","P0D70"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נהג יציאת מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D76:["A/C Compressor Control Module Output Driver Temperature Sensor Performance","P0D76 checks whether the compressor's output driver (IGBT) temperature sensor agrees with other temperatures after a cold soak. GM's 2011 Volt fails it when the IGBT reading differs from outside air by more than 11 °C and from the CPU sensor by more than 7 °C after the compressor has been off for over 21,600 s, with coolant and outside air within 15 °C of each other; 35 of 40 samples at 250 ms.",["Drifting IGBT temperature sensor in the compressor inverter","Outside air or CPU temperature used for comparison is wrong","Power stage still warm when the test ran"],["Check engine light after two trips","Usually no noticeable change in A/C operation"],4,1,["P0D75","P0D77","P0D78","P0D71","P0072","P0073"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נהג יציאת מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D77:["A/C Compressor Control Module Output Driver Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0D77 is the high-signal circuit code of the output driver (IGBT) temperature sensor in the electric A/C compressor. As with an NTC sensor, the high signal shows up as an impossibly cold temperature: GM sets it when the IGBT reading is at or below −40 °C, in all Volt and ELR tables from 2011 to 2019. The first-generation Volt needs 30 of 60 samples at 50 ms and evaluates only with outside air above −7 °C;",["Open circuit in the IGBT temperature sensor path","Cracked solder joint on the inverter board","Faulty value transmission to the host controller"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning switched off"],4,1,["P0D78","P0D76","P0D75","P0D79","P0D72"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נהג יציאת מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D78:["A/C Compressor Control Module Output Driver Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0D78 is set when the output driver (IGBT) temperature sensor of the electric A/C compressor signals low, which for an NTC sensor means an implausibly hot value. GM uses 274 °C or more in the 2011–2015 Volt and the 2016 ELR, and above 150 °C in the 2016–2019 Volt; detection takes 30 of 60 samples at 50 ms, 40 of 50 at 100 ms or 4 of 5 s, Type B with MIL after two trips.",["Short circuit in the IGBT temperature sensor path","Real overheating of the power stage from high load or poor condenser cooling","Faulty value transmission to the host controller"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning switched off"],4,1,["P0D77","P0D76","P0D75","P0D79","P0D73"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נהג יציאת מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D79:["A/C Compressor Control Module Output Driver Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D79 marks an erratic signal from the output driver temperature sensor, the sensor that guards the compressor's power transistors: values flicker or drop out briefly instead of staying wrong. No manufacturer publishes set criteria. GM's 2019 Volt table lists P0D79 next to P0D77 and P0D78 as an IGBT-sensor circuit fault that suspends the plausibility checks, which shows the code is in use, but without thresholds.",["Cold or cracked solder joint at the IGBT sensor","Loose low-voltage connector or ground at the compressor","Switching noise from the inverter coupling into the sensor signal"],["Air conditioning cuts out repeatedly","Code appears sporadically"],4,0,["P0D75","P0D76","P0D77","P0D78","P0D74"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נהג יציאת מודול בקרת מדחס מזגן — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D7A:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current Low","Electric A/C compressors run a three-phase motor whose phase currents U, V and W are measured inside the inverter. P0D7A belongs to phase U. GM's 2011–2015 Volt and 2016 ELR test the sensors while the motor is stopped and the speed request is zero, when no current should flow: a phase U reading of +68 A or more in 30 of 60 samples at 50 ms sets P0D7A, Type B with MIL after two trips, and a 12 V reset is needed…",["Offset or failure of the phase U current sensor in the inverter","Fault in the inverter's current measuring circuit"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning does not start"],4,1,["P0D7B","P0D7C","P0D7E","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — ערך נמוך"],P0D7B:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current High","P0D7B is the second phase U current code of the electric A/C compressor. In GM's first-generation Volt (2011–2015) and the 2016 Cadillac ELR it sets when the phase U current sensor reads −68 A or less although the motor is stopped and no speed is requested, counted as 30 failures in 60 samples at 50 ms; Type B, MIL after two trips, with a 12 V reset required before a pass.",["Phase U current sensor reading negative at standstill","Fault in the inverter's current evaluation"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning stays off"],4,1,["P0D7A","P0D7D","P0D7F","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — ערך גבוה"],P0D7C:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current Low","P0D7C relates to phase V of the three-phase motor in an electric A/C compressor. GM documents it in the 2011–2015 Volt and 2016 ELR tables as a standstill check: with the compressor speed request at 0 rpm the measured phase V current should be near zero, and a value of +68 A or above in 30 of 60 samples (50 ms each) sets the code.",["Offset or failure of the phase V current sensor in the inverter","Fault in the inverter's current measuring circuit"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning inoperative"],4,1,["P0D7D","P0D7A","P0D7E","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — ערך נמוך"],P0D7D:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current High","P0D7D marks a phase V current reading of the electric A/C compressor that is far negative while the motor should be idle. GM's first-generation Volt and the 2016 ELR set it at −68 A or less, judged in 30 of 60 samples every 50 ms whenever the speed request is zero, as Type B with MIL after two trips. A 12 V reset is needed before the diagnostic can report a pass again.",["Phase V current sensor reading negative at standstill","Fault in the inverter's current evaluation"],["Check engine light after two trips","No cooling from the air conditioning","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be limited"],4,1,["P0D7C","P0D7B","P0D7F","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — ערך גבוה"],P0D7E:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current Low","P0D7E applies to phase W, the third winding of the electric A/C compressor motor. GM's 2011–2015 Volt and 2016 Cadillac ELR check the W-phase current sensor while the motor is at rest: a reading of +68 A or more in 30 out of 60 samples at 50 ms sets the code, Type B with the MIL after two trips, and only a 12 V reset re-enables a pass.",["Offset or failure of the phase W current sensor channel","Common supply or reference fault in the inverter (several phases affected)"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning does not run"],4,1,["P0D7F","P0D7A","P0D7C","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — ערך נמוך"],P0D7F:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current High","P0D7F is the negative-direction current code for phase W of the electric A/C compressor motor. In GM's first-generation Volt and the 2016 ELR the inverter reports a W-phase current of −68 A or lower while the motor is stopped and no speed is requested; 30 such readings in 60 samples at 50 ms set the code, Type B with MIL after two trips, and a 12 V reset is required before it can pass.",["Phase W current sensor reading negative at standstill","Fault in the inverter's current measuring circuit"],["Check engine light after two trips","Air conditioning inoperative"],4,1,["P0D7E","P0D7B","P0D7D","P0D6F"],"זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — ערך גבוה"],P0D80:["Battery Charger Input Circuit/Open","P0D80 means the on-board charger sees no usable AC supply at its input even though the charging handshake says power should be there. Ford documents the check for its plug-in hybrids in the OBD summaries for model years 2014 to 2018: with the cable connected, the EVSE active and the S2 switch closed, an input below 85 VAC lasting 30 seconds sets the code, and Ford's code dictionary marks it as a MIL code.",["Charging station or outlet delivers no AC after the handshake (fault, tripped breaker)","Open AC contact or wire between charge inlet and charger","Fault in the charger's input stage or voltage measurement"],["Vehicle does not charge although the cable is plugged in","Charging fault message or flashing fault pattern at the charge port","Check engine light"],2,1,["P0D81","P0D82","P0D83","P0D84","P0D27","P0D90","P0D3D"],"כניסת מטען סוללה — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0D81:["Battery Charger Input Circuit Range/Performance","P0D81 is the plausibility code for the charger's AC input: a signal is present and electrically valid, yet contradicts the stage the charging sequence has reached. No manufacturer documents this code publicly with thresholds.",["Charging station switches AC on before the vehicle has closed S2 (faulty or welded EVSE relay)","Charger's input voltage measurement reports an implausible value","Improvised supply that feeds the inlet directly without a proper EVSE"],["Charging does not start or stops shortly after plugging in","Charging fault message"],2,0,["P0D80","P0D82","P0D83","P0D84","P0D90","P0D28","P0D3E"],"כניסת מטען סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D82:["Battery Charger Input Circuit Low","P0D82 reports that the charger's input circuit signal has dropped below the lowest value the module accepts as electrically valid. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; Ford, for instance, folds missing input into P0D80 with its 85 VAC/30 s limit.",["Input sense line or harness shorted to ground","Moisture or corrosion at the charge inlet terminals","Failed input stage inside the on-board charger"],["Charging does not start","Charging ends earlier than expected","Charging fault message"],4,0,["P0D80","P0D81","P0D83","P0D84","P0D27","P0D3F"],"כניסת מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D83:["Battery Charger Input Circuit High","P0D83 is set when the charger reads its input circuit above the highest electrically valid level, i.e. a reading the measuring stage could not produce from a healthy mains connection. No manufacturer publishes a threshold for it. Likely culprits are a sense line shorted to a voltage source, a damaged inlet whose pins bridge to one another, or a measuring channel in the charger that has drifted or failed.",["Input sense line shorted to a voltage source","Damaged charge inlet with bridged or burnt contacts","Drifted or failed measuring channel in the charger"],["Charger refuses to charge","Charging fault message"],4,0,["P0D80","P0D81","P0D82","P0D84","P0D28","P0D40"],"כניסת מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D84:["Battery Charger Input Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D84 stands for an input signal that keeps jumping or dropping out during a charge rather than sitting steadily at a wrong value. There is no public manufacturer description of the code. In practice such patterns come from contacts that make and break: a worn or loose charging plug, a heat-damaged inlet socket, a cable damaged by being driven over, or a loose connector between inlet and charger.",["Worn, loose or heat-damaged charging plug or inlet contacts","Damaged charging cable (crushed, kinked)","Supply sagging under load, e.g. long extension lead","Loose connector between inlet and charger"],["Charging stops and restarts repeatedly","Charging takes noticeably longer"],2,0,["P0D80","P0D81","P0D82","P0D83","P0D41"],"כניסת מטען סוללה — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D85:["Battery Charging Output Voltage Too High","P0D85 means the charger is putting more voltage onto the traction battery side than it should. In Ford's 2019 plug-in hybrid OBD summary the battery controller compares the charger's output voltage with its own request and flags a surplus of 20.82 V lasting 20 seconds.",["Voltage control fault inside the on-board charger/DC-DC unit","Open high-voltage fuse or P/N line between charger and battery (output unloaded)","Control unit software error (e.g. Porsche DME, reprogramming)"],["Charging stops","Hybrid system warning and check engine light"],4,1,["P0D20","P0D21","P0D4F","P0D22","P0D5C"],"מתח יציאת טעינת סוללה — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0D86:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit A/Open","Many plug-in vehicles light up the charge inlet to show whether the plug is recognised, charging is running or a fault exists; a Ford summary describes a four-segment ring, a supplier patent multi-coloured LEDs at the port. P0D86 says the control module driving channel A of this indicator finds no continuity in that circuit.",["Indicator connector at the charge inlet unplugged or not latched","Broken wire in the harness near the charge port flap","Failed LED or lamp element of channel A"],["Part of the charge port lighting stays dark","Charging status not visible at the inlet, charging itself works"],2,0,["P0D87","P0D88","P0D89","P0D8C","P0D2C"],""],P0D87:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit A Low","P0D87 flags channel A of the charge port status indicator as pulled low, which in practice means a short to ground somewhere between driver and lamp. Such a fault only shows when the channel is switched on: the driver tries to raise the line and current flows straight to ground.",["Chafed wire of channel A touching body ground","Water ingress into the charge inlet lighting","Internally shorted LED module"],["Indicator channel A stays dark when it should light","Charging status hard to read at the port"],2,0,["P0D86","P0D88","P0D8A","P0D8D","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0D88:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit A High","P0D88 is stored when channel A of the charge port status indicator sits at a high level although the driver is holding it off, typically because the line touches a voltage-carrying wire or the driver stage leaks. The fault is therefore seen in the switched-off state; GM's Volt evaluated its own charge-status LED (P0D2C) for short to battery only while the LED was meant to be dark.",["Channel A wire shorted to a voltage-carrying wire (pinched harness)","Bridged or corroded connector at the indicator","Leaking or failed output stage in the control module"],["Indicator channel A lights constantly or glows faintly","Port shows a misleading charging status"],2,0,["P0D86","P0D87","P0D8B","P0D8E","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0D89:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit B/Open","P0D89 reports lost continuity in the second output channel, B, of the charge port status indicator. Where an indicator has several channels, they usually stand for different colours or segments, as in the multi-colour port lighting of a supplier patent; which one is B depends on the vehicle, and no manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Break in the channel B wire or its connector pin","Failed LED or segment of channel B","Shared connector or ground at the inlet (when A and C are also affected)"],["One colour or segment of the port lighting missing","Charging works normally"],2,0,["P0D8A","P0D8B","P0D86","P0D8C"],""],P0D8A:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit B Low","P0D8A means channel B of the charge port status indicator is held at ground level when it ought to rise, i.e. a short to ground on the B line or inside its lamp. The inlet is one of the most exposed electrical points of the car: rain, car-wash water and road salt reach it every time the flap opens, so corroded or wet contacts that bridge to ground are a frequent finding. No manufacturer documents P0D8A publicly;",["Wet or corroded contacts at the inlet lighting bridging to ground","Channel B wire chafed through to body ground","Shorted LED or segment of channel B"],["Colour or segment B stays dark","Charging status only partly shown"],2,0,["P0D89","P0D8B","P0D87","P0D8D","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך נמוך"],P0D8B:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit B High","P0D8B is set when the B line of the charge port status indicator carries voltage while its driver has it switched off. The detection logic mirrors what GM published for the Volt's charge-status LED (P0D2C): a short to battery is looked for during the off phase. No manufacturer documents P0D8B itself.",["Insulation damage where the harness bends at the flap, contact with a supply wire","Connector bridged by moisture","Driver output in the control module stuck on"],["Colour or segment B stays lit","Displayed charge level does not match the actual state"],2,0,["P0D89","P0D8A","P0D88","P0D8E","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך גבוה"],P0D8C:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit C/Open","P0D8C points to an open third channel, C, of the charge port status indicator. Only systems with at least three separately driven lighting outputs use it, for instance one per colour in an RGB-type port lamp of the kind a supplier patent describes, or individual ring segments. No manufacturer documents the code publicly.",["Lamp connector left unplugged or pin pushed back after work at the inlet","Failed LED of channel C (on integrated inlets the whole inlet assembly)","Broken channel C wire"],["One colour or segment of the port lighting missing","Fault appears after body or inlet repair"],2,0,["P0D8D","P0D8E","P0D86","P0D89"],""],P0D8D:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit C Low","P0D8D marks channel C of the charge port status indicator as shorted toward ground: when the module switches C on, the line does not reach the expected level and the driver registers overcurrent or a low voltage. As with A and B, the code is defined in the standard but no manufacturer publishes its own conditions for it;",["Short to ground at the far end of the harness inside the inlet housing","Shorted LED or segment of channel C","Grounded driver output inside the control module"],["Colour or segment C stays dark","Whole port indicator may switch off"],2,0,["P0D8C","P0D8E","P0D87","P0D8A","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ C — ערך נמוך"],P0D8E:["Battery Charger Coupler Status Indicator Control Circuit C High","P0D8E is recorded when channel C of the charge port status indicator shows a high level that the driver did not command, a short to a supply voltage or a driver that no longer turns off. Following the pattern GM published for the Volt's charge-status LED, this is checked while the output is meant to be inactive. No manufacturer documents P0D8E publicly.",["Chafed channel C wire touching a supply wire at the flap mechanism","Connector bridged by water","Output stage in the control module stuck on"],["Colour or segment C lit permanently","Port shows a fault or complete status that is not real"],2,0,["P0D8C","P0D8D","P0D88","P0D8B","P0D2C"],"מעגל בקרת חיווי סטטוס מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ C — ערך גבוה"],P0D8F:["Battery Charger A Cooling System Performance","P0D8F means the cooling of on-board charger A is not keeping the unit within its temperature limits, so the charger has to restrict its function. Mercedes documents the code with failure type 97 for the AMG GLE 53 Hybrid (bulletin LI47.70-P-079963, 2025): AC charging stops after about 15 minutes, the cluster reports AC charging inoperative and severely limited hybrid performance, and alongside P0D8F the charger…",["Low coolant level or air in the charger's cooling circuit","Coolant lines connected to the wrong circuit (Mercedes AMG GLE 53 Hybrid)","Failed coolant pump of the low-temperature circuit","Blocked low-temperature radiator or inactive cooling fan"],["AC charging stops after a short time or runs at reduced power","Warning that AC charging is inoperative","Hybrid drive performance limited"],2,2,["P2DB6","P0D24","P0D67"],"מערכת קירור מטען סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0D90:["Battery Charger Input Frequency Incorrect","P0D90 is stored when the AC supplied to the on-board charger does not have a believable mains frequency. No official manufacturer document available to the public names the code with a threshold.",["Charging from a source with unstable frequency (small generator, inverter)","Faulty portable charging unit or wall box","Fault in the charger's frequency measurement"],["Often no noticeable effect apart from the stored code","Charging interrupted at certain supply points"],1,0,["P0D80","P0D81","P0D27","P0D28"],""],P0D91:["Battery Charger Coupler Lock Control Circuit/Open","P0D91 means the module that drives the charge-plug latch sees its lock output as electrically open: when it commands the latch closed, no current flows through the wiring and actuator winding. Without a confirmed lock, the car normally refuses to start a charging session, because the plug could be withdrawn under load.",["Connector at the lock actuator loose, corroded or backed out","Broken wire in the harness between control unit and inlet","Motor winding inside the lock actuator burnt through","Driver stage or software of the controlling module (Mercedes names both)"],["Vehicle does not charge on AC","Check engine light on (Mercedes)","Charging system warning in the instrument cluster"],2,1,["P0D92","P0D93","P0D94","P0D95","P0D9E"],"נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0D92:["Battery Charger Coupler Lock Control Circuit Low","P0D92 says the voltage on the lock control output stays too low, typically because the drive line is shorted to ground or the actuator draws far too much current, so the module cannot energise the latch properly and shuts its driver off. No manufacturer publishes this exact code.",["Lock drive wire chafed and shorted to body ground","Actuator jammed or binding, drawing excessive current","Water in the actuator connector at the inlet","Driver output of the controlling module defective"],["Charging session does not start after plugging in","Charge plug is not held in the inlet","Charging fault message in the cluster or app"],2,0,["P0D91","P0D93","P0D94","P0D96"],"נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0D93:["Battery Charger Coupler Lock Control Circuit High","P0D93 reports a lock control circuit that reads higher than the module expects, classically a drive line shorted to a supply voltage. Ford shows that mechanical damage leads there as well: TSBs 22-2355 and 23-2014 list P0D93:00, alongside P0D94, P0D98, P0D9E and P0DA0, as stored by the SOBDM (secondary on-board diagnostic module) of Mach-E, e-Transit and F-150 Lightning built 2021–2023.",["Locking pin broken or missing after the plug was pulled while latched (Ford)","Lock drive wire shorted to a supply voltage","Internal electrical fault in the lock actuator","Driver stage of the controlling module defective"],["DC fast charging (level 3) inoperative","Charger Service Required indicator in the cluster (Ford)","Locking pin visibly broken or absent at the inlet"],2,0,["P0D91","P0D92","P0D94","P0D98","P0D9E","P0DA0"],"נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0D94:["Battery Charger Coupler Lock Control Circuit Performance","P0D94 is the functional check of the locking step: the wiring tests fine, yet after a lock command the feedback from the actuator does not confirm that the pin arrived in its locked position. Two makers publish it. Ford's TSBs 22-2355 and 23-2014 tie it to a broken or missing pin in the DC charge-port lock actuator of 2021–2023 Mach-E, e-Transit and Lightning models, cured with a new actuator.",["Locking pin broken or missing (Ford)","Software fault in the on-board charger, fixed by update (Audi)","Actuator mechanism binding from dirt, ice or wear","Charge plug not fully seated, so the pin cannot engage"],["Charging aborts or does not start; DC fast charging unavailable","Charge cable cannot be removed except with the manual release","Charge LED changes from green to red, AC charging restricted message (Audi)"],2,0,["P0D91","P0D93","P0D98","P0D9E","P0DA0"],"נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0D95:["Battery Charger Coupler Unlock Control Circuit/Open","P0D95 concerns the release direction: the output that withdraws the locking pin shows an open circuit when the module commands unlock. Because J2012 assigns lock and unlock separate control circuits, the module watches each drive direction on its own, and a break can disable only the release. That is the more awkward failure, since the cable may stay latched in the inlet after charging;",["Open unlock drive wire or loose pin in the actuator connector","Motor winding or internal contact of the actuator open","Corrosion in the connector at the inlet from road splash","Unlock driver stage of the controlling module defective"],["Charge cable stays locked in the inlet after charging","Plug can only be freed with the emergency release","Charging system fault message"],2,0,["P0D96","P0D97","P0D98","P0D91"],""],P0D96:["Battery Charger Coupler Unlock Control Circuit Low","P0D96 indicates that the voltage on the unlock control output is too low, which usually means the release line is shorted to ground or the actuator pulls an overcurrent as soon as it is energised in the release direction. To protect its driver, the module stops commanding, so the pin stays where it is. If it was latched, the plug remains captive until the emergency release is used;",["Unlock drive wire shorted to ground at a chafe point","Actuator seized, for example by ice or dirt, causing overcurrent","Moisture in the actuator connector","Unlock driver stage of the module defective"],["Charge plug cannot be unplugged after charging","Emergency release needed to free the cable","Next charging session refused"],2,0,["P0D95","P0D97","P0D98","P0D92"],""],P0D97:["Battery Charger Coupler Unlock Control Circuit High","P0D97 reports that the unlock control output sits higher than it should while the module is not driving it, or does not follow the command, which points to a release line touching a supply voltage.",["Unlock drive wire shorted to a supply wire in the harness","Internal short inside the lock actuator","Unlock driver stage of the module stuck on"],["Charging session stops or does not start","Plug is not held firmly in the inlet","Charging system fault message"],2,0,["P0D95","P0D96","P0D98","P0D93"],""],P0D98:["Battery Charger Coupler Unlock Control Circuit Performance","P0D98 is set when the release command is carried out electrically but the feedback does not confirm that the pin has retracted, or the actuator never reaches its open end stop. Ford is the only maker that names it publicly: TSBs 22-2355 and 23-2014 list P0D98:00 in the SOBDM of Ford's electric Mach-E, e-Transit and Lightning pickup (2021–2023), together with P0D93, P0D94 and the lock position codes P0D9E/P0DA0.",["Actuator pin damaged by pulling the plug while latched (Ford)","Release movement blocked by ice, dirt or a worn gear train","Lock position feedback reporting incorrectly"],["DC fast charging (level 3) inoperative","Charger Service Required indicator (Ford)","Plug hard to remove or held in the inlet"],2,0,["P0D95","P0D94","P0D93","P0D9E","P0DA0"],""],P0D99:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor A Circuit","P0D99 flags a general electrical fault in temperature sensor A of the charging inlet, the element that tells the car how hot the power contacts are. At high current, contact resistance heats the pins; the reading lets the car throttle or stop charging (TE Connectivity, US10960781B2).",["Loose low-voltage connector at the on-board charger (smart)","Damaged sensor wiring between inlet and charger","Temperature element in the inlet failed (usually replaced with the inlet harness)","Evaluation input in the charger module defective"],["High-voltage battery cannot be charged","Charging only at reduced power","Charging system fault message"],2,0,["P0D9A","P0D9B","P0D9C","P0D9D","P0E5F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D9A:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0D9A covers a coupler temperature signal that is electrically valid but does not make sense, for example a rise that the charging current cannot explain or a value that disagrees with the rest of the system.",["Oversized or damaged terminals in the charging station plug (GM)","Charging software calibration, update pending or available (GM)","Raised contact resistance at the vehicle inlet pins","Temperature element drifting out of its characteristic"],["MIL on (GM)","Charging fails or stops, often at busy public stations","Code returns only after a drive of about 10 minutes (GM)"],2,1,["P0D99","P0D9B","P0D9C","P0D9D","P0E5F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0D9B:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0D9B means the voltage of coupler temperature sensor A has dropped below its valid window. Low refers to the signal, and what that implies about temperature depends on the element: with an NTC a low voltage stands for an extremely hot reading, whereas the PTC chain and Pt 1000 sensors Phoenix Contact builds into its inlets lose resistance as they cool, so in the usual pull-up wiring a low signal looks implausibly…",["Sensor signal wire shorted to ground","Moisture or corrosion bridging the sensor contacts in the inlet","Temperature element internally shorted","Diagnosis misled by swapped fault texts in the charger (Bentley)"],["Charging refused or limited","Charging system fault message","Fault stored as intermittent or static in the charger"],2,0,["P0D99","P0D9A","P0D9C","P0D9D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0D9C:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0D9C is stored when the signal of coupler temperature sensor A climbs above its valid window, the typical result of an open sensor loop, an unplugged connector or a short to a supply voltage.",["Open sensor wire or connector at the inlet or charger","Sensor signal wire shorted to a supply voltage","Temperature element open inside the inlet","Diagnosis misled by swapped fault texts in the charger (Bentley)"],["Charging stops immediately or does not start","Charging system fault message","Fault stored as intermittent or static in the charger"],2,0,["P0D99","P0D9A","P0D9B","P0D9D","P0E5F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0D9D:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0D9D is logged when the signal of temperature sensor A in the charging inlet jumps or drops out for short moments instead of failing for good. The sensor sits beside the power pins so the car can cut back or end charging once contact resistance heats them; TE Connectivity patents US10960781B2 and US11117478B2 name thermistors and resistance thermometers for this job. No manufacturer documents P0D9D publicly.",["Sensor wiring between inlet and charger with a loose or chafed contact","Low-voltage connector at the on-board charger not fully seated (seen on smart for sibling codes)","Moisture or corrosion in the inlet harness connector","Temperature element in the inlet with an internal intermittent break"],["Charging stops now and then and resumes on a later attempt","Charging power reduced for a while without an obvious reason","Charging fault message that comes and goes"],2,0,["P0D99","P0D9A","P0D9B","P0D9C","P0E5F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0D9E:["Battery Charger A Coupler Lock Position Sensor Circuit","The lock position sensor tells the vehicle whether the pin that holds the charging plug in the inlet is extended or retracted. Without that feedback the car cannot confirm a latched plug and refuses fast charging. A GM patent, US8936482B2, measures how far the actuator has moved the pin, with a Hall element, a potentiometer or an end-stop switch as options. P0D9E is the general circuit fault of this feedback.",["Actuator pin broken or missing after the plug was pulled while latched (Ford)","Open or shorted feedback wire between lock actuator and control module","Corroded or loose connector at the lock actuator","Evaluation input of the controlling module defective"],["DC fast charging inoperative","Charger Service Required message in the cluster (Ford)","Locking pin visibly damaged or absent at the inlet"],2,0,["P0D9F","P0DA0","P0DA1","P0DA2","P0D93","P0D98"],"חיישן מצב נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0D9F:["Battery Charger A Coupler Lock Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0D9F covers a lock position signal that is electrically in range but does not fit the situation: the pin is reported in a position the actuator was not told to reach, or it gets there too late. The sensing principle in GM patent US8936482B2 works on pin travel, so a jammed or worn mechanism shows up here as well as a drifting sensor.",["Loose low-voltage contacts at the 22 kW on-board charger (smart)","Lock mechanism sluggish from ice, dirt or a worn gear train","Position sensor in the actuator drifting or misaligned"],["Vehicle will not start and reports a connected charging cable (smart)","High-voltage battery does not charge","Fault may appear only at the start or end of charging, with cold or warm parts"],2,0,["P0D9E","P0DA0","P0DA1","P0DA2","P0D98"],"חיישן מצב נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0DA0:["Battery Charger A Coupler Lock Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0DA0 means the lock position feedback sits at the bottom of its range, typically from a signal wire shorted to ground. Mitsubishi documents it for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: with the IGCT supply on, the PHEV-ECU detects a low input from the lock position reading. The sensor sits in the charge connector lock actuator, part of the inlet harness;",["Lock position signal line shorted to ground (Mitsubishi)","Actuator pin broken after the plug was pulled while latched (Ford)","Position sensor in the inlet harness outside its resistance specification","PHEV-ECU input defective"],["DC fast charging not possible","Charger Service Required message (Ford)","Plug is not latched or charging ends right after start"],2,0,["P0DA1","P0D9E","P0D9F","P0DA2","P0D93","P0D94"],"חיישן מצב נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0DA1:["Battery Charger A Coupler Lock Position Sensor Circuit High","P0DA1 is the high-side fault of the lock position feedback: the signal stays near its upper limit, which points to an open circuit somewhere in the sensor loop rather than a grounded wire. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV, Mitsubishi sets it in the PHEV-ECU when the supply is on and a high input from the lock position reading is seen.",["Ground connection of the lock actuator connector interrupted","Open signal line between inlet harness and PHEV-ECU","Position sensor in the inlet harness open or far above 16.8 kOhm","PHEV-ECU input defective"],["Charging plug lock not confirmed, DC charging refused","Charging system message, no MIL documented"],2,0,["P0DA0","P0D9E","P0D9F","P0DA2"],"חיישן מצב נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0DA2:["Battery Charger A Coupler Lock Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0DA2 is stored when the lock position feedback flickers or drops out briefly, while it never sits at one limit long enough for the low or high verdict. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Loose or corroded contact at the lock actuator connector","Inlet harness chafed where it moves with the charge port flap or body","Worn lock mechanism letting the pin rattle near a switching point"],["DC fast charging aborts at random","Lock status message that appears and clears again"],2,0,["P0D9E","P0D9F","P0DA0","P0DA1"],"חיישן מצב נעילת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0DA3:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit","The inverter of drive motor B, usually the rear electric motor in all-wheel-drive hybrids, measures its own DC link voltage; sensor A delivers that value to the motor controller. P0DA3 is the general circuit fault for this channel, with no finer verdict such as low or high. No manufacturer documents P0DA3 publicly.",["Voltage sensing circuit inside the rear motor control unit defective","Loose high-voltage cable or terminal at the rear inverter","Low-voltage connector or harness to the inverter controller faulty","High-voltage fuse loose or blown"],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Rear electric drive reduced or unavailable","Reduced power or no READY state"],4,3,["P0DA4","P0DA5","P0DA6","P0DA7","P0D2D","P0C7A"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0DA4:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0DA4 flags a rear inverter voltage that is electrically valid but disagrees with the other voltage readings on the same high-voltage bus. Mitsubishi spells out the check for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: once the power drive unit has at least 8 V, voltage control is off, CAN is healthy and the rear value is available, all three comparisons must fail together, namely capacitor 2 main, capacitor 2 sub and capacitor 1 each…",["Rear inverter voltage sensing drifts against the other bus readings","Power drive unit faulty (Mitsubishi final repair step)","Underlying fault in the rear motor control unit (P0C7A, P0DA5, P0DA6, P30D1)","Battery management unit reporting incorrectly"],["Plug-in hybrid system warning and MIL on","Rear motor power limited","Code returns only after the OBD-II drive cycle"],4,3,["P0DA3","P0DA5","P0DA6","P0DA9","P0C7A","P0D2E"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0DA5:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Low","P0DA5 reports that the rear inverter voltage signal sits at the bottom of its range, the classic result of an open circuit, a short to ground or no high voltage arriving at all. Mitsubishi sets it in the rear motor control unit of the 2018 Outlander PHEV when, with the propulsion system active, the sensor output stays below 0.5 V, roughly 10 V on the bus, for 0.3 s.",["Rear high-voltage battery cable (P and N lines) loose or damaged","High-voltage fuse not seated or loose bus bar in the battery","Sensing circuit in the rear motor control unit failed","Fault inside the traction battery pack"],["MIL and plug-in hybrid warning on","Rear motor drive switched off","Possible no-READY condition if other units also lose voltage"],4,3,["P0DA6","P0DA3","P0DA4","P0D2F"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0DA6:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit High","P0DA6 is the upper-limit fault of the rear inverter voltage signal: the reading sticks near the top, as with a short to the sensor supply, or the bus really is overvolted. Mitsubishi checks it in the rear motor control unit of the 2018 Outlander PHEV whenever that unit is powered with 8 to 16 V; a sensor output above 4.7 V, standing for about 440 V, held for 0.3 s sets the code.",["Rear motor control unit sensing faulty when no other unit reports high voltage (Mitsubishi)","Fault in the high-voltage circuit or battery when front and generator units also report high","Signal shorted to the sensor supply inside the inverter"],["MIL and plug-in hybrid warning on","Rear motor drive switched off, reduced power"],4,3,["P0DA5","P0DA3","P0DA4","P0C7A","P0D30"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0DA7:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0DA7 is meant for a rear inverter voltage reading that jitters or drops out briefly without staying out of range long enough for P0DA5 or P0DA6. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",["Low-voltage connector at the rear inverter with intermittent contact","High-voltage terminal at the rear inverter not tightened properly","Voltage sensing electronics failing with temperature or vibration"],["Hybrid warning that appears and clears","Brief loss of rear drive power"],4,3,["P0DA3","P0DA5","P0DA6","P0D31"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0DA8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage/Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Correlation","P0DA8 compares the traction battery voltage with the voltage measured at the inverter of drive motor A; both should match closely once the contactors are closed. Denso cites earlier diagnostics that blame a voltage sensor when the two differ by more than a set amount (US9819293B2).",["Cracked solder joint in the motor power inverter (Honda Civic/ILX Hybrid)","Battery cell voltage sensor software misreading inverter noise (Honda Accord Hybrid recall)","High-voltage battery junction box faulty (Ford Mach-E)","Battery-side or inverter-side voltage sensor drifting"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Fail-safe electric mode with strongly limited speed (Honda)","Discharged 12 V battery (Ford Mach-E)"],4,3,["P0DA9","P0D2D","P0D2E","P0B3B"],""],P0DA9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage/Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Correlation","P0DA9 is the motor B counterpart of P0DA8: the controller compares traction battery voltage with the voltage seen at the inverter of drive motor B, typically the rear motor, and logs the code when the two drift apart with the contactors closed. No manufacturer documents P0DA9 publicly.",["Voltage sensing in the motor B inverter drifting","Battery pack voltage measurement in the battery management faulty","High resistance in a contactor, fuse or cable between battery and rear inverter"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Rear electric drive limited or disabled"],4,3,["P0DA8","P0DA4","P0DA3"],""],P0DAA:["Battery Charging System Isolation Fault","P0DAA reports that the resistance between the charging-side HV bus and chassis ground has fallen too low, so the charging path or the battery is no longer safely insulated. GM documents the logic for the 2014 Chevrolet Volt and Cadillac ELR in the HPC2 (VICM): in charge-only mode the active isolation resistance is measured, at most once per wake-up;",["Coolant leaking into the battery pack (GM)","Insulation loss within battery cells or sections (GM)","Outdated HPCM2/BECM calibration (GM, 2011–2014)","Damaged insulation in the on-board charger or charging cables"],["Vehicle will not charge","MIL on (GM Type A)","Low coolant level in the battery coolant reservoir"],4,1,["P0AA6"],""],P0DAB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Performance","P0DAB is the overall verdict on cell balancing: the battery controller sees that bleeding charge off the fuller cells no longer brings the pack back into line, so the spread between cells stays too wide. CARB 13 CCR 1968.2 (e)(15.2.3) requires hybrids to check exactly this. Mazda sets it on the 48 V M Hybrid Boost of the CX-90 and CX-70 (MHB module: P0DAB:17; SGC2/DMCM: P0DAB:00) with the check engine light;",["Battery controller software that judges balancing too strictly (FCA BPCM and Mazda MSP66 reflash)","Cells aged unevenly so the spread grows faster than balancing can correct it","Internal fault in the battery assembly (Mazda 48 V battery replaced when the code returns at once)","One or more balancing switches or resistors no longer working"],["Check engine light and hybrid system warning in the cluster","Mild hybrid assist, start-stop and regeneration switched off (engine-only operation on Mazda)","Reduced electric range or usable battery capacity"],4,3,["P0DAC","P0DB0","P0DB4","P0DB8","P0DBC","P0BBE","P0DE6","P0DE7"],"איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DAC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit A","P0DAC reports an electrical fault in balancing circuit A, meaning the wiring, switch or monitoring chip behind it, not a balancing result. Audi uses it on the e-tron GT (2022-2026) in battery management address 008C and spells it out as an electrical fault of cell controller 1. Bulletin 2074056 explains that a short power interruption while the 12 V battery is reconnected can log this entry;",["Brief 12 V supply interruption, e.g. while reconnecting the 12 V battery (Audi e-tron GT)","Defective cell monitoring controller or balancing switch of circuit A","Loose or corroded sense/balancing lead connector at the module"],["Battery management warning or red onboard supply lamp","Vehicle may refuse to start or enter drive readiness","Diagnostic tester proposes replacing HV battery modules"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DAD","P0DAE","P0DAF","P0DB0"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A"],P0DAD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit A Stuck On","P0DAD means balancing circuit A keeps draining its cells although the controller has not asked for it: the bleed switch is stuck closed and the resistor keeps pulling those cells down. Ford uses the code in the BECM of its hybrids;",["Balancing transistor of circuit A shorted (fails closed)","Monitoring chip or its drive output keeps the switch enabled","Controller software leaving a balancing request active"],["Hybrid or EV battery warning with the MIL","Cells of one group noticeably lower after the car has been parked","Range or electric assistance decreasing over days"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DAC","P0DAE","P0DAF","P0DB1"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DAE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit A Stuck Off","With P0DAE the controller has commanded balancing on circuit A, yet nothing happens: the switch stays open or the bleed resistor is broken, so the targeted cells keep their charge. Ford documents the code for its hybrid BECM;",["Bleed resistor of circuit A open or its solder joint cracked","Balancing transistor failed open","Broken balancing/sense lead or terminal between module and cells"],["MIL and battery system warning","Charging ends early because individual cells reach full first","Growing cell voltage spread in the live data"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DAC","P0DAD","P0DAF","P0DB2"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DAF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit A Range/Performance","P0DAF is a plausibility code: circuit A switches and is electrically intact, but the effect on the cell voltages does not match what the controller expects. FCA is the visible user. Ram 1500 pickups with the 48 V eTorque system (2019-2024, bulletin 08-030-24 and its yearly versions) list P0DAF among the MIL codes fixed by new BPCM software.",["Battery pack control module software with an oversensitive check (FCA bulletins 08-030-24, 08-186-26)","Weak or deeply discharged cells that respond differently to balancing, e.g. after cold cranking","Bleed resistor or switch of circuit A with changed resistance"],["MIL on, often together with other battery codes","Vehicle may not start or cannot be switched off (Jeep Cherokee hybrid)","Reduced mild hybrid assistance"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DAC","P0DAD","P0DAE","P0DE6","P0DB3"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DB0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit B","P0DB0 flags an electrical defect in the second balancing circuit, B. The battery controller finds an open, short or implausible voltage on the switch, resistor or lead serving that cell group, independent of whether balancing would be needed right now.",["Damaged balancing/sense lead or connector of cell group B","Cell monitoring chip or controller of group B defective","Short 12 V supply dropout during service work"],["Battery system warning and MIL","Electric drive or assistance possibly limited"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB1","P0DB2","P0DB3","P0DAC","P0DB4"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B"],P0DB1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit B Stuck On","P0DB1: in circuit B the bleed path stays closed even after the controller withdrew the command. The cells of group B are drained without pause, so their voltage falls behind the others, worst during long standstill. Detection usually compares commanded and reported switch state; GM's eAssist logic for a related code needs 7 failed of 10 samples before a Type B entry.",["Shorted balancing transistor in circuit B","Drive output of the monitoring chip stuck active","Moisture or contamination on the module board creating a leakage path"],["MIL with battery warning","Group B cells clearly low after parking","Usable charge and range shrinking"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB0","P0DB2","P0DB3","P0DAD"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DB2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit B Stuck Off","Stuck off on circuit B (P0DB2) is the passive failure: the command arrives, but the bleed resistor of that group carries no current, so its high cells are never trimmed. LG Chem's patent EP2730933A1 tests for this by switching the balancing circuit on and off and watching how the voltage across a diagnosis resistor reacts; no change means a dead circuit.",["Bleed resistor of group B burnt open","Balancing transistor of circuit B no longer switching","Open terminal or cell tap connection in group B"],["MIL and battery warning","Charging stops before the expected state of charge","High cell voltages in group B in live data"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB0","P0DB1","P0DB3","P0DAE"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DB3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit B Range/Performance","P0DB3 concerns how well circuit B works rather than whether it switches. The controller balances group B for a set time and then checks the outcome; if the cells moved too little, too much or in an unexpected direction, the result is called implausible.",["Controller software with too narrow expectations (cf. FCA updates for P0DAF)","Aged or high-resistance cell in group B reacting atypically","Bleed resistor value drifted so balancing current is wrong"],["MIL, sometimes without any noticeable drivability change","Cell spread in group B not shrinking after charging"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB0","P0DB1","P0DB2","P0DAF"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DB4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit C","P0DB4 belongs to the third balancing channel, C, and points to its hardware: an interrupted or shorted lead, a failed switch or a monitoring chip reporting nonsense for that cell group. A common test method sits in LG Chem's EP2730933A1, where a diagnosis resistor between cell and balancing circuit lets the controller see a defined voltage step each time the circuit is toggled;",["Fault in a lead shared by voltage sensing and balancing in group C","Cell monitoring chip of group C defective","Corroded module connector after water ingress"],["MIL with battery or hybrid warning","Further cell voltage codes stored alongside","Electric operation possibly reduced"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB5","P0DB6","P0DB7","P0DB0","P0DB8"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C"],P0DB5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit C Stuck On","Circuit C stuck on (P0DB5) means the cells of group C bleed energy permanently, also with the car switched off. Designers guard against exactly this: the LaunchPoint patent US8598840B2 lets every balance enable expire on a timer unless the central controller renews it, so a lost command cannot leave a switch on.",["Balancing transistor in circuit C failed closed","Enable or watchdog logic of the module not releasing the switch","Leakage path on the module board"],["MIL with battery warning, often after standing","Low cell voltage codes in group C as a follow-up","Electric range dropping"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB4","P0DB6","P0DB7","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DB6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit C Stuck Off","P0DB6 says that the bleed function of group C does not respond: commanded on, circuit C stays dead. In LG Chem's US20110285539A1 such a case shows up because a test capacitor charged to cell voltage is not emptied through the balancing path and keeps a high residual voltage. Typical culprits are an open resistor, a transistor that no longer conducts or a broken cell tap. While driving there is little to feel;",["Bleed resistor of circuit C open","Balancing transistor of circuit C not conducting","Broken cell tap or weld in group C"],["MIL with battery warning","Shorter charging sessions and lower usable capacity"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB4","P0DB5","P0DB7","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DB7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit C Range/Performance","P0DB7 covers a circuit C that works electrically but whose outcome does not add up. A typical trigger is a cell in group C that needs trimming far more often or far less often than its neighbours. Southwest Electronic Energy's patent US9097774B2 counts balancing events per cell and flags one whose count runs away, because that hints at an internal short. So this code can point at the cells as well as at the circuit.",["Cell in group C with rising self-discharge or an internal short","Controller software with unsuitable plausibility limits","Bleed resistor of circuit C out of tolerance"],["MIL, often with no other immediate effect","One cell in group C repeatedly lower than the rest"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB4","P0DB5","P0DB6","P0BBE"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DB8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit D","P0DB8 is the circuit code for balancing channel D. Here the controller sees a hardware problem: the switch, resistor or cell tap of group D reports a state that cannot be valid, typically an open lead or a short to a neighbouring line.",["Open balancing terminal or lead in group D","Short between neighbouring sense/balancing lines","Monitoring chip of group D defective"],["MIL and battery system message","Possible limitation of electric power"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB9","P0DBA","P0DBB","P0DB4","P0DBC"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ D"],P0DB9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit D Stuck On","A stuck-on circuit D (P0DB9) drains group D continuously. The usual check is simple: does the switch state reported back equal the state the controller asked for? GM applies this on the eAssist battery for a related code, sampling every 200 ms and logging a Type B fault after 7 mismatches in 10. Here the reported state stays on while the command says off.",["Balancing transistor of circuit D shorted","Status feedback of the module wrong, switch actually fine","Drive output of the monitoring chip stuck on"],["MIL with battery warning","Group D cells and their local temperature deviating","Loss of charge while parked"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DB8","P0DBA","P0DBB","P0DB5"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ D — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DBA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit D Stuck Off","P0DBA: circuit D refuses to bleed. Toshiba's patent US20130026994A1 lists an open balancing terminal as one of the faults a monitoring chip should report, and this is the typical root of a stuck-off result. Because no charge is taken away from the high cells in group D, the pack's top end is governed by them; the charger and the controller stop earlier and the displayed range creeps down.",["Open balancing terminal or cell tap in group D","Bleed resistor of circuit D open","Balancing transistor of circuit D failed open"],["MIL and battery warning","Range slowly decreasing, charging finishing early"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB8","P0DB9","P0DBB","P0DB6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ D — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DBB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit D Range/Performance","For P0DBB the controller judges the effect of balancing in circuit D as implausible. A clear reference is GM's eAssist key-off test for a related code: engaging the bleed load has to pull the cell at least 0.1 V away from its rest value. A result outside the expected window, even with intact wiring, is what a performance code captures.",["Cell in group D with changed internal resistance or capacity","Plausibility limits in the controller software too tight","Bleed resistor of circuit D drifted"],["MIL without obvious driving symptoms","Cell spread in group D persisting after long charging"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DB8","P0DB9","P0DBA","P0DB7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ D — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DBC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit E","P0DBC is set for an electrical fault in the fifth balancing channel, E: the controller cannot trust the path through which group E would be bled, because a lead, switch or resistor reads open, shorted or out of range.",["Open or shorted balancing lead in group E","Monitoring chip responsible for group E faulty","Module connector of group E loose or corroded"],["MIL and battery warning","Electric drive possibly restricted"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DBD","P0DBE","P0DBF","P0DB8","P0DC0"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E"],P0DBD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit E Stuck On","With P0DBD the controller finds circuit E active when it should be idle. LG Chem's EP2730933A1 offers a way to see it: with the balancing circuit commanded off, the voltage across the diagnosis resistor should match the idle pattern; if it looks like the active pattern, current is still flowing.",["Balancing transistor of circuit E shorted","Drive line of the switch held active by the monitoring chip","Leakage on the module board after moisture"],["MIL with battery warning","Power reduced as group E cells drop","Charge loss after a few days parked"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DBC","P0DBE","P0DBF","P0DB9"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DBE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit E Stuck Off","P0DBE means circuit E does nothing when told to bleed. The LaunchPoint patent US8598840B2 describes reading the cells while a planned bleed phase is running and treating a balancing side that shows no effect as faulted; this is the logic behind a stuck-off verdict. That patent also keeps a second balancing path in reserve, a reminder that some designs can carry on with reduced function.",["Bleed resistor of circuit E open","Balancing transistor of circuit E does not turn on","Enable signal for group E not reaching the module"],["MIL and battery warning","Apparent loss of capacity over weeks"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DBC","P0DBD","P0DBF","P0DBA"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DBF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit E Range/Performance","P0DBF tells that balancing in circuit E produces results the controller cannot explain, though the circuit switches. One reason can be a cell that keeps needing correction: the Southwest Electronic Energy patent US9097774B2 tracks how often each cell gets balanced and treats an outlier as a warning of internal shorting, well before overheating.",["Cell in group E with increased self-discharge","Software limits for the balancing check too narrow","Bleed resistor of circuit E out of tolerance"],["MIL, usually without immediate drivability change","Recurring deviation of a single cell in group E"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DBC","P0DBD","P0DBE","P0DBB"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DC0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit F","P0DC0 marks an electrical fault in balancing circuit F, the sixth cell group whose bleed path the battery controller can switch. Audi lists P0DC000 for the 2016 A3 e-tron in bulletin 93 17 04 (2046556/1), together with the circuit codes for groups A to H and B200000. Owners noticed that charging failed now and then or that the car claimed a charger was plugged in when none was.",["Battery controller software that starts CAN communication too late (Audi A3 e-tron, J840/JX1 update)","Open or chafed balancing/sense lead of cell group F","Defective cell monitoring chip serving group F"],["Charging fails intermittently","Cluster shows a connected charger although no cable is plugged in","Several balancing circuit codes stored at the same time"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC1","P0DC2","P0DC3","P0DBC","P0DC4"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F"],P0DC1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit F Stuck On","P0DC1 says that the bleed path of cell group F appears to stay engaged. Ford lists it for the BECM of its full and plug-in hybrids in the OBD system summaries of model years 2014 to 2019. The battery there is split into fourteen balancing circuits, A to N, each looking after six cells.",["Bleed transistor of group F shorted, so the resistor stays connected","Monitoring chip output keeps the switch of circuit F enabled","A cell of group F with high self-discharge mimicking a drained group"],["Check engine light with hybrid battery warning after a cold start","Cells of group F clearly below the pack average after standstill","Reduced electric range or assistance"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC0","P0DC2","P0DC3","P0DBD","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DC2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit F Stuck Off","With P0DC2 the controller concludes that circuit F does not bleed when asked to, so the fullest cells of group F are never trimmed. Ford's 2014 hybrid OBD summary applies the same 75 mV deviation test over the six cells of each circuit as for the stuck-on code, but the 2014 to 2018 versions want the deviation seen at two power-ups in a row, before the contactors close.",["Bleed resistor of group F burnt open","Bleed transistor of circuit F no longer conducting","Loose cell tap or connector pin on the balancing lead"],["Charging ends earlier than usual, displayed full charge gives less range","Cells of group F remain highest after charging","Check engine light, usually without noticeable driving change"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC0","P0DC1","P0DC3","P0DBE","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DC3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit F Range/Performance","P0DC3 is the plausibility code for balancing circuit F, and Volvo is a documented user. On the SPA Twin Engine plug-in hybrids (XC90, XC60, S90, V90, model years 2016-2019) the BECM stores P0DC300 as an intermittent fault, listed alongside the range/performance codes of circuits A to E.",["BECM software version with faulty balancing evaluation (Volvo TJ 33931)","Cells of group F with changed capacity respond differently to bleeding","Bleed resistor value drifted so the balancing current is off target"],["Displayed electric range fluctuates or is unreasonably low","Combustion engine runs more often than expected in hybrid mode","Check engine light, fault often stored as intermittent"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC0","P0DC1","P0DC2","P0DBF","P0DAF","P0B54"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DC4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit G","P0DC4 points to the hardware path of balancing circuit G: lead, switch, resistor or the chip that drives them. On the 2016 Audi A3 e-tron it appears as P0DC400 in bulletin 93 17 04, never alone but in a row with the circuit codes of groups A to H. When so many channels fail at once, a shared cause is far more likely than eight broken circuits;",["Common software or communication cause when circuit codes A to H appear together (Audi A3 e-tron)","Bleed FET of channel G failing the on-state check","Corroded or loose connector on the cell harness of group G"],["Hybrid battery warning with check engine light","External charging refused now and then","Scan tool lists several circuit codes plus a control module fault"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC5","P0DC6","P0DC7","P0DC0","P0DC8"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G"],P0DC5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit G Stuck On","P0DC5 reports that the bleed switch of group G seems to stay closed. Ford lists the code for the BECM of its 2018 hybrids and plug-ins; there, a sticking circuit is inferred at start-up, with contactors open, from any of its six cells straying more than 75 mV from the pack average. A more direct test can sit in the monitoring chip.",["Balancing switch of group G stuck in conduction","Enable signal from the controller or chip never withdrawn","Moisture or contamination forming a leakage path on the module board"],["Check engine light and hybrid battery warning","Low cell voltage codes may follow after longer parking","Electric range decreasing from day to day"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC4","P0DC6","P0DC7","P0DC1","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DC6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit G Stuck Off","P0DC6 is set when circuit G stays idle although balancing is requested, so group G cannot give up charge. Ford names the code in its 2015 hybrid OBD summary and decides it from the start-up voltage comparison of the six cells per circuit.",["Shunt resistor of group G open, no current can bypass the cells","Balancing switch of group G does not close on command","Pin backed out of the cell harness connector"],["Electric range shrinks gradually over weeks","Charging stops before the indicated target","Check engine light without immediate drivability change"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC4","P0DC5","P0DC7","P0DC2","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DC7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit G Range/Performance","P0DC7 means circuit G switches but its effect on the cells does not fit the controller's model. The code is not tied to passive bleeding. Ford's patent application US20120293129A1 describes active balancing, where charge taken from the highest cell is moved to weaker ones, for example by a switched capacitor or a flyback transformer, and the transfer time depends on the gap between highest and lowest cell.",["Controller software with too tight plausibility limits","Weak transfer stage in active balancing (capacitor or transformer aged)","Cells of group G aged so that they react differently to correction"],["Check engine light with hybrid system message","Cell spread in group G does not shrink after balancing","Usable battery capacity slightly reduced"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC4","P0DC5","P0DC6","P0DC3","P0DCB"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ G — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DC8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit H","P0DC8 covers an electrical fault of balancing circuit H. Audi's bulletin 93 17 04 for the 2016 A3 e-tron lists P0DC800 as the last of its circuit codes; no letter beyond H appears there, which suggests eight circuits in that pack. Since the fix was software that let CAN communication begin on time, the entry can also stem from the controller not hearing its cell electronics early enough.",["Cell electronics not answering in time after wake-up (Audi software fix)","Damaged internal bus or supply wiring to the monitoring board of group H","Open bleed path or lead in circuit H"],["Charging not possible at times","Hybrid battery warning and check engine light","Fault returns after clearing until software is updated"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC9","P0DCA","P0DCB","P0DC4","P0DCC"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H"],P0DC9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit H Stuck On","P0DC9: the bleed path of group H keeps conducting. Ford lists the code for its 2016 hybrid and plug-in BECM, judged at start-up from the cell spread within the six-cell circuit. Besides draining charge, a switch that cannot open turns energy into heat around the clock.",["Bleed FET of group H shorted and unaffected by thermal pause","Heat damage on the monitoring board around the bleed resistors of H","Chip logic keeping the balancing enable of channel H active"],["Check engine light and hybrid battery warning","Group H noticeably lower than the rest after parking","Locally raised temperature reading in the battery module"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DC8","P0DCA","P0DCB","P0DC5","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DCA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit H Stuck Off","P0DCA flags circuit H as inactive: balancing was ordered, but group H keeps its charge. Ford includes the code in its 2017 hybrid OBD summary, again decided by the start-up comparison of six cells per circuit with the pack average. Not every pause is a fault, which makes this code tricky.",["Bleed resistor of group H open","Broken cell tap or weld on the balancing lead of H","Intended stop (timer, thermal pause) wrongly judged as no response by the software"],["Full charge reached earlier, with less range than before","Cells of group H stay at the top of the cell list","Check engine light"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC8","P0DC9","P0DCB","P0DC6","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DCB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit H Range/Performance","P0DCB is raised when circuit H works electrically but the result of a balancing run does not match the plan. Ford's patent US9145064B2 shows how such a plan can be made: from each cell's capacity surplus and the pack charge current the controller computes how long that cell's resistor must stay active during charging so that all cells reach the target together.",["Wrong capacity or state-of-charge estimate for the cells of group H","Battery controller software (update first, as Volvo does for P0DC3)","Bleed resistor of H drifted from its nominal value"],["Check engine light, often only after charging sessions","Group H remains out of line after a full charge","Fluctuating range estimate"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DC8","P0DC9","P0DCA","P0DC7","P0DCF"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ H — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DCC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit I","P0DCC concerns the hardware of balancing circuit I, the ninth group, so only packs split into at least nine balancing channels can raise it. No maker publishes this exact code: Ford's hybrid summaries assign letter I only to the stuck codes P0DCD and P0DCE, and Audi's A3 e-tron bulletin stops at H.",["Broken balancing or sense lead of group I","Corroded connector between cell group I and its monitoring board","Defective monitoring chip for channel I"],["Hybrid battery warning with check engine light","Electric drive or charging limited by the battery controller"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DCD","P0DCE","P0DCF","P0DC8","P0DD0"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I"],P0DCD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit I Stuck On","P0DCD tells that circuit I is drained although no balancing is wanted. In Ford's 2014 hybrid OBD summary the code belongs to the start-up check with open contactors; that early version only sets it after the 75 mV spread within the six-cell group has shown up on two consecutive power-ups. A diagnosis must also know when a switch is on deliberately.",["Balancing switch of group I permanently conducting","Driver stage of the monitoring chip stuck active on channel I","Software leaving a deliberate switch activation (e.g. chip warming) active"],["Check engine light with hybrid battery warning at start-up","Group I lower than the rest after longer standstill","Shorter electric range"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DCC","P0DCE","P0DCF","P0DC9","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DCE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit I Stuck Off","P0DCE means the bleed path of group I does not respond to a balancing request. Ford lists it in the 2019 OBD summary for its full and plug-in hybrids, where the BECM discharges only the highest cells to match the rest and runs this check for 100 ms at power-up before the contactors close. Because the code concerns a switch that never conducts, nothing happens suddenly while driving.",["Open bleed resistor in circuit I","Balancing switch of group I that no longer closes","Cell tap of group I loose at the busbar"],["Electric range decreases over many charging cycles","Cells of group I highest at the end of charging","Check engine light without noticeable effect while driving"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DCC","P0DCD","P0DCF","P0DCA","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DCF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit I Range/Performance","P0DCF closes the series for the ninth balancing group: circuit I is electrically intact, yet balancing does not bring its cells to where the controller intended. No maker publishes this code. Ford's hybrid OBD summaries cover letter I only with the stuck codes P0DCD and P0DCE.",["Cells of group I aged differently, so the planned balancing time no longer fits","Battery controller calibration with an unsuitable target or time window","Bleed resistor of circuit I off its nominal value, giving too little current"],["Check engine light, typically noticed after charging","Group I stays apart from the other cells after a full charge","Slowly shrinking electric range"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DCC","P0DCD","P0DCE","P0DCB","P0DD3"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ I — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DD0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit J","P0DD0 reports an electrical fault in balancing circuit J, the tenth group. Only packs split into ten or more balancing channels can set it; Audi's A3 e-tron bulletin ends its circuit codes at H and Volvo's SPA Twin Engine journal at F, so those packs never reach J. No manufacturer documents P0DD0 publicly, and Ford uses J only for stuck faults.",["Open balancing or sense terminal of group J (lead, crimp or weld)","Loose or corroded connector between group J and its monitoring board","Faulty monitoring chip channel serving circuit J"],["Hybrid battery warning with check engine light","Charging or electric drive limited by the battery controller"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD1","P0DD2","P0DD3","P0DCC","P0DD4"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J"],P0DD1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit J Stuck On","P0DD1: group J's bleed path keeps drawing current after balancing should have ended. Ford's 2014 OBD summary for its hybrids and plug-ins assigns the code to the BECM, which checks at power-up, before the contactors close, for 100 ms: if one of the six cells in the circuit sits more than 75 mV away from the mean of all cells, and this repeats on two consecutive power cycles, the fault is stored.",["Balancing transistor of group J shorted and conducting permanently","Balancing enable for channel J not withdrawn by the monitoring electronics","Cell in group J with high self-discharge producing the same voltage pattern"],["Check engine light and hybrid battery warning after switching on","Group J clearly lower than the rest after parking","Shorter electric range"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD0","P0DD2","P0DD3","P0DCD","P0DD5","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DD2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit J Stuck Off","P0DD2 marks circuit J as unable to bleed: balancing is commanded, but the six cells of group J keep their charge. Ford lists the code in its 2015 hybrid OBD summary with the same power-up test as for the stuck-on case, run for 100 ms with the contactors still open, two consecutive power cycles and the 75 mV limit against the average cell voltage.",["Bleed resistor of circuit J open or burnt","Balancing switch of group J no longer closing","Interrupted balancing lead between cell tap and board"],["Check engine light without noticeable change in driving","Cells of group J at the top of the cell list at the end of charging","Usable battery capacity declining over time"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD0","P0DD1","P0DD3","P0DCE","P0DD6","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DD3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit J Range/Performance","P0DD3 is a plausibility verdict on circuit J: the switch reacts, but the effect of balancing on group J does not match what the controller expected. No maker documents this code publicly, so the reasoning rests on CARB and on how balancing is planned.",["Wrong charge estimate for the cells of group J","Battery controller software or calibration (check for updates first)","Balancing path of J with raised resistance, so less charge moves than planned"],["Check engine light, often only after several charges","Group J remains out of line despite balancing","Unsteady range prediction"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD0","P0DD1","P0DD2","P0DCF","P0DD7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ J — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DD4:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit K","P0DD4 is the electrical fault code for balancing circuit K, the eleventh group, so it only exists on packs with at least eleven balancing channels. Public maker documents for this exact code were not found; Ford's summaries give K only to the stuck faults P0DD5 and P0DD6.",["Open wire or loose cell tap in group K","Corrosion or moisture at the connector of the monitoring board for K","Defective monitoring chip or external balancing part of channel K"],["Check engine light and hybrid system warning","Missing or implausible cell voltages for group K in the scan tool","Reduced charging or drive power"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD5","P0DD6","P0DD7","P0DD0","P0DD8"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K"],P0DD5:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit K Stuck On","P0DD5 tells that circuit K is discharging its cells without a balancing request. Ford's 2016 hybrid OBD summary links the code to the BECM start-up check: before the contactors close it looks at the six cells of the circuit for 100 ms and fails it when one of them is more than 75 mV away from the average, confirmed on a second power cycle.",["Shorted balancing switch of group K","Heat damage or contamination bridging the balancing path of K on the board","Driver stage of the monitoring chip holding channel K on"],["Check engine light at start-up","Group K drops faster than the other cells while parked","Locally higher battery module temperature"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD4","P0DD6","P0DD7","P0DD1","P0DD9","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DD6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit K Stuck Off","P0DD6: group K's bleed path stays dead when balancing is called for. Ford includes it in its 2017 hybrid OBD summary; the BECM decides at power-up with open contactors, within 100 ms and over two power cycles, using the 75 mV deviation of one of the six cells from the average.",["Open bleed resistor in circuit K","Balancing switch of group K failing to turn on","Cracked solder joint in the balancing path of K"],["Check engine light while the car drives normally","Group K highest at the end of charging","Electric range decreasing over many charges"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD4","P0DD5","P0DD7","P0DD2","P0DDA","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DD7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit K Range/Performance","P0DD7 points to balancing of group K that works, but not within the expected limits. No maker publishes this code. A monitoring chip can deliver exactly this kind of finding: Infineon's TLE9012DQU checks every channel during active balancing for overcurrent and undercurrent through a sense resistor and switches balancing off when either threshold is crossed.",["Balancing current of K below the expected value (aged resistor, raised contact resistance)","Controller calibration with too short balancing time for group K","A cell in group K drifting faster than balancing can compensate"],["Check engine light, often after charging","Spread of group K returns soon after a balancing session","Fluctuating range estimate"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD4","P0DD5","P0DD6","P0DD3","P0DDB"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ K — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DD8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit L","P0DD8 concerns the hardware of balancing circuit L, the twelfth group. No manufacturer documents it publicly; Ford, whose hybrid BECM runs through letter N, uses L only in the stuck codes P0DD9 and P0DDA.",["Open resistor, switch or solder joint in circuit L","Poor connection of the cell taps of group L","Faulty voltage measuring channel next to circuit L"],["Hybrid battery warning and check engine light","Power or charging limited"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD9","P0DDA","P0DDB","P0DD4","P0DDC"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L"],P0DD9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit L Stuck On","P0DD9: the balancing path of group L will not switch off. The 2018 edition of Ford's hybrid OBD summary contains the code; the BECM catches it at power-up with the contactors still open, in a 100 ms check that compares each of the six cells of circuit L with the average and fails above 75 mV, again over two power cycles.",["Balancing switch of group L shorted","Balancing command for L not cleared by the monitoring electronics","Switch status feedback of channel L reporting on although the switch is open"],["Check engine light after switching on","Group L lower than the others after standing","Less electric range"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DD8","P0DDA","P0DDB","P0DD5","P0DDD","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DDA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit L Stuck Off","P0DDA says circuit L does not discharge when it is told to. In Ford's 2019 hybrid OBD summary the start-up test has been simplified: it still runs for 100 ms before the contactors close, but the requirement of two consecutive power cycles is gone, and the 75 mV deviation of a cell from the average must instead persist for 0.1 s. Ford's 2014 code list flags the code for the MIL.",["Balancing switch of group L stays open when commanded on","Bleed resistor of circuit L open","Missing gate drive for the balancing switch of L"],["Check engine light with no change in drivability","Group L reaches full charge before the rest","Gradual loss of usable capacity"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD8","P0DD9","P0DDB","P0DD6","P0DDE","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DDB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit L Range/Performance","P0DDB stands for balancing of group L whose result is out of range although the circuit answers. No maker documents P0DDB publicly. A patent by Southwest Electronic Energy, US9097774B2, adds an important angle: it counts how often each cell has to be balanced and flags a cell that needs it far more or less often than the others, since an internal short can be the reason.",["Cell in group L with an internal soft short, needing balancing again and again","Controller calibration of expected balancing effect for L","Drifted bleed resistor of circuit L"],["Check engine light, sometimes only after long parking","Group L drifting low between charges","Range estimate varying from day to day"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DD8","P0DD9","P0DDA","P0DD7","P0DDF","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ L — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DDC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit M","P0DDC reports an electrical fault of balancing circuit M, the thirteenth group, which only large packs with at least thirteen channels possess. No maker publishes this exact code; in Ford's BECM documents M appears only as stuck on (P0DDD) and stuck off (P0DDE).",["Broken sense or balancing wire of group M","Damaged or poorly seated connector at the board for M","Fault in the monitoring electronics of channel M"],["Check engine light and hybrid warning","Battery controller restricting power or charging"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DDD","P0DDE","P0DDF","P0DD8","P0DE0"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M"],P0DDD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit M Stuck On","P0DDD reports a balancing path of group M that keeps conducting. Ford's 2015 hybrid OBD summary places it in the BECM monitor, judged at power-up before the contactors close: 100 ms, six cells per circuit, more than 75 mV from the average, two consecutive power cycles. A properly working chip should never leave a channel on indefinitely.",["Shorted balancing switch of group M that no timer can stop","Damaged external balancing transistor on the board for M","High self-discharge cell in group M imitating a stuck switch"],["Check engine light at start-up","Group M noticeably low after overnight parking","Raised module temperature near group M"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DDC","P0DDE","P0DDF","P0DD9","P0DE1","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DDE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit M Stuck Off","P0DDE means group M is not bled when it should be. Ford carries the code in its 2016 hybrid OBD summary with the usual BECM start-up rule: 100 ms with open contactors, six cells per circuit, a 75 mV gap to the average, confirmed over two power cycles. A balancing channel can also be stopped on purpose, which a diagnosis must keep apart from a real failure.",["Bleed resistor of circuit M open","Balancing switch of group M that no longer turns on","Intended balancing stop for M misjudged as no response by the software"],["Check engine light while driving feels normal","Group M highest at the end of each charge","Less usable battery energy over time"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DDC","P0DDD","P0DDF","P0DDA","P0DE2","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DDF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit M Range/Performance","P0DDF covers group M when balancing responds but its outcome is not credible. No manufacturer publishes this code. Ford patent US9145064B2 shows what the expected outcome can be built on: the controller derives a bleed time per cell from that cell's surplus capacity and the charging current, so that all cells arrive at the target together at the end of charging.",["Stored capacity value for cells of group M out of date","Battery controller software (update before repair)","Bleed resistor of M drifted"],["Check engine light following charging sessions","Group M not aligned with the rest after full charge","Inconsistent range display"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DDC","P0DDD","P0DDE","P0DDB","P0DE3"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ M — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DE0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit N","P0DE0 flags a wiring or driver fault in balancing circuit N, the fourteenth and last letter of the series; the next code, P0DE4, belongs to a different system. Packs with more balancing groups than fourteen must therefore map several groups to one letter or use their own codes.",["Open balancing terminal or lead of group N","Corroded or loose connector at the last module board","Monitoring chip channel for N defective"],["Hybrid battery warning with check engine light","Electric operation or charging restricted"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DE1","P0DE2","P0DE3","P0DDC","P0DAC"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N"],P0DE1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit N Stuck On","P0DE1 is the last stuck-on code of the series: group N keeps bleeding when it should rest. Ford lists it in every hybrid OBD summary from 2014 to 2019; in the 2017 edition the BECM spends 100 ms after key-on, while the contactors are still open, checking whether any of the six cells of circuit N sits more than 75 mV from the average, with two consecutive power cycles needed.",["Balancing switch of group N permanently conducting","Monitoring electronics keeping the balancing command for N active","Cell of group N with an internal short causing the same voltage drop"],["Check engine light after switching on","Group N drops while the car is parked","Shorter electric range"],4,3,["P0DAB","P0DE0","P0DE2","P0DE3","P0DDD","P0DE6"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DE2:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit N Stuck Off","P0DE2 closes Ford's list of balancing codes: circuit N does not discharge when commanded. Ford's 2018 hybrid summary has the BECM judge this during a 100 ms window at key-on, contactors still open, comparing each of the six cells with the average against 75 mV over two power cycles. A switch that stays open has no immediate effect on driving, but the imbalance adds up.",["Open bleed resistor in circuit N","Balancing switch of group N not turning on","Cell tap of group N detached from the busbar"],["Check engine light without driving complaint","Charging ends early because group N is full first","Electric range shrinking over months"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DE0","P0DE1","P0DE3","P0DDE","P0DE7"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DE3:["Hybrid/EV Battery Cell Balancing Circuit N Range/Performance","P0DE3 is the final code of the balancing series and judges whether balancing of group N achieves a believable result. No maker documents it publicly. The Wikipedia article on battery balancing explains why such a check matters: unless the upper and lower cut-offs line up with the weakest cell, the energy the pack can take in and give back stays limited, so a mismatched group throttles the whole battery.",["Cells of group N aged unevenly","Controller estimates or calibration for group N off","Bleed path of N with too little current"],["Check engine light, mostly after charging","Group N never quite levels with the other groups","Reduced and unsteady range"],4,1,["P0DAB","P0DE0","P0DE1","P0DE2","P0DDF","P0DD3"],"מעגל איזון תא סוללת מתח גבוה‏ N — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0DE4:["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Over Temperature","P0DE4 reports that the electric motor of auxiliary transmission fluid pump A has run too hot. Such a pump keeps the transmission pressurised while the engine is stopped, either by start-stop or during electric driving in a hybrid. No manufacturer documents P0DE4 publicly.",["Low, aged or wrong transmission fluid making the pump work harder","Binding rotor or worn bearing in the pump motor","Transmission running hot (towing, heat, restricted fluid cooler)","Faulty temperature evaluation in the pump controller or outdated software"],["Start-stop no longer shuts the engine off","Electric driving limited, engine keeps running","Harsh engagement when pulling away after a stop","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,3,["P0C2A","P0C1B","P277D","P0C28","P0C26"],"מנוע משאבת שמן תיבה חשמלי/עזר‏ A — חום יתר"],P0DE5:["Charge Status Indicator B Control Circuit/Open","P0DE5 flags an interrupted drive circuit for charge status indicator B, a second lamp or LED that tells the owner whether the vehicle is charging, for instance one at the charge port next to the one on the dashboard. Which light gets the letter B is up to the maker. No manufacturer documents P0DE5 publicly.",["Broken wire or unplugged connector to indicator B","Burnt-out LED or failed indicator unit","Corroded contacts in the weather-exposed charge port area","Failed output driver in the controlling module"],["Charge status indicator B stays dark while charging","Charging works normally","Warning lamp possible depending on the manufacturer"],2,1,["P0E41","P0E42","P0D2C","P0E3F","P0E40"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0DE6:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cell Voltage Low","P0DE6 means the battery controller has measured at least one cell of the high-voltage pack below its lower voltage limit. It concerns a real reading, not the sensing lines (P0B3D and similar), and it looks at the weakest cell rather than the charge of the whole pack (P0A7D). Ford pairs it with P0A7D on 2020 Escape and Explorer hybrids drained by long standing or failed starts;",["Pack deeply discharged after long standing or repeated failed starts","Battery controller software judging too strictly (e.g. cold cranking)","Single weak cell with high self-discharge or lost capacity","Cell balancing circuit draining one cell"],["Vehicle will not start or switch to ready","Reduced power, no electric driving","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],4,3,["P0DE7","P0A7D","P0AFA","P0BBD","P0DAF","P0B3D"],"תא מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — מתח נמוך"],P0DE7:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cell Voltage High","P0DE7 is stored when a single cell of the high-voltage pack climbs above the upper voltage limit the battery controller permits. Overcharged lithium-ion cells age quickly and can become unsafe, so the controller usually restricts charging and regenerative braking until the cell is back in range.",["Battery controller software or calibration (documented by Ram)","Cell balancing no longer bleeding off the highest cell","Aged cell with raised internal resistance overshooting during regen","Drifting cell monitoring chip reporting too high a value"],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Weaker regenerative braking, charging limited","Mild-hybrid assist or start-stop unavailable"],4,3,["P0DE6","P0AFB","P0B3E","P0DAF","P0BBD"],"תא מארז סוללת מתח גבוה — מתח גבוה"],P0DE8:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Control Circuit/Open","P0DE8 reports an open or otherwise faulty drive circuit for coolant control valve C, a third switchable valve in the coolant loop of the hybrid or EV battery. What it switches depends on the layout, for example whether coolant passes through the radiator, the air-conditioning chiller or a heater, and the maker decides which valve is called C. No manufacturer documents P0DE8 publicly.",["Broken wire or loose connector at valve C","Open coil or motor winding inside valve C","Corroded pins from coolant or water ingress","Failed driver stage in the controlling module"],["Battery temperature control works only in a fallback mode","Charging or drive power reduced when the pack is too hot or cold","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,3,["P0DEB","P0DEC","P0DE9","P0E22","P0D1A","P0CDF"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0DE9:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Performance/Stuck Off","P0DE9 says coolant control valve C is electrically fine but does not do its job: it fails to reach the commanded position or remains in its off state. No manufacturer documents P0DE9 publicly.",["Valve body seized or sluggish from deposits","Worn actuator motor or gearing in valve C","Low coolant or air in the battery loop distorting the temperature check","Overly tight diagnostic calibration"],["Battery heats up faster under load or while fast charging","Charging power or drive power limited","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,1,["P0DEA","P0DE8","P0CE0","P0D1B","P0CE5"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — ביצועים לא תקינים או תקוע במצב כבוי"],P0DEA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Stuck On","P0DEA means coolant control valve C stays in its active position even though the controller has asked it to return. A wiring check cannot see this, because the circuit is intact; the controller needs a position feedback, as on GM's Volt valve with its own sensor codes, or it infers the state from coolant and pack temperatures that keep moving the wrong way. No manufacturer documents P0DEA publicly.",["Valve jammed in the active position by debris or wear","Driver stage that does not switch off","Software misreading the temperature response"],["Pack too cold in winter or too warm in summer","Reduced range or charging speed","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,1,["P0DE9","P0DE8","P0CE1","P0D1C","P0CE5"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — תקוע במצב פעיל"],P0DEB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Control Circuit Low","P0DEB is set when the drive line of coolant control valve C reads too low, which almost always means a short to ground somewhere between controller and valve. No manufacturer documents P0DEB publicly.",["Chafed wire shorting to body ground","Coolant or water in the valve connector","Internal short in the valve motor"],["Valve C no longer moves, temperature control in fallback mode","Charging or drive power reduced at temperature extremes","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,3,["P0DEC","P0DE8","P0E22","P0D1D","P0CE2"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0DEC:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Control Circuit High","P0DEC is the short-to-voltage case for coolant control valve C: its drive line reads high at a moment when the controller expects it low. No manufacturer documents P0DEC publicly. For valve A, GM's 2011 Volt checks driver line 1 for a high level while the valve should be stopped or moving forward, again needing 90 failures in 100 checks for a Type B code.",["Wire pinched against a supply line","Driver stage in the module not switching off","Moisture bridging pins in the connector"],["Valve C runs to an end position and stays there","Pack temperature drifts, power or charging limited","Malfunction indicator lamp on"],2,3,["P0DEB","P0DE8","P0E22","P0D1E","P0CE3","P0CE7"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0DED:["Pedestrian Alert Control Module Circuit/Open","P0DED points to an open or faulty hard-wired circuit between the powertrain side and the pedestrian alert control module, the unit that makes a quiet hybrid or EV audible at low speed. It is not a network fault, which is U013F, nor a speaker fault, which uses B0122 and B0123. No manufacturer documents P0DED publicly.",["Broken wire or loose connector at the alert module","Blown fuse or interrupted supply to the module","Internal fault of the pedestrian alert module"],["No artificial driving sound at low speed or in reverse","Pedestrian alert warning message in the instrument cluster"],2,0,["P0DEE","P0DEF","U013F","B0122","B0123"],""],P0DEE:["Pedestrian Alert Control Module Circuit Low","P0DEE is set when the hard-wired circuit to the pedestrian alert control module sits below its expected voltage, typically from a short to ground. No manufacturer documents P0DEE publicly, so the exact signal behind it, whether supply, wake-up or a command line, is not known in detail.",["Chafed or crushed wire shorting to ground","Water ingress in a connector near bumper or wheel arch","Internal short in the alert module"],["Pedestrian warning sound missing","Warning message in the instrument cluster","Fuse for the alert module may be blown"],2,0,["P0DEF","P0DED","U013F","B0122"],""],P0DEF:["Pedestrian Alert Control Module Circuit High","P0DEF reports a voltage above the permitted window on the wired link to the pedestrian alert control module, most often because a wire touches a supply line or the line has lost its ground reference. No manufacturer documents P0DEF publicly.",["Wire pinched against a 12 V supply line","Loose or corroded ground connection of the module","Internal fault of the alert module or its driver"],["Pedestrian sound missing or sounding at the wrong time","Warning message in the instrument cluster","Possible 12 V battery drain while parked"],2,0,["P0DEE","P0DED","U013F","B0123"],""],P0DF0:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0DF0 is the catch-all circuit code for temperature sensor B in the generator inverter, the power stage that drives the generator of a two-machine hybrid. The controller sets it when the sensor line is electrically faulty but the fault is not sorted into low, high or erratic. No manufacturer documents P0DF0 publicly.",["Loose, corroded or damaged connector at the inverter or power unit","Thermistor or its signal path inside the inverter module faulty","Faulty evaluation input in the generator control unit"],["Hybrid or engine warning light on","Reduced generator output, weaker charging while driving","Hybrid system may switch to a restricted mode"],4,3,["P0DF1","P0DF4","P0DF5","P0DF6","P0DF2","P0BCC","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0DF1:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0DF1 means the signal of generator inverter temperature sensor B drops out or jumps for short moments and then looks normal again. Typical triggers are temperature steps faster than the power module could physically heat or cool, or brief readings that resemble an open line.",["Loose or fretted terminal in the inverter connector","Harness chafing or vibration damage near the power unit","Intermittent contact inside the inverter module"],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional short loss of generator power","Fault often not reproducible at the workshop"],3,3,["P0DF0","P0DF4","P0DF5","P0DF6","P0DF3","P0BD0"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0DF2:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit","P0DF2 covers an electrical fault on temperature sensor C of the generator inverter that the controller cannot assign to a more specific low, high or intermittent code. Which power switch carries sensor C is up to the manufacturer; in Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV the third high-side IGBT sensor sits on the W phase, and its faults are logged only as P0DF7 to P0DF9. No manufacturer documents P0DF2 itself.",["Thermistor inside the IGBT module defective","Connector or signal line fault between module and controller","Faulty input stage in the generator control unit"],["Hybrid or engine warning light on","Generator power reduced as a precaution","Higher engine use or weaker battery recharge"],4,3,["P0DF3","P0DF7","P0DF8","P0DF9","P0DF0","P0BCC","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0DF3:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0DF3 is logged when sensor C in the generator inverter delivers an unsteady signal: short spikes, gaps or values that change faster than the module temperature ever could. Such faults often start at connectors that loosen under engine vibration and repeated heating cycles, or at pins attacked by moisture.",["Terminal losing contact under vibration or heat cycles","Moisture or corrosion in the inverter connector","Intermittent connection inside the inverter module"],["Warning light that appears only now and then","Brief drops in generator power","Code stored as history fault without current symptoms"],3,3,["P0DF2","P0DF7","P0DF8","P0DF9","P0DF1","P0BD0"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ C — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0DF4:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0DF4 reports that temperature sensor B of the generator inverter delivers an electrically valid signal whose value does not fit the other sensors. Mitsubishi documents the check for the Outlander PHEV: a single time in each drive cycle, following at least 600 s of standstill and with the IGBTs switched off, the V-phase high-side sensor may not differ by more than 47 °C from the other two phase sensors for 0.1 s;",["Control software misdetection (Volvo T8, ECM update available)","Sensor in the power module drifted or stuck at one value","Internal fault in the power drive unit (Mitsubishi replaces the unit)"],["Message such as Propulsion System Service Required","Engine warning light on","Turtle symbol and reduced power in some cases"],4,3,["P0DF5","P0DF6","P0DF0","P0DF1","P0DF7","P0BCD","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0DF5:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0DF5 sets when the signal voltage of generator inverter temperature sensor B stays below its valid window. Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV checks this continuously: if the V-phase high-side IGBT sensor reads at most 1.25 V during 0.08 s while the generator control unit has at least 8 V supply, P0DF5-00 is stored.",["Signal line shorted to ground","Shorted thermistor inside the IGBT module","Internal fault of the power drive unit"],["Engine or hybrid warning light on","Generator output limited, engine runs more often","Displayed inverter temperature implausibly high"],4,3,["P0DF6","P0DF4","P0DF0","P0DF1","P0DF8","P0BCE","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0DF6:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0DF6 is the high-signal case for sensor B in the generator inverter. In the Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi stores P0DF6-00 when the V-phase high-side IGBT sensor voltage reaches at least 3.75 V during 0.08 s, again only with a GCU supply of at least 8 V. A voltage at the upper limit mostly points to an interrupted sensor line, a lost reference ground or a cracked element;",["Open signal line or backed-out terminal","Lost sensor reference ground","Cracked sensor element or internal fault of the power drive unit"],["Engine or hybrid warning light on","Inverter temperature shown far too low","Generator power reduced"],4,3,["P0DF5","P0DF4","P0DF0","P0DF1","P0DF9","P0BCF"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0DF7:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","P0DF7 is the plausibility code for temperature sensor C of the generator inverter. The idea behind it: after a long rest every power switch should be at roughly the same temperature, so a sensor that stands out is suspect. In Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV the W-phase high-side sensor is compared once per trip, after 600 s or more of soak and with the IGBTs off, against the U- and V-phase sensors;",["Sensor C drifted or stuck inside the IGBT module","Internal fault of the power drive unit","Control software misjudging the comparison"],["Engine warning light on after a cold start","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Possibly reduced generator power"],4,3,["P0DF8","P0DF9","P0DF2","P0DF3","P0DF4","P0BCD","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0DF8:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Low","P0DF8 tells that temperature sensor C in the generator inverter delivers a voltage below its valid range. Mitsubishi uses the code in the Outlander PHEV for the W-phase high-side IGBT sensor: a reading at or under 1.25 V for 0.08 s, with at least 8 V GCU supply, stores P0DF8-00, and the check runs all the time.",["Signal line of sensor C shorted to ground","Shorted sensor or internal fault of the power drive unit","Coolant or moisture inside the inverter electronics"],["Hybrid or engine warning light on","Generator power limited","Implausibly high inverter temperature in live data"],4,3,["P0DF9","P0DF7","P0DF2","P0DF3","P0DF5","P0BCE","P0A3E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0DF9:["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit High","P0DF9 is stored when the C temperature sensor of the generator inverter reports a voltage at or beyond the upper end of its window. For the Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi defines the limit as 3.75 V for 0.08 s on the W-phase high-side IGBT sensor, monitored continuously whenever the GCU has at least 8 V. An open line, a backed-out terminal in the inverter connector or a broken sensor element are the usual explanations.",["Open signal line of sensor C","Terminal pushed back in the inverter connector","Broken sensor element inside the power drive unit"],["Engine or hybrid warning light on","Inverter temperature stuck at a very low value","Generator output held back"],4,3,["P0DF8","P0DF7","P0DF2","P0DF3","P0DF6","P0BCF"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר גנרטור‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0DFA:["Generator Phase U-V-W Current Sensor Correlation","In a three-phase machine the currents of phases U, V and W add up to nearly zero at every instant, so a sum far from zero shows that at least one current sensor is wrong. P0DFA is the code for this cross-check on the generator side. Mitsubishi documents it for the Outlander PHEV: with the IGBTs switching, the summation check must not report a failure, and with them off the sum must stay below 140 A;",["12 V voltage drop at key-on (Ford SSM 50151)","One phase current sensor with offset or gain error","Internal fault of the inverter or power drive unit"],["Stop Safely Now or hybrid system warning","Vehicle may not start or ready mode is refused","Generator shut down, restricted driving"],4,3,["P0E00","P0E01","P0E04","P0E08","P0BFD","P0A71","P0A74","P0A77"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ U-V-W — חוסר התאמה בין האותות"],P0DFB:["Generator Position Exceeding Learn Limit","The position sensor of a hybrid generator, normally a resolver, is never mounted exactly in line with the magnetic poles of the rotor. The remaining angle error is determined once and saved in the controller;",["Offset learning interrupted or run under wrong conditions","Resolver rotor or stator shifted after repair or damage","Fault or wrong software in the generator control unit"],["Hybrid system warning and engine warning light","Generator not used, reduced charging or engine start problems","Code returns after clearing until relearned"],4,3,["P0DFC","P0A4B","P0A4C","P0C64","P0C65","P0C17"],""],P0DFC:["Generator Position Sensor Not Learned","P0DFC is stored when the controller has no valid learned position offset for the generator resolver. Ford explains it in the OBD summary of its power-split hybrids: the code appears when the resolver was not configured at the assembly plant or after the inverter system controller has been replaced, and the check fails if the value held in EEPROM is outside its accepted range.",["Inverter or hybrid controller replaced without resolver learning","Software fault in the inverter generator module (Volvo, update available)","Stored offset lost or corrupted in EEPROM"],["Engine warning light on","Generator or hybrid functions restricted","Sometimes only stored as history code without symptoms"],4,3,["P0DFB","P0C17","P0A4B","P0C64","P0A50"],"חיישן מצב גנרטור — לא בוצע לימוד"],P0DFD:["Battery Charger Coolant Control Valve Control Circuit/Open","P0DFD flags an open or otherwise faulty drive circuit of the valve that controls coolant flow through the on-board charger. Such a valve lets the thermal system send coolant to the charger during AC charging and bypass it while driving. No manufacturer documents P0DFD publicly.",["Broken wire or unplugged connector at the valve","Open coil or motor winding in the valve","Faulty output stage in the controlling module"],["Charging slower than usual or interrupted","Charging or service message","Driving usually unaffected"],2,0,["P0DFE","P0DFF","P0CDF","P0D1A","P0DE8"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מטען סוללה — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0DFE:["Battery Charger Coolant Control Valve Control Circuit Low","P0DFE means the control line of the charger coolant valve reads low, usually because it is shorted to ground somewhere between driver and valve. No manufacturer documents P0DFE publicly. GM's 2011 Volt tables show how such a check works on battery coolant valve A (P0CE2): with system voltage above 9 V and the valve commanded in reverse, a low sense level on drive 1 counts as a failure, and the code needs a fault in…",["Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground","Coolant or water in the valve connector","Internal short in the valve actuator"],["Reduced charging power, longer charging time","Charger warning or charging stops","Driving normally unaffected"],2,0,["P0DFD","P0DFF","P0CE2","P0D1D","P0DEB"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0DFF:["Battery Charger Coolant Control Valve Control Circuit High","P0DFF is the short-to-voltage case for the valve that cools the on-board charger. The controller sees the drive line high while commanding it low. No manufacturer documents P0DFF publicly. The closest published logic is GM's 2011 Volt monitor for battery coolant valve A (P0CE3).",["Control wire shorted to a supply voltage","Output stage in the controlling module will not switch off","Internal fault in the valve actuator"],["Charging slower or charger warning","Valve audibly running to its end stop","Driving normally unaffected"],2,0,["P0DFE","P0DFD","P0CE3","P0D1E","P0DEC"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E00:["Generator Phase U Current Sensor Circuit","P0E00 is the general circuit code for the current sensor in phase U of the generator. Each phase sensor, usually a Hall-type device inside the inverter, outputs a voltage around a mid-point when no current flows. No manufacturer documents P0E00 publicly.",["Supply or reference fault of the current sensor","Defective phase U current sensor inside the inverter","Faulty internal connection between sensor and control board"],["Hybrid system warning and engine warning light","Generator switched off, reduced performance","Vehicle may not reach ready mode"],4,3,["P0E01","P0E02","P0E03","P0E04","P0E08","P0DFA","P0BE5"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ U — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E01:["Generator Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0E01 flags an implausible zero point of the U-phase current sensor inside the generator inverter. Sensors of this kind put out a voltage near the middle of their span while no current flows, so the test is only run when the machine cannot carry current.",["Zero point of the phase U current sensor drifted (sensor inside the power drive unit)","Unstable sensor supply or reference voltage inside the inverter","Evaluation circuit of the generator control unit faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator output reduced, less charging while driving","Code often returns after clearing at the next standstill check"],4,3,["P0E00","P0E02","P0E03","P0E05","P0E09","P0DFA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ U — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E02:["Generator Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0E02 is stored when the output of the generator inverter's phase U current sensor falls to the bottom of its range, a level no real current would produce. Mitsubishi uses it on the Outlander PHEV: from model year 2023 the generator control unit, supplied with at least 8 V, sets P0E02-00 once the U-phase signal drops to 0.5 V or lower and stays there for 0.2 s; unlike the zero-point test, no standstill is needed.",["Sensor signal shorted to ground or sensor output failed low","Open signal or supply connection of the sensor inside the power drive unit","Input stage of the generator control unit faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator power limited or generator not used","Battery charge falls faster in hybrid operation"],4,3,["P0E00","P0E01","P0E03","P0E06","P0E0A"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ U — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E03:["Generator Phase U Current Sensor Circuit High","P0E03 is the upper-limit fault of the current sensor that measures phase U of the generator inverter: its signal is pinned near the supply rail. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV, Mitsubishi lets the generator control unit set P0E03-00 when the U-phase voltage climbs to at least 4.5 V and remains there for 0.2 s with the unit supplied by at least 8 V;",["Sensor signal shorted to its supply voltage","Current sensor in the power drive unit failed with high output","Lost sensor ground or reference inside the inverter"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator switched off or strongly limited","Engine runs more often without charging the battery"],4,3,["P0E00","P0E01","P0E02","P0E07","P0E0B","P0A71"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ U — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E04:["Generator Phase V Current Sensor Circuit","P0E04 is the general circuit code for the generator inverter's V-phase current sensor, meant for an electrical fault that is not narrowed down to offset, low or high. No manufacturer documents P0E04 publicly.",["Electrical fault in the V-phase sensor circuit inside the inverter","Poor contact at the inverter or generator control unit connector","Generator control unit faulty"],["Hybrid or engine warning light on","Reduced generator output"],4,3,["P0E05","P0E06","P0E07","P0E00","P0E08","P0DFA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ V — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E05:["Generator Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0E05 reports an implausible zero point of the current sensor in phase V of the generator inverter. Mitsubishi describes it only for the 2023 Outlander PHEV, the first of its generator units with a sensor in phase V; the earlier model measured just U and W. The test mirrors that of the other phases and runs at rest: IGBTs off, generator below 20 rpm, control unit fed with at least 8 V.",["Zero point of the phase V current sensor drifted","Disturbed sensor supply or reference in the power drive unit","Generator control unit evaluation faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Possibly also the phase sum code P0DFA stored","Less generator support during hybrid driving"],4,3,["P0E04","P0E06","P0E07","P0E01","P0E09","P0DFA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ V — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E06:["Generator Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0E06 means the phase V current sensor of the generator inverter delivers a signal at the floor of its range. The 2023 Outlander PHEV is the only documented user: Mitsubishi's generator control unit, running on 8 V or more, stores P0E06-00 when the V-phase voltage remains at 0.5 V or below for 0.2 s, and the MIL comes on.",["V-phase sensor signal shorted to ground or sensor failed low","Open sensor signal or supply inside the power drive unit","Input of the generator control unit faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator power limited"],4,3,["P0E04","P0E05","P0E07","P0E02","P0E0A"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ V — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E07:["Generator Phase V Current Sensor Circuit High","P0E07 is set when the phase V current sensor in the generator inverter reports a signal stuck high. Mitsubishi's 2023 Outlander PHEV has its generator control unit flag P0E07-00 as soon as the V-phase voltage is at 4.5 V or above for 0.2 s while its own supply is at least 8 V; MIL and hybrid warning both illuminate.",["V-phase sensor signal shorted to its supply","Common sensor supply in the power drive unit too high or faulty","Current sensor failed with high output"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator switched off or limited","Other phase current sensor codes stored at the same time"],4,3,["P0E04","P0E05","P0E06","P0E03","P0E0B"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ V — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E08:["Generator Phase W Current Sensor Circuit","P0E08 covers an electrical fault of the generator inverter's W-phase current sensor that falls outside the offset, low and high categories. No manufacturer documents P0E08 publicly. Unlike phase V, the W-phase sensor exists on both Outlander PHEV generations Mitsubishi describes: the 2018 model uses it as current sensor 2, the 2023 model as one of three, yet both log its faults only as P0E09, P0E0A and P0E0B.",["Electrical fault in the W-phase sensor circuit inside the inverter","Loose or corroded connector at the power drive unit","Generator control unit faulty"],["Hybrid or engine warning light on","Reduced generator output, battery charged less while driving"],4,3,["P0E09","P0E0A","P0E0B","P0E00","P0E04","P0DFA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ W — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E09:["Generator Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0E09 is the zero-point check of the phase W current sensor in the generator inverter. Mitsubishi uses it on two Outlander PHEV generations. The 2018 code chart lists it as an offset fault of current sensor 2, the second of only two generator current sensors.",["Zero point of the phase W current sensor drifted","Sensor supply or reference in the power drive unit unstable","Evaluation in the generator control unit faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Rough or reduced generator operation","Fault reappears at the next standstill check after clearing"],4,3,["P0E08","P0E0A","P0E0B","P0E01","P0E05","P0DFA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ W — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E0A:["Generator Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Low","With P0E0A the phase W current sensor of the generator inverter reports a signal at or near zero volts. Mitsubishi covers this code in its Outlander PHEV service information: the 2018 chart attributes it to a short to ground or an open circuit of current sensor 2, and in the 2023 model the generator control unit sets P0E0A-00 after the W-phase voltage has stayed at 0.5 V or lower for 0.2 s with at least 8 V supply,…",["W-phase sensor signal shorted to ground or sensor failed low","Open sensor signal or supply inside the power drive unit","Input stage of the generator control unit faulty"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator largely out of operation","Battery is charged noticeably less while driving"],4,3,["P0E08","P0E09","P0E0B","P0E02","P0E06"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E0B:["Generator Phase W Current Sensor Circuit High","P0E0B marks a phase W current sensor signal in the generator inverter that sits at the top of its range. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV, Mitsubishi's generator control unit records P0E0B-00 when the W-phase voltage stays at no less than 4.5 V over a period of 0.2 s with a supply of at least 8 V, and turns on the MIL; the 2018 code chart names a short to the supply or a short circuit of current sensor 2.",["W-phase sensor signal shorted to its supply voltage","Current sensor in the power drive unit failed with high output","Lost sensor ground or reference inside the inverter"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator switched off or limited"],4,3,["P0E08","P0E09","P0E0A","P0E03","P0E07","P0E0C"],"חיישן זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E0C:["Generator Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0E0C concerns the three power phases between generator inverter and generator, not the current sensors. The J2012 title fits the use Mitsubishi documents for the 2023 Outlander PHEV under its own label of generator current deviation: with at least 50 A commanded, the generator between 500 and 4,500 rpm and 8 V supply, a U, V or W current stuck within about 17 A either side of zero for 0.4 s, or a failed comparison…",["Loose or burnt high-voltage phase terminal between inverter and generator","Phase cable or generator winding interrupted","Power drive unit does not drive one phase (switch or driver fault)"],["MIL and hybrid system warning light on","Generator no longer charges, engine runs without series hybrid support","Possible noise or vibration from the generator under load"],4,3,["P0E0D","P0E0E","P0DFA","P0C05","P0A6F","P0A72","P0A75"],"פאזת גנרטור‏ U-V-W — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0E0D:["Generator Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0E0D is the low member of the generator phase circuit group: a U, V or W line between inverter and generator is pulled toward the negative high-voltage rail or to ground, for instance through damaged insulation or a lower power switch that conducts when it should not. No manufacturer documents P0E0D publicly.",["Phase cable or winding insulation damaged, leakage to ground","Lower IGBT of one phase leaking or shorted","Phase voltage evaluation in the inverter faulty"],["Hybrid system warning light, possibly with insulation fault message","Generator shut down"],4,3,["P0E0C","P0E0E","P0C06","P0A6F","P0A72","P0A75"],"פאזת גנרטור‏ U-V-W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E0E:["Generator Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0E0E reports a generator phase line that reads high: one of the U, V or W connections is held near the positive high-voltage potential, typically by an upper power switch that stays on or by a short to the positive rail. No manufacturer documents P0E0E publicly;",["Upper IGBT of one phase shorted or not switching off","Phase line shorted to the positive high-voltage rail","Phase monitoring in the inverter faulty"],["Hybrid system warning light and MIL on","Generator shut down, no charging while driving"],4,3,["P0E0C","P0E0D","P0C07","P0A71","P0A74","P0A77"],"פאזת גנרטור‏ U-V-W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E0F:["Generator Inverter Power Supply A Circuit/Open","P0E0F is set when the generator inverter loses one of its internal supplies, supply A, or the line to it is interrupted. Which supply carries the letter A is up to the manufacturer; usually it is the low-voltage feed of the gate drivers that switch the power transistors. No manufacturer documents P0E0F publicly.",["Gate driver supply inside the inverter failed","12 V feed, fuse or connector of the inverter interrupted","Deep 12 V dips during start-up"],["Hybrid system warning light and MIL on","Generator out of operation, vehicle may not reach ready state"],4,3,["P0E10","P0E11","P2CC5","P0C0B"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר גנרטור‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0E10:["Generator Inverter Power Supply A Circuit Low","P0E10 means supply A of the generator inverter is present but too low. Ford lists the code in the HEV OBD system summary for its power-split hybrids (OBDSM1901): in the paragraph on the gate signal lines of motor and generator, P0E10 appears with P0BCE and P0BCF among the generator-side open-circuit faults, while the motor side is covered by P0C0C.",["Gate drive supply or gate signal path in the inverter faulty (Ford)","Low 12 V supply to the inverter, e.g. weak battery or poor ground","Corroded or loose inverter connector"],["Hybrid system warning light and MIL on","Generator limited or switched off, reduced hybrid performance"],4,3,["P0E0F","P0E11","P2CC6","P0C0C","P0BCE","P0BCF"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר גנרטור‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E11:["Generator Inverter Power Supply A Circuit High","P0E11 is stored when supply A of the generator inverter is above its permitted range. Overvoltage on the gate driver feed can overstress the control inputs of the power transistors, so controllers usually react by blocking the inverter. No manufacturer documents P0E11 publicly.",["Voltage regulator of the gate driver supply in the inverter faulty","Short between two internal supply rails","Overvoltage in the 12 V system (DC/DC converter or charging fault)"],["Hybrid system warning light and MIL on","Generator blocked, no charging while driving"],4,3,["P0E0F","P0E10","P2CC7","P0C0D"],"אספקת מתח אינוורטר גנרטור‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E12:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater A Control Circuit/Open","P0E12 concerns the command circuit of battery coolant heater A, the electric element that warms the coolant sent to the hybrid or EV battery when the pack is too cold for charging or full power. The controller flags it when this circuit is open or otherwise electrically faulty. No manufacturer documents P0E12 publicly.",["Broken or chafed wire in the heater A command circuit","Loose, corroded or unlocked connector at the heater or controller","Open circuit inside the heater A assembly","Failed output driver in the controlling module"],["Check engine light on","Slow or reduced charging in cold weather","Engine runs more often than usual on cold days","Reduced electric power until the pack warms up"],3,3,["P0E13","P0E14","P0E15","P0E16","P0C47"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E13:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater A Control Circuit Low","P0E13 is the low-signal case on the command line of battery coolant heater A: the controller reads the line near ground at a moment when it expects a higher level, which points to a short to ground or a driver that cannot pull the circuit up. No manufacturer documents P0E13 publicly.",["Command wire shorted to ground, e.g. chafed against the body","Moisture or coolant in the heater connector","Internal short in the heater's control input","Output driver in the controller stuck low"],["Check engine light on","Battery heater does not work at low temperatures","Longer charging times in winter"],3,3,["P0E12","P0E14","P0E15","P0E17","P0C48"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E14:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater A Control Circuit High","P0E14 is logged when heater A's control wire sits too high, typically because it touches a supply voltage or because a pulled-up input has lost its connection. No manufacturer documents P0E14 publicly; GM's 2016 Volt reports open, ground and voltage faults of its heater transistor together as P1EC3 (Type B).",["Command wire shorted to a supply or neighbouring live wire","Open connection on a pulled-up heater input reading as high","Output driver in the controller that will not switch off"],["Check engine light on","Heater shut down by its own overheat protection","Battery heating unavailable or erratic in cold weather"],3,3,["P0E12","P0E13","P0E15","P0E18","P0C49"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E15:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater A Control Performance","P0E15 means heater A can be addressed electrically but does not deliver the heat it was asked for. Stellantis uses the code on Chrysler Pacifica plug-in hybrids from 2017 onward and the 2021–2023 Jeep Wrangler 4xe for the high-voltage electric coolant heater (ECH), a LIN-controlled unit that on these models heats the cabin loop. The ECH locks itself after low or high voltage, lost communication or overheating;",["Low coolant, leaks or trapped air in the heater loop","Hybrid control software that needs an update (Stellantis HCP/AHCP)","Heater unit permanently locked or internally failed","Faulty auxiliary coolant pump or restricted hoses","High- or low-voltage supply fault or LIN bus problem at the heater"],["Check engine light on","Message that electric mode is unavailable, engine keeps running","Little or no heat from the electric heater","Permanent code may block an emissions inspection"],3,3,["P0E12","P0E13","P0E14","P0E19","P0C4A"],"בקרת גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E16:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater B Control Circuit/Open","P0E16 is the open-circuit code for a second battery coolant heater, B. Why a car has two: some thermal systems pair the pack heater with another heater, for example one for the cabin loop, and can route its heat to the battery. A patent from Magna E-Car describes exactly that fallback when the battery pack heater fails, so the letter depends on how the maker splits the heaters.",["Broken wire in the heater B command circuit","Loose or corroded connector at heater B","Open element or electronics inside heater B"],["Check engine light on","Battery warms up slowly in cold weather","Reduced charging power at low temperatures"],3,3,["P0E17","P0E18","P0E19","P0E12"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E17:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater B Control Circuit Low","P0E17 reports that the command signal to battery coolant heater B stays too low, most often from a short to ground between controller and heater. No manufacturer documents P0E17 publicly. Some designs give the second heater a different job, such as warming the cabin loop, and only borrow it for the pack when needed, as a Magna E-Car patent outlines;",["Heater B command wire shorted to ground","Water or coolant in the heater B connector","Internal short at the control input of heater B"],["Check engine light on","Second heater does not support battery warm-up","Slower charging in winter"],3,3,["P0E16","P0E18","P0E19","P0E13"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E18:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater B Control Circuit High","P0E18 says the command line of battery coolant heater B reads high when the controller expects it low, for example from contact with a supply wire or an interrupted pulled-up input. No manufacturer documents P0E18 publicly. The risk here is a heater that cannot be switched off. Many coolant heaters therefore carry their own guard;",["Heater B command wire shorted to a voltage source","Controller driver stuck on","Interrupted pulled-up input at heater B"],["Check engine light on","Heater B switched off by its overheat protection","Battery heating unavailable in cold weather"],3,3,["P0E16","P0E17","P0E19","P0E14"],"גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E19:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Heater B Control Performance","P0E19 means heater B is commanded and electrically intact but the expected warming does not follow. No manufacturer documents P0E19 publicly. How such a test works is visible in GM's 2016 Volt, where the battery heater performance code P1EC6 runs once per drive or charge: during an active heat request the pack inlet coolant must gain more than 3 °C within 154.1 s, otherwise a Type B fault is stored;",["Air pocket or low coolant level in the battery loop","Heater B element producing too little heat","Weak pump, restricted hose or coolant valve in the wrong position","Inlet coolant temperature sensor reading wrongly"],["Check engine light on","Battery stays cold for a long time after a cold start","Reduced charging and drive power in winter"],3,3,["P0E16","P0E17","P0E18","P0E15","P0C4A"],"בקרת גוף חימום נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E1A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump B Control Circuit/Open","P0E1A reports an open or otherwise faulty command circuit for battery coolant pump B, a second electric pump found where the battery loop is split or a separate circuit feeds a chiller or heater. No manufacturer documents P0E1A publicly. For pump A, GM's 2019 tables show how the controller judges such a line by current: while running, below 200 mA or above 3 A is a fault, while off, below 120 µA;",["Broken wire in the pump B command circuit","Loose or corroded connector at pump B","Failed electronics inside pump B","Failed pump output in the controlling module"],["Check engine light on","Battery or electronics temperatures rise faster under load","Reduced power or charging rate"],2,3,["P0E1B","P0E1C","P0E1D","P0E1E","P0C47","P2CF4","U0695"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E1B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump B Control Circuit Low","P0E1B narrows a pump B command fault down to a level below the expected window, normally because the control or PWM wire has a ground fault, or because a driver that cannot raise it. No manufacturer documents P0E1B publicly.",["Pump B control or PWM wire shorted to ground","Coolant or water in the pump connector","Control module software fault, as seen by Ford on the pump A sibling","Internal short at the pump B control input"],["Check engine light on","Pump B does not run","Power limited when the components it cools get hot"],2,3,["P0E1A","P0E1C","P0E1D","P0C48","P2CF5"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E1C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump B Control Circuit High","P0E1C is set when the command signal to battery coolant pump B stays above what the controller allows, for instance because the line is shorted to battery voltage or, on a pulled-up PWM input, interrupted. No manufacturer documents P0E1C publicly.",["Pump B control wire shorted to battery voltage","Interrupted pulled-up PWM input at the pump","Control module software fault, as Ford found for pump A"],["Check engine light on","Pump B runs constantly or not at all","Reduced power under heavy load"],2,3,["P0E1A","P0E1B","P0E1D","P0C49","P2CF6"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E1D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump B Control Performance","P0E1D says pump B accepts its command electrically but the effect the controller looks for does not appear, such as the expected flow, speed or temperature change in its loop. No manufacturer documents P0E1D publicly. The model is pump A on the 2011 Chevrolet Volt: once the pump has run for over 60 s, the pack inlet coolant temperature must have shifted by at least 5 °C, otherwise a Type B code is set.",["Air trapped in the loop after a coolant service","Worn pump B or debris blocking its impeller or inlet","Low coolant level or leak in the battery loop","Coolant temperature sensor giving an implausible value"],["Check engine light on","Battery or electronics temperatures higher than normal","Gurgling noise from the coolant loop","Power or charging rate limited"],2,3,["P0E1A","P0E1B","P0E1C","P0C4A","P2CF6","P2CF7"],"בקרת משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E1E:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump B Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0E1E does not describe the wire or the pump directly but the switching stage that drives pump B: that driver has reported excess current or overheating and has protected itself. Such smart switches measure their own state; a TI automotive high-side switch, for example, turns off at about 150 °C junction temperature or on reaching its current limit and signals the fault on a diagnostic pin.",["Pump B binding or seized, drawing too much current","Partial short in the pump motor or its wiring","Driver in the module overheating due to location or poor cooling","Degraded output driver in the control module"],["Check engine light on","Pump B switches off intermittently","Battery or electronics temperature rises, power is limited"],3,3,["P0E1F","P0E1A","P0E1B","P0E1C","P2CF5"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0E1F:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0E1F reports that the output stage switching battery coolant pump A has hit its current or temperature limit. Ford uses the code on its 2020 hybrids. On the Escape HEV built up to 10 June 2020, bulletin 20-2221 lists P0E1F in the battery energy control module beside pump and battery codes and blames strategies in the BECM software, fixed by reprogramming PCM and BECM.",["Battery energy control module software (Ford 2020 hybrids)","Pump A binding or seized and drawing excess current","Partial short in the pump motor or harness","Output driver overheating in the module"],["Check engine light on","Battery coolant pump stops intermittently","Hybrid power limited when the battery gets warm"],2,3,["P0E1E","P0C47","P0C48","P0C49","P0CFF","P2B29"],"משאבת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0E20:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve A Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0E20 points at the driver that powers coolant control valve A: its output has exceeded a current limit or run too hot and switched itself off. No manufacturer documents P0E20 publicly. Valve A is often a small motor actuator; on the 2011 Chevrolet Volt it is driven in both directions through two drive lines, each with its own ground and voltage short checks (90 failures in 100 samples at 6.25 ms, Type B).",["Valve A sticking or jammed so the actuator stalls","Partial short in the valve actuator motor or its wiring","Degraded valve driver in the control module"],["Check engine light on","Valve A stays in its last position","Battery runs too warm or too cold, power or charging limited"],2,3,["P0E21","P0E22","P0CDF","P0CE2","P0CE3","U0636"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ A — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0E21:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve B Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0E21 is the overload or overheat report of the driver for coolant control valve B. No manufacturer documents P0E21 publicly. What B means differs between makers: on the 2011 Chevrolet Volt, GM's own codes for valve B (P1EC7, P1EC8) actually watch the second drive line of the same valve motor that serves as valve A, whereas other layouts have a physically separate second valve for a chiller or radiator branch.",["Valve B binding so its actuator stalls against the stop","Short or low resistance in the valve B actuator or harness","Degraded driver stage in the control module"],["Check engine light on","Coolant routing does not change as commanded","Battery temperature drifts, power or charging limited"],2,3,["P0E20","P0E22","P0D1A","P0D1D","P0D1E","U0637"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ B — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0E22:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Control Valve C Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0E22 tells that the driver for a third coolant control valve, C, has protected itself against excessive current or heat. No manufacturer documents P0E22 publicly, and none of the reviewed GM, Ford, Stellantis or Mercedes documents uses a valve C at all. Three valves suggest a loop with several branches, for example radiator, air-conditioning chiller and heater, with C switching one of them.",["Valve C stiff or jammed by deposits","Shorted turn in the valve C actuator motor","Degraded driver stage in the control module"],["Check engine light on","One branch of the coolant loop no longer switches","Battery overcooled in winter or kept too warm in summer"],2,3,["P0E20","P0E21","P0DE8","P0DEB","P0DEC"],"שסתום בקרת נוזל קירור מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ C — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0E23:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor G Circuit","P0E23 is the catch-all circuit code of drive motor inverter temperature sensor G, a thermistor that watches the power switches of the motor inverter. It stands for an electrical fault the controller cannot pin down as low, high or erratic. No manufacturer documents P0E23 publicly.",["Temperature sensor or sensing board inside the inverter failed","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector","Faulty evaluation input in the motor control unit"],["Hybrid system warning light and MIL on","Reduced electric drive power"],4,3,["P0E24","P0E25","P0E26","P0E27","P0E28","P0BE0"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ G — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E24:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor G Circuit Range/Performance","P0E24 is the plausibility test of inverter temperature sensor G: the signal is electrically fine, but the temperature it reports does not fit. Mitsubishi uses the code on the 2023 Outlander PHEV for IGBT sensor number 7 in the front motor control unit, labelled U-phase H2.",["Sensor G reads offset against the other IGBT sensors","Fault on the sensing circuit of the power drive unit","Uneven inverter cooling, e.g. air in the coolant circuit"],["MIL and plug-in hybrid warning light on","Often no noticeable change in driving"],4,1,["P0E23","P0E25","P0E26","P0E27","P0E29","P0AEE","P0BE1"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ G — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E25:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor G Circuit Low","P0E25 means the voltage of inverter temperature sensor G has fallen below its valid window. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV, Mitsubishi assigns it to the U-phase H2 sensor of the front motor control unit and sets P0E25-00 once that signal does not rise above 1.25 V during 0.08 s with a unit supply of 8 V upward; the MIL is switched on together with the hybrid warning lamp. Mitsubishi states only the voltage.",["Sensor signal shorted to ground inside the inverter","Thermistor internally shorted","Damaged sensing board, e.g. after coolant ingress"],["MIL and hybrid warning light on","Motor power limited as if the inverter were overheating"],4,3,["P0E26","P0E23","P0E24","P0E27","P0E2A","P0AEF","P0BE2"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ G — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E26:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor G Circuit High","P0E26 is logged when the signal of inverter temperature sensor G climbs above its permitted range. In the 2023 Outlander PHEV it belongs to the front motor unit's U-phase H2 sensor: at least 3.75 V lasting 0.08 s, with at least 8 V supply to the unit, sets P0E26-00 together with MIL and hybrid warning light.",["Open sensing line or broken thermistor","Lost sensor reference ground","Signal shorted to the sensor supply"],["MIL and hybrid warning light on","Reduced electric drive power as a precaution"],4,3,["P0E25","P0E23","P0E24","P0E27","P0E2B","P0AF0","P0BE3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ G — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E27:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor G Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E27 marks short dropouts or jumps in the signal of inverter temperature sensor G. Between the events the reading is normal again, which is what separates it from the steady faults P0E25 and P0E26. No manufacturer documents P0E27 publicly; Mitsubishi, the one documented user of sensor G on the 2023 Outlander PHEV, checks that sensor for plausibility and for both voltage limits only.",["Intermittent contact at an inverter connector","Cracked solder joint on the sensing board","Thermistor with unstable resistance"],["Warning light that comes and goes","Brief drops in electric drive power"],4,3,["P0E23","P0E24","P0E25","P0E26","P0E2C","P0BE4"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ G — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E28:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor H Circuit","Sensor H is the eighth temperature sensor of a drive motor inverter, and P0E28 is its general circuit code for an electrical fault without a more precise low, high or erratic finding. No manufacturer documents P0E28 publicly. Mitsubishi, whose 2023 Outlander PHEV is the documented user of the H series, calls the part FMCU IGBT temperature sensor 8 (V-phase H2) and files its faults under P0E29, P0E2A and P0E2B;",["Sensor H or its sensing board in the inverter failed","Connector or internal wiring fault in the inverter","Evaluation input of the motor control unit faulty"],["Hybrid warning light and MIL on","Limited motor torque"],4,3,["P0E29","P0E2A","P0E2B","P0E2C","P0E23","P0BE0"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ H — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E29:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor H Circuit Range/Performance","With P0E29 the controller doubts the reading of inverter temperature sensor H although its voltage is valid. On the 2023 Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi uses it for IGBT sensor 8 of the front motor unit, the V-phase H2 position. The cold-start comparison is the same one used for sensors 1 to 7: soak of 600 s or more, IGBTs off, supply at least 8 V, test window of 1.5 s;",["Sensor H offset against the other IGBT sensors","Measuring circuit in the power drive unit faulty","Uneven cooling of the inverter"],["MIL and hybrid warning light on","Driving usually unaffected"],4,1,["P0E28","P0E2A","P0E2B","P0E2C","P0E24","P0AF3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ H — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E2A:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor H Circuit Low","P0E2A reports that inverter temperature sensor H delivers a voltage below its lower limit. Mitsubishi's 2023 Outlander PHEV assigns the code to IGBT sensor 8 (V-phase H2) of the front motor control unit, triggered by a voltage at or under 1.25 V lasting 0.08 s while the supply is 8 V or higher; the MIL comes on.",["Signal of sensor H shorted to ground","Shorted thermistor element","Moisture or coolant damage on the sensing board"],["MIL and hybrid warning light on","Power reduction similar to an overheating inverter"],4,3,["P0E2B","P0E28","P0E29","P0E2C","P0E25","P0BE2"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ H — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E2B:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor H Circuit High","P0E2B is the upper-limit fault of inverter temperature sensor H. Mitsubishi documents it for the 2023 Outlander PHEV, where sensor H is IGBT sensor 8 at the V-phase H2 position of the front motor unit: a signal reaching 3.75 V for 0.08 s while the unit receives 8 V or more sets P0E2B-00, and the function list shows MIL and hybrid warning light for it.",["Interrupted signal path of sensor H","Missing sensor ground","Thermistor element gone open"],["MIL and hybrid warning light on","Drive power may be limited"],4,3,["P0E2A","P0E28","P0E29","P0E2C","P0E26","P0BE3"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ H — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E2C:["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor H Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E2C covers an unsteady signal from inverter temperature sensor H: it flickers, jumps or drops out for moments and then recovers. That pattern differs from P0E2A and P0E2B, where the voltage stays beyond a limit. No manufacturer documents P0E2C publicly. Mitsubishi watches its sensor 8 on the 2023 Outlander PHEV only for plausibility and the two voltage limits and gives no erratic code for it.",["Connector contact affected by vibration","Corroded terminal in the sensor circuit","Hairline crack in the sensing board"],["Intermittent warning light","Occasional short power reductions"],4,3,["P0E28","P0E29","P0E2A","P0E2B","P0E27","P0BE4"],"חיישן טמפרטורת אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ H — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E2D:["Internal Control Module Hybrid/EV Battery Monitor Performance","P0E2D is an internal self-test result of the module that supervises the high-voltage pack: the part that measures cell voltages, usually a dedicated monitor chip with its own converter and reference, does not behave as expected. It casts doubt on the measurement itself, not on a cell.",["Cell monitoring circuit in the battery control module faulty","Software error in the battery control module","Interrupted cell voltage sense line"],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL on","Reduced power or vehicle not ready to drive"],4,3,["P0A1F","P060B","P0BBD","P0AFA"],""],P0E2E:["Battery Charger A Fuse","P0E2E concerns the high-voltage fuse that protects the branch from the battery to on-board charger A. Battery managements usually judge such a fuse by comparing the voltage ahead of and behind it, as Volvo describes for its HV fuses.",["False entry from the battery management software (Audi Q5 PHEV)","Fuse opened after a short in the on-board charger or its cable","Faulty voltage measurement at the fuse"],["Electrical system fault message","No AC charging or vehicle does not start"],4,0,["P0A95","P0E2F","P0E30"],"נתיך מטען סוללה‏ A"],P0E2F:["High Voltage Fuse B","P0E2F says the battery management has found a second high-voltage fuse open, or believes it has. Several makers use it. Mercedes calls it electrical fuse B, the battery-internal fuse protecting auxiliary HV consumers in its plug-in hybrids: a shorted belt starter-alternator or another short in that line blows it, the engine no longer starts and only electric driving remains;",["Short circuit in an HV consumer on the protected branch","False detection by the battery management or DC/DC wake-up fault","Faulty voltage measurement behind the fuse"],["Engine does not start, electric driving only (plug-in hybrids)","Vehicle not ready to drive or propulsion warning","MIL or HV drive system warning on"],4,3,["P0A95","P0E30","P0E2E"],"נתיך מתח גבוה‏ B"],P0E30:["High Voltage Fuse C","P0E30 is the code for a third fuse in the high-voltage system, after A (P0A95) and B (P0E2F). No manufacturer documents P0E30 publicly. How such a fuse is checked can be seen from the siblings: Volvo and Polestar have the battery control module compare the voltage measured after each fuse output with the pack voltage, and Porsche numbers its Taycan fuses as fuse and fuse 2 under P0A95 and P0E2F.",["Short circuit in a consumer on the third fused branch","Faulty voltage measurement behind the fuse","Software error in the battery control module"],["HV system warning message","Consumers on the affected branch without function"],4,3,["P0A95","P0E2F","P0E2E"],"נתיך מתח גבוה‏ C"],P0E31:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor A Circuit","P0E31 is the general circuit code of voltage sensor A in a DC/DC converter, the measurement the converter or its controller uses to regulate output voltage. It is used for electrical faults that do not fit the low, high or erratic categories. P0E31 itself is not documented publicly by any manufacturer.",["Voltage measurement inside the DC/DC converter failed","Wiring or connector fault on the converter","Poor ground connection of the converter"],["Warning light and possibly 12 V battery warning","Engine auto-stop disabled or reduced 12 V supply"],3,1,["P0E32","P0E33","P0E34","P0E35","P0E36"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E32:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor A Range/Performance","P0E32 flags a voltage reading of DC/DC converter sensor A that is electrically valid but does not match a reference. GM's 2025 engine tables (25OBDG03A, ECM) apply it to a converter that holds the 12 V supply steady during auto-starts: in bypass mode the raw output voltage may differ from the ECM run/crank voltage by no more than 2 V, and Type B (two trips) follows once 320 of 400 samples, taken every 12.5 ms, fail.",["Voltage measurement in the converter drifting","Voltage drop on supply, ground or reference wiring","Control software needing an update (FCA, Mercedes)","Weak 12 V battery distorting the comparison during cranking"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Red 12 V warning message (Mercedes)","Auto stop/start unavailable"],3,1,["P0E31","P0E33","P0E34","P0E35","P0E37"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E33:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor A Low","P0E33 means the controller reads an implausibly low value from voltage sensor A of a DC/DC converter, typically because the signal wire is shorted to ground. GM documents it in its 2025 engine control tables (25OBDG03A) for a converter that keeps the 12 V network stable while the engine restarts automatically: the raw voltage value 1 must drop below 1 V, and 320 failing samples out of 400 taken every 12.5 ms, about…",["Sensor signal wire chafed through to ground","Voltage measurement inside the converter failed","Moisture in the converter connector bridging to ground"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Auto stop/start no longer available","Possible 12 V voltage dips during engine restarts"],3,1,["P0E31","P0E32","P0E34","P0E35","P0E38"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ A — ערך נמוך"],P0E34:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor A High","With P0E34 the controller sees a voltage on sensor A of the DC/DC converter that is too high to be real, which points to the signal being shorted to a battery feed or another live wire. In GM's 2025 tables (25OBDG03A) the ECM flags it when raw voltage value 1 exceeds 28 V, far above anything a 12 V converter output should reach.",["Signal wire shorted to a battery feed or other live circuit","Lost reference ground of the measurement, signal floats high","Faulty measuring circuit in the converter"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Engine auto-stop disabled"],3,1,["P0E31","P0E32","P0E33","P0E35","P0E39"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ A — ערך גבוה"],P0E35:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","P0E35 is reserved for a voltage sensor A signal that jumps or drops out briefly instead of staying wrong: the reading is erratic, but not low or high long enough for P0E33 or P0E34 to mature. No manufacturer documents P0E35 publicly.",["Loose or spread terminal in the converter connector","Ground connection that fails under vibration","Cracked solder joint in the converter's measuring circuit"],["Warning light that appears and disappears","Start-stop works only now and then"],3,1,["P0E31","P0E32","P0E33","P0E34","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ A — אות לא יציב"],P0E36:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor B Circuit","P0E36 is the catch-all circuit code for the second voltage measurement of a DC/DC converter, sensor B. Where a converter reads both its input and its output, or monitors one rail twice, the second path gets the letter B; FCA names it voltage sensor 2 on the 2021 Ram 1500 eTorque. No manufacturer documents P0E36 itself in public sources.",["Open sense wire or unplugged connector","Second measuring path inside the converter failed","Poor ground of the converter"],["Warning light, possibly 12 V battery warning","Start-stop disabled or converter output reduced"],3,1,["P0E31","P0E37","P0E38","P0E39","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E37:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor B Range/Performance","P0E37 says that voltage sensor B delivers a believable number which nevertheless disagrees with what the vehicle measures elsewhere. GM's 2025 engine tables (25OBDG03A) check raw voltage value 2 of the converter against the ECM run/crank voltage: in bypass mode a gap above 2 V counts, and 320 failed samples out of 400 at 12.5 ms set a Type B code.",["Second voltage measurement in the converter drifting","Voltage drop on supply or ground wiring distorting the comparison","Controller software needing an update (FCA eTorque)"],["MIL on, after one or two trips depending on maker and software","Start-stop unavailable"],3,1,["P0E32","P0E36","P0E38","P0E39","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E38:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor B Low","P0E38 is stored when the second voltage reading of the DC/DC converter, sensor B, sits at the bottom of its scale. GM uses it in the 2025 engine tables (25OBDG03A) with the same limits as on sensor A, only applied to raw voltage value 2: under 1 V, confirmed by 320 of 400 samples taken at 12.5 ms intervals and only checked at 6.6 V battery voltage or more.",["Sensor B signal wire shorted to ground","Failed second measuring circuit in the converter","Bent or corroded pin in the converter connector"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Auto stop/start inhibited"],3,1,["P0E33","P0E36","P0E37","P0E39","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ B — ערך נמוך"],P0E39:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor B High","P0E39 reports that sensor B of the DC/DC converter reads far too high, a pattern GM attributes to a short to battery. The 2025 engine tables (25OBDG03A) set the bar at more than 28 V for raw voltage value 2, again needing 320 failing samples within 400 at 12.5 ms and a battery voltage of at least 6.6 V, with Type B confirmation over two trips.",["Sensor B signal shorted to a voltage-carrying wire","Open reference ground letting the signal float high","Failed measuring circuit for sensor B"],["MIL on after two trips (GM)","Start-stop switched off"],3,1,["P0E34","P0E36","P0E37","P0E38","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ B — ערך גבוה"],P0E3A:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","P0E3A covers an unsteady signal from voltage sensor B of a DC/DC converter: short spikes, dropouts or noise that never last long enough for the hard low or high codes. Publicly, no manufacturer documents P0E3A.",["Harness rubbing intermittently where it moves with the engine","Poor terminal tension in the sensor B circuit","Electrical interference on an unshielded sense line"],["Sporadic warning light, sometimes cleared on its own","Start-stop unavailable from time to time"],3,1,["P0E35","P0E36","P0E37","P0E38","P0E39"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ B — אות לא יציב"],P0E3B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Coolant Pump Overspeed","P0E3B indicates that the electric pump circulating coolant through the high-voltage battery turns faster than commanded. A pump that suddenly spins too freely has usually lost its load, because it is moving air instead of coolant after a leak, a low reservoir or a poorly bled system. No manufacturer documents P0E3B publicly.",["Low coolant level or leak, pump running partly dry","Air left in the battery loop after a repair","Faulty pump electronics or speed feedback signal"],["Hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced charging or drive power when the battery warms up"],2,3,["P0E3C","P0C4A","P0C47","U0694"],"משאבת נוזל קירור סוללת מתח גבוה — מהירות יתר"],P0E3C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Coolant Pump Underspeed","P0E3C is set when the battery coolant pump stays below the speed it has been asked for. GM documents it specifically for the 2024–25 Chevrolet Corvette E-Ray: the battery energy control module (K16) stores P0E3C, and the ECM may add P0A7B to switch on the MIL. Bulletin 24-NA-160 traces it to air collecting in the hoses next to the pump after an earlier repair;",["Air trapped around the pump after earlier service (GM 24-NA-160)","Worn pump bearing or debris slowing the impeller","Low supply voltage or poor ground at the pump"],["MIL on, with P0A7B stored in the ECM (Corvette E-Ray)","Electric front-axle drive limited when the battery gets hot"],2,3,["P0E3B","P0A7B","P0C4A","P0C47","U0694"],"משאבת נוזל קירור סוללת מתח גבוה — מהירות נמוכה מדי"],P0E3D:["Control Pilot Indicator Control Circuit Low","P0E3D concerns the lamp or LED that shows the state of the pilot connection to the charging station, not the pilot signal itself. Low means the driver circuit reads too little voltage, typically because the line to the LED is shorted to ground. No manufacturer documents P0E3D publicly.",["Wire to the indicator LED shorted to ground at the charge port","Shorted LED or indicator unit","Faulty output driver in the controlling module"],["Pilot indicator stays dark when it should light","Charging itself usually unaffected"],2,0,["P0E3E","P0D2B","P0CF3","P0CF4"],"חיווי פיילוט בקרה — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E3E:["Control Pilot Indicator Control Circuit High","P0E3E is the high-side counterpart for the control pilot indicator circuit: the module senses voltage on the LED line when there should be little or none, the classic sign of a short to a supply wire. Publicly, no manufacturer documents P0E3E.",["Indicator wire chafed against a supply wire near the charge port","Output stage in the module stuck on","Water in the charge port connector bridging pins"],["Pilot indicator lit or flickering without reason","Misleading charge port light while charging works"],2,0,["P0E3D","P0D2B","P0CF3","P0CF4"],"חיווי פיילוט בקרה — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E3F:["Charge Status Indicator A Control Circuit Low","P0E3F reports that the output feeding charge status indicator A sits too low in voltage. That light confirms to the owner that the vehicle is charging, and the expected cause is an LED line touching ground. No manufacturer documents P0E3F publicly.",["LED supply wire pinched or chafed to ground","Charge status LED shorted internally","Failed driver in the controlling module"],["Charge status light stays off during charging","Charging continues normally"],2,0,["P0E40","P0D2C","P0E41","P0DE5"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E40:["Charge Status Indicator A Control Circuit High","P0E40 is logged when the drive circuit of charge status indicator A carries voltage although the light is commanded off, typically because the line touches a live wire. P0E40 has no public documentation from any manufacturer.",["Indicator wire shorted to a battery-fed circuit","Output driver no longer switching off","Moisture bridging pins in the indicator connector"],["Charge status light on although nothing is charging","Owner misled about the actual charge state"],2,0,["P0E3F","P0D2C","P0E42","P0DE5"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E41:["Charge Status Indicator B Control Circuit Low","P0E41 refers to a second charge status light, indicator B, whose driver circuit is pulled too low, usually by a short to ground. Which light carries the letter B is the maker's choice; a lamp at the charge port alongside the dashboard light would be a typical pair. No manufacturer documents P0E41 publicly, and the GM Volt and ELR tables only cover indicator A.",["Indicator B wire pinched to ground near the charge port flap","Water ingress into the charge port light assembly","Shorted LED in indicator B"],["Second charge light (e.g. at the port) stays dark","Main charge indicator and charging unaffected"],2,0,["P0E42","P0DE5","P0E3F","P0D2C"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E42:["Charge Status Indicator B Control Circuit High","P0E42 means the driver circuit of charge status indicator B shows voltage at a moment when the light should be switched off, pointing to a short to a supply wire or a driver that stays on. P0E42 appears in no public manufacturer document.",["Indicator B wire touching a live wire at the charge port","Wet or corroded contacts bridging to a supply pin","Output driver for indicator B stuck on"],["Second charge light glowing or flickering when not charging","Charging itself works"],2,0,["P0E41","P0DE5","P0E40","P0D2C"],"חיווי סטטוס טעינה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E43:["Hybrid/EV Powertrain Control Module B","P0E43 is the module-B counterpart of P0A1D: it reports that a second hybrid/EV powertrain controller has detected an internal failure or can no longer be trusted. Vehicles with two such processors, for example GM's Bolt EV with a second powertrain module labelled K114B (HPCM2), are where the letter B makes sense. No manufacturer documents P0E43 publicly.",["Internal processor or memory fault in module B","Software fault curable by reprogramming","Supply, ground or internal bus fault at the module"],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced propulsion power or no ready mode"],4,3,["P0A1D","P0AC4","U0293"],"מודול בקרת מערכת ההנעה היברידי/חשמלי‏ B"],P0E44:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense E Circuit","P0E44 is the basic circuit code for the fifth pack voltage measurement, channel E, which follows channels A to D (P0ABA, P0B14, P0B19, P0B1E) in the code list. The battery controller stores it when this input is electrically unusable, for example open, without a plausible raw value or failing a self-test of its measuring stage, and none of the more specific low, high or erratic codes fits.",["Open or unplugged voltage sense lead or connector inside the pack","Faulty measuring stage or input of the battery controller","Blown fuse or open resistor in the sense path"],["Hybrid or high-voltage system warning lit","Reduced electric assist or limited battery power","Vehicle may not switch to ready mode"],4,3,["P0E45","P0E46","P0E47","P0E48","P0E49","P0B1E","P0ABA"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E45:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense E Circuit Range/Performance","P0E45 says pack voltage measurement E delivers a value that is electrically valid but does not fit the rest of the battery data. Such a rationality test usually compares the channel with the summed block or cell voltages, or with a second pack measurement. No manufacturer documents P0E45 publicly.",["Corroded or loose sense connection adding resistance and skewing the reading","Drifting measuring stage or outdated software in the battery controller","Fault in the reference value, e.g. a block or cell voltage channel"],["Warning light with reduced hybrid or electric power","Displayed state of charge or range jumps unexpectedly"],4,3,["P0E44","P0E46","P0E47","P0E48","P0E4A","P0ABB"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E46:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense E Circuit Low","P0E46 is stored when pack voltage measurement E reads below the lowest value the connected battery could physically present. That points to the measuring path, such as a sense lead shorted to ground, a blown sense fuse or a failed input stage, not to an empty battery. No manufacturer documents P0E46 publicly.",["Sense lead of channel E shorted to ground or chassis","Blown fuse or open resistor in the sense path pulling the reading down","Defective input stage of the battery controller"],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive or drops out of ready mode","Hybrid system warning and MIL lit","Electric drive or engine assist unavailable"],4,3,["P0E44","P0E45","P0E47","P0E48","P0E4B","P0ABC","P0AFA"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E47:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense E Circuit High","P0E47 means pack voltage measurement E reports more voltage than the pack can reach even when fully charged. A sense lead touching a higher potential, a broken divider resistor or a damaged input of the measuring chip are the usual explanations; real overcharging is unlikely when only one channel shows it. P0E47 is not documented by any manufacturer in public.",["Sense lead of channel E chafed through and touching a higher potential","Broken divider resistor or lost reference in the measuring circuit","Damaged measuring input or software fault in the battery controller"],["MIL and hybrid warning on","Charging or regenerative braking restricted","Electric drive limited or blocked"],4,3,["P0E44","P0E45","P0E46","P0E48","P0E4C","P0ABD"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E48:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense E Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E48 marks pack voltage measurement E as unsteady: the signal jumps, drops out for fractions of a second or changes faster than a battery voltage can, and then returns to normal. Because the fault comes and goes, it is often found only as a stored code after a brief warning.",["Loose, pushed-back or corroded terminal on the channel E sense lead","Chafed sense lead that shorts briefly under vibration","Intermittent fault in the battery controller input"],["Warning light that comes and goes","Brief losses of electric power, often on rough roads","Stored code without a current fault"],4,3,["P0E44","P0E45","P0E46","P0E47","P0E4D","P0ABE"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E49:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense F Circuit","Channel F is the sixth and last pack voltage measurement in this series and comes after channel E (P0E44 to P0E48). P0E49 is its general circuit fault: the battery controller cannot use the input, for instance because the sense line is open, the measuring chip reports an internal error or the raw value is missing, while no clear low, high or erratic pattern applies. No manufacturer documents P0E49 publicly.",["Open sense lead or disconnected connector of channel F","Internal fault of the voltage measuring chip or battery controller","Moisture or corrosion in the pack-side connector"],["High-voltage warning and reduced power","Vehicle may refuse to become ready"],4,3,["P0E4A","P0E4B","P0E4C","P0E4D","P0E44","P0AF8"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E4A:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense F Circuit Range/Performance","P0E4A reports that pack voltage measurement F lies within its electrical range but disagrees with a reference value. No manufacturer documents P0E4A publicly. GM's 2019 hybrid tables show two plausibility checks such a channel could face: P0ABB fails when pack voltage and the summed cell voltages differ by more than 12 V in 20 of 25 samples at 25 ms, and P0AF8 compares the pack sensor with the inverter bus voltage,…",["Contact resistance at a sense terminal distorting the channel F value","Fault in the comparison value, such as the inverter bus or cell voltage measurement","Measuring drift or software issue in the battery controller"],["MIL on in the first trip with reduced battery power","Implausible state of charge display"],4,3,["P0E49","P0E4B","P0E4C","P0E4D","P0E45","P0AF8","P0ABB"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E4B:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense F Circuit Low","P0E4B sets when pack voltage measurement F falls below its plausible minimum, which normally points to a sense path shorted to ground, an open sense fuse or a defective input of the battery controller. No manufacturer documents P0E4B publicly.",["Channel F sense lead shorted to ground or pack housing","Open fuse or resistor in the channel F sense path","Failed analog input of the battery controller"],["No ready mode or contactors do not close","MIL and high-voltage warning on"],4,3,["P0E49","P0E4A","P0E4C","P0E4D","P0E46","P0ABC"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E4C:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense F Circuit High","P0E4C means pack voltage measurement F reports a value above anything the battery can produce. Common reasons are a sense line in contact with a higher potential, a lost ground reference in the measuring circuit or a faulty analog input, so the channel rather than the cells is suspect. No manufacturer documents P0E4C publicly.",["Channel F sense line touching a higher potential","Lost ground reference or broken divider in the measuring circuit","Faulty analog input or software of the battery controller"],["Warning lamp and limited electric drive","Charging interrupted or not started"],4,3,["P0E49","P0E4A","P0E4B","P0E4D","P0E47","P0ABD","P0DE7"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E4D:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense F Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E4D flags pack voltage measurement F as intermittent: short dropouts, spikes or steps that a real high-voltage battery cannot make, followed by normal readings again. Where a vehicle cross-checks several voltage sensors, as GM does in its 2019 hybrids between the pack sensor and the inverter or charger bus (P0AF8), such glitches can also surface as correlation faults. No manufacturer documents P0E4D publicly.",["Loose or corroded terminal in the channel F sense circuit","Sense lead that loses contact when the harness moves","Interference or intermittent fault in the measuring electronics"],["Intermittent warning light","Sporadic power limitation","Additional correlation codes stored"],4,3,["P0E49","P0E4A","P0E4B","P0E4C","P0E48","P0AF8"],"חישת מתח מארז סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E4E:["Engine Failed to Crank","P0E4E reports that the hybrid system commanded an engine start but the crankshaft did not turn, or did not reach the cranking speed it expected. It differs from P0A0F, where the engine is turned over but does not fire and keep running. In most hybrids a motor-generator fed by the high-voltage battery cranks the engine, so causes range from a seized engine or transaxle to missing battery energy or a drive fault.",["High-voltage battery too low to crank, often after repeated failed starts","Mechanically seized engine or blocked transaxle","Fault in the cranking motor-generator, its inverter or a conventional starter","Crankshaft speed not detected because of a sensor or signal fault"],["Engine does not turn over when the hybrid system calls for it","Vehicle runs electrically only or shows a stop-safely warning","No start at all once the battery is depleted"],4,2,["P0A0F","P0AFA","P0615","P0335"],""],P0E4F:["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E4F covers coolant pump A of the motor electronics loop, which cools the inverter and drive motor, and sets when the signal exchanged with the pump becomes unstable rather than permanently open or shorted.",["Loose, corroded or wet pins in the pump A connector","Chafed command or feedback wire that loses contact intermittently","Failing electronics inside the pump sending an unstable feedback signal","Unstable 12 V supply or poor ground at the pump"],["MIL or hybrid system warning","Power reduction when inverter or motor temperature rises","Fault may clear itself and return later"],2,3,["P0A05","P0A06","P0A07","P0C73","P0E50"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת מנוע חשמלי‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל הבקרה"],P0E50:["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump B Control Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E50 is the intermittent or erratic fault of the control circuit for coolant pump B, the second electric pump some hybrids use in the inverter and motor electronics cooling. No manufacturer documents P0E50 publicly. The closest published example is Ford's pump A version, P0E4F, stored when the pump's feedback leaves its expected frequency or duty cycle for over 1 s.",["Intermittent contact at the pump B connector or a harness splice","Pump B electronics producing an unstable signal","Control module software evaluating the pump signal too strictly"],["Warning light that appears now and then","Temporary power reduction under high load or heat"],2,3,["P0B38","P0B39","P0B3A","P0C74","P0E4F"],"משאבת נוזל קירור אלקטרוניקת מנוע חשמלי‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל הבקרה"],P0E51:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor A Circuit","P0E51 is the general circuit fault of current sensor A in a DC/DC converter, the element that tells the converter's controller how many amps flow through its power stage. The code covers a signal path that is broken in a way the controller cannot sort into low, high or implausible. No manufacturer documents P0E51 publicly.",["Current sensor inside the converter defective (usually not replaceable separately)","Loose, corroded or damaged sensor connection or harness","Fault on the converter control board or its sensor supply","Converter or hybrid control software needs an update"],["Hybrid or 12 V battery warning in the instrument cluster","12 V battery is charged poorly or not at all","Reduced electric power or limited hybrid functions"],4,3,["P0E52","P0E53","P0E54","P0E55","P0E9B"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E52:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0E52 is the plausibility test of current sensor A: the signal is electrically valid, yet the measured current does not fit what the converter is doing. Two makers name it. FCA lists it for the 2021 Ram 1500 with the 3.6 litre eTorque mild hybrid; bulletin 08-037-21 brings new HCP software and turns P0E52 from a two-trip into a one-trip code, so the MIL may light after a single faulty drive.",["Control software with unsuitable limits (FCA eTorque HCP update)","Internal fault of the inverter system controller (Ford hybrid)","Current sensor drifting or offset out of tolerance","High resistance at sensor connector or ground"],["MIL on, often after only one drive","Limited power with Stop Safely Now or Service Vehicle Soon message","Reduced eTorque assist or start/stop function"],4,3,["P0E51","P0E53","P0E54","P0E55","P0E9C"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E53:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor A Circuit Low","P0E53 means the signal of DC/DC converter current sensor A sits below its valid window. On a Hall-type sensor with a mid-scale zero point, that usually points to a short to ground, a missing sensor supply or an open signal wire pulled low, not to a real drop in current. No manufacturer documents P0E53 publicly.",["Signal line shorted to ground","Sensor supply missing or too low","Current sensor or converter electronics defective"],["Hybrid or charging-system warning lamp","12 V battery discharges because the converter does not supply it","Reduced power or electric drive not available"],4,3,["P0E51","P0E52","P0E54","P0E55","P0E9D"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E54:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor A Circuit High","P0E54 reports a current sensor A signal above the range the DC/DC converter accepts. Mercedes-Benz uses it explicitly: its bulletin LI54.10-P-079533, covering the EQB (series 243) and the G-Class including the electric G 580 (series 465), defines P0E5400 as a short to positive of the converter's current sensor A.",["Converter stuck in a fault state after incorrect wake-up (Mercedes 243/465)","Signal line shorted to a supply voltage","Current sensor or converter electronics defective"],["Red 12 V battery message","White or yellow high-voltage system warnings","Reduced drive power"],4,3,["P0E51","P0E52","P0E53","P0E55","P0E9E","P0C3B"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E55:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E55 deals with a current sensor A reading that flickers or vanishes for moments instead of failing for good. FCA documents it repeatedly for the Ram 1500 with eTorque mild hybrid, where the DC/DC converter sits in the 48 V battery pack and is run by the battery pack control module (BPCM). Bulletin 08-030-24 for the 2019 model lists P0E55 among ten BPCM codes;",["Battery pack control module software (FCA eTorque, reflash)","Loose or corroded contact in the sensor path","Current sensor or converter electronics failing intermittently"],["MIL on","12 V battery not charging, flat soon after driving","Limp-home mode and start/stop disabled"],4,3,["P0E51","P0E52","P0E53","P0E54","P0E9F"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ A — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E56:["DC/DC Converter Input Voltage Too Low","P0E56 says the voltage arriving at the input of the DC/DC converter, normally the high-voltage bus of the traction battery, is lower than the converter needs to work. Unlike the sensor codes, the reading itself is believed; the supply is the problem. No manufacturer documents P0E56 on an official public server.",["Traction battery deeply discharged or limited by its management system","Main contactor not closing or high-voltage cable/connector fault","Input voltage measurement inside the converter faulty"],["12 V battery warning, battery discharges while driving","Hybrid system warning, vehicle may not reach ready mode","Reduced power"],4,3,["P0E57","P0A94","P0CA2","P0E31"],"מתח כניסת ממיר DC/DC — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0E57:["DC/DC Converter Input Voltage Too High","P0E57 is set when the DC/DC converter sees more voltage on its input than it is rated for. Excess input voltage stresses the switching transistors and input capacitors, so a converter typically stops working to protect itself. No manufacturer documents P0E57 on an official public server. Supplier data show the limits involved: Poclain specifies 96 V as the highest continuous input for its 48 V converter.",["High-voltage bus driven too high by boost stage, regeneration or charging","Input voltage measurement in the converter reads too high","Hybrid or battery control software needs an update"],["Hybrid system or 12 V battery warning","Converter switches off temporarily, 12 V voltage drops","Reduced power or limited regeneration"],4,3,["P0E56","P0A94","P0CA3","P0C79"],"מתח כניסת ממיר DC/DC — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0E58:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor C Circuit","P0E58 stands for a general circuit fault of the third temperature sensor in a DC/DC converter, after sensors A (P0C38 ff.) and B (P0C3D ff.). Which part of the converter sensor C watches is up to the maker. At Ford it is the inductor of the variable voltage converter, the boost stage of its full hybrids, but Ford uses only the plausibility code P0E59 for it. No manufacturer documents P0E58 publicly.",["Sensor connection or wiring damaged or corroded","Temperature sensor in the converter defective","Sensor reference or evaluation circuit in the converter faulty"],["Hybrid system warning","Reduced electric assist or power"],4,3,["P0E59","P0E5A","P0E5B","P0E5C","P0C38","P0C3D","P2C8C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E59:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","P0E59 is the best-documented member of this group. Ford's hybrid OBD summaries from 2015 to 2019 assign it to the inductor temperature sensor of the variable voltage converter and monitor it without pause once 500 ms have passed since power-up, provided the 12 V supply lies between 6 and 19 V.",["Inductor temperature sensor drifting, reading off after cold soak","Inductor really overheating, e.g. due to weak electronics cooling","Hybrid control software (FCA HCP update)","Short or open in the sensor circuit (Ford range limits)"],["MIL on, at FCA after one faulty drive","Reduced hybrid assist or electric driving","Hybrid system warning"],4,3,["P0E58","P0E5A","P0E5B","P0E5C","P0C39","P0C3E","P2C8D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E5A:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Low","P0E5A means the voltage from DC/DC converter temperature sensor C has fallen below its valid window. With the NTC thermistors common in power electronics, a low voltage translates into an implausibly hot reading, so a grounded signal wire or a failed sensor is far more likely than genuine overheating. No manufacturer documents P0E5A publicly.",["Sensor signal shorted to ground","Thermistor internally shorted","Evaluation circuit in the converter faulty"],["Hybrid system warning","Converter derated, reduced electric power"],4,3,["P0E58","P0E59","P0E5B","P0E5C","P0C3A","P2C8E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E5B:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor C Circuit High","P0E5B is stored when the signal of DC/DC converter temperature sensor C rises above its valid window. On an NTC circuit with pull-up resistor, that is the typical picture of an unplugged connector, a broken wire or a short to a supply voltage, and it reads as a converter far colder than possible. No manufacturer documents P0E5B publicly.",["Connector unplugged or sensor wire broken","Signal shorted to a supply voltage","Thermistor open internally"],["Hybrid system warning","Power limited as a precaution"],4,3,["P0E58","P0E59","P0E5A","P0E5C","P0C3B","P2C8F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E5C:["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E5C flags a sensor C signal that changes faster than the part it watches could heat or cool. A converter inductor or heat sink has thermal mass, so a jump of many degrees within a few milliseconds points to a contact problem in the circuit, not to the converter itself. No manufacturer documents P0E5C publicly.",["Loose or widened terminal at the sensor or converter connector","Moisture or corrosion in the connector","Chafed wire with intermittent contact"],["Hybrid warning that comes and goes","Short episodes of reduced electric power"],3,3,["P0E58","P0E59","P0E5A","P0E5B","P0C3C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E5D:["DC/DC Converter Enable Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E5D adds the intermittent case to the DC/DC converter enable codes P0A11 (open), P0A12 (low) and P0A13 (high). Through the enable line a hybrid or body controller switches the converter on and off; if that line flickers, the converter keeps stopping and restarting.",["Loose or corroded terminal on the enable line","Poor ground reference between controller and converter","Chafed enable wire touching ground intermittently","Output stage in the commanding controller faulty"],["12 V battery warning that comes and goes","Fluctuating system voltage, flickering lights","Hybrid warning, possibly reduced power"],3,3,["P0A11","P0A12","P0A13","P0A08"],"אפשור ממיר DC/DC — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E5E:["Battery Charger Control Module A Processor Performance","P0E5E reports that the processor of the on-board charger's control unit fails its self-tests. Mitsubishi spells this out for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: P0E5E-00 means a ROM checksum (CRC) error or a RAM read/write error in the charger control; the code lights both the PHEV warning and the MIL. In the Volkswagen group the code is stored by the battery management module (address 8C).",["Control electronics of the on-board charger defective (unit replacement)","Corrupt or outdated charger software","Unstable 12 V supply or ground of the charger during start-up"],["Plug-in charging impossible or interrupted","MIL and hybrid/PHEV warning, sometimes right after charging"],4,1,["P0D67","P2D78","P2D77"],"מעבד מודול בקרת מטען סוללה‏ A — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0E5F:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature A Too High","P0E5F is not a sensor fault: the coupler temperature sensor A works, but the charging inlet really got too hot. Three makers document it. Ford links it on the 2021–2023 Mustang Mach-E (stored in the SOBDM) to a red flashing charge indicator and no charging from a 48 A level 2 station, while 32 A or less may still work; SSM 51866 calls for a new charge port.",["Excessive contact resistance in the vehicle charge port (Ford replaces the port)","Worn or damaged plug of the charging cable or station","High ambient temperature or direct sun while charging (Mercedes)","Charging control software (GM update announced)"],["Charging stops or runs at reduced power","Charge indicator red or flashing red, warning in app or cluster","Charging restarts only after unplugging and replugging"],4,1,["P0D99","P0D9A","P0EEE","P2E4E","P2E53"],"טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ A — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0E60:["Battery Charge Mode Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E60 concerns a wired control line that tells the on-board charger which mode to run in, set by the module that coordinates charging. Range/Performance means the line shows a valid level, but its state or response disagrees with the command. No manufacturer documents P0E60 or the modes it selects publicly.",["High resistance or damage on the mode control line","Input stage of the on-board charger faulty","Output stage or software of the commanding module faulty"],["Plug-in charging does not start or stops","Charging runs at an unexpected rate"],4,0,["P0E61","P0E64","P0E65","P0E66","P0E67"],"מצב טעינת סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E61:["Battery Charger A Enable Circuit/Open","P0E61 means the 12 V enable line that wakes or permits the on-board charger has a circuit fault, most often an open. GM documents it codegenau. In the 2016 Volt the VICM watches this charger enable circuit; in the 2019 hybrid tables the HPC2 module does so. The test reads a fault flag reported by the driver hardware and runs when system voltage is at least 10.2 V;",["Enable wire open or connector at charger/module not seated","Output driver in the controlling module (VICM/HPC2) faulty","Enable input of the on-board charger defective"],["Plug-in charging does not start","MIL on after two trips","Battery heating in cold weather may be unavailable"],3,1,["P0E62","P0E63","P0E64","P2E35","P0E65"],"אפשור מטען סוללה‏ A — מעגל תקול או פתוח"],P0E62:["Battery Charger A Enable Circuit Low","P0E62 reports that the enable line of on-board charger A is held at a lower level than its commanded state allows, typically by a short to ground. If the module drives the line high to permit charging, the charger never sees the request. Should the line instead carry a low-active signal, a grounded wire could leave the charger permitted at all times, which is why manufacturers separate low from high.",["Enable wire chafed and shorted to ground","Enable input of the charger shorted internally","Driver stage in the commanding module faulty"],["Charging does not start when plugged in","Charging system warning"],3,0,["P0E61","P0E63","P0E64","P2E36"],"אפשור מטען סוללה‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E63:["Battery Charger A Enable Circuit High","P0E63 is the high-level counterpart: the enable signal to charger A sits above the level its commanded state allows, for instance because it touches a 12 V feed. In practice the charger may then be woken although no charging is requested, which can drain the 12 V battery while parked, or it may ignore a switch-off command. No manufacturer documents P0E63 publicly.",["Enable wire shorted to a 12 V supply","Enable input of the charger faulty","Driver stage in the commanding module stuck on"],["12 V battery drained while parked","Charger active or charging not ending as expected","Charging system warning"],3,0,["P0E61","P0E62","P0E64","P2E37"],"אפשור מטען סוללה‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E64:["Battery Charger A Enable Circuit Range/Performance","P0E64 covers an enable line that is electrically intact while the charger still does not respond as it should. Ford documents it in its 2019 hybrid OBD summary, there titled Battery Charger Enable Circuit Range/Performance: the check runs once the charger is in its ready state and the charge wake-up signal Chrg_Wkup has gone high, and it fails when the battery energy control module still reports not ready after 30…",["Charger and BECM do not complete start-up within 30 s (software or wake-up timing)","Battery energy control module not waking or not reporting ready","High resistance on the wake-up line producing a marginal level"],["Charging does not start although the cable is connected","Charging system warning or charge port light shows a fault"],4,0,["P0E61","P0E62","P0E63","P2E38","P0D20"],"אפשור מטען סוללה‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E65:["Battery Charger Control Circuit/Open","P0E65 flags a fault on the wired command line through which the hybrid control unit operates the on-board charger, most often an interrupted line. GM uses it codegenau: in the 2016 Volt the VICM runs the test, in the 2019 hybrid tables (19OBDG01) the HPC2 module.",["Broken or disconnected charger control wire","Corroded or pushed-out terminal at charger or control unit","Defective output driver in the commanding control unit"],["Vehicle does not charge when plugged in","MIL after the second trip with the fault (GM)","Charging fault message in the instrument cluster"],2,1,["P0E66","P0E67","P0E68","P0E61"],"מטען סוללה — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E66:["Battery Charger Control Circuit Low","With P0E66 the controller finds the charger command line pulled lower than the state it is driving, which typically means the wire touches ground somewhere between the control unit and the on-board charger. If the command is active-high, the charger simply never receives it; a grounded line can also overload the output stage that drives it. No manufacturer documents P0E66 publicly.",["Control wire chafed through to body ground","Moisture in a connector bridging the signal to ground","Internal short in the charger's command input"],["Charging does not start after plugging in","Charge indicator shows a fault or goes out"],2,0,["P0E65","P0E67","P0E68","P0E62"],"מטען סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E67:["Battery Charger Control Circuit High","P0E67 is set when the charger command line reads higher than commanded, for instance close to battery voltage while the control unit is holding it off. A short to a live wire is the classic cause; with some driver designs an open line also floats high and lands here.",["Control wire shorted to a battery feed or other supply","Open line read as high by the driver circuit","Output stage of the control unit stuck switched on"],["Charging aborted or not offered at all","Charger wakes up or runs without a charging request"],2,0,["P0E65","P0E66","P0E68","P0E63"],"מטען סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E68:["Battery Charger Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E68 is the plausibility code of the charger command line: no short or open is present, yet the charger does not react to the command in the expected way or in the expected time. Typical triggers are a charger that wakes too slowly, a voltage drop on the line that leaves the level ambiguous, or software in the two modules that disagrees. No manufacturer documents P0E68 publicly.",["Charger and control unit software not matching","Charger responding late or not at all to the command","Excess resistance in the line distorting the signal level"],["Charging starts late, stops or fails intermittently","Charge timer or scheduled charging not working"],2,0,["P0E65","P0E66","P0E67","P0E64"],"מטען סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E69:["Control Pilot Indicator Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E69 belongs to the output that drives the small lamp or LED showing the state of the pilot connection to the charging station. Range/Performance means the driver circuit reports a state that does not fit what the module commanded, without a clear short or open. No manufacturer documents P0E69 publicly.",["Indicator LED aged or partially failed, drawing the wrong current","Loose contact at the charge port lamp connector","Faulty feedback of the LED driver in the control unit"],["Charge port indicator flickers, stays dark or shows the wrong state","Charging itself usually unaffected"],1,0,["P0D2B","P0E3D","P0E3E","P0CF4"],"חיווי פיילוט בקרה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E6A:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Circuit","P0E6A is the general code for the precharge contactor of the battery charging system. Before the main charging contactors close, this small contactor feeds the charger side through a resistor so the voltage can rise gently towards pack voltage; without it the main contacts would arc. The code collects faults of this branch that fit none of the more specific siblings. No manufacturer documents P0E6A publicly.",["Precharge resistor burnt open or overheated","Worn or sluggish precharge contactor in the charging path","Charger-side voltage measurement reading wrongly"],["Charging session aborts right at the start","Charging not possible, driving usually still possible"],4,0,["P0E6B","P0E6C","P0E6D","P0D26","P0AE1"],"מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E6B:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Circuit Stuck Closed","P0E6B means the precharge contactor of the charging system appears to stay closed although it has been told to open, usually because its contacts have welded. The battery management notices this when the charger side carries voltage before any precharge was requested, or when it does not drop after switch-off. That is a safety matter: the charging path may remain live. No manufacturer documents P0E6B publicly.",["Precharge contactor contacts welded together","Coil kept energised by a control line fault","Residual voltage from another high-voltage unit misread as a closed contactor"],["Charging blocked and high-voltage warning displayed","Vehicle may refuse to switch to ready mode"],4,2,["P0E6A","P0E6C","P0AE2"],"מעגל מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — תקוע במצב סגור"],P0E6C:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Circuit Stuck Open","P0E6C reports that the precharge contactor of the charging system does not close, or that no current flows through the precharge branch, even though the command was given. The charger side then cannot be brought up to pack voltage, the main charging contactors stay open and the session ends before any energy flows. No manufacturer documents P0E6C publicly.",["Open precharge resistor interrupting the branch","Burnt or contaminated contactor contacts not conducting","Contactor coil not pulling in due to low supply"],["Charging stops a few seconds after plugging in","Repeated clicking from the battery area at charge start"],4,0,["P0E6A","P0E6B","P0D26","P0D09","P0AE3"],"מעגל מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — תקוע במצב פתוח"],P0E6D:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Control Circuit/Open","P0E6D concerns the low-voltage side of the charging precharge contactor: the coil circuit through which the control unit switches it, typically interrupted. Unlike P0E6C, which judges what happens on the high-voltage side, this code comes from the driver output itself. No manufacturer documents P0E6D publicly.",["Broken coil control wire or unplugged connector","Open winding in the contactor coil","Failed driver stage in the battery management unit"],["Charging cannot be started","High-voltage system fault message"],4,0,["P0E6E","P0E6F","P0E70","P0D0A","P0AE4"],"מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E6E:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E6E is stored when the coil circuit of the charging precharge contactor neither shows a clean short nor an open, but its electrical behaviour does not match the command: coil current outside the expected window, a slow pull-in, or feedback that lags behind the switching command. Rising contact resistance in the connector, a coil with changed resistance or a weak supply to the driver are typical.",["Increased contact resistance in the coil connector","Contactor coil resistance out of specification","Low supply voltage at the contactor driver"],["Charging starts only on some attempts","Fault appears mostly in cold weather or with a weak 12 V battery"],4,0,["P0E6D","P0E6F","P0E70","P0AE5"],"מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E6F:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Control Circuit Low","P0E6F points to the coil control line of the charging precharge contactor sitting too low, most likely shorted to ground. With a high-side driver the coil then cannot be energised, so precharge never starts; the driver itself may shut down to protect itself. No manufacturer documents P0E6F publicly.",["Coil control wire shorted to ground inside or near the battery","Shorted turns in the contactor coil","Driver stage switched off by its overcurrent protection"],["Charging does not start, contactor click missing","High-voltage fault message when plugging in"],4,0,["P0E6D","P0E6E","P0E70","P0AE6"],"מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E70:["Battery Charging System Precharge Contactor Control Circuit High","P0E70 is the high-side counterpart of P0E6F: the coil line of the charging precharge contactor carries voltage although the driver is off. A short to a 12 V feed can energise the coil without a command, which would let the precharge branch conduct unasked; the battery management therefore usually locks out charging. No manufacturer documents P0E70 publicly.",["Coil control wire shorted to a 12 V supply","Driver stage in the battery management unit stuck on","Open ground return of the coil leaving the line at supply level"],["Charging locked out with a high-voltage fault message","Contactor clicks without a charging request"],4,0,["P0E6D","P0E6E","P0E6F","P0AE7","P0D0A"],"מגען טעינה מוקדמת מערכת טעינת סוללה — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E71:["Generator Torque Delivered Performance","P0E71 compares the torque that the hybrid generator or motor-generator actually provides with the value requested by the hybrid control. If the two drift apart beyond a calibrated margin, the code is set.",["Fault in the inverter stage feeding the generator","Rotor position sensor (resolver) delivering a wrong angle","Control software needing an update","Damaged generator winding or internal contamination"],["MIL and service wrench lamp on (Ford)","Reduced power or emergency running mode","Engine runs more often, hybrid assistance missing"],4,3,["P0A92","P0A90","P061B"],""],P0E72:["Control Pilot Signal Low Frequency","P0E72 refers to the pulse train that an AC charging station places onto the pilot wire. Under SAE J1772, matched by IEC 61851-1, this is a rectangular ±12 V signal at 1 kHz, and its duty cycle tells the car how much current it may draw. The vehicle measures the period of this signal;",["Charging station or portable charger generating an off-spec pilot","Faulty charging adapter or extension in the cable path","Pilot measuring circuit in the vehicle charger misreading"],["AC charging does not start or stops at one station","Charging works normally at other stations"],1,0,["P0E73","P0CF4","P0CF3"],""],P0E73:["Control Pilot Signal High Frequency","P0E73 is set when the control pilot pulses from the charging station arrive faster than the nominal 1 kHz. Above the permitted band the vehicle can no longer be sure that it decodes the duty cycle, and with it the allowed charging current, correctly; in practice it refuses or ends the session.",["Electrical interference or ringing on the pilot line","Charging station with a faulty pilot oscillator","Damaged charging cable or corroded charge port pin","Faulty pilot input in the on-board charger"],["AC charging refused or stopped shortly after start","Fault tied to a specific station or cable"],1,0,["P0E72","P0CF4","P0CF6"],""],P0E74:["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Internal Isolation Fault","P0E74 reports that the insulation between the cells or internal high-voltage parts of the traction battery and its housing has become too weak, as measured inside the pack. P0AA6, by contrast, covers isolation of the whole high-voltage system.",["Coolant leak or water ingress inside the battery pack","Chafed or damaged insulation of internal high-voltage wiring","Faulty isolation measurement in the battery management"],["Battery or high-voltage warning lamp on","Vehicle may not enter ready mode or blocks charging","Electric driving disabled"],4,2,["P0AA6","P0DAA"],""],P0E75:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Circuit/Open","P0E75 is the basic electrical code for coolant bypass valve A: the control unit finds that the output switching the valve, or the wire leading to it, does not form a closed circuit, in most cases because of an open. Valves of this kind usually divert coolant between loops, for example between the engine and a heater loop that keeps the cabin warm while a hybrid drives electrically.",["Broken wire or loose connector on the valve A control circuit","Burnt or internally open coil/motor in valve A","Coolant or water ingress corroding the valve connector pins","Faulty output driver in the engine or hybrid control module"],["Check engine light","Little or no cabin heat, especially in electric mode","Engine warms up more slowly or runs more often than usual"],2,1,["P0E76","P0E77","P0E78","P0E7B","P2682"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ A — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E76:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Low","With P0E76 the voltage on the control line of coolant bypass valve A stays lower than the driver expects for its current state, which points to a short to ground somewhere in the harness or inside the actuator. What happens next depends on the driver layout: a low-side output may leave the valve energised all the time, a high-side output can no longer move it at all, and the driver may switch off to protect itself.",["Chafed control wire touching body or engine ground","Internal short in the valve A actuator","Moisture bridging pins in the valve connector"],["Check engine light","Valve stays in one position, heating behaves oddly","Possible blown fuse if the short is hard"],2,1,["P0E75","P0E77","P0E78","P0E79","P2682"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E77:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Circuit High","P0E77 is logged when valve A's drive line carries more voltage than the driver should allow, typically because the wire has rubbed through against a 12 V feed. A low-side driver then sees battery voltage while it is trying to pull the line down and usually reports overcurrent; with some designs an open line can float high and end up here too. In either case the valve no longer follows commands reliably.",["Control wire shorted to a battery or ignition feed","Open circuit that the driver interprets as high","Faulty valve A actuator electronics"],["Check engine light","Cabin heat unstable or missing"],2,1,["P0E75","P0E76","P0E78","P0E7D","P2683"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E78:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E78 is the plausibility code for the actuator circuit of bypass valve A. Neither an open nor a short is present, yet current, feedback or response time of the actuator do not fit what the module has commanded: a coil whose resistance has drifted, a motor that labours against a sticky shaft, or a supply that sags under load.",["Deposits or wear making the valve A mechanism sluggish","High resistance at connector or ground of the valve circuit","Low supply voltage to the actuator under load"],["Check engine light","Heating output varies from trip to trip"],2,1,["P0E75","P0E76","P0E77","P0E79","P0E7A","P0E7E"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E79:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Stuck/Open","P0E79 reports that bypass valve A apparently remains open although the module has asked it to close. The control unit cannot see the valve directly; it infers the position from temperatures, typically by comparing two coolant sensors that should drift apart when the loops are separated. No manufacturer documents P0E79 publicly.",["Valve A jammed open by debris or a broken internal part","Actuator no longer moves the valve","Hoses swapped at the valve after a repair","Skewed coolant temperature sensor misleading the check"],["Weak cabin heat in electric mode","Engine starts more often to supply heat","Check engine light"],2,1,["P0E7A","P0E78","P0E7F","P26AB"],""],P0E7A:["Coolant Bypass Valve A Control Stuck Closed","P0E7A is the reverse of P0E79: valve A should open and let coolant pass, but the temperature picture says it has stayed shut. Depending on what the valve connects, the result is a loop that gets no flow from the engine side, so heater output or heat transfer drops, or a branch that heats up because it is cut off from circulation. No manufacturer documents P0E7A publicly.",["Debris or deposits blocking valve A","Actuator or gear stuck in the closed position","Air pocket after a coolant service blocking flow"],["Little or no cabin heat","Rising coolant temperature in the affected loop","Check engine light"],2,1,["P0E79","P0E78","P0E80","P26B1"],"בקרת שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ A — תקוע במצב סגור"],P0E7B:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Circuit/Open","P0E7B covers an interrupted control circuit of the second coolant bypass valve, B. The module sees no proper load on its output, so the valve cannot be positioned and stays wherever it last was or springs back to its rest position.",["Broken wire or unplugged connector at valve B","Internal open in the valve B actuator","Missing supply or ground at a networked valve actuator"],["Check engine light","Heating or engine warm-up behaves differently"],2,1,["P0E7C","P0E7D","P0E7E","P0E75","U063C"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0E7C:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Circuit Low","P0E7C says that the wiring which operates bypass valve B reads too low, typically because a wire or the actuator itself has a ground fault. Stellantis documents the code: bulletin 18-071-26 (revisions A and B, October 2026) for 2025 RAM 1500 pickups fitted with the Hurricane straight-six, SO or HO twin-turbo and bulletin 18-082-26 for the 2025 Wagoneer and Grand Wagoneer name it among the faults cured by new engine…",["Engine control software needing the update from the manufacturer bulletin (Stellantis 3.0 Hurricane)","Valve B control wire shorted to ground","Internal short in the valve B actuator"],["Check engine light","Often no noticeable driving symptom"],2,1,["P0E7B","P0E7D","P0E7E","P0E76","U063C"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0E7D:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Circuit High","P0E7D is stored when the line driving valve B sits above its expected level, for instance with the wire chafed against a powered line or a driver that cannot pull the circuit down. Stellantis names this code in the same software bulletins as its low sibling: 18-071-26 for the 2025 RAM pickup with Hurricane power, plus 18-082-26 (SO engine) and 18-083-26 (HO engine) for the 2025 Wagoneer and Grand Wagoneer.",["Outdated engine control software (Stellantis bulletins 18-071-26, 18-082-26, 18-083-26)","Valve B control wire shorted to a voltage source","Open circuit pulled high by the module input"],["Check engine light","Other stored codes after the same software issue"],2,1,["P0E7B","P0E7C","P0E7E","P0E77","U063C"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0E7E:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0E7E marks a control circuit of coolant bypass valve B that is electrically intact but does not respond as calibrated: actuator current, feedback or timing lie outside the window the software expects. Stellantis lists P0E7E most often of the valve B codes.",["Engine control calibration fixed by bulletin 18-106-25 or 18-071-26 (Stellantis)","Valve B actuator moving slowly or not reaching its target","Corroded connector raising circuit resistance"],["Check engine light","Usually no change in driving behaviour"],2,1,["P0E7B","P0E7C","P0E7D","P0E7F","P0E78"],"שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל הבקרה"],P0E7F:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Stuck/Open","P0E7F means coolant bypass valve B is judged to stay open while the module wants it closed. Stellantis documents it on the Chrysler Pacifica plug-in hybrid with the 3.6 litre engine: bulletin 07-008-23 (June 2023) covers cars built from 3 November 2022 to 10 February 2023, where machining debris from the cylinder head water jackets can collect inside the three-way valve.",["Machining debris lodged in the three-way coolant valve (Pacifica Hybrid, bulletin 07-008-23)","Worn or broken internals in valve B","Calibration issue addressed by an engine control update (3.0 Hurricane)"],["MIL on","Electric drive mode unavailable, engine runs instead","Reduced or unstable cabin heat"],2,3,["P0E80","P0E7E","P0E79","U063C"],""],P0E80:["Coolant Bypass Valve B Control Stuck Closed","P0E80 reports that bypass valve B has stayed shut although flow through it is requested. Stellantis gives the criterion for the Jeep Wrangler plug-in hybrid (2021 to 2023): the code sets once engine coolant and the inlet of the electric coolant heater differ by more than 40 °F, about 22 K.",["Hoses at the three-way valve shifted by one port (Wrangler 4xe, bulletin 07-006-23)","Valve blocked by machining debris (Pacifica Hybrid, bulletin 07-008-23)","Valve B actuator seized in the closed position"],["Check engine light","Intermittent loss of cabin heat","Electric mode may be disabled"],2,3,["P0E7F","P0E7E","P0E7A","U063C"],"בקרת שסתום מעקף נוזל קירור‏ B — תקוע במצב סגור"],P0E81:["Auxiliary Cooling System Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","P0E81 is the general circuit code for temperature sensor A in an auxiliary cooling loop, a coolant circuit separate from the main engine loop, such as a heater loop of a plug-in hybrid or a loop that cools electric drive parts. The module sees a signal it cannot use at all, without classing it as too low, too high or implausible. No manufacturer documents P0E81 publicly.",["Loose, corroded or unplugged sensor connector","Failed temperature sensor A","Damaged signal or reference wire"],["Check engine light","Heater or auxiliary pump runs on default values"],1,1,["P0E82","P0E83","P0E84","P2183","P0EA7"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מערכת קירור עזר‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E82:["Auxiliary Cooling System Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0E82 is set when temperature sensor A of the auxiliary cooling loop delivers a clean electrical signal whose value does not make sense against other temperatures. No manufacturer documents P0E82 publicly, but Ford's 2019 hybrid summary spells out such a plausibility test for its heater-loop sensor ECT2 under P2183. After at least 360 minutes of soak both coolant sensors must agree within 30 °F;",["Sensor A reading drifted out of tolerance","Stuck bypass valve keeping the loops from mixing","Low coolant or air around the sensor tip","Corroded terminals shifting the signal"],["Check engine light","Cabin heat regulation off target"],2,1,["P0E81","P0E83","P0E84","P2183","P0E7F"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מערכת קירור עזר‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E83:["Auxiliary Cooling System Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0E83 means the signal voltage of temperature sensor A in the auxiliary cooling loop has fallen below its valid floor. Coolant sensors are normally NTC thermistors whose resistance drops as they warm, so a low voltage reads as extremely hot coolant. When the value is beyond anything physically possible, the cause is usually a signal wire touching ground or a shorted sensor rather than real overheating.",["Sensor signal wire shorted to ground","Internally shorted sensor A","Genuinely very hot coolant in the auxiliary loop"],["Check engine light","Coolant pump or fan running continuously","Implausibly high temperature shown on a scan tool"],1,1,["P0E81","P0E82","P0E84","P2184"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מערכת קירור עזר‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E84:["Auxiliary Cooling System Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0E84 is the high-voltage case for sensor A of the auxiliary loop: the signal sits near the reference voltage, which an NTC thermistor would only produce at extreme cold. Far more often the circuit is open, from an unplugged connector, a broken wire or a sensor with an internal break, or the signal line is shorted to a voltage source.",["Unplugged connector or broken sensor wire","Sensor A open internally","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"],["Check engine light","Scan tool shows the loop at minimum temperature","Less precise cabin heating"],1,1,["P0E81","P0E82","P0E83","P2185"],"חיישן טמפרטורת נוזל קירור מערכת קירור עזר‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E85:["Battery Charger B Temperature Too High","P0E85 reports that the second charger stage or second on-board charger, labelled B, has become hotter than its own protection limit allows. Like the A counterpart P0D24, it is a heat verdict, not a sensor fault: the charger trusts its reading and cuts back power or stops to save its semiconductors. No manufacturer documents P0E85 with set conditions in public sources.",["Too little coolant or air flow through charger B (low coolant, pump, blocked fins)","Internal defect in the power stage of charger B","Long full-power charging in very hot surroundings"],["AC charging slows down noticeably or stops before the battery is full","Charging fault message or flashing charge-port indicator"],4,0,["P0D24","P2DB6","P0E86","P0E89"],"טמפרטורת מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0E86:["Battery Charger B Input Voltage Too Low","P0E86 means charger B sees less voltage at its input than it needs to work, although charging has been requested. It mirrors P0D27, which carries no letter and is used for the first charger. No manufacturer documents P0E86 publicly. Ford's plug-in hybrid summary (OBDSM1901) does list the opposite case on the same input, P0E87, which shows that the B input codes are in real use.",["Weak supply at the socket or charging station, long or undersized house wiring","Faulty charging cable, adapter or extension lead","Worn or burnt contacts in the charge inlet or its wiring to the charger","Voltage measurement inside charger B reads too low"],["AC charging does not start or stops shortly after starting","Charging works at another socket or station"],2,0,["P0E87","P0D27","P0E8B","P0E88"],"מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0E87:["Battery Charger B Input Voltage Too High","P0E87 flags an input voltage for charger B above what the charger accepts. Ford uses the code in its plug-in hybrid OBD summary (OBDSM1901) in an unusual way: it is part of a plausibility test run on the battery controller (BECM) and judges the charging voltage request that reaches the charger.",["Implausible charging voltage request from the battery control module (Ford logic)","Software fault in the charger or battery control module","Real overvoltage from the supply or charging station"],["Charging is refused or ends early","Amber wrench lamp in the instrument cluster (Ford)"],2,0,["P0E86","P0D28","P0E8B","P0E89"],"מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0E88:["Battery Charger B Input Current Too Low","P0E88 is set when charger B takes in less current than it should for the charging power it is asked to deliver, with voltage present. Its counterpart without a letter is P0D29. No manufacturer documents P0E88 in public sources. The neighbour P0E89 for too much current is documented by Ford, Toyota, Isuzu and Mercedes, so the input current of a B charger is clearly monitored.",["Charger B power stage does not reach the requested power","High resistance at inlet contacts or in the charging cable","Charging station or cable signals a lower current limit than expected"],["AC charging takes much longer than usual","Charging stops or a charging fault is shown"],2,0,["P0E89","P0D29","P0E8A","P0E86"],"זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך נמוך מדי"],P0E89:["Battery Charger B Input Current Too High","P0E89 reports more input current into charger B than permitted, and it is the best documented code of this series. Toyota lists P0E8919 on 2021 to 2023 RAV4 Prime XSE models with the 6.6 kW charger, together with P0D2A19 and PFC boost faults; the MIL is on, plug-in charging fails and the charger assembly is replaced (T-SB-0067-22).",["Defective power factor correction or power stage in the charger (Toyota RAV4 Prime)","Calibration or software of the vehicle or charging control unit (Isuzu VCU, Mercedes DC charging unit, Ford request logic)","Current ripple or overcurrent from the charging station (Mercedes)"],["MIL or EV system warning lamp on","Plug-in charging stops or does not work at all","Vehicle does not start after DC fast charging (Mercedes)"],4,1,["P0E88","P0D2A","P0E8A","P0E87"],"זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0E8A:["Battery Charger B Input Current Sensor Circuit","P0E8A concerns the measurement, not the current itself: the sensor that tells charger B how much current enters at its input delivers no usable signal. It is the general circuit code; range, low and high faults of the same sensor sit at P2D85, P2D83 and P2D84, and the sensor of charger A uses P0D38. No manufacturer documents P0E8A publicly.",["Current sensor or its evaluation inside charger B defective","Open or shorted sensor wiring where the sensor is external","Charger software misjudges the sensor signal"],["AC charging does not start or is aborted","Charging fault indication; driving remains possible"],4,0,["P2D83","P2D84","P2D85","P0D38","P0E88","P0E89"],"חיישן זרם כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E8B:["Battery Charger B Input Voltage Sensor Circuit","P0E8B covers an electrical fault in the voltage measurement at the input of charger B: the controller gets no valid reading of the supply voltage. Range, low and high faults of this sensor have their own codes, P2E84, P2E85 and P2E86; charger A uses P0D3D for its first input voltage sensor. Some code lists call it input voltage sensor A of charger B. No manufacturer documents P0E8B publicly.",["Voltage sensing circuit inside charger B defective","Loose or corroded connector, damaged sensing lead","Charger software fault"],["Plug-in charging is not possible","Charging fault message or charge-port indicator signals a fault"],4,0,["P2E84","P2E85","P2E86","P0D3D","P0E86","P0E87"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E8C:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit","The inverter of drive motor A may measure its high-voltage DC level at more than one point; sensor B is the second of those channels. P0E8C is the general circuit code for it, used when the signal is missing or electrically invalid without a clear low, high or plausibility verdict. Sensor A of the same inverter uses P0D2D, and a mismatch between both is P2E1A. No manufacturer documents P0E8C itself.",["Sensing circuit for voltage channel B inside the power electronics defective","Low-voltage connector or harness of the inverter loose or damaged","Software fault in the motor control unit"],["Hybrid or EV system warning together with the MIL","Reduced electric drive power or vehicle not ready to drive"],4,3,["P0E8D","P0E8E","P0E8F","P0E90","P0D2D","P2E1A"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E8D:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0E8D says that voltage sensor B of the drive motor A inverter delivers a value that is electrically fine but disagrees with other readings of the same bus. Mitsubishi details the check for the 2023 Outlander PHEV, where sensor B is the main channel at capacitor 2 (V2M): with the boost converter switched off and the unit powered with 8 V or more for 0.5 s, a gap of at least 100 V to the rear motor unit, or of at…",["Fault in the power electronics or its voltage measurement (Mitsubishi, Audi)","Drift of voltage channel B so that it no longer matches the other channels","Wrong reference value from another high-voltage unit (rear motor unit, battery management)"],["Red high-voltage system warning at start, stop safely (Audi)","MIL and hybrid warning, reduced or no electric drive"],4,3,["P0E8C","P0E8E","P0E8F","P0D2E","P2E1A","P0C0C"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E8E:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Low","P0E8E means the output of inverter voltage sensor B for drive motor A has fallen to the bottom end of its scale. On the 2023 Outlander PHEV Mitsubishi assigns it to the main measuring channel of capacitor 2 (V2M) in the power drive unit and sets it when that signal stays at no more than 0.45 V for 0.16 s, provided the unit has at least 8 V supply; monitoring runs continuously.",["Measuring circuit for capacitor 2 inside the power drive unit failed","Signal line shorted to ground or open","No high voltage on the bus because of a blown fuse or open contactor path"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Vehicle not ready to drive or only engine power available"],4,3,["P0E8F","P0E8C","P0E8D","P0D2F"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E8F:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit High","P0E8F is the upper-limit fault of inverter voltage sensor B on drive motor A. Mitsubishi's 2023 Outlander PHEV sets it when the main channel at capacitor 2 (V2M) reports at least 4.75 V for 0.16 s, again with the unit supplied at 8 V or above, and lists it as a range check maximum.",["Measuring circuit for capacitor 2 inside the power drive unit defective","Signal shorted to sensor supply or lost sensor ground","Actual overvoltage on the high-voltage bus"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Electric drive switched off or strongly reduced"],4,3,["P0E8E","P0E8C","P0E8D","P0D30","P0C79"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E90:["Drive Motor A Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E90 is meant for a voltage sensor B signal of the drive motor A inverter that flickers, jumps or drops out for moments without staying wrong long enough for the low or high code. No manufacturer documents P0E90 publicly.",["Loose or corroded low-voltage connector on the inverter","Chafed or partly broken signal lead","Measuring circuit in the power electronics failing intermittently with heat or vibration"],["Hybrid warning that comes and goes","Short power interruptions or jerky electric drive"],4,3,["P0E8C","P0E8D","P0E8E","P0E8F","P0D31"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ A B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E91:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit","P0E91 is the circuit code for the second DC voltage channel, sensor B, in the inverter of drive motor B, usually the second electric machine or the rear axle motor. It applies when this signal is absent or electrically unusable without a more precise verdict; its partner channel A is coded P0DA3, and the matching code for drive motor A is P0E8C.",["Voltage channel B measuring circuit in the motor B inverter defective","Connector or harness of the motor B inverter loose or damaged","Software fault in the motor B control unit"],["Hybrid or EV warning with MIL","Loss of the second drive motor (for example no all-wheel drive), reduced power"],4,3,["P0E92","P0E93","P0E94","P0E95","P0DA3","P0E8C"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E92:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0E92 is stored when voltage sensor B of the drive motor B inverter gives a reading inside its electrical range that does not fit the other voltage values of the high-voltage system. No manufacturer documents P0E92 publicly.",["Voltage channel B of the motor B inverter drifts","Internal fault of the motor B inverter or motor unit","Wrong comparison value from battery management or the front power electronics"],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","Second drive motor switched off, reduced acceleration"],4,3,["P0E91","P0E93","P0E94","P0DA4","P0E8D"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E93:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Low","With P0E93 the controller sees the B voltage channel in the motor B inverter stuck near the bottom of its signal range, as happens with a short to ground, an open signal path or a failed measuring circuit. No manufacturer documents P0E93 publicly.",["Measuring circuit for channel B in the motor B inverter failed","Signal line shorted to ground or open","High-voltage cable or connection to motor B open, no voltage at the inverter"],["MIL and EV system warning","Rear or second motor without power, front-drive only"],4,3,["P0E94","P0E91","P0E92","P0DA5","P0E8E"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E94:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit High","P0E94 records that voltage sensor B in the drive motor B inverter reports a value at the top of its signal range. A short from the signal to a supply, an interrupted sensor ground or a defective measuring stage are the usual explanations; a genuine overvoltage would normally show on the other channels too. No manufacturer documents P0E94 publicly.",["Measuring stage for channel B in the motor B inverter defective","Signal shorted to a supply or lost sensor ground","Real overvoltage on the high-voltage bus"],["MIL and EV system warning","Second drive motor deactivated, less traction and power"],4,3,["P0E93","P0E91","P0E92","P0DA6","P0E8F"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E95:["Drive Motor B Inverter Voltage Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E95 closes the sensor B series for drive motor B: the signal is unstable, with brief dropouts or spikes, rather than permanently low, high or implausible. No manufacturer documents P0E95 publicly.",["Corroded or loose connector on the motor B inverter","Harness damaged where it moves with the axle or suspension","Measuring circuit in the motor B inverter failing intermittently"],["EV or hybrid warning that appears and clears again","Second motor drops out briefly, uneven traction"],4,3,["P0E91","P0E92","P0E93","P0E94","P0DA7","P0E90"],"חיישן מתח אינוורטר מנוע הנעה‏ B B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E96:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor C Circuit","P0E96 opens the series for a third voltage measurement in a DC/DC converter, sensor C. It is the general circuit code, meant for a missing or electrically invalid signal; range, low, high and erratic faults of the same sensor follow from P0E97 to P0E9A. Where a converter has more than two voltage channels, for example input and output plus a redundant reading, C names the third.",["Measuring circuit for channel C inside the converter defective","Loose connector or damaged sensing lead","Software fault in the converter or its controller"],["12 V or converter warning, possibly with MIL","Start-stop or 12 V support restricted"],4,1,["P0E97","P0E98","P0E99","P0E9A","P0E31","P0E36"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E97:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","P0E97 is the plausibility code for the third voltage channel of a DC/DC converter: sensor C delivers an electrically valid value that does not agree with what the controller expects. Two makers document it. Audi names P0E9700 for the 2020-2021 Q5 and 2021 A7 plug-in hybrids, stored now and then by the J841 electronics of the electric drive, with symptom code 45146;",["Converter or power electronics software with too tight plausibility limits","Converter stuck in an error state after an incorrect wake-up","Drifting voltage measurement inside the converter"],["MIL on, often without a noticeable driving fault","12 V battery or high-voltage warning messages in the cluster","Fault entry appears sporadically and returns after clearing"],4,1,["P0E96","P0E98","P0E99","P0E9A","P0E32","P0E37"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E98:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor C Circuit Low","P0E98 is set when the C voltage channel of a DC/DC converter is stuck at the lowest end of its range, typically because the measuring line is grounded or the sensing stage has lost its supply. No manufacturer documents P0E98 publicly.",["Sensor C signal line shorted to ground","Measuring stage without supply or with an open signal path","Internal defect in the converter's measuring circuit"],["MIL on, depending on the manufacturer's rating","Converter output reduced or switched to a fallback value","Start-stop or 12 V support may be disabled"],3,1,["P0E99","P0E96","P0E97","P0E9A","P0E33","P0E38"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E99:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor C Circuit High","With P0E99 the controller finds the voltage sensor C reading of a DC/DC converter pinned near its upper limit. That points to the signal touching a battery feed or reference supply, or to a broken sensor ground that lets the input float up, rather than to a real converter overvoltage. No manufacturer documents P0E99 publicly.",["Sensor C signal shorted to battery voltage or reference supply","Open sensor ground letting the signal float high","Actual overvoltage from a faulty converter regulation"],["MIL and charging or 12 V warning possible","Converter limits or stops its output","Start-stop function unavailable"],3,1,["P0E98","P0E96","P0E97","P0E9A","P0E34","P0E39"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E9A:["DC/DC Converter Voltage Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E9A completes the sensor C voltage series of a DC/DC converter. It is meant for a signal that jumps, flickers or drops out for brief moments, which the low and high tests miss because they need a fault to persist. No manufacturer documents P0E9A publicly.",["Loose or corroded terminal in the sensor C path","Harness chafing intermittently at a moving point","Cracked solder joint in the converter's measuring circuit"],["Warning lamp that appears and disappears","Short interruptions of start-stop or 12 V support","Code often stored as history only"],3,1,["P0E96","P0E97","P0E98","P0E99","P0E35","P0E3A"],"חיישן מתח ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0E9B:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor B Circuit","P0E9B opens the series for the second current measurement of a DC/DC converter, sensor B. It covers a signal that is absent or unusable on the electrical level and that the controller cannot classify as low, high or implausible; those cases follow as P0E9C to P0E9F. No manufacturer documents P0E9B itself.",["Fault in the converter's internal current sensing","Connector or wiring fault on the sensor B path","Control software misjudging the signal"],["High-voltage or 12 V system warning","Converter output reduced, 12 V battery charged less","Possibly reduced drive power"],4,3,["P0E9C","P0E9D","P0E9E","P0E9F","P0E51","P0EA0"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0E9C:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0E9C means current sensor B of a DC/DC converter gives a value within its electrical window that does not match the operating state, for example a current that stays put while the load changes, or that differs from the figure the controller derives from voltages and switching. No manufacturer documents P0E9C publicly.",["Current sensor B drifting or with a shifted zero point","Fault in the converter power stage causing unexpected currents","Software with an unsuitable plausibility window"],["MIL, sometimes after a single trip","Reduced converter output and weak 12 V charging","Power reduction warning possible"],4,3,["P0E9B","P0E9D","P0E9E","P0E9F","P0E52","P0EA1"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0E9D:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor B Circuit Low","P0E9D reports that the signal of DC/DC converter current sensor B has dropped below its valid range. Porsche documents it for the Taycan of model years 2020 to 2024 as P0E9D00, current sensor 2 of the converter with a short to ground, logged by the HV power electronics on the front axle (A49), the rear axle (A50) or both.",["Current monitoring threshold set too low in the software","Sensor B signal shorted to ground","Sensor supply missing inside the power electronics"],["Red warning to stop the vehicle safely","Fault no longer active after an ignition cycle","Drive power reduced or vehicle not ready to drive"],4,3,["P0E9E","P0E9B","P0E9C","P0E9F","P0E53","P0EA2"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0E9E:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor B Circuit High","P0E9E flags the B current channel of a DC/DC converter reading above its ceiling: the raw signal lies above anything the sensor should output. With a Hall sensor whose zero lies mid-scale, this usually means the signal line touches the supply or the reference ground has opened. No manufacturer documents P0E9E publicly.",["Sensor B signal shorted to supply voltage","Open reference ground of the current sensor","Converter in an error state after wake-up, cleared by reset"],["Red 12 V battery or HV warning","Converter stops supplying the 12 V network","Reduced drive power possible"],4,3,["P0E9D","P0E9B","P0E9C","P0E9F","P0E54","P0EA3"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0E9F:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0E9F is reserved for a current sensor B signal of a DC/DC converter that is unsteady: it spikes, drops out or shows noise yet never stays out of bounds for long. Publicly, no maker describes P0E9F.",["Loose terminal or connector on the sensor B path","Solder joint that opens under heat or vibration","Electrical interference on the measuring line"],["Warnings that come and go","Brief converter derating","Code stored without a current fault"],4,3,["P0E9B","P0E9C","P0E9D","P0E9E","P0E55","P0EA4"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0EA0:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor C Circuit","P0EA0 covers circuit faults of a third current channel inside a DC/DC converter, sensor C. Converters with several phases or with separate input and output sensing can carry more than two current channels; the letter C simply names the third. It is used when that reading is missing or cannot be decoded and no sharper diagnosis fits; plausibility, too-low, too-high and unstable signals have P0EA1 to P0EA4.",["Defect in the converter's third current sensing channel","Damaged connector or wiring on the sensor C path","Software fault in the signal evaluation"],["Warning for the high-voltage or 12 V system","Converter limited to a reduced output","MIL depending on the manufacturer"],4,3,["P0EA1","P0EA2","P0EA3","P0EA4","P0E51","P0E9B"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EA1:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","P0EA1 stands for a sensor C current reading that is electrically plausible yet disagrees with the converter's operating point or with its sister channels. If a converter measures several phase currents, those values should track each other, and one channel that deviates is a typical trigger. No manufacturer documents P0EA1 publicly.",["Sensor C channel drifting away from the other channels","Fault in the converter phase measured by sensor C","Plausibility software in need of an update"],["MIL, possibly after one trip","Reduced 12 V charging current","Converter or drive power derated"],4,3,["P0EA0","P0EA2","P0EA3","P0EA4","P0E52","P0E9C"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EA2:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor C Circuit Low","P0EA2 is stored when current sensor C of a DC/DC converter reports a raw value under its lower limit. Because current sensors in these units normally place their zero-current output at mid-supply, a value near zero volts rarely means low current; it points to a ground short, a lost sensor supply or a broken signal path. No manufacturer documents P0EA2 publicly.",["Sensor C signal shorted to ground","Missing supply voltage of the current sensor","Monitoring threshold in the software set too tight"],["Warning lamp and possibly a stop request","Converter switched off or derated","12 V battery discharges while driving"],4,3,["P0EA3","P0EA0","P0EA1","P0EA4","P0E53","P0E9D"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EA3:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor C Circuit High","P0EA3 marks a current sensor C signal above the top of its accepted window. Electrically this means the signal line touching a voltage source, an open sensor ground or a failed output stage of the sensor; a genuine overcurrent of the converter is a different matter and has its own code, P0D33. No manufacturer documents P0EA3 publicly.",["Sensor C signal shorted to supply","Open ground of the current sensor","Converter error state after wake-up"],["12 V or high-voltage warning in the cluster","Converter output stopped","Reduced performance possible"],4,3,["P0EA2","P0EA0","P0EA1","P0EA4","P0E54","P0E9E","P0D33"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EA4:["DC/DC Converter Current Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0EA4 ends the current sensor C series and describes a signal that wavers or breaks off momentarily instead of failing steadily. No manufacturer documents P0EA4 publicly. A comparable published fault exists for the first channel: FCA bulletin 08-333-25 covers P0E55-00, the erratic code of current sensor A, on the 2025 Ram 1500 eTorque V6: there the 12 V battery loses charge, in bad cases until the pickup has to be…",["Loose plug or damaged pin on the sensor C path","Outdated control software evaluating the signal too strictly","Connection that opens with temperature changes"],["Sporadic warnings without a lasting fault","12 V battery not kept fully charged","Start-stop occasionally disabled"],4,3,["P0EA0","P0EA1","P0EA2","P0EA3","P0E55","P0E9F"],"חיישן זרם ממיר DC/DC‏ C — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0EA5:["DC/DC Converter B Performance","P0EA5 says that a second DC/DC converter, B, does not deliver what it is asked to, for example the commanded output voltage or current, although its sensors give no circuit fault. FCA documents it as DC/DC Converter 2 Performance for the 2019 Jeep Cherokee (KL) with the 3.2 litre V6 and engine start/stop: PCM flash bulletins 18-023-19 REV.",["Engine or powertrain control software needing an update","Internal fault of converter B","Poor supply or ground connection of converter B"],["MIL on","Engine does not restart after an auto-stop, start-stop disabled","Voltage dips in the 12 V network, for example audio dropouts"],2,1,["P0A94","P0EA6","P0D32","P0CA2"],"ממיר DC/DC‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EA6:["DC/DC Converter B Over Temperature","P0EA6 means the second DC/DC converter, B, has reached a temperature beyond its permitted limit. Unlike a temperature sensor code, the reading itself is accepted as true; the unit is simply too hot and will normally reduce or stop its output to protect itself.",["Cooling air or heat sink of converter B obstructed","Sustained high load on the 12 V network","Control software misjudging the temperature condition","Weak internal power stage of converter B"],["MIL on","Start-stop unavailable while the converter is hot","Electrical consumers affected during engine restart"],2,1,["P0D32","P0EA5","P0A94","P0D33"],"ממיר DC/DC‏ B — חום יתר"],P0EA7:["Auxiliary Cooling System Performance","P0EA7 reports that an auxiliary cooling loop, a coolant circuit kept apart from the main engine circuit, does not achieve the cooling effect the controller expects. Volvo documents the code for its T8 plug-in hybrids of model years 2016 to 2019 (XC90, S90, XC60, S60 and others): the engine control module stores P0EA773, fault type actuator stuck closed, and turns on the check-engine light.",["Thermostat in the auxiliary loop sticking closed (Volvo T8, earlier part design)","Low coolant level or trapped air in the auxiliary loop","Weak or failed auxiliary coolant pump","Module software reporting the fault wrongly (Ford Mustang GTD)"],["Check-engine light on","Often no noticeable change in driving behaviour","Possible power limits on electric drive parts that run too warm"],2,1,["P0E81","P0E82","P0E83","P0E84"],"מערכת קירור עזר — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EA8:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor B Circuit","P0EA8 is the general circuit code for the second high-voltage measurement of an electric A/C compressor, labelled sensor B. Such compressors draw their power from the traction battery and convert it in a built-in inverter; the module reads the supply voltage to control the motor and protect its power stage. Here the B signal is missing or unusable as a whole, without being classed as low, high or implausible.",["Interrupted voltage sense path inside or at the compressor","Fault on the compressor's inverter or control board","Poorly seated high-voltage or signal connector at the compressor"],["Air conditioning does not cool","Fault message for the climate system","Battery cooling via refrigerant may be unavailable during charging"],4,0,["P0EA9","P0EAA","P0EAB","P0EAC","P0D68"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EA9:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor B Circuit Performance","P0EA9 means the B voltage reading reported by the electric A/C compressor looks electrically valid but does not fit other information, usually the high voltage reported by the battery system that feeds the compressor directly. No manufacturer documents P0EA9 publicly.",["Drifting voltage measurement inside the compressor module","Faulty data exchange between compressor and hybrid controller","Wrong battery voltage reference from the hybrid or battery controller"],["Air conditioning switches off or works only now and then","Fault often recorded without any other symptom"],4,0,["P0EA8","P0EAA","P0EAB","P0D69","P2D41"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EAA:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor B Circuit High","P0EAA flags a B voltage reading at the top of, or beyond, the plausible range of the compressor supply. Since the battery cannot really deliver that much, the cause usually lies in the measurement itself: an open or shorted sense path, a failed voltage divider inside the compressor or a wrong reference. No manufacturer documents P0EAA publicly.",["Failed voltage divider or reference in the compressor module","Sense line shorted to a higher potential","Actual overvoltage on the high-voltage bus"],["Compressor stops, cabin is not cooled","Warning in the climate control display"],4,0,["P0EAB","P0EA9","P0EA8","P0D6A","P2D43"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EAB:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor B Circuit Low","P0EAB is set when the B voltage channel of the electric A/C compressor reports far less than the high-voltage battery actually supplies while the contactors are closed. Typical reasons are a sense line shorted to ground, a broken internal connection or a sensing stage without supply; a supply interrupted upstream, for instance by a high-voltage fuse, produces the same picture and should be ruled out first.",["Voltage sense line shorted to ground","High-voltage supply to the compressor interrupted (fuse, connector)","Measuring stage in the compressor module without supply"],["No cooling from the air conditioning","Charging power may be reduced if the battery cannot be cooled"],4,0,["P0EAA","P0EA9","P0EA8","P0D6B","P2D42"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EAC:["A/C Compressor Motor Voltage Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0EAC points to a B voltage signal that jumps or drops out for short moments instead of staying wrong. The module sees sudden changes no real battery voltage can make, or values that come and go. Loose high-voltage or signal connectors at the compressor, vibration damage to the harness, moisture in a plug and internal contact problems on the inverter board are the usual suspects.",["Loose or corroded connector at the compressor","Harness chafed or cracked by engine and road vibration","Cracked solder joint on the compressor control board"],["Air conditioning cuts out briefly and then resumes","Fault entry returns after clearing"],4,0,["P0EA8","P0EA9","P0EAA","P0EAB","P0D6C"],"חיישן מתח מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0EAD:["A/C Compressor Motor Current Sensor Circuit","P0EAD covers the current sensor of an electric A/C compressor, which measures how much current the motor drive takes from the high-voltage link. The controller needs this value to limit power and to shield the power stage and the high-voltage pack from overload. P0EAD is the general circuit code: the reading is absent or useless as a whole, with no low, high or plausibility verdict.",["Failed current sensing element in the compressor power module","Open internal connection between sensor and control board","Compressor or climate module software error"],["Compressor does not start, no cooling","Fault stored in the climate or compressor module"],4,0,["P0EAE","P0EAF","P0EB0","P0D6F"],"חיישן זרם מנוע מדחס מזגן — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EAE:["A/C Compressor Motor Current Sensor Range/Performance","P0EAE says the compressor current reading is electrically valid but does not fit the operating state, for example a value that does not match motor speed or load. Audi documents the code for the 2016 A3 e-tron: P0EAE00 is stored as a passive or sporadic entry in the Climatronic module J255, the MIL is lit at the request of another module over the vehicle network while the engine module holds no code, and P26FD00 may…",["Calculation error in the climate module software (Audi A3 e-tron, update available)","Drifting current measurement in the compressor","Internal compressor fault confirmed by the guided test"],["MIL on without an engine module fault code","Air conditioning may or may not be restricted","Entry stored as passive or sporadic"],4,1,["P0EAD","P0EAF","P0EB0","P26FD","P0D6F"],"חיישן זרם מנוע מדחס מזגן — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EAF:["A/C Compressor Motor Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0EAF reports that the compressor current signal sits at the bottom of its measuring range. In a running compressor this cannot be true, so the module suspects the measuring chain: a grounded signal path, a sensing chip without supply or an offset error inside the power module. No manufacturer documents P0EAF publicly.",["Current signal path shorted to ground","Current sensor without supply voltage","Offset error of the measurement in the power module"],["Compressor shut down, warm air from the vents","Climate system fault message"],4,0,["P0EB0","P0EAD","P0EAE","P0D6F"],"חיישן זרם מנוע מדחס מזגן — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EB0:["A/C Compressor Motor Current Sensor Circuit High","P0EB0 flags a current sensor signal at the top end of its range, beyond what the compressor drive could ever draw. It differs from a genuine overcurrent such as P0D6F, where the measurement is trusted and the motor is overloaded: here the signal itself is suspect, for example after a short to a supply voltage or a failed sensing element. No manufacturer documents P0EB0 publicly.",["Failed sensing element in the compressor inverter","Current signal shorted to a supply voltage","Real overload from a stiff compressor or excessive refrigerant charge"],["Compressor switches off right after starting","No cooling, fault message in the climate display"],4,0,["P0EAF","P0EAD","P0EAE","P0D6F"],"חיישן זרם מנוע מדחס מזגן — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EB1:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current Sensor Circuit","Electric A/C compressors run a three-phase permanent-magnet motor whose inverter measures the phase currents in order to commutate it and limit torque. P0EB1 is the general circuit fault of the phase U current sensor: the controller gets no usable signal and gives no finer verdict on it. P0EB1 is not described in any public maker document.",["Failed phase U current sensor in the compressor inverter","Open signal path between sensor and inverter controller","Fault on the inverter control board"],["Compressor does not run, cabin stays warm","Climate system warning"],4,0,["P0EB2","P0EB3","P0EB4","P0EBB","P0EC0","P0D7A"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EB2:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current Sensor Circuit High","P0EB2 is set when the phase U current sensor of the electric A/C compressor sends a signal above its valid window. Current sensors in such inverters often output a voltage centred on zero current, so a stuck-high output reads like a large current in one direction even with the motor at rest. No manufacturer documents P0EB2 publicly.",["Damaged Hall element of the phase U sensor","Phase U signal shorted to the sensor supply","Defect on the inverter board affecting the U channel"],["Compressor will not start or stops at once","No cooling during hot weather"],4,0,["P0EB3","P0EB1","P0EB4","P0EB5","P0EB8","P0D7A"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EB3:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0EB3 means the phase U current signal has fallen below its permitted range. Depending on the sensor design this looks like a large negative current or like no signal at all; either way the controller can no longer trust the U-phase value it needs to drive the motor smoothly. No maker documents P0EB3 publicly.",["Phase U sensing path shorted to ground","Supply to the phase U sensor missing","Internal fault in the compressor inverter"],["Air conditioning inoperative","Fault message after switching the A/C on"],4,0,["P0EB2","P0EB1","P0EB4","P0EB6","P0EB9","P0D7B"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EB4:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase U Current Sensor Range/Performance","P0EB4 concerns the plausibility of the phase U current reading of an electric A/C compressor. The value is in range but disagrees with what the controller can work out for itself: in a motor fed by three wires the phase currents must add up to roughly zero, and each phase should follow the commanded current pattern. A standing offset or a wrong gain while running are typical findings.",["Offset or gain drift of the phase U sensor","Ageing measurement circuit on the inverter board","Winding fault in the compressor motor distorting the currents"],["Compressor runs roughly or noisily before switching off","Reduced or intermittent cooling"],4,0,["P0EB1","P0EB2","P0EB3","P0EB7","P0EBA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ U — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EB5:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current Sensor Circuit High","P0EB5 is the high-range fault of the phase V current sensor in the compressor inverter. The reading lies above the window the sensor can produce in normal service, which points to a short towards supply voltage, a defective sensing element or an internal fault in the power module rather than to real overcurrent.",["Defective phase V sensing element","Phase V signal shorted towards supply voltage","Internal fault in the compressor power module"],["Compressor switched off, no cooling","Battery may warm up more during fast charging"],4,0,["P0EB6","P0EB7","P0EBB","P0EB2","P0EB8","P0D7C"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EB6:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0EB6 reports that the phase V current signal reads below its permissible minimum. The compressor's power module cannot regulate phase V without it and stops the motor. Candidates are a sensing path pulled to ground, a sensor that has lost its supply or a defect on the inverter board; a problem in the refrigerant circuit cannot produce this code. No manufacturer documents P0EB6 publicly.",["Phase V sensing path pulled to ground","Phase V sensor has lost its supply","Defect in the V channel of the inverter board"],["No air conditioning","Fault stored in the compressor control module"],4,0,["P0EB5","P0EB7","P0EBB","P0EB3","P0EB9","P0D7D"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EB7:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current Sensor Range/Performance","With P0EB7 the compressor's phase V current value passes the electrical limits but is not credible. The controller compares it with the other two phases and with the current it is commanding; a persistent offset, a reading that lags or is scaled wrongly, or a three-phase sum far from zero all point at the V sensor. No manufacturer documents P0EB7 publicly.",["Persistent offset or scaling error of the phase V sensor","Slow or filtered V-phase measurement on the inverter board","Fault in the V winding or its internal lead"],["Uneven compressor running or humming noise","Cooling weakens, then fails"],4,0,["P0EB5","P0EB6","P0EBB","P0EB4","P0EBA"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EB8:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current Sensor Circuit High","P0EB8 indicates that the phase W current sensor of the compressor inverter delivers a value above its usable range. Since the motor is not actually drawing such a current, a short to a positive supply in the sensing circuit or a failed sensor element is the most probable explanation. The W-phase series runs high, low and plausibility from P0EB8 to P0EBA, while the general W circuit code sits apart at P0EC0.",["Failed phase W sensor element","W-phase sensing circuit shorted to a positive supply","Fault in the W input of the inverter controller"],["A/C stops working, warning shown","Compressor will not restart until the fault is cleared"],4,0,["P0EB9","P0EBA","P0EC0","P0EB2","P0EB5","P0D7E"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EB9:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0EB9 is stored when the phase W current signal drops beneath its lower limit, for instance after the signal wire has made contact with ground or after the sensor has lost its feed. The value is then useless for motor control, and the compressor module shuts the drive down rather than run it on two phases of information. No manufacturer documents P0EB9 publicly.",["Phase W measuring line shorted to ground","Phase W sensor without supply","Defect of the W measurement inside the compressor module"],["No cooling, compressor silent","Climate fault indicated"],4,0,["P0EB8","P0EBA","P0EC0","P0EB3","P0EB6","P0D7F"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EBA:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current Sensor Range/Performance","P0EBA covers an implausible phase W current reading in an electric A/C compressor: the signal stays in range, yet it does not agree with the commanded current, the rotor position or the two other phases. No manufacturer documents P0EBA publicly. GM's 2011 Volt used a standstill check for sensor offsets on phase W, filed under the older codes P0D7E and P0D7F at plus or minus 68 A, 30 of 60 samples, Type B.",["Ageing phase W current sensor","Fault in the W measurement on the inverter board","Rotor position estimate disturbed, making the W current look wrong"],["Compressor runs unevenly, then stops","Cooling capacity drops"],4,0,["P0EB8","P0EB9","P0EC0","P0EB4","P0EB7"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EBB:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase V Current Sensor Circuit","P0EBB is the general circuit code for the compressor's phase V current sensor, numbered after the W-phase codes instead of at the start of the V series. The module receives no usable V-phase value at all and does not classify it further as high, low or implausible. Without that value the field-oriented control of the three-phase motor cannot work, so the drive stops. No manufacturer documents P0EBB publicly.",["Failed phase V sensor or inverter board","Open phase V sensing path","Loose internal plug between sensor and board"],["Compressor does not run","Air conditioning and possibly battery cooling unavailable"],4,0,["P0EB5","P0EB6","P0EB7","P0EB1","P0EC0","P0D7C"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ V — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EBC:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","P0EBC is the general circuit fault of the first refrigerant temperature sensor in the air conditioning loop: the control module gets no usable reading from it and does not narrow the fault down to high or low. Volkswagen lists P0EBC00 for the 2021 ID.4 as an electrical fault of refrigerant temperature sensor 1 in the A/C module (address 0008).",["Internal electrical fault of the refrigerant pressure/temperature sender (VW G395)","Corroded connector or damaged wiring to the sensor","Faulty sensor input in the climate control module"],["Air conditioning inoperative or not cooling","Fault entry stored in the climate control module"],4,0,["P0EBD","P0EBE","P0EBF","P0EC1","P0530"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ A — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EBD:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0EBD means the signal voltage of refrigerant temperature sensor A sits at the top of its range. With the usual NTC thermistor that equals an extremely cold reading, and in practice it points to an open signal path, a disconnected plug or a short to the reference voltage. No manufacturer documents P0EBD publicly.",["Open signal or ground wire, or unplugged connector","Thermistor open internally","Signal wire shorted to the reference or supply voltage"],["Compressor stays off or A/C output is reduced","Implausibly low refrigerant temperature in live data"],2,0,["P0EBE","P0EBC","P0EBF","P0EC2","P0538"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ A — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EBE:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0EBE is logged when the voltage from refrigerant temperature sensor A falls close to zero. For an NTC element a low voltage stands for high temperature, so the controller sees refrigerant that seems far too hot. Real heat at that level is unlikely; a signal wire chafed through to ground or a thermistor shorted internally is the usual finding. No manufacturer publishes P0EBE.",["Signal wire chafed and shorted to ground","Thermistor shorted internally","Moisture bridging the connector pins"],["A/C compressor switched off as a precaution","Live data shows an unrealistically hot refrigerant"],2,0,["P0EBD","P0EBC","P0EBF","P0EC3","P0537"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ A — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EBF:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","P0EBF reports that sensor A gives an electrically valid but implausible refrigerant temperature. Three makers name it. Audi stores P0EBF00 as passive or sporadic in the Climatronic module of the 2016 A3 e-tron; bulletin 2047037 blames a miscalculation in the refrigerant self-check and points to TSB 2046942. VW's 2021 ID.4 links it to the G395 sender, which is replaced.",["Software misjudgement after preconditioning or self-check (Volvo, Audi; update available)","Deviating pressure/temperature sender (VW G395)","Wrong refrigerant charge distorting the comparison values"],["Check engine lamp on (Volvo) or MIL requested by the climate module (Audi)","Fault stored as intermittent or sporadic","A/C may cool poorly or not at all"],4,1,["P0EBC","P0EBD","P0EBE","P0EC4","P0531","P0EAE","P0D71"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ A — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EC0:["A/C Compressor Motor Phase W Current Sensor Circuit","P0EC0 is the umbrella circuit code for the phase W current sensor in the inverter of an electric A/C compressor. It applies when no valid W-phase value arrives at all, without a split into high, low or implausible; those cases have their own codes P0EB8 to P0EBA. Like the phase V circuit code P0EBB it is numbered out of sequence. No manufacturer documents P0EC0 publicly.",["Failed W-phase current sensor or inverter board","Interrupted W-phase sensing path inside the inverter","Missing supply voltage for the current sensors"],["Electric A/C compressor does not start","No cabin cooling, battery cooling via refrigerant may be limited"],4,0,["P0EB8","P0EB9","P0EBA","P0EBB","P0EB1","P0D7E","P0D7F"],"חיישן זרם פאזת מנוע מדחס מזגן‏ W — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EC1:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0EC1 covers a circuit fault of a second refrigerant temperature sensor, found mainly on electrified cars whose heat pump or battery chiller measures the refrigerant at several points. The module receives no usable value from sensor B. No manufacturer publishes P0EC1 or says which of its sensors counts as B.",["Failed sensor B or combined pressure/temperature sender","Wiring, connector or shared LIN line fault","Mixed part versions on one LIN line after a repair"],["Heat pump or A/C switched off or limited","Fault stored in the climate control module"],4,0,["P0EC2","P0EC3","P0EC4","P0EBC"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EC2:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","With P0EC2 the signal of refrigerant temperature sensor B is stuck near the reference voltage. An NTC thermistor would only get there at extreme cold, so an open circuit somewhere in the loop is far more likely. No manufacturer documents P0EC2 publicly.",["Open signal or ground line between sensor B and module","Connector not fully seated after A/C work","Sensor B open internally"],["Very low refrigerant temperature shown for sensor B","Heating via heat pump or cooling unavailable"],2,0,["P0EC3","P0EC1","P0EC4","P0EBD","P0538"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EC3:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0EC3 stands for a sensor B signal pulled down toward ground. Because a thermistor of the NTC type lowers its resistance as it warms, the module reads a refrigerant temperature far above anything the system could reach. Typical culprits are a harness pinched against the body, water inside the plug or a sensor that has failed shorted. P0EC3 appears in no public manufacturer document.",["Harness pinched against the body, signal shorted to ground","Water ingress in the sensor plug","Sensor B shorted internally"],["A/C compressor held off, cabin not cooled","Charging or driving power possibly limited if battery cooling depends on the loop"],2,0,["P0EC2","P0EC1","P0EC4","P0EBE","P0537"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EC4:["A/C Refrigerant Temperature Sensor B Range/Performance","P0EC4 is a plausibility fault: sensor B delivers a voltage inside its normal window, yet the temperature it reports does not agree with other information. No manufacturer documents P0EC4 publicly.",["Sensor B drifting out of tolerance","Sensor not seated properly on the pipe, poor thermal contact","Incorrect refrigerant charge shifting the comparison values"],["Uneven or weak cooling, compressor cycling oddly","Fault often appears only after a long standstill"],2,0,["P0EC1","P0EC2","P0EC3","P0EBF","P0531"],"חיישן טמפרטורת גז מזגן‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EC5:["A/C Compressor Motor Speed Sensor Range/Performance","P0EC5 means the speed information of the electrically driven compressor motor is implausible: the motor does not turn at the speed the controller expects, or the reported value contradicts other data. Mazda uses the code on the 2024 to 2026 CX-90 PHEV and 2025 to 2026 CX-70 PHEV, describing P0EC5:00 in the PCM as an error signal coming from the compressor.",["Internal short in the electric compressor after overcurrent (Mazda PHEV)","Blown high-voltage A/C fuse cutting compressor power","Mechanical drag or lack of oil keeping the motor below target speed"],["A/C does not cool","Accompanying compressor voltage, communication or insulation codes"],4,0,["P0D6D","P0D6F","P0EAE","P26FD","U0605","P0AA6"],"חיישן מהירות מנוע מדחס מזגן — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EC6:["A/C Power Supply Relay Control Circuit/Open","P0EC6 concerns the control side of the relay that switches power to air conditioning components, typically the compressor or climate electronics. The module driving the relay coil notices that its output does not behave as commanded, most often because the coil circuit is open. No manufacturer documents P0EC6 publicly, and the exact wiring differs by maker.",["Relay coil burnt open or relay missing","Open control wire or loose relay socket terminal","Failed output driver in the controlling module"],["A/C or electric compressor completely without function","No relay click when A/C is requested"],2,0,["P0EC7","P0EC8","P0645"],"ממסר אספקת מתח מזגן — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0EC7:["A/C Power Supply Relay Control Circuit Low","P0EC7 is set when the control line of the relay feeding the A/C reads too low a voltage, usually because it is shorted to ground. What the driver feels depends on how the coil is switched: with a low-side driver a grounded line can keep the relay energised even with the A/C off, while with a high-side driver the relay simply cannot be switched on. No manufacturer documents P0EC7 publicly.",["Relay control line chafed and shorted to ground","Shorted turns in the relay coil","Output stage of the module stuck low"],["A/C power stays on with A/C switched off, or never switches on","Possible battery drain while parked if the relay stays energised"],2,0,["P0EC6","P0EC8","P0646"],"ממסר אספקת מתח מזגן — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0EC8:["A/C Power Supply Relay Control Circuit High","P0EC8 indicates that the relay control circuit for the A/C power supply carries too high a voltage, typically a short to a battery-fed wire. A low-side driver then pushes current straight into that short when it switches on; modern output stages detect the overcurrent, turn themselves off and log this code, so the relay stays open and the A/C components get no power.",["Control line shorted to a battery-fed wire","Relay coil with too low resistance overloading the driver","Incorrect relay type fitted"],["A/C has no power and stays off","Relay may stay energised permanently on high-side switched designs"],2,0,["P0EC6","P0EC7","P0647"],"ממסר אספקת מתח מזגן — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0EC9:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve A Stuck/Open","P0EC9 reports that refrigerant distribution valve A, a solenoid valve that splits refrigerant between branches of the A/C circuit, stays open or does not close on command. Such a fault is usually detected indirectly, because pressure or temperature behind the valve fails to react when it should be shut.",["Faulty self-test calculation in the climate control software (Audi A3 e-tron, fixed by update)","Valve held open by dirt, debris or a sticking armature","Weak solenoid coil that no longer pulls the valve fully shut"],["MIL on without a code in the engine control module","Code stored passive/sporadic in the climate control module","Reduced or uneven cooling in one branch, or no noticeable change at all"],4,1,["P2611","P2612","P2613","P266F","P0EE2","P0EBF","P0EAE"],""],P0ECA:["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor System - Multiple Sensor Correlation","P0ECA treats the temperature sensors of the hybrid or traction battery as one system: the battery controller compares the readings with each other and flags the group when one of them strays from the rest or shows an impossible value. Ford documents it for its 2019 hybrid and plug-in models.",["One battery thermistor drifting away from the others","Battery controller software misjudging the comparison (Bentley super capacitor, update 114)","Loose or corroded connector on the temperature sense harness inside the pack","Genuine local hot spot, e.g. a blocked cooling channel at one module"],["Hybrid or battery warning, MIL depending on manufacturer","Reduced electric power or charging rate","On the Bentayga, Dynamic Ride fault message and no roll compensation"],4,3,["P0ECB","P0A9C","P0AC6","P0A9E","P0AC8","P0A1F"],""],P0ECB:["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sensor System - Multiple Sensor Correlation","P0ECB indicates that the voltage measurements of the hybrid/EV battery contradict each other. Battery controllers usually capture the same voltage along more than one path, for example the whole pack directly and as the sum of all cell readings, or each half of the pack against its own cells; once these paths drift apart, at least one sensor must be wrong. No manufacturer documents P0ECB publicly.",["Drifting pack or half-pack voltage sensor in the battery controller","Loose or corroded cell sense lead falsifying the cell sum","Internal fault of the cell monitoring electronics","Measurement taken on the wrong side of a contactor that is not fully closed"],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Limited electric power, unstable state of charge display","Contactors may stay open, vehicle may not reach ready mode"],4,3,["P0ECA","P0ABB","P0B15","P0B1A","P0ECC","P0ED0"],""],P0ECC:["Hybrid/EV Battery E Voltage","P0ECC is the general voltage code for hybrid/EV battery E. The letters A to F mark separately monitored sub-batteries, modules or energy stores of one system; E is the fifth, so the code can only occur where a manufacturer divides its storage into at least five such units.",["Aged or damaged cells in unit E","Fault in the voltage sense leads or connectors of unit E","Battery controller measuring or software fault"],["Battery or hybrid warning, MIL","Reduced electric power and range"],4,3,["P0ECD","P0ECE","P0ECF","P0ED0","P0B2F","P0ABB"],"מתח סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E"],P0ECD:["Hybrid/EV Battery E Voltage Unstable","P0ECD flags an unstable voltage in hybrid/EV battery E: the value moves faster or further than the controller tolerates, whereas a lasting offset would set P0ECE or P0ECF. No manufacturer documents P0ECD publicly.",["Cell with high internal resistance whose voltage collapses under load","Loose busbar screw or cell connector in unit E","Corroded or intermittent voltage sense terminal","Battery controller software, curable by update"],["Intermittent battery warning, often only under load","Power drops during acceleration","MIL on"],4,3,["P0ECC","P0ECE","P0ECF","P0ED1","P0B24","P0BBE"],""],P0ECE:["Hybrid/EV Battery E Voltage Low","P0ECE sets when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery E remains below the window the controller expects for the present load and charge. Its counterparts are P0ECF for too high and P0ECD for unstable readings. No manufacturer documents P0ECE publicly.",["Unit E deeply discharged after long standstill or a high self-discharge cell","Internal short or capacity loss in a module of unit E","Balancing switch stuck on and draining one cell group","Voltage sense fault reading low"],["Reduced electric range and acceleration","Engine starts more often, EV mode unavailable","MIL and battery warning"],4,3,["P0ECC","P0ECD","P0ECF","P0ED2","P0B25","P0ABC"],"סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — מתח נמוך"],P0ECF:["Hybrid/EV Battery E Voltage High","P0ECF is stored when unit E of the hybrid/EV battery exceeds its upper voltage limit. It mirrors P0ECE, while unstable readings belong to P0ECD. No manufacturer documents P0ECF publicly. Ford's overcharge logic for its 2019 hybrids gives a sense of the limits involved: with the charge contactors open, a cell above 4.5 V on plug-in models or 4.35 V on full hybrids for 2 or 3 seconds counts as a fault under the…",["Cell balancing of unit E no longer works, group overcharged","Charge or regeneration control error","Voltage sense channel reading too high"],["Charging or regenerative braking blocked","Battery warning, MIL","Vehicle may switch off the high-voltage system"],4,3,["P0ECC","P0ECD","P0ECE","P0ED3","P0C30","P0ABD"],"סוללת מתח גבוה‏ E — מתח גבוה"],P0ED0:["Hybrid/EV Battery F Voltage","P0ED0 is the general voltage code of hybrid/EV battery F, the sixth and last unit in this letter series. It reports an implausible voltage of the unit without separating unstable (P0ED1), low (P0ED2) and high (P0ED3) readings. Only storage systems divided into six or more separately monitored units can produce it, and no manufacturer documents P0ED0 publicly.",["Aged or damaged cells in unit F","Open or high-resistance voltage sense lead of unit F","Cell monitoring electronics or software fault"],["MIL and battery warning","Reduced electric power"],4,3,["P0ED1","P0ED2","P0ED3","P0ECC","P0B2F","P0ECB"],"מתח סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F"],P0ED1:["Hybrid/EV Battery F Voltage Unstable","P0ED1 reports that the voltage of hybrid/EV battery F fluctuates beyond the permitted band instead of staying steadily off, which would be P0ED2 or P0ED3. No manufacturer documents P0ED1 publicly.",["Cell group of unit F drifting because balancing cannot keep up","Loose busbar or cell connector","Sense lead or connector with intermittent contact"],["Warning that comes and goes, often over bumps or under load","Short power reductions while driving","MIL on"],4,3,["P0ED0","P0ED2","P0ED3","P0ECD","P0B24","P0BBD"],""],P0ED2:["Hybrid/EV Battery F Voltage Low","P0ED2 means hybrid/EV battery F delivers less voltage than the controller predicts from current draw and state of charge. No manufacturer documents P0ED2 publicly. Published undervoltage limits exist for whole packs: GM's hybrid tables fail the pack below 24 V (P0ABC), and Ford sets P0A7D during cranking if its pack reads under 184.8 V on plug-in or 167.2 V on full hybrids, or if the weakest cell drops below 2.1 V…",["Aged module in unit F with lost capacity","Cell with internal leakage drained during standstill","High-resistance connection inside unit F dropping voltage under load"],["Less power in electric mode","Shorter electric range","MIL and battery warning"],4,3,["P0ED0","P0ED1","P0ED3","P0ECE","P0ABC","P0A7D"],"סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — מתח נמוך"],P0ED3:["Hybrid/EV Battery F Voltage High","P0ED3 is logged once battery F of the hybrid/EV system climbs above its permitted maximum voltage. It is the high member of the F series, next to P0ED2 (low) and P0ED1 (unstable). No manufacturer documents P0ED3 publicly. GM's hybrid tables show how strictly overvoltage is treated at pack level: above 479 V the P0ABD monitor fails within one trip.",["Balancing circuit of unit F failed, cells overcharged","Measuring channel of unit F reading too high","Charger or regeneration control overshooting"],["Charging refused or regenerative braking weak","MIL and battery warning"],4,3,["P0ED0","P0ED1","P0ED2","P0ECF","P0ABD","P0AFB"],"סוללת מתח גבוה‏ F — מתח גבוה"],P0ED4:["Generator Phase X Current","P0ED4 is the general current fault for phase X of the hybrid generator. J2012 names the generator phases U, V and W and adds a separate X, Y, Z series, which presumably serves machines with a second three-phase winding set fed by its own inverter bridge; which vehicles use it is not published, and no manufacturer documents P0ED4. Low and high cases are P0ED5 and P0ED6.",["Fault in the inverter bridge or current regulation for phase X","Phase X current sensor out of tolerance","Loose or damaged high-voltage connection of phase X"],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Reduced charging, engine may start less smoothly"],4,3,["P0ED5","P0ED6","P0ED7","P0EDA","P0A6F","P0DFA"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ X"],P0ED5:["Generator Phase X Current Low","P0ED5 means that phase X of the hybrid generator carries, or appears to carry, too little current while the generator is supposed to deliver torque. No manufacturer documents P0ED5 publicly. Mitsubishi publishes a comparable check for the U, V and W phases of the 2023 Outlander PHEV: with at least 50 A requested and the generator between 500 and 4,500 rpm, a phase current that stays within roughly 17 A of zero for…",["Open or loose phase X cable or terminal","Inverter power switch for phase X not conducting","Burnt or broken winding connection in the generator","Current sensor stuck near zero"],["Weak or no battery charging by the generator","Rough engine start or vibration from the generator","MIL and hybrid warning"],4,3,["P0ED4","P0ED6","P0ED8","P0EDB","P0A70","P0E0C"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ X — ערך נמוך"],P0ED6:["Generator Phase X Current High","P0ED6 is stored when the current in phase X of the hybrid generator exceeds its permitted limit. No manufacturer documents P0ED6 publicly. Ford gives figures for the comparable U, V and W codes P0A71, P0A74 and P0A77 on its 2019 power-split hybrids: the motor/generator control unit declares overcurrent once the generator current magnitude passes 300 A for 400 microseconds, or 15 A for 200 ms just after power-up,…",["Turn-to-turn or ground short in the phase X winding","Inverter power switch failed closed","Current sensor reading too high (false overcurrent)"],["Sudden hybrid system shutdown or loss of ready mode","MIL and hybrid warning"],4,3,["P0ED4","P0ED5","P0ED9","P0EDC","P0A71"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ X — ערך גבוה"],P0ED7:["Generator Phase Y Current","P0ED7 is the general current code for phase Y, the middle phase of the generator's X-Y-Z set. Without being narrowed to low (P0ED8) or high (P0ED9), it says that the Y current no longer behaves as the inverter controller expects. No manufacturer documents P0ED7 publicly, and it is not known which production machines label their phases X, Y and Z.",["Inverter fault affecting the phase Y bridge","Phase Y current sensor fault","Phase cables mixed up or poorly seated after repair work"],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Limited generator operation"],4,3,["P0ED8","P0ED9","P0ED4","P0EDA","P0A72"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Y"],P0ED8:["Generator Phase Y Current Low","P0ED8 reports too little current in phase Y of the hybrid generator. No manufacturer documents P0ED8 publicly. Ford's 2019 hybrid summary describes a phase circuit test at power-up for the U-V-W phases: the motor/generator control unit declares a phase circuit fault if less than 5 A is seen over the 100 ms test window.",["Open or corroded phase Y cable or terminal","Inverter switch for phase Y not turning on","Phase Y current sensor reading too low"],["Generator delivers less charging power","Uneven running or noise from the transaxle","MIL and hybrid warning"],4,3,["P0ED7","P0ED9","P0ED5","P0EDB","P0A73"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Y — ערך נמוך"],P0ED9:["Generator Phase Y Current High","P0ED9 is set when phase Y of the hybrid generator draws more current than allowed. No manufacturer documents P0ED9 publicly. Overcurrent in a generator phase is a protection event: the inverter must switch off within fractions of a millisecond, before its power semiconductors are destroyed. The scale is illustrated by Ford's limits for the U-V-W phases, 300 A for 400 microseconds in operation.",["Short in the phase Y winding or to the housing","Inverter switch stuck on","Current sensor reading too high","Chafed phase cable shorting to ground"],["Hybrid system shuts down, vehicle may lose ready mode","MIL and hybrid warning"],4,3,["P0ED7","P0ED8","P0ED6","P0EDC","P0A74"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Y — ערך גבוה"],P0EDA:["Generator Phase Z Current","P0EDA is the general current fault of phase Z, the last of the three X-Y-Z generator phases, without the detail of P0EDB (low) or P0EDC (high). No manufacturer documents P0EDA publicly. In a three-phase set the currents add up to almost zero at every instant, so a controller can calculate one phase from the other two or use the sum as a cross-check;",["Phase Z current sensor offset or failure","Inverter fault on the phase Z bridge","Poor contact at the phase Z high-voltage terminal"],["Hybrid warning, often with further phase or sum codes","Reduced charging and generator assist"],4,3,["P0EDB","P0EDC","P0ED4","P0ED7","P0DFA","P0A75"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Z"],P0EDB:["Generator Phase Z Current Low","P0EDB means phase Z of the hybrid generator shows less current than the commanded operating point calls for. No manufacturer documents P0EDB publicly. Besides an absolute floor, published checks also look at the gap between actual and target current: Mitsubishi's Outlander PHEV fails a U-V-W phase not only when it hovers near zero under demand but also when it misses its target current.",["Loose or corroded phase Z high-voltage terminal","Power switch for phase Z no longer conducting","Broken conductor in the phase Z winding","Phase Z current sensor offset"],["Humming or vibration from the hybrid transaxle","Reduced generator power and charging","MIL and hybrid warning"],4,3,["P0EDA","P0EDC","P0ED5","P0ED8","P0A76","P0E0C"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Z — ערך נמוך"],P0EDC:["Generator Phase Z Current High","P0EDC is the overcurrent code for phase Z of the hybrid generator and ends the X-Y-Z series. No manufacturer documents P0EDC publicly; the closest published logic is Ford's for its U-V-W generator phases (P0A71, P0A74, P0A77), which also flags any current above 15 A in the first 200 ms after power-up, a moment when the machine should be idle.",["Damaged or leaking power module for phase Z","Insulation fault or turn short in the phase Z winding","Current sensor with excessive gain"],["Hybrid system shutdown, engine may not start","MIL and hybrid warning, possibly with sum check codes"],4,3,["P0EDA","P0EDB","P0ED6","P0ED9","P0A77","P0DFA"],"זרם פאזת גנרטור‏ Z — ערך גבוה"],P0EDD:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack B Deterioration","P0EDD is the state-of-health verdict for the second section of a traction battery: section B has lost so much capacity or gained so much internal resistance that it falls below the maker's limit. P0A7F covers section A, P0EDE to P0EE1 sections C to F. Audi counts the Q5 PHEV (2020-2024) battery in groups and names P0EDD00 as group 2 deterioration in the battery management unit 008C;",["Battery module or cell group in section B aged beyond its limit","Battery controller software misjudging state of health (FCA reflash cases)","Pack defect covered by a warranty extension (Pacifica Hybrid)"],["MIL or hybrid system warning on","Noticeably shorter electric range","Engine runs more often, less electric assist"],4,3,["P0A7F","P0EDE","P0EDF","P0BBE","P0A80"],""],P0EDE:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack C Deterioration","P0EDE flags the third battery section, C, as worn out. The controller estimates each section's usable capacity and resistance from voltage behaviour under load and at rest; if section C drifts too far from its new-state values, it is judged deteriorated.",["Cells in battery group 3 aged (capacity loss, higher resistance)","Strong voltage spread inside the section","Controller estimate wrong, module still healthy"],["Check engine lamp on","Displayed electric range too low","Electric driving mode cannot be selected or ends early"],4,3,["P0EDD","P0EDF","P0A7F","P0BBD"],""],P0EDF:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack D Deterioration","P0EDF belongs to the fourth section of the traction battery. It is not a sensor or wiring code but a capacity and power judgement: the battery controller has concluded that section D can no longer deliver what the maker expects of a healthy unit. Audi maps P0EDF00 on the Q5 PHEV to battery group 4 in the battery management unit 008C.",["High-voltage module of group 4 worn out","Single weak cell pulling the section down","Evaluation logic of the battery controller"],["MIL on","Reduced electric range and power"],4,3,["P0EDE","P0EE0","P0EDD","P0A7F"],""],P0EE0:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack E Deterioration","With P0EE0 the controller reports ageing of section E, the fifth part of the traction battery. A section is usually a module or a group of cell blocks with its own monitoring; once its share of the pack has lost too much capacity or become too resistive, the whole battery can no longer reach its rated energy, which shows up first as shorter electric range.",["Capacity loss of battery group 5","Cell voltages in the section spread too far (often with P0BBD)","Faulty state-of-health estimate"],["Electric range shown too low","Engine warning lamp on","Less power in electric mode"],4,3,["P0EDF","P0EE1","P0A7F","P0BBD"],""],P0EE1:["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack F Deterioration","P0EE1 is the last code of the P0E range for section ageing and covers section F. The series runs A (P0A7F) and B to F (P0EDD to P0EE1); for further sections J2012 continues in the P2 block, with G and H as P2CCC and P2CCD. Audi uses exactly this chain on the Q5 PHEV: groups 1 to 8 of the battery are reported as P0A7F00, P0EDD00 to P0EE100, P2CCC00 and P2CCD00 in unit 008C, and P0EE100 is group 6.",["Module of battery group 6 aged","Section aged faster due to heat or poor cooling","Evaluation error of the battery controller"],["MIL on","Shorter electric range"],4,3,["P0EE0","P2CCC","P2CCD","P0A7F","P0EDD"],""],P0EE2:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve B Stuck/Open","P0EE2 says the second refrigerant distribution valve, B, stays open or fails to close. In plug-in hybrids and EVs such shut-off valves route refrigerant either to the cabin evaporator or to a chiller that cools the battery; Egelhof sells solenoid versions with 12 V or 24 V coils for exactly this use. The fault is inferred, since the module sees pressures or temperatures that would not occur with the valve shut.",["Thermal management software misjudging the valve (Audi J1024)","Valve seat contaminated or armature stuck open","Weak coil that no longer moves the armature"],["MIL on","Weaker cabin or battery cooling","Often no noticeable effect beyond the warning"],4,1,["P0EC9","P0EE3","P0EE4","P0EE5","P0EE6"],""],P0EE3:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve B Control Circuit/Open","P0EE3 concerns the wiring between the controlling module and refrigerant valve B, most often an interrupted path. With the output switched off, the driver normally sees a defined voltage through the coil; with it switched on, current flows. If neither picture appears, for instance because a pin has backed out of the connector or the coil winding is broken, the circuit is declared open.",["Loose, corroded or pushed-back pin at the valve connector","Broken coil winding in valve B","Broken wire in the harness to the valve"],["Warning lamp on","Valve B no longer switches, affected refrigerant branch stays as it is"],2,1,["P0EE4","P0EE5","P0EE6","P0EE2","P2611"],"שסתום חלוקת גז מזגן‏ B — מעגל הבקרה תקול או פתוח"],P0EE4:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve B Control Circuit Low","P0EE4 is stored when the control line of refrigerant valve B is pulled to a lower voltage than the driver commands, usually by a short to ground. With a high-side driver the valve would then stay energised or the output would be overloaded; with a low-side driver the coil may be switched on permanently. No manufacturer documents P0EE4 publicly.",["Control wire chafed through to ground","Brittle or corroded valve connector","Coil winding shorted to the valve body"],["Warning lamp on","Valve B behaves as if permanently energised or not at all"],2,1,["P0EE3","P0EE5","P0EE6","P2612"],"שסתום חלוקת גז מזגן‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל הבקרה"],P0EE5:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve B Control Circuit High","P0EE5 means the control line of refrigerant valve B carries more voltage than its commanded state allows. If the driver switches the coil to ground, a short to a 12 V feed is the classic reason; with some driver types an open coil can also float the line high.",["Control wire shorted to a 12 V feed","Open coil letting the line float high","Faulty output stage in the controlling module"],["Warning lamp on","Cabin or battery cooling not working as requested"],2,1,["P0EE4","P0EE3","P0EE6","P2613"],"שסתום חלוקת גז מזגן‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל הבקרה"],P0EE6:["A/C Refrigerant Distribution Valve B Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","P0EE6 is reported by the output stage itself: the switch that powers refrigerant valve B has measured too much current or got too hot and has protected itself. Modern smart high-side switches do this on chip; TI's TPS1HB16-Q1, for example, has an adjustable current limit between 4.4 and 49 A, shuts down at about 150 C junction temperature with roughly 20 C hysteresis, and flags the event on its diagnostic output.",["Shorted turns in the valve B coil raising the current","Partial short in the valve wiring","Control module output stage overheating"],["Warning lamp on","Valve B switched off, cooling branch not served"],2,1,["P0EE3","P0EE4","P0EE5","P266F"],"שסתום חלוקת גז מזגן‏ B — זרם או חום גבוהים מדי בדרייבר של מעגל הבקרה"],P0EE7:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0EE7 is the general circuit code for the second temperature sensor of the charging inlet. High-current inlets carry several sensors; the supplier Sailtran fits one PT1000 at the L1 pin and two independent PT1000 at DC+ and DC- of its CCS2 inlet, so A and B often stand for two different contacts. The code applies when the sensor B circuit is faulty in a way the module does not sort into low, high or implausible.",["Loose low-voltage connector between inlet and charger","Damaged sensor wire in the inlet harness","Failed sensor element B (usually means a new inlet)"],["Charging refused or stopped","Charge indicator shows a fault"],2,0,["P0EE8","P0EE9","P0EEA","P0EEB","P0D99"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EE8:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0EE8 is set when sensor B in the charging inlet delivers an electrically valid value that does not fit the situation. A plausibility test may compare it with sensor A on the neighbouring pin, with the outside temperature after a long rest, or with the heating expected for the charge current. If B reads far colder than A during a high-current session, or shows a sudden rise without current, it fails.",["Worn station plug heating one contact unevenly","Drifting sensor element B","Sensor B lost thermal contact to its pin"],["Charging reduced or aborted","Fault only at certain stations"],2,0,["P0EE7","P0EE9","P0EEA","P0EEB","P0D9A","P0EEE"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EE9:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0EE9 reports that the voltage of coupler temperature sensor B lies under its valid window. What that means thermally depends on the element. An NTC thermistor loses resistance when heated, so a low signal reads as an extremely hot pin.",["Sensor B signal wire shorted to ground","Moisture or water in the charging inlet","Shorted sensor element B"],["Charging not started or stopped","Reduced charging power"],2,0,["P0EE7","P0EE8","P0EEA","P0EEB","P0D9B"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EEA:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0EEA is the opposite case: the sensor B signal of the charging inlet sits above the valid window. In a pull-up design that is what an unplugged connector, a broken sensor wire or a short to a supply voltage looks like. With a PT1000, whose resistance grows with heat, a high reading suggests an overheated contact, so the charger may stop at once;",["Unplugged connector or broken sensor B wire","Signal wire shorted to a supply voltage","Open sensor element B inside the inlet"],["Charging aborted at once or refused","Fault in the charge indicator"],2,0,["P0EE7","P0EE8","P0EE9","P0EEB","P0D9C","P0EEE"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EEB:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0EEB catches a sensor B signal from the charging inlet that jumps or drops out for moments. A metal contact and its plastic housing have real thermal mass, so a change of many degrees within milliseconds cannot be a true temperature and points to the circuit. No manufacturer documents P0EEB publicly.",["Loose contact in the sensor B circuit or at the charger plug","Moisture that comes and goes in the inlet connector","Cracked sensor element with temperature-dependent contact"],["Charging stops sporadically","Fault cannot be reproduced at once"],2,0,["P0EE7","P0EE8","P0EE9","P0EEA","P0D9D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0EEC:["Battery Energy Control Module B Performance","P0EEC says a second battery energy control module, B, has failed its own internal checks. Large or split packs can carry more than one such controller, each watching cell voltages, temperatures and current of its part. No manufacturer documents P0EEC publicly.",["Software fault or watchdog reset in module B","Low or unstable 12 V supply to the module","Internal hardware fault of module B"],["MIL or hybrid warning on","Limited electric drive or charging"],4,3,["P0A1F","U0413","U0111","P0A7F"],"מודול בקרת אנרגיית סוללה‏ B — ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EED:["Hybrid/EV Battery System Discharge Circuit Performance","P0EED concerns the active discharge path on the high-voltage side, the circuit that drains the DC link capacitors once the contactors have opened so that no dangerous voltage lingers. The code says this path does not perform as intended, whereas P0C76 only judges how long the voltage takes to fall.",["Loose or arcing high-voltage cable terminal at the inverter module (FCA 08-107-17)","Discharge resistor or its switching transistor open or degraded","Internal fault in the inverter or its voltage measurement","Main contactor sticking closed so the bus stays charged"],["MIL or hybrid system warning on","Loss of propulsion or vehicle will not enter ready mode","Further high-voltage codes such as P0C76 or P0C77 stored at the same time"],4,3,["P0C76","P0C75","P0C77","P0AFA","P0D5E"],"פריקת מערכת סוללת מתח גבוה — ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EEE:["Battery Charger Coupler Temperature B Too High","P0EEE is the overheating verdict for the second measuring point of the charging inlet: sensor B works electrically, but the contact it watches has become too hot, so charging is throttled or stopped. Electrical faults of sensor B have their own codes P0EE7 to P0EEB; overheating at point A is P0E5F, at points C and D P2E4E and P2E53.",["Software of the charging diagnostic module misjudging the temperature (Ford SSM 55080)","Worn, dirty or corroded pins in the charging inlet","Loose crimp or screw connection behind the inlet pin raising contact resistance","Worn plug of the charging station heating the contact"],["DC fast charging stops or does not start","Charging power reduced while AC charging still works","Charging fault message in the cluster or app"],4,0,["P0E5F","P2E4E","P2E53","P0EE7","P0EEA"],"טמפרטורת מחבר טעינה מטען סוללה‏ B — ערך גבוה מדי"],P0EEF:["Drive Motor A Control Module Overtemperature","P0EEF reports that the control module of drive motor A, the inverter electronics that switch the motor phases, has run too hot. The temperature reading itself is considered valid; faults of the inverter temperature sensors, such as P0AEF, are separate codes. No manufacturer documents P0EEF publicly.",["Low coolant level or air in the power electronics cooling loop","Electronics coolant pump weak or failed","Long full-load operation such as towing or climbing in high ambient heat","Degraded power semiconductors or thermal interface inside the module"],["Reduced drive power or hybrid system warning","MIL on (manufacturer-dependent)","Electric drive switched off until the module cools"],3,3,["P0C11","P0C12","P0C13","P0A78","P0A1B","P0AEF"],"מודול בקרת מנוע הנעה‏ A — חום יתר"],P0EF0:["Battery Charger Coupler Unlock Disabled - High Voltage Present","P0EF0 describes a safety lockout rather than a broken actuator: the vehicle keeps the charging connector locked because it still measures hazardous voltage at the inlet. CharIN's CCS design guide permits unlocking only below 60 V, and EVgo's interoperability guide for vehicle makers asks for release once current is close to zero, the inlet is under 60 V DC and the station has changed to state B1.",["DC charging contactor stuck closed, inlet stays connected to the battery","Charging station does not switch off or discharge its output","Faulty inlet voltage measurement reporting voltage that is not there","Charging control software ending the session in the wrong order"],["Charging cable cannot be unplugged after charging","Charging fault message, sometimes only at certain stations","Emergency release of the connector needed"],4,0,["P0D95","P0D98","P0D91","P0D9E","P0D08"],""],P0EF1:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0EF1 is the general circuit code for the temperature sensor of drive motor C, the third traction motor on vehicles with three or more electric machines. It must not be confused with P2DB1, which means sensor C inside drive motor A. Volvo Trucks lists P0EF1 with failure type 09, component failure, for its electric trucks in remote diagnostic bulletin RD21-022;",["Loose, corroded or damaged connector of the motor C temperature sensor","Chafed or broken sensor wiring","Temperature sensor in the winding failed","Controller configuration not matching the installed motor (Volvo RD21-022)"],["Reduced drive power","Warning lamp for the electric drive","Motor temperature shown as invalid in the scan tool"],3,3,["P0EF2","P0EF3","P0EF4","P0EF5","P0A30","P2DB1"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EF2:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Range/Performance","P0EF2 flags a plausibility error of the drive motor C temperature sensor: its signal is electrically fine, yet the value does not fit what the controller expects. No manufacturer documents P0EF2 publicly.",["Thermistor characteristic drifted with age","Sensor no longer in thermal contact with the winding","Corroded pin adding resistance and shifting the reading","Comparison sensor such as coolant or oil temperature reading wrong"],["Warning lamp after a long standstill, often at the first start of the day","Usually no noticeable drivability change at first","Power limitation of motor C in some cases"],3,3,["P0EF1","P0EF3","P0EF4","P0EF6","P0A31","P0A2B"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים"],P0EF3:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0EF3 is stored when the signal voltage of the drive motor C temperature sensor drops below its valid range. With the usual NTC thermistor on a pull-up resistor, a low voltage stands for an extremely hot winding, so electrically the suspects are a short to ground, a pinched harness or a sensor with an internal short. No manufacturer documents P0EF3 publicly.",["Signal wire shorted to ground or motor housing","Thermistor shorted internally","Water or coolant in the connector bridging the pins"],["Drive power of motor C reduced immediately","Implausibly high motor temperature in the live data","Warning lamp on"],3,3,["P0EF4","P0EF1","P0EF2","P0EF5","P0A32","P0A2C"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EF4:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0EF4 reports a drive motor C temperature signal above the plausible window. For an NTC sensor on a pull-up this equals an impossibly cold winding, the picture produced by an open sensor, a broken wire, an unplugged connector or a short to the reference voltage. No manufacturer documents P0EF4 publicly.",["Sensor connector unplugged, loose or with pushed-back pin","Broken signal or ground wire of the sensor","Thermistor open internally","Signal wire shorted to the 5 V reference"],["Reduced power of motor C","Motor temperature stuck at the lowest value in the live data","Warning lamp on"],3,3,["P0EF3","P0EF1","P0EF2","P0EF5","P0A33","P0A2D"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EF5:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0EF5 covers a drive motor C temperature signal that jumps or drops out for short moments instead of staying wrong. Because of the thermal mass of a traction motor, the winding temperature can only change slowly; a step of many degrees within milliseconds therefore points to the wiring and not to the motor.",["Loose or corroded pins in the motor connector","Harness chafing at brackets or moving parts","Cracked sensor lead inside the motor that opens with vibration or heat"],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Short power interruptions of motor C, often on rough roads","Jumping motor temperature in the live data"],3,3,["P0EF1","P0EF3","P0EF4","P0A34","P0A2E"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — אות לא יציב במעגל"],P0EF6:["Drive Motor C Temperature Sensor Over Temperature","P0EF6 is not an electrical fault: the drive motor C temperature sensor delivers a believable value, but that value has passed the motor's protection limit. No manufacturer documents P0EF6 publicly. For motor B on the 2016 Cadillac ELR, GM sets P0A35 when the temperature exceeds 149 °C and does not fall back below a reset threshold, Type A, and only while the range check P0A31 is passing.",["Low coolant or oil level, or air in the motor cooling circuit","Failed coolant or oil pump, or clogged cooler","Long full-load phases such as towing, track use or steep climbs","Shorted turns in the stator winding generating extra heat"],["Clearly reduced power until the motor has cooled down","Warning about drive system overheating","MIL on (manufacturer-dependent)"],3,3,["P0A35","P0A2F","P0EF1","P0EF2","P0EEF"],"חיישן טמפרטורת מנוע הנעה‏ C — חום יתר"],P0EF7:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor B Circuit","P0EF7 stands for an electrical fault in the second input voltage sensor of on-board charger A, the measuring channel that reads the AC supply voltage coming from a wallbox, station or household outlet. Chargers designed for several phases or split-phase supply may carry more than one such channel; the first one, sensor A, uses P0D3D to P0D41.",["Fault in the voltage sensing stage inside the charger","Open or high-resistance signal line between sensor and controller","Corroded low-voltage connector at the charger"],["AC charging does not start or stops shortly after start","Charging fault message in the cluster or app","DC fast charging usually unaffected"],4,0,["P0EF8","P0EF9","P0EFA","P0EFB","P0D3D"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A B — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EF8:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0EF8 is set when input voltage sensor B of charger A delivers a value that is electrically valid but wrong for the situation. No manufacturer documents P0EF8 publicly. GM's rationality test for sensor A, P0D3E in its 2019 plug-in hybrid tables, illustrates the idea: while the car moves at 16 km/h or more, when no plug can be connected, the sensor duty ratio must stay between 8 and 12 %;",["Offset drift of the voltage sensing electronics in the charger","Partial internal fault of the charger input stage","Charger software misjudging the plausibility window"],["AC charging interrupted or slower than expected","Code may set while driving without any charging attempt","Charging fault message"],4,0,["P0EF7","P0EF9","P0EFA","P0EFB","P0D3E"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A B — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EF9:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor B Circuit Low","P0EF9 means the signal of input voltage sensor B in charger A has fallen below its valid range. No manufacturer documents P0EF9 publicly. GM's 2019 plug-in hybrid summary fails the matching sensor A code P0D3F when the duty cycle is 8 % or less but above zero, or stuck at 100 %, in at least 30 samples out of a window of 40 (Type B); a short to ground or a missing sensor supply produce that picture.",["Internal fault of the charger measuring stage","Sensor signal shorted to ground","Missing supply voltage of the sensing circuit"],["AC charging refused","Charging fault message right after plugging in","DC fast charging usually still possible"],4,0,["P0EFA","P0EF7","P0EF8","P0D3F","P0D27"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A B — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EFA:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor B Circuit High","With P0EFA, the signal from the second input voltage sensor of charger A, sensor B, lies above the plausible window. No manufacturer documents P0EFA publicly. GM's comparable check for sensor A, P0D40 in its 2019 plug-in hybrid tables, trips at a duty cycle of 92 % or more but below 100 %, or at exactly 0 %, in 30 of 40 samples, and is rated Type B.",["Defective measuring stage on the charger board","Open signal line pulled high by the input circuit","Signal shorted to a supply voltage"],["AC charging refused or ends after a few seconds","Charging fault message","Indicated charging power implausible"],4,0,["P0EF9","P0EF7","P0EF8","P0EFB","P0D40"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A B — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0EFB:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor C Circuit","P0EFB flags an electrical fault on the third AC input voltage channel of on-board charger A, sensor C. Channel A reports through P0D3D to P0D41, channel B through P0EF7 to P0EFA; a third channel fits chargers that read each phase of a three-phase supply separately. This is the catch-all circuit code, used when the signal is unusable without being sorted into range, low or high (P0EFC, P0EFD, P0EFE).",["Failed measuring channel C on the charger board (divider, amplifier or converter input)","Open or loose sense connection for this phase inside the charger or at the AC inlet harness","Charger software marking a valid channel as faulty"],["AC charging does not start or is limited","Possible reduced charging power on three-phase stations","Charging fault shown in the cluster or app"],4,0,["P0EFC","P0EFD","P0EFE","P0EF7","P0D3D"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A C — תקלה במעגל החשמלי"],P0EFC:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","With P0EFC, sensor C of charger A delivers an electrically sound signal whose value conflicts with the charger's other readings. No manufacturer documents P0EFC publicly. For sensor A, GM shows such a cross-check on the 2011 Volt: the charger module compares the AC peak voltage with two independent bulk voltage readings, and if both differ by more than 34 V for 512 ms within 640 ms, P0D3E is stored as Type A.",["Voltage dips, brown-out or power cut at the outlet or station during charging (GM PIC5803 for the sensor A code)","Faulty or poorly calibrated charging cord set or wallbox","Gain or offset drift of measuring channel C in the charger","Charger software needing an update"],["Charging stops part-way or does not reach full charge","Fault appears only at certain outlets or stations","Charging fault message, sometimes with a warning lamp"],4,0,["P0EFB","P0EFD","P0EFE","P0EF8","P0D3E"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A C — טווח/ביצועים לא תקינים במעגל"],P0EFD:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor C Circuit Low","P0EFD reports that the reading of input voltage sensor C in charger A sits below its plausible band. No manufacturer documents P0EFD publicly. GM's 2011 Volt charger module gives a reference for sensor A: once AC is detected at the inlet, a peak value under 90 V for 1500 ms within a 1875 ms window sets P0D3F, Type A with the warning lamp after one trip.",["Defective divider or converter input for channel C in the charger","One phase missing at the supply (tripped breaker or fault in the station or cable)","Sense path of channel C shorted to ground"],["AC charging refused right after plugging in","Charging works on a different station or outlet","Charging fault message"],4,0,["P0EFE","P0EFB","P0EFC","P0EF9","P0D3F","P0D27"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A C — אות נמוך במעגל"],P0EFE:["Battery Charger A Input Voltage Sensor C Circuit High","P0EFE is stored when channel C of charger A reports more input voltage than a household or station supply can deliver. No manufacturer documents P0EFE publicly. For sensor A, GM's 2011 Volt charger module trips P0D40 when the measured AC peak exceeds 422 V for 160 ms of a 200 ms window, rated Type A; this window is far shorter than the one for the low code, presumably because overvoltage endangers the input stage.",["Open lower resistor in the voltage divider of channel C","Sense signal shorted to the converter reference or a supply rail","Actual overvoltage or surge from the supply"],["AC charging stops within seconds of starting","Displayed supply voltage unrealistically high","Charging fault message"],4,0,["P0EFD","P0EFB","P0EFC","P0EFA","P0D40"],"חיישן מתח כניסת מטען סוללה‏ A C — אות גבוה במעגל"],P0FFF:["Not Documented - No Defined Meaning","P0FFF has no documented meaning. CARB requires the SAE J2012-defined codes such as P0xxx and P2xxx wherever possible and allows manufacturer-defined codes only in the ranges J2012 sets aside for them, such as P1xxx. None of the public J2012 title lists checked for obdex assigns a code in the P0Fxx block, and no vehicle maker, regulator filing or bulletin in the NHTSA archive uses P0FFF.",[],[],0,0,[],""]};export{e as default};
